window.CATALOG=[{"id":"J-01","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-01-1945-09","s":"J","no":1,"d":"1945-09","d2":"1945-10","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 1 (September-October 1945)","v":"","pg":203,"o":"Covering the foundational two months of the Allied occupation (September–October 1945), this initial summation records the establishment of General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers (GHQ/SCAP) in Tokyo under General Douglas MacArthur and the simultaneous installation of U.S. Army Military Government in South Korea under the XXIV Corps. In Japan, SCAP instituted indirect governance through the Japanese cabinet and Diet while enforcing rapid demilitarization, dissolving the imperial military, and issuing the historic 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive. That directive abolished thought-control police, repealed repressive statutes like the Peace Preservation Act, freed political prisoners, precipitated the collapse of the Higashikuni Cabinet, and initiated constitutional revision. The report portrays widespread economic paralysis across both territories: catastrophic coal deficits following the liberation of Korean and Chinese forced laborers, acute food shortages worsened by devastating September typhoons, uncontrolled black markets, and fiscal turmoil. In Korea, authorities confronted the crippling economic and political division along the 38th parallel, the rise of dozens of competing political factions, and popular resistance to allied trusteeship. For historians, the report provides invaluable baseline statistical surveys, structural blueprints of occupation administration, and candid accounts of early policy improvisation.","h":["General Douglas MacArthur established GHQ SCAP in Tokyo on 2 October 1945, activating nine Special Staff Sections to supervise non-military occupation policies.","SCAP issued the 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive, repealing the Peace Preservation Law, abolishing thought-control police, and freeing 507 political prisoners.","Prime Minister Prince Higashikuni's cabinet resigned en bloc on 5 October following SCAP's civil liberties directive, replaced on 9 October by Baron Kijuro Shidehara.","The four major Zaibatsu combines—Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda, and Sumitomo—agreed to voluntary dissolution plans transferring their holding company securities to a liquidation commission.","SCAP ordered the arrest of former Premier Hideki Tojo, his Pearl Harbor Cabinet, and approximately 300 suspected war criminals.","General MacArthur proclaimed U.S. Military Government in Korea on 7 September 1945, refusing recognition to domestic provisional regimes amid partition at the 38th parallel.","Japanese coal production plunged from 3.4 million metric tons in June 1945 to under 600,000 tons in October following the liberation of forced laborers."],"k":["SCAP","USAMGIK","Bill of Rights Directive","Zaibatsu dissolution","demilitarization","38th parallel","repatriation","Peace Preservation Law","Hideki Tojo","Shidehara Cabinet","coal shortage","price control"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Includes the title page, document documentation sheet, War Department transmittal memorandum, and general table of contents for the initial monthly summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,27],"pp":"1","s":"Executive summaries of SCAP organization, initial political and social reforms in Japan, economic stabilization and demilitarization, and civil administration in Korea."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[8,11],"pp":"3","s":"Details the establishment of GHQ SCAP in Tokyo, activation of nine Special Staff Sections, indirect control mechanisms via the Japanese Government's Central Liaison Office, and the administrative framework for Military Government in Korea."},{"t":"Section 2. Political and Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[12,17],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes political reforms including the 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive, fall of the Higashikuni Cabinet and formation of the Shidehara Cabinet, dismissal of 4,800 thought-police officials, release of political prisoners, emergence of new political parties, and early war crimes arrests."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[18,23],"pp":"13","s":"Reviews the demilitarization of war industries, initial plans for dissolving Zaibatsu holding companies, legalization of trade unions, severe coal and food shortages, strict foreign trade and exchange controls, and the closure of 29 wartime colonial banks."},{"t":"Section 4. Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[24,27],"pp":"19","s":"Surveys early operations of U.S. Military Government in South Korea, the impact of division at the 38th parallel, replacement of Japanese personnel, emergence of dozens of political factions, critical coal shortages, and prevention of winter typhus epidemics."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[28,45],"pp":"23","s":"Covers government administration, political reorganization, legal affairs, public safety, and intelligence activities in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[30,37],"pp":"25","s":"Details the operation of indirect administration through the Japanese Diet and Ministries, the fall of the Higashikuni Cabinet and formation of the Shidehara Cabinet, prefectural governorship shake-ups, early constitutional revision committees, proposed woman suffrage, and the emergence of competing political parties."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal, Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[38,45],"pp":"33","s":"Reports the complete demobilization of the armed forces, abrogation of peace preservation laws, release of political prisoners, purge of 4,800 secret police, arrest orders for Tojo and 300 war criminals, and implementation of press censorship."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[46,135],"pp":"41","s":"Comprehensive report on natural resources, industry, transportation, foreign trade, labor, communications, rationing and price control, and finance in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[48,59],"pp":"43","s":"Documents severe food deficits aggravated by September typhoon damage to rice crops, the establishment of authorized fishing zones amid fuel and net shortages, urban charcoal shortages, and a catastrophic drop in coal production following the liberation of forced laborers."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[60,77],"pp":"55","s":"Surveys industrial stagnation, authorization of monthly truck production, 24-hour shipyard repair operations, severe iron and steel declines due to coal shortages, textile deficits, plans to dissolve wartime control associations, and restrictions on scientific research including cyclotrons."},{"t":"Section 3. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[78,83],"pp":"73","s":"Assesses operational conditions across transportation sectors, noting relative railroad resilience despite passenger overcrowding, severe deterioration of motor vehicles, and the reduction of active merchant shipping to 908,000 gross tons allocated primarily to coastal coal and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 4. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[84,89],"pp":"79","s":"Outlines foreign trade controls limiting imports to vital necessities paid by approved exports, reports identified export surpluses such as raw silk, details emergency coal exports to Korea and Hong Kong, and reviews the legacy of wartime military trading companies."},{"t":"Section 5. Labor","lv":2,"p":[90,95],"pp":"85","s":"Covers labor procurement for Allied forces, projected unemployment of over four million, restoration of prewar protective labor standards, rebirth of independent labor unions under a unified federation, and violent strikes among liberated Korean and Chinese coal miners."},{"t":"Section 6. Communications","lv":2,"p":[96,111],"pp":"91","s":"Details emergency rehabilitation of war-damaged telephone and telegraph networks for Allied use, censorship of international radio circuits, redistribution of seized military vacuum tubes, slow manufacturing recovery of communications equipment, and restrictions on electronics research."},{"t":"Section 7. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[112,119],"pp":"107","s":"Reports the breakdown of commodity distribution, reduction of the staple food ration to 294 grams, critical newsprint and textile shortages, pervasive black-market pricing up to 260 times official ceilings, and efforts to reform price control mechanisms."},{"t":"Section 8. Finance","lv":2,"p":[120,135],"pp":"115","s":"Details fiscal distress, currency stabilization at ¥42 billion, closure and liquidation of 29 wartime colonial banks, impounding of gold and silver reserves, unsettled war damage insurance guarantees, and initial dissolution plans for the four major Zaibatsu combines."},{"t":"Part IV: Social Services","lv":1,"p":[136,177],"pp":"131","s":"Reports on public health, welfare administration, medical supplies, education reform, religious freedom, cultural preservation, and media liberalization in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[138,151],"pp":"133","s":"Surveys damaged hospital facilities, relief programs for over 83,000 persons, venereal disease control directives, emergency typhus prevention measures among repatriates, transfer of military medical stockpiles, and the delivery of relief supplies to Hiroshima."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[152,177],"pp":"147","s":"Details educational demilitarization and teacher screening, abolition of the Religious Bodies Law, plans to separate State Shinto from the state, dissolution of the Domei news monopoly, and the introduction of democratic radio, film, and theatrical programs."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[178,203],"pp":"173","s":"Surveys political developments, economic conditions, and social affairs under United States Army Military Government in South Korea."},{"t":"Section 1. Political","lv":2,"p":[180,191],"pp":"175","s":"Details the establishment of direct U.S. Military Government, the impact of partition at the 38th parallel, purging of Japanese officials and police, intense rivalry among 54 political parties, the return of Syngman Rhee, and widespread hostility toward trusteeship."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic","lv":2,"p":[192,197],"pp":"187","s":"Reports the establishment of a free market in rice, severe fertilizer and fuel deficits caused by severed supplies from North Korea, emergency coal shipments from Japan, reorganization of railroads and communications, and strict financial controls."},{"t":"Section 3. Social","lv":2,"p":[198,203],"pp":"193","s":"Covers the reopening of secondary schools with Korean-language instruction, expansion of Military Government press and radio information services, creation of the Bureau of Public Health, mass immunization against typhus, and refugee relief operations at Fusan and Seoul."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-01-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-01-1945-09-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":1,"d":"1945-09","d2":"1945-10","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 1 (September-October 1945): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":14,"o":"This initial monthly summation covers non-military activities in public health and welfare across Japan during September and October 1945, representing the earliest comprehensive SCAP assessment of Japan's domestic medical and social conditions following surrender. The report documents the acute deterioration caused by wartime neglect and air raids, including damage to over 1,000 civilian hospitals, paralyzed medical manufacturing, substandard nursing and dental training, and the disruption of municipal sanitation and water treatment. Occupation authorities moved quickly to establish administrative control by issuing directives for communicable disease reporting, narcotics prohibition, weekly hospital bed censuses, and the demilitarization of the Japanese Red Cross. Key public health concerns included widespread outbreaks of diphtheria and dysentery, epidemic typhus among Korean coal miners and repatriates, and severe shortages of food, clothing, and housing, though per capita nutrition was targeted at 1,800 calories daily. For historians, this report is invaluable as a baseline inventory of Japan's devastated postwar welfare infrastructure, the early mechanics of SCAP indirect rule, the repatriation of roughly 150,000 Koreans, and emergency medical relief delivered to atomic bomb victims in Hiroshima.","h":["On 6 September, approximately 12 tons of emergency medical supplies were dispatched through the International Red Cross for relief of victims in Hiroshima.","A SCAP directive issued on 12 October prohibited narcotic plant cultivation, banned narcotics export, and froze all existing opium, cocaine, heroin, and marijuana stocks.","SCAP ordered the Japanese government to inventory and transfer all military medical and dental stockpiles to the Home Ministry for civilian distribution.","Approximately 150,000 of an estimated 2,000,000 Koreans residing in Japan were repatriated through Fusan by 31 October using returning Japanese shipping.","Allied air raids destroyed or damaged 1,083 civilian hospitals with 53,007 beds across 46 prefectures, representing about 25 percent of Japan's civilian hospitals.","Communicable disease reports through 13 October recorded 57,729 cases of dysentery, 50,877 cases of diphtheria, 28,787 cases of typhoid, and 1,900 cases of typhus.","A standard prefectural Venereal Disease Control Ordinance was mandated, instituting compulsory weekly examinations and introducing penicillin therapy for high-risk groups."],"k":["Public Health and Welfare","Hiroshima relief","narcotics control","repatriation of Koreans","communicable diseases","typhus control","hospital administration","Japanese Red Cross","social insurance","medical supplies","venereal disease control","sanitary engineering"],"toc":[{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"133","s":"Reports the wartime neglect of health and welfare, return of military medical stockpiles to civilian use, implementation of narcotics control, and new directives for disease reporting and hospital bed tracking."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[2,3],"pp":"134","s":"Covers disorganized welfare administration, 83,502 persons receiving relief, coverage data for five social insurance programs, demilitarization of the Japanese Red Cross, and repatriation of approximately 150,000 Koreans."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[4,4],"pp":"136","s":"Details air raid damage to 1,083 civilian hospitals, treatment of 78,000 veterans in military facilities, and nationwide capacity of 3,335 hospitals with 356,143 beds operating at two-thirds capacity with sufficient medical personnel."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[5,6],"pp":"137","s":"Surveys collapsed veterinary services, reports 1944 livestock census figures and 1945 animal disease outbreaks, and notes that commercial animal slaughter is operating at only 10 percent of normal with substandard dairy sanitation."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"139","s":"Assesses war devastation to dental practice and equipment manufacture, noting that 75 percent of pre-adolescent children suffer severe dental problems and 7,000 of Japan's 23,000 dentists were displaced by air raids."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"140","s":"Reports substandard nursing education and midwife licensing, the absorption of 34,000 nurses into wartime military service, and the complete lack of female professional representation in nursing associations and licensing boards."},{"t":"Medical Supply","lv":1,"p":[9,9],"pp":"141","s":"Covers paralyzed medical supply manufacturing, dispatch of 12 tons of emergency supplies to Hiroshima, transfer of Japanese military medical stockpiles to civilian channels, and the 12 October directive freezing and controlling narcotics."},{"t":"Legal","lv":1,"p":[10,10],"pp":"142","s":"Reviews public health laws, institutes mandatory weekly examinations and penicillin therapy under a standard prefectural venereal disease ordinance, and studies civil service reforms to elevate technical specialists over administrative bureaucrats."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[11,14],"pp":"143","s":"Reports communicable disease totals including over 50,000 cases each of diphtheria and dysentery, epidemic typhus among Korean laborers, inadequate water chlorination, port quarantine protocols, and nutritional targets of 1,800 calories per day."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-03","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-03-1945-12","s":"J","no":3,"d":"1945-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 3 (December 1945)","v":"","pg":134,"o":"Monthly Summation No. 3 covers non-military activities of GHQ/SCAP in Japan and USAMGIK in southern Korea during December 1945. In Japan, decisive structural reforms reshaped political and social life. The extraordinary Diet session enacted landmark democratic legislation, including the Trade Union Law, the Agricultural Lands Adjustment Law, and an Election Law granting women suffrage. Concurrently, SCAP directives disestablished State Shinto, banned militaristic textbooks, ordered dissolution of eighteen Zaibatsu holding companies, and established the International Prosecution Section. War crimes proceedings intensified with the apprehension of 205 high-ranking suspects—precipitating Prince Fumimaro Konoye's suicide—and the first Yokohama military trial convicting Tatsuo Tsuchiya. Acute coal shortages, currency expansion, and low urban food reserves hindered industrial recovery. In Korea, the Moscow Conference agreement proposing a four-power five-year trusteeship triggered nationwide strikes and mass protests led by Kim Koo, while General Hodge repudiated the authority of the Korean People's Republic. For historians, this report provides crucial statistical baselines from the November 1945 census, primary documentation of demilitarization policies, and essential evidence of early Cold War tensions across Northeast Asia.","h":["The 89th Imperial Diet dissolved after passing the Trade Union Law, the Agricultural Lands Adjustment Law, and an Election Law establishing women's suffrage.","SCAP issued the Shinto Directive on 15 December, completely separating State Shinto from governmental administration, public finance, and school curricula.","Militaristic and ultra-nationalistic textbooks in morals, geography, and Japanese history were banned by SCAP directive on 21 December pending replacement.","Former Prime Minister Prince Fumimaro Konoye committed suicide on 16 December rather than surrender as an indicted major war criminal suspect.","In Japan's first war crimes trial at Yokohama, military commission judges convicted Tatsuo Tsuchiya of prisoner abuse and sentenced him to life imprisonment.","Eighteen major holding companies, including Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda, and Sumitomo, and 325 subsidiaries were designated as restricted concerns for dissolution.","Announcement of the Moscow Conference's four-power trusteeship plan provoked massive nationwide Korean protests, strikes, and demonstrations coordinated by Kim Koo's Anti-Trusteeship Committee.","Lieutenant General John R. Hodge publicly denounced the Korean People's Republic, declaring Military Government the sole lawful governing authority in South Korea."],"k":["SCAP","USAMGIK","Trade Union Law","Shinto Directive","textbook screening","agrarian reform","Zaibatsu dissolution","war crimes trials","repatriation","Moscow Conference","Korean trusteeship","Korean People's Republic"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"","s":"Title page, overall table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, maps, and tables for the December 1945 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[3,12],"pp":"1","s":"Executive summary of non-military activities in Japan and Korea during December 1945."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[4,4],"pp":"3","s":"General Orders No. 20 established the International Prosecution Section for war crimes trials, while Civil Administration in Korea created the Provincial Affairs and Korean Civil Service Sections."},{"t":"Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[4,6],"pp":"3","s":"The 89th Diet passed election, agrarian land, and trade union laws before dissolving; Prince Konoye committed suicide to avoid war crime arrest; and Tatsuo Tsuchiya received a life sentence in the first Yokohama war crimes trial."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"9","s":"Coal production rose 53 percent to 840,000 metric tons; the first Trade Union Law was enacted; eighteen holding companies and 325 subsidiaries were designated restricted concerns; and Bank of Japan note issues expanded."},{"t":"Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[9,10],"pp":"13","s":"State Shinto was separated from government support; militaristic textbooks in morals, geography, and history were banned; census figures showed sharp population declines in major cities; and over 654,000 repatriates departed Japan."},{"t":"Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"17","s":"The Moscow Conference's announcement of a four-power trusteeship provoked nationwide Korean resentment; political coalition efforts continued under Kim Koo; and free marketing of rice was ordered suspended as of 1 January 1946."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[13,23],"pp":"21","s":"Surveys political developments, public safety, legal affairs, and war crimes prosecutions in Japan."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,16],"pp":"23","s":"The 89th Diet passed women's suffrage, land reform, and trade union laws before dissolution; numerous parties formed, including the Japan Cooperative and New Japan Women's parties; and the Cabinet abolished the Board of Information."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"29","s":"Metropolitan Tokyo crime statistics showed no postwar crime wave; the Economic Police Section was eliminated during police demilitarization; political prisoners had their voting rights restored; and hidden military aircraft engines were seized in Shizuoka."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[19,23],"pp":"33","s":"Seven directives ordered the arrest of 205 war criminal suspects, bringing Sugamo Prison internments to 445; Tatsuo Tsuchiya received a life sentence at Yokohama; and trials of camp commanders Yuri and Furushima commenced."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[24,76],"pp":"42","s":"Surveys Japanese natural resources, manufacturing, commerce, finance, and industrial labor conditions."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[25,32],"pp":"45","s":"Government rice collection lagged at 30 percent of quota, prompting increased purchase prices and subsidies; soil surveys evaluated reclamation potential; and naval fuel oil releases stimulated commercial fish landings while curbing black-market sales."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[33,39],"pp":"61","s":"Surveys detailed operations at Japan's largest modern sawmill and the Amagi Imperial Forest; coal production rebounded 53 percent to 840,000 tons; and declassified wartime data revealed excessive overexpansion in subsidized mining industries."},{"t":"Industry","lv":2,"p":[40,52],"pp":"75","s":"Steel operations remained depressed at five percent of capacity; 228 vessels underwent repair in shipyards; the textile industry reorganized into a democratic association; and the Diet repealed sixteen major wartime industrial control laws."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[53,54],"pp":"99","s":"Coastal tankers transported vital petroleum products; seventeen radio navigation beacons were restored; domestic electric heater use caused urban distribution system overloads; and coal shortages restricted gas service in Tokyo to three hours daily."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[55,55],"pp":"105","s":"Imperial Ordinance established the Board of Trade to manage foreign commerce; imports were limited to salt and kerosene, while coal and silkworm eggs were exported; and an inventory was ordered for 50,000 exportable silk bales."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[56,60],"pp":"106","s":"Promulgation of the Labor Union Law catalyzed rapid unionization across mining and transport; workers settled major disputes at Yomiuri and Keisei Railway with wage increases; and occupational registration recorded 3.19 million unemployed persons."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[61,64],"pp":"116","s":"Trunk cable reconstruction advanced, Sender Composition Message service to the United States handled record holiday traffic, 1946 radio receiver production was projected at 3.1 million sets, and captured military communications equipment was disassembled for civilian parts."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[65,70],"pp":"125","s":"Critical urban food reserves dipped to two-to-eight days; government rice purchase prices and producer subsidies were increased; official fish ceiling prices were reestablished; and petroleum prices surged 280 to 400 percent following subsidy abolition."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[71,73],"pp":"136","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation expanded to 51.09 billion yen driven by government advances and commercial borrowing; monthly bank condition reporting was instituted; and repatriates were permitted to bring in yen-denominated savings books and bonds."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[74,76],"pp":"143","s":"Diet supplementary budgets funded food and salt production; military pensions were suspended, reducing war expenses; the Big Four Zaibatsu reported 3.15 billion yen in assets; and SCAP placed eighteen holding companies under strict financial restrictions."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[77,93],"pp":"148","s":"Reports on public health, medical administration, welfare, education, religious directives, and media democratization in Japan."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[78,86],"pp":"151","s":"DDT dusting checked typhus outbreaks among Hokkaido coal miners; military hospitals were transferred to civilian use, doubling bed capacity; the November census recorded 71,996,477 residents; and a 30,000-person nutrition survey was conducted in Tokyo."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[87,93],"pp":"169","s":"SCAP issued the historic Shinto Directive abolishing state sponsorship and banned militaristic textbooks in morals, geography, and history; an American educational mission was requested; and media programs broadcast the 'Now It Can Be Told' war history."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[94,104],"pp":"182","s":"Covers political, economic, and social activities under US military government in South Korea."},{"t":"Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[95,98],"pp":"185","s":"Announcement of a four-power trusteeship at the Moscow Conference ignited widespread Korean strikes and anti-trusteeship mass demonstrations led by Kim Koo; General Hodge repudiated the Korean People's Republic; and a National Bar Association was established."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[99,102],"pp":"193","s":"Despite a 15.97-million-koku rice harvest, market withholding forced the suspension of free trading and reinstatement of price ceilings; Japanese-owned agricultural lands were transferred to the New Korea Company; and Japanese branch banks were merged into Korean institutions."},{"t":"Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[103,104],"pp":"200","s":"A Korean was appointed Director of Education; Korean Pacific Press began independent operations with Associated Press; Seoul University graduated its first public health class; and emergency diphtheria antitoxin shipments were flown in from Japan."},{"t":"Charts, Graphs, Maps and Tables","lv":1,"p":[105,134],"pp":"Following page 206","s":"Collection of full-page charts, graphs, maps, and tables referenced throughout the monthly report, illustrating Japanese and Korean economic, social, and demographic statistics."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-03-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-03-1945-12-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":3,"d":"1945-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 3 (December 1945): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":18,"o":"This December 1945 monthly report details early U.S. Occupation efforts to stabilize public health, welfare, and sanitation across Japan amidst severe post-surrender dislocation. Major developments include the containment of typhus outbreaks in Hokkaido using DDT dusting, the introduction of quarantine protocols at repatriation ports, and the directed transfer of 292 Imperial Army and Navy hospitals to the Ministry of Health and Welfare to nearly double civilian bed capacity. SCAP established civilian relief responsibilities, initiated comprehensive urban nutrition surveys in Tokyo to evaluate starvation rumors, and distributed surrendered military medical reserves while combating severe shortages of penicillin, vaccines, and raw materials. Legally, SCAP enacted venereal disease control measures, protected technical health personnel from indiscriminate government layoffs, and received petitions for self-governance from the national nurses’ association. For historians, the report provides invaluable baseline data on early postwar conditions, including the complete November 1945 population census showing extensive wartime urban-to-rural displacement, detailed livestock and disease statistics, repatriation volumes of Koreans and Japanese nationals, and the initial institutional rebuilding of Japan’s public welfare administration.","h":["SCAP ordered the transfer of 292 Japanese Army and Navy hospitals with 156,725 beds to the Ministry of Health and Welfare for civilian use.","By 30 December 1945, 924,584 Japanese nationals returned to Japan, while 654,523 Koreans, Chinese, Formosans, and Ryukyuans were repatriated from the Home Islands.","The 1 November 1945 census recorded Japan's population at 71,996,477, showing major urban populations like Tokyo falling by up to 60 percent due to wartime dispersal.","A large-scale nutrition survey across Tokyo examined 33,000 residents and 2,777 households, finding no evidence supporting rumors of widespread civilian starvation deaths.","Emergency DDT dusting and quarantine procedures reduced weekly typhus cases among Hokkaido coal miners from 151 in early November to 44 in December.","SCAP intervened in Japanese Cabinet civil service retrenchment plans to prevent indiscriminate 50 percent personnel cuts among technical public health staff."],"k":["public health","repatriation","typhus control","DDT delousing","hospital administration","medical supplies","nutrition survey","1945 population census","venereal disease control","veterinary hygiene","public welfare relief","nursing education"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Contains the report title, table of contents, and organizational overview for the Public Health and Welfare section."},{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"151","s":"Reports disease incidence including typhus and smallpox control, post-mortem findings showing no starvation deaths, relief directives, and the ordered transfer of military hospitals to civilian use."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[2,2],"pp":"152","s":"Directs the Japanese government to administer destitution relief and distribute military reserve stocks, while reporting the repatriation of 654,523 non-Japanese from Japan and 924,584 Japanese nationals to the home islands."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[3,3],"pp":"153","s":"Civilian hospital capacity reached 142,926 beds with declining in-patient numbers, while SCAP ordered the transfer of 292 military hospitals with 156,725 beds to the Health Ministry for civilian use."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[4,7],"pp":"154","s":"Reports the 1945 livestock census showing declines in cattle and poultry, horse breeding operations, animal disease outbreaks including swine cholera in Osaka, and slaughterhouse and dairy inspection statistics."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"158","s":"Shortages of equipment, supplies, and housing hampered dental practices, though manufacturing facilities were deemed adequate if fuel and materials are provided; 295 dentists resumed practice in November."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"158","s":"Efforts began to standardize nursing education and prefectural licensing, a national refresher course trained 250 public health nurses from 46 prefectures, and the Nurses Association petitioned for self-governing status."},{"t":"Supply","lv":1,"p":[8,10],"pp":"158","s":"Details the distribution of returned military medical stocks and controlled drugs, emergency airlift of DDT for typhus control, production bottlenecks in biologicals and penicillin, and the impounding of raw opium stocks."},{"t":"Legal","lv":1,"p":[11,11],"pp":"161","s":"Covers the new venereal disease control ordinance, SCAP intervention to prevent indiscriminate 50 percent personnel cuts of technical staff in the Health Ministry, and deficient drug standards legislation."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[12,18],"pp":"162","s":"Covers communicable disease control including DDT dusting, water supply chlorination, Tokyo nutrition surveys refuting starvation rumors, port quarantine operations, and the November 1945 census revealing dramatic wartime urban-to-rural population shifts."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946)","v":"","pg":160,"o":"Summation No. 4 documents non-military activities in Japan and American-occupied Korea during January 1946, marking a pivotal phase of early postwar transformation. In Japan, the Occupation accelerated institutional democratization through the landmark 4 January purge directives, the Emperor’s New Year rescript renouncing divinity, the abolition of licensed prostitution, and preparations for national elections. Allied authorities promulgated the Charter for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, completed initial war crimes trials in Yokohama, and impounded 394 industrial plants for reparations. Concurrently, Japan confronted severe socioeconomic distress: acute food shortages, rampant black markets, and an expanding Bank of Japan note issue, counterbalanced by surging labor unionization and recovering coal production. In Southern Korea, the formal establishment of USAMGIK was overshadowed by massive public unrest following the Moscow Conference trusteeship announcement, while severe coal shortages and rice hoarding paralyzed the economy. For historians, this summation provides extraordinary empirical baselines: complete tabulations of Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda, and Sumitomo corporate subsidiaries, 20-year trends in agricultural output, national employment census data, nationwide disease maps, and the structural reorganization of Japanese education and police administration.","h":["The Emperor issued a New Year Rescript reaffirming the Meiji Charter Oath and explicitly renouncing the concept of imperial divinity.","SCAP issued landmark 4 January directives ordering the removal of militarists from public office and abolishing ultra-nationalistic political organizations.","The International Military Tribunal for the Far East was established by special proclamation, and its formal Charter was approved.","SCAP directed the Japanese Government to abrogate all laws permitting licensed prostitution and annul all contracts binding women to prostitution.","Allied authorities impounded 394 industrial plants, including 294 aircraft and arsenal facilities, as the initial step in interim reparations.","Nationwide protests and strikes erupted across Southern Korea opposing the Moscow Conference agreement on a four-power trusteeship.","The first war atrocity trials at Yokohama concluded, resulting in two death sentences and one life imprisonment sentence.","Bank of Japan note issue expanded to ¥56 billion as the government halted direct overdraft borrowing to curb inflation."],"k":["SCAP purge directives","Imperial Rescript","humanity declaration","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","USAMGIK","Moscow Agreement","trusteeship protests","licensed prostitution","reparations","zaibatsu dissolution","labor unions","rice collection","black markets","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover, table of contents, and list of 50 charts, graphs, maps, and tables for the January 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of non-military activities in Japan and Korea for January 1946."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"1","s":"SCAP established the International Military Tribunal for the Far East and the General Accounting Section, Sixth Army was deactivated with Eighth Army assuming control, and USAMGIK was formally established in Korea."},{"t":"Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"2","s":"The 4 January purge directives prompted reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet, the Emperor renounced divinity in his New Year rescript, licensed prostitution was abolished, national elections were scheduled, and initial war crimes trials concluded in Yokohama."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"7","s":"Allied custody was established over 394 industrial plants for reparations, coal output rose 41 percent, Bank of Japan note issue reached ¥56 billion, and new capital levy and war-profit tax bills were drafted."},{"t":"Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[10,11],"pp":"11","s":"A Tokyo nutrition survey showed half of food was obtained through black markets, typhus was checked while smallpox spread, 143 textbooks were cleared of militaristic content, and professional educators were appointed to lead the Education Ministry."},{"t":"Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[12,12],"pp":"14","s":"USAMGIK assumed direct control over local military government units, widespread anti-trusteeship demonstrations erupted, a Korean constabulary was formed, and emergency rice collection efforts struggled against hoarding."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[13,30],"pp":"17","s":"Political activities, civil administration, public safety, legal matters, and war crimes in Japan."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,20],"pp":"19","s":"SCAP's purge directives prompted a major reshuffle of the Shidehara Cabinet and local governments, the Emperor issued an Imperial Rescript denying divinity, Communist leader Sanzo Nozaka returned from exile, and Japan's administrative authority was limited to the four main islands."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"33","s":"Authorities combated widespread black-market operations and lethal bootleg whiskey, abolished the Keihei-tai auxiliary police while rearming civil police with revolvers, interned former German Ambassador Stahmer, and instituted mandatory censorship screening for all motion pictures."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,30],"pp":"41","s":"The Supreme Commander approved the Charter for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, SCAP ordered the arrest of 171 war crimes suspects including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka, and Yokohama military commissions handed down two death sentences."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[31,128],"pp":"53","s":"Economic activities, natural resources, industry, commerce, labor, and finance in Japan."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[32,48],"pp":"55","s":"Government rice collections lagged at only 20 percent of quotas due to rampant black markets, the Agricultural Association Law was amended toward democratization, and a fuel-oil link system was instituted to stimulate fish deliveries."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[49,69],"pp":"82","s":"Severe shortages of charcoal and lumber hampered urban heating and reconstruction, while coal production rebounded 41 percent following increased rations for miners, and geological surveys evaluated domestic nonferrous metal, cement, and quarrying capacities."},{"t":"Industry","lv":2,"p":[70,85],"pp":"119","s":"Allied authorities took custody of 394 aircraft plants, arsenals, and laboratories for reparations, while acute coal shortages suppressed industrial output across iron, steel, chemicals, textiles, and motor vehicles despite ambitious new housing construction targets."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[86,95],"pp":"151","s":"Railroads struggled with neglected rolling stock while planning widespread electrification and fare increases, commercial shipping remained crippled at a quarter of prewar capacity, and electric power consumption rose sharply due to residential space heating."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,97],"pp":"169","s":"Foreign trade operated strictly under SCAP direction, with imports arriving of U.S. Army surplus flour, diesel oil, and medicines, while initial exports of raw silk, coal, pitch, and mining timbers were dispatched to Korea, China, and Hong Kong."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[98,102],"pp":"175","s":"Labor unions expanded rapidly at two per day and planned a national federation, winning substantial wage increases to offset soaring living costs, while an official registration recorded 32.7 million employed and 3.3 million unemployed."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[103,106],"pp":"185","s":"International radio and telephone circuits were restored with the United States and Britain, signal facilities were overhauled to support occupational forces, and production goals were established for 2.1 million radio receivers despite critical vacuum tube shortages."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[107,117],"pp":"191","s":"Staple food collections lagged, leaving Hokkaido cities with as little as two days of reserves, while the government abandoned vegetable auctions to reimpose official price ceilings on perishable foods amid surging living costs."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[118,122],"pp":"211","s":"The Bank of Japan's currency issue expanded to a record ¥56 billion, direct government overdraft borrowing was halted, commercial banks restricted credit to essential producers, and liquidation of closed colonial and Axis financial institutions proceeded."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"221","s":"SCAP ordered strict fiscal retrenchment and the abolition of the Special Account for War Expenses, draft capital levy and war profit tax laws were prepared, and 41 companies including Oji Paper were added to the restricted Zaibatsu schedule."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[129,152],"pp":"233","s":"Public health, welfare, education, religion, and information media in Japan."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[130,142],"pp":"235","s":"SCAP directed curbs on rural-to-urban migration, the Welfare Ministry estimated 8,000,000 people needed relief, smallpox outbreaks triggered a nationwide vaccination drive, and repatriation through January processed over 1.2 million returning Japanese and 720,000 outbound foreign nationals."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[143,152],"pp":"261","s":"Professional educators were appointed to head the Ministry of Education, English translations were required for textbook screening, Shinto was severed from state control, and media campaigns promoted democratic ideals and explained SCAP's political purge."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[153,160],"pp":"279","s":"Political, economic, and social activities under USAMGIK in Southern Korea."},{"t":"Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[154,156],"pp":"281","s":"Intense political turmoil and strikes erupted across Southern Korea following the Moscow Agreement's trusteeship announcement, coalition talks between Right and Left stalled, USAMGIK established Special Judicial Officers, and recruitment began for the Korean Constabulary."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[157,158],"pp":"287","s":"Rampant rice hoarding persisted despite ceiling prices and incentive programs, foreign trade was placed under rigid licensing control, the Chosen Stock Exchange was dissolved, and severe coal shortages from Japan crippled southern Korean manufacturing."},{"t":"Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[159,160],"pp":"291","s":"Educational planning committees drafted democratic school programs, free civic newspapers achieved over one million circulation, procedures were established to recover cultural property taken by Japan, and emergency relief and disease control aided incoming northern refugees."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04-nlm","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01-nlm","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946)","v":"NLM copy","pg":306,"o":"Summation No. 4 documents non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan and southern Korea during January 1946, a pivotal month of institutional overhaul and democratic reform. In Japan, SCAP issued the sweeping 4 January purge directives banning militaristic organizations and disqualifying wartime leaders from public office, triggering a crisis and reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet. Emperor Hirohito issued his historic New Year Rescript renouncing imperial divinity, while SCAP abolished licensed prostitution, promulgated the Charter for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, and placed 394 industrial plants under custody for reparations. Severe economic strains persisted, marked by lagging food collections, runaway inflation, a ¥56 billion banknote issue, and critical coal and charcoal shortages, which catalyzed rapid labor unionization. In Korea, General Orders No. 1 formally integrated the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Nationwide civil unrest erupted following the Moscow Conference trusteeship announcement, prompting US-Soviet talks in Seoul, while severe rice hoarding forced emergency economic controls. For historians, the report provides invaluable contemporary statistical data, organograms, and candid accounts of early demilitarization, institutional democratization, and the divergent political trajectories of occupied Japan and Korea.","h":["SCAP issued the 4 January purge directives removing militarists from public office and dissolving ultra-nationalistic societies, prompting a reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet.","Emperor Hirohito promulgated the 1 January New Year Rescript reaffirming the Meiji Charter Oath and renouncing the doctrine of imperial divinity.","The Charter of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East was promulgated on 19 January to try major war criminals in Tokyo.","SCAP ordered the abolition of licensed prostitution on 21 January, nullifying all contracts binding women to involuntary servitude.","Allied authorities placed 394 Japanese aircraft factories, arsenals, and laboratories under custody as an initial step toward interim reparations.","General Orders No. 1 established the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK), centralizing civil administration under non-tactical military government control.","Violent Korean demonstrations erupted across southern Korea opposing the Moscow Conference five-year trusteeship proposal, threatening general strikes until calmed by USAFIK leaders."],"k":["SCAP purge directives","Imperial Rescript","humanity declaration","Shidehara Cabinet","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","licensed prostitution abolition","interim reparations","USAMGIK","Moscow Conference trusteeship","rice hoarding","labor unionization","Bank of Japan note issue"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Contains the title pages, printed table of contents for the entire report, and list of charts, graphs, maps, and tables."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[11,26],"pp":"1","s":"Introductory summary of non-military activities in Japan and Korea during January 1946."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[13,13],"pp":"3","s":"Reports the establishment of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, creation of the General Accounting Section, transfer of Sixth Army territory to Eighth Army, and establishment of USAMGIK in Korea."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[13,16],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the 4 January purge directives, the Emperor's New Year Rescript denying divinity, reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet, political party preparations for national elections, abolition of licensed prostitution, and war crimes apprehensions."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes coal production gains, Allied custody of 394 industrial plants for reparations, currency expansion to ¥56 billion, food collection lags, and rapid labor union formation."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"11","s":"Summarizes communicable disease control, Tokyo nutrition surveys, the abolition of licensed prostitution, textbook screening, Shinto directive compliance, and the appointment of professional educators to the Ministry of Education."},{"t":"Section 5: Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"15","s":"Summarizes the integration of USAMGIK, Korean demonstrations against the Moscow Conference trusteeship decision, judicial and constabulary reorganization, rice hoarding countermeasures, and educational planning."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[27,62],"pp":"17","s":"Division covering Japanese political developments, civil administration, public safety, legal opinions, and war crimes proceedings."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[29,42],"pp":"19","s":"Details the far-reaching impact of SCAP's 4 January purge directives, Shidehara Cabinet reorganization, the Emperor's renunciation of divinity, party maneuvers following Sanzo Nozaka's return, and the severance of Japanese administrative ties with overseas areas."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[43,50],"pp":"33","s":"Reports on public safety, black-market rings, crackdowns on poisonous bootleg liquor, disbanding of the auxiliary Keibei-tai, rearming civil police with revolvers, internment of former German Ambassador Stahmer, and media censorship."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[51,62],"pp":"41","s":"Details legal opinions on occupation law, apprehension of 171 war crimes suspects including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka, three Yokohama atrocity convictions, and the promulgation of the IMTFE Charter."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[63,242],"pp":"53","s":"Division reporting on natural resources, commerce, industry, public utilities, labor, communications, rationing, and finance in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[65,92],"pp":"55","s":"Analyzes historical food crop trends, lagging government rice collections caused by hoarding, Diet amendments democratizing the Agricultural Cooperative Association, critical vegetable seed shortages, and fishing fleet rehabilitation."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[93,128],"pp":"83","s":"Reviews timber zones, severe urban charcoal shortages, a 41 percent increase in coal production to 1.18 million tons, mineral mining operations, and surveys of hydroelectric resources."},{"t":"Section 3: Industry","lv":2,"p":[129,160],"pp":"119","s":"Reports taking custody of 394 plants for interim reparations, general production declines due to the New Year holiday and fuel shortages, rolling mill outputs, and national housing construction allotment plans."},{"t":"Section 4: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[161,178],"pp":"151","s":"Covers railroad rehabilitation and increased coal allocations, acute coastal shipping shortages, electric power production and labor settlements, and strict daily gas ration limits."},{"t":"Section 5: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[179,182],"pp":"169","s":"Reports the arrival of U.S. Army flour and fuel imports, the preparation of raw silk, silkworm eggs, and seedlings for export, coal exports to Hong Kong and Korea, and restrictions on pearl transactions."},{"t":"Section 6: Labor","lv":2,"p":[183,192],"pp":"173","s":"Surveys union formation expanding by two a day, preparatory meetings for a National Federation of Labor, wage-price disparities, worker 'production control' disputes, and seamen recruitment for repatriation."},{"t":"Section 7: Communications","lv":2,"p":[193,200],"pp":"183","s":"Details rehabilitation of war-damaged wire facilities, reopening of radiotelephone service to the U.S. and radiotelegraph to London, appointment of an Advisory Committee for Broadcasting, and postal service surveys."},{"t":"Section 8: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[201,220],"pp":"191","s":"Reports widespread staple food withholding, critical food shortages in Hokkaido, the results of a 16,020-family Tokyo nutrition survey, the re-establishment of vegetable price ceilings, and rampant black-market activities."},{"t":"Section 9: Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[221,230],"pp":"211","s":"Reports Bank of Japan note circulation reaching ¥56 billion, commercial bank credit restrictions, prohibition of government overdrafts with the central bank, and the liquidation of colonial banks."},{"t":"Section 10: Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[231,242],"pp":"221","s":"Covers SCAP fiscal directives limiting monthly General Account expenditures to ¥2 billion, the submitted 1946-47 budget, draft capital levy and war profit tax laws, national debt figures, and Zaibatsu restriction lists."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[243,288],"pp":"233","s":"Division covering public health, welfare, relief, social insurance, education, religion, cultural monuments, and information media."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[245,270],"pp":"235","s":"Details directives restricting rural-to-urban migration, relief for eight million needy citizens, repatriation movements, control of typhus and smallpox outbreaks, Tokyo nutrition surveys, and the abolition of licensed prostitution."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[271,288],"pp":"261","s":"Reports the appointment of professional educators to the Education Ministry, textbook screening and deletions, separation of Shinto from the state, broadcasting reorganization, and censorship of militaristic films."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[289,306],"pp":"279","s":"Division reporting on political, economic, and social developments in the U.S. Army Military Government zone in southern Korea."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[291,296],"pp":"281","s":"Covers the establishment of USAMGIK under General Orders No. 1, widespread Korean protests against the Moscow Agreement trusteeship decision, US-Soviet joint conferences, creation of the Korean Constabulary, and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[297,300],"pp":"287","s":"Reports severe rice hoarding and emergency distribution countermeasures, the formation of the Korean Agriculture Association, silk industry reorganization, mineral and electric power coordination, and dissolution of the Chosen Stock Exchange."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[301,306],"pp":"291","s":"Details educational planning conferences, teacher institutes, expansion of radio broadcasting (JODK) and weekly news publications, restitution of looted cultural assets, refugee disease control, and dissolution of the Japanese medical monopoly."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":26,"o":"This monthly report covers public health and welfare activities in Japan for January 1946 under the early Allied Occupation. Major developments include SCAP directives restricting civilian migration into devastated major cities, the Japanese Ministry of Health and Welfare’s estimate of 8,000,000 persons requiring relief, and the logistics of massive bilateral repatriation movements. The report documents major public health initiatives: addressing widespread smallpox outbreaks and regional typhus cases, expanding civilian hospital capacity by 73,502 beds through military facility transfers, standardizing biological products like penicillin and vaccines, and establishing rigorous controls over narcotics and returned Japanese military medical stocks. Notably, on 21 January SCAP issued a landmark directive abolishing licensed prostitution and voiding all contracts binding women to the sex trade. For historians, this summation provides critical statistical benchmarks, including nationwide 1945 communicable disease rates across 46 prefectures, comprehensive veterinary and social insurance figures, and detailed dietary data from a survey of 30,000 Tokyo residents revealing an average daily intake of 1,970 calories and extensive reliance on the black market.","h":["On 21 January, SCAP directed the Japanese Government to abolish licensed prostitution and nullify all contracts binding women to the practice.","The Ministry of Health and Welfare estimated 8,000,000 persons in Japan were in need of relief as of 1 January 1946.","By 27 January 1946, 730,458 foreign nationals had been repatriated from Japan, while 1,245,930 Japanese ex-servicemen and civilians had returned.","Since October 1945, civilian hospital capacity expanded by 413 facilities and 73,502 beds, largely through transfers of former military hospitals.","On 27 January, SCAP ordered comprehensive legislation establishing strict annual registration, licensing, and transaction accounting for all narcotic dealers.","Smallpox spread widely across Japan in January, with major epidemic foci in Hyogo, Aichi, Nagasaki, and Hokkaido prompting nationwide vaccination planning.","A December nutrition survey of 30,000 Tokyo residents showed an average intake of 1,970 calories, with nearly half obtained from black-market sources."],"k":["Public Health and Welfare","repatriation","licensed prostitution","relief","communicable diseases","smallpox","typhus","narcotic control","nutrition survey","medical supplies","social insurance","hospital administration"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Report header and printed table of contents for Section 1, Public Health and Welfare."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"235","s":"SCAP directed restrictions on migration to large cities, while Japan estimated 8,000,000 persons needing relief. Repatriation operations continued with 730,458 foreign nationals departing Japan and 1,245,930 Japanese returning."},{"t":"Hospital Administration","lv":1,"p":[4,4],"pp":"238","s":"Reconstruction and military hospital transfers added 73,502 civilian beds since October 1945, reducing bed occupancy below 40 percent despite stable patient totals."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[4,6],"pp":"238","s":"The section reviews dairy and animal disease legislation, summarizes December 1945 animal disease outbreaks, and catalogs veterinary biological production and estimates for 1944 to 1946."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"241","s":"Precious metals were released for dental use and dental goods received transport priority, while a warehouse fire destroyed a three-month supply of one-third of essential dental materials."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"241","s":"Japanese public health nursing regulations were evaluated, Red Cross first-aid manuals were approved for reprinting, and the Ministry of Health and Welfare organized three new graded public health nursing courses."},{"t":"Medical Supply","lv":1,"p":[8,13],"pp":"242","s":"Production and distribution of medicines were stimulated through priority allocations and price increases, while returned military medical stocks and narcotics were concentrated, inventoried, and placed under strict controls."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":1,"p":[14,14],"pp":"248","s":"SCAP directed the abolition of licensed prostitution and nullification of binding contracts, while the Ministry of Health and Welfare submitted a ministry-wide reorganization and personnel reduction plan."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[15,26],"pp":"249","s":"The report surveys 1945 communicable disease incidence, responds to smallpox and typhus outbreaks, and details public water rehabilitation, biological assay requirements, Tokyo dietary nutrition surveys, and venereal disease control."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-05","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-05-1946-02","s":"J","no":5,"d":"1946-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 5 (February 1946)","v":"","pg":158,"o":"Summation No. 5 covers non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan and the U.S. Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) during February 1946. In Japan, SCAP and the Shidehara Cabinet vigorously enforced the 4 January purge directives, disqualifying roughly 90 percent of former Diet members and dissolving 120 ultranationalist organizations, while postponing national elections to 10 April. Confronting rampant inflation and critical food shortages, the Japanese government promulgated emergency ordinances blocking bank deposits, mandating currency conversion, expropriating hoarded staple crops, and establishing price controls. Meanwhile, labor unions gained legal recognition under the new Labor Union Law, with membership approaching one million, as workers increasingly deployed 'production control' strikes. In social affairs, SCAP directed the abolition of licensed prostitution, implemented mass typhus control in Osaka, and expanded democratic media dissemination. In southern Korea, USAMGIK established the Representative Democratic Council headed by Syngman Rhee, enacted strict regulations on political parties, instituted forced rice collections, and concluded initial interzone administrative agreements with Soviet authorities. For historians, this report provides invaluable contemporary statistical data and policy documentation detailing the structural democratization, economic crises, and institutional transitions across postwar Japan and Korea.","h":["The Japanese Government enforced the 4 January purge directives, barring about 90 percent of former Diet members and ordering 120 ultranationalist organizations dissolved.","General elections in Japan were postponed from 21 March to 10 April 1946 to allow additional time for candidate screening and anti-corruption regulations.","The Supreme Commander appointed nine judges to the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, designating Sir William Flood Webb of Australia as President.","Emergency Imperial ordinances were promulgated blocking bank deposits, converting old yen currency, and authorizing the compulsory expropriation of hoarded staple foodstuffs.","The Japanese Government enacted legislation abolishing the system of licensed prostitution and nullifying all contracts between prostitutes and their managers.","In Korea, USAMGIK established the Representative Democratic Council under Syngman Rhee, and U.S. and Soviet commanders concluded agreements on interzone transportation and communication."],"k":["Political Purge","Constitutional Revision","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Currency Conversion","Deposit Freeze","Rice Expropriation","Labor Union Law","Production Control","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Abolition of Licensed Prostitution","USAMGIK","Representative Democratic Council"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"","s":"Contains the cover, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the February 1946 summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[3,14],"pp":"1","s":"Executive summaries of non-military activities in Japan and Korea during February 1946."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"5","s":"Reports no changes in GHQ SCAP organization, while USAMGIK redesignated several bureaus and created the Korean Economic Advisory Board."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[6,8],"pp":"5","s":"Summarizes political purge enforcement affecting 90 percent of former Diet members, postponement of elections to 10 April, political party maneuvers, civil service reform, and war crimes tribunal preparations."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[9,10],"pp":"11","s":"Summarizes lagging rice collections, modest gains in coal and steel production, emergency currency conversion and deposit freeze ordinances, and import-export planning for China and Korea."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"18","s":"Summarizes relief program consolidation, repatriation milestones, textbook screening, separation of Shinto from state control, and the expansion of democratic media programs."},{"t":"Section 5: Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[13,14],"pp":"19","s":"Summarizes USAMGIK reorganization, establishment of the Representative Democratic Council under Syngman Rhee, the US-Soviet conference, compulsory rice collection under Ordinance No. 45, and currency controls."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[15,33],"pp":"33","s":"Detailed reports on government administration, public safety, police, intelligence, legal affairs, and war crimes prosecutions in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[16,24],"pp":"33","s":"Details enforcement of the 4 January purge directives dissolving 120 militarist organizations, postponement of the general election to 10 April, political party platforms, civil service restructuring, and the Emperor's inspection tours."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[25,29],"pp":"43","s":"Reports civil unrest over food shortages, police raids on hoarded goods, surveys of the police system by American experts Lewis J. Valentine and Oscar Olander, and censorship operations."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,33],"pp":"53","s":"Details restriction of Japanese court jurisdiction over UN nationals, apprehension of 81 war crime suspects, first military commission trials in Yokohama, and appointment of nine judges to the International Military Tribunal."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[34,131],"pp":"61","s":"Detailed monthly reports on agriculture, industries, commerce, labor, finance, and economic stabilization in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"63","s":"Covers the 1945-46 rice collection reaching only 50 percent of quota, sweet potato production trends, fuel allotments for Hokkaido herring fleets, seed oyster culture in Miyagi, and democratization of fishermen's cooperatives."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,59],"pp":"79","s":"Surveys sawmill capacity and critical lumber shortages, February coal production reaching 1,364,000 metric tons, coal operator subsidies, petroleum exploration plans, and sub-marginal nickeliferous iron ore deposits."},{"t":"Section 3: Industry and Industrial Reparations","lv":2,"p":[60,61],"pp":"111","s":"Reviews industrial production limited by coal and labor shortages, establishment of the SCAP Reparations Branch, and Japanese government custody and maintenance of aircraft factories and arsenals designated for reparations."},{"t":"Section 4: Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[62,68],"pp":"115","s":"Presents production statistics and stocks for aluminum, copper, zinc, lead, and ferroalloys; crude and refined petroleum output; emergency construction plans; ship repair activities; and heavy chemical manufacturing."},{"t":"Section 5: Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[69,76],"pp":"129","s":"Reports on food processing including flour milling and sake brewing, declining pulp and paper production, sheet glass and refractories, reorganization of electrical manufacturing associations, and motor vehicle production and demand."},{"t":"Section 6: Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[77,84],"pp":"145","s":"Covers general textile declines following New Year shutdowns, preparations to receive 20,000 short tons of American raw cotton, raw silk ceiling price increases, and output data for rayon, wool, knitting, and leather."},{"t":"Section 7: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[85,94],"pp":"161","s":"Reports motor transport shortages, railway deficits prompting 250 to 300 percent rate increases, coastal shipping operations, hydro-electric load increases necessitating supplemental steam plants, and gas utility restrictions."},{"t":"Section 8: Communications","lv":2,"p":[95,101],"pp":"177","s":"Details reorganization of the Board of Communications, rehabilitation of long-distance carrier cables, expanded trans-Pacific radiotelephone service, communication equipment manufacturing, and financial statements of major communication companies."},{"t":"Section 9: Labor","lv":2,"p":[102,105],"pp":"191","s":"Reports promulgation of procedural regulations for the Labor Union Law, union membership approaching one million, cabinet debates over 'production control' strikes, wage adjustments, and coal miner recruitment."},{"t":"Section 10: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[106,107],"pp":"199","s":"Outlines foreign trade policies through the revolving Foreign Trade Fund, the 1946 export plan, shipments of raw cotton from the United States, and interim trade of coal, timber, and seeds with Korea and China."},{"t":"Section 11: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[108,119],"pp":"203","s":"Details Imperial Ordinance No. 66 authorizing compulsory expropriation of hoarded food, revised ceiling prices for consumer goods, coal allocation schedules, emergency urban housing construction, and economic police controls over street vendors."},{"t":"Section 12: Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[120,123],"pp":"225","s":"Documents emergency financial ordinances blocking bank deposits and enforcing currency conversion, rising Bank of Japan note issues, restrictions on financial mergers, and liquidation of colonial banks."},{"t":"Section 13: Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[124,131],"pp":"233","s":"Examines national budget adjustments under inflation, abolition of the Special Account for War Expenses, local government borrowing, Imperial Household asset appraisals, and anti-monopoly measures against the Big Four zaibatsu."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[132,147],"pp":"247","s":"Comprehensive monthly reports on public health, welfare, relief, educational reform, religious affairs, and information media in Japan."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[133,141],"pp":"249","s":"Details the unified public relief program, nationwide smallpox vaccination, mass DDT dusting to suppress a major typhus outbreak in Osaka, veterinary hygiene surveys, and abolition of licensed prostitution."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[142,147],"pp":"267","s":"Reports library censorship abrogation, textbook screening, abolition of the Home Ministry's Shrine Board, protection of cultural properties, and information campaigns on democracy, labor unions, and women's suffrage."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[148,158],"pp":"279","s":"Reports on political, economic, and social developments in the American occupation zone of Korea under USAMGIK."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[149,152],"pp":"281","s":"Details USAMGIK reorganization, political party regulation under Ordinance No. 55, formation of the Representative Democratic Council, US-Soviet joint conference agreements, and recruitment for the Korean Constabulary and Coast Guard."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[153,156],"pp":"289","s":"Reports compulsory rice collections under Ordinance No. 45, acute commercial fertilizer shortages due to the cutoff from North Korea, reopening of factories in Seoul, 75 percent railway curtailment, and deposit of Japanese currency."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[157,158],"pp":"297","s":"Documents school attendance exceeding pre-war levels, reopening of Ewha Girls College and Seoul College of Law, smallpox control with domestic vaccines, and municipal incorporation of refugee relief societies in Seoul."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-05-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-05-1946-02-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":5,"d":"1946-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 5 (February 1946): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":19,"o":"This February 1946 monthly report details public health, welfare administration, and disease control across occupied Japan during early post-surrender reconstruction. Key developments center on SCAP directives reforming Japanese relief administration into a centralized, non-discriminatory public assistance program that explicitly abolished military pensions and preferential treatment for ex-servicemen. To avert humanitarian crisis, Occupation authorities released former military stocks—including over 111,000 metric tons of cereals and 430,000 blankets—and supervised relief for foreign nationals. In public health, the report highlights an aggressive response to an escalating Osaka typhus outbreak through a mass DDT dusting campaign reaching over 90 percent of the city's populace, alongside rapid expansion of smallpox vaccine production exceeding 10 million doses monthly. The report also documents the rehabilitation of 2,579 bombing-displaced dentists, a new national public health nursing curriculum, and strict narcotics controls including nationwide heroin confiscation. Historians will find valuable quantitative baselines on social insurance funds, civilian hospital capacity, communicable disease curves, and repatriation flows tracking 829,000 repatriated foreign nationals and 1.46 million returning Japanese, illustrating the institutional transition from Imperial wartime systems to demilitarized civilian governance.","h":["SCAP directive overhauled public assistance, establishing a centralized relief system based strictly on need and abolishing preferential pensions and aid for military ex-servicemen.","Mass DDT dusting campaign in Osaka reached over 90 percent of the population to suppress a serious typhus outbreak exceeding 400 cases.","Distribution of relief reserves from returned military stocks began, releasing 430,000 blankets, 150,000 overcoats, 200,000 uniforms, and 111,457 metric tons of cereals.","Smallpox vaccine production accelerated sharply, surpassing the February monthly goal of 10,250,000 doses to combat widespread smallpox outbreaks across Japan.","Repatriation totals through 24 February 1946 reached 768,209 Koreans, 30,695 Chinese, 18,532 Formosans, 12,187 Ryukyuans, and 1,465,980 returning Japanese.","Japanese authorities ordered the nationwide confiscation of heroin and set deadlines to prosecute unauthorized possession of the narcotic drug.","Of 5,932 dentists displaced by wartime bombing, 2,579 were rehabilitated and re-established in practice despite lingering equipment shortages."],"k":["public assistance","typhus control","DDT dusting","smallpox vaccine","repatriation","social insurance","military pensions","narcotics control","public health nursing","veterinary inspection","medical supplies","displaced dentists"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Contains the cover title and printed table of contents for Section I (Public Health and Welfare) of Monthly Summary No. 5."},{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"1","s":"Reports a Japanese plan to restrict rural-to-urban migration, veterinary inspections in southern Japan, smallpox vaccine production increases, and an emergency mass DDT dusting campaign in Osaka that reached over 90 percent of residents to suppress typhus."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[3,5],"pp":"3","s":"Issues a directive establishing unified, non-discriminatory public assistance that eliminates veteran preferences, releases military blankets and clothing reserves, reviews social insurance fund balances, organizes relief for foreign nationals, and oversees mass repatriation movements."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[6,6],"pp":"6","s":"Reports inspections of medical supply storage and narcotic safeguarding at Tachikawa National Hospital and references civilian hospital bed capacity and patient statistics across prefectures."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[6,7],"pp":"6","s":"Surveys former Japanese army veterinary institutions and Tokyo Veterinary College, reviews November-December 1945 meat and milk inspections, and compiles animal disease incidence tables including a report on the 1945 equine encephalitis outbreak."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"8","s":"Reports increased sales of controlled dental items in January 1946 and tracks the rehabilitation and re-establishment of practice of 2,579 bombing-displaced dentists amid equipment shortages."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[9,9],"pp":"9","s":"Transmits the Ministry of Health's new public health nursing curriculum effective April 1946, assists plans for a Kyushu training institute, and prepares articles on Japanese nursing for publication."},{"t":"Supply","lv":1,"p":[9,12],"pp":"9","s":"Details distribution of new pharmaceuticals and returned military supplies, mandates heroin confiscation, accelerates smallpox vaccine manufacturing past 10 million monthly doses, and explores Korean pyrophyllite rock for regional DDT powder production."},{"t":"Charts","lv":1,"p":[13,19],"pp":"","s":"Presents statistical tables and charts on Japanese school expenditures, communicable disease incidence, medical supply production values, public health nurse training curricula, civilian hospital capacities, and repatriation flows through late February 1946."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-06","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-06-1946-03","s":"J","no":6,"d":"1946-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 6 (March 1946)","v":"","pg":131,"o":"Covering March 1946, this summation documents a critical transitional month in the early Allied occupation of Japan, marked by profound constitutional, political, and economic initiatives. Most notable is the publication of the SCAP-approved draft Constitution of Japan, featuring popular sovereignty, imperial demotion to a state symbol, and the Article 9 renunciation of war. In politics, intense campaigning and voter education unfolded ahead of the 10 April general election—the first featuring female suffrage—alongside rigorous surveillance by Occupation Forces and broadened purges of wartime elites. Economically, SCAP authorized the first post-war raw silk shipments to the United States to generate foreign credits, while Japanese authorities tackled soaring inflation through an emergency monetary conversion that drastically contracted Bank of Japan note circulation. The report also highlights the enforcement of the Labor Union Law, the investigation of zaibatsu war profits and indemnity claims, and aggressive public health measures deploying DDT dusting teams against typhus epidemics. For historians, this report provides exceptional primary documentation, including the full text of the draft constitution, pre-election campaign finance data, zaibatsu asset structures, and initial administrative preparations for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East.","h":["Publication on 6 March of the proposed new Japanese Constitution featuring popular sovereignty and the renunciation of war.","Preparations for the 10 April general election, including the enfranchisement of women and strict non-interference directives to Occupation Forces.","General Douglas MacArthur upholds the Manila military commission's death sentence for General Tomoyuki Yamashita for war crimes in the Philippines.","Arrival of the United States Education Mission headed by Dr. George D. Stoddard to assist in reforming Japanese schooling.","Bank of Japan note issue drops from ¥61.8 billion to ¥15.2 billion following emergency monetary conversion measures.","First post-war raw silk export shipments of 3,000 bales sent to New York to establish Japanese commodity credit.","Enforcement of the Labor Union Law on 1 March and inauguration of the tripartite Central Labor Relations Committee.","Establishment of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East courtroom in the War Ministry Building in Tokyo."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","General Election","Women's Suffrage","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","War Crimes Trials","Currency Conversion","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Labor Union Law","Raw Silk Exports","Typhus Control","United States Education Mission"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, general table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, maps, and tables for the March 1946 summation report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[4,10],"pp":"1","s":"Summary of monthly non-military developments in Japan."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"Establishes the Office of the Civil Property Custodian under SCAP and amends directives to include the Izu Islands north of and including Sofu Gan within Japan."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,8],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes publication of the draft constitution, preparations and party campaigns for the 10 April general election, extension of the purge directive, and establishment of occupation provost courts."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,9],"pp":"9","s":"Reviews relaxed industrial controls, rail transport gains, the first export shipment of raw silk to New York, emergency currency conversion, and the wartime asset growth of the Zaibatsu."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,10],"pp":"12","s":"Outlines smallpox and typhus control measures, repatriation progress, the arrival of the U.S. Education Mission, separation of Shinto from the state, and women's campaign activities."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[11,36],"pp":"15","s":"Political developments, civil administration, public safety, and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[12,27],"pp":"17","s":"Presents the full draft constitution renouncing war and vesting sovereignty in the people, political party campaigns and pre-campaign spending, surveillance rules for the general election, and new purge directives."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[28,29],"pp":"47","s":"Details black-market raids including seized raw silk and contraband opium, traffic safety drives, the arrival of Lewis J. Valentine's police advisory mission, and military censorship operations."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,36],"pp":"53","s":"Reports legal status of Korean residents, establishment of Eighth Army provost courts, apprehension and trial of military war criminals, Tomoyuki Yamashita's death sentence confirmation, and preparations for the Tokyo war crimes tribunal."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,114],"pp":"65","s":"Economic activities, natural resources, manufacturing, public utilities, commerce, and finance."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[38,41],"pp":"67","s":"Details the Japanese Government's proposed agrarian reform program, Kanto Plain soil surveys, Hokkaido herring season preparations, Bonin Islands whaling, and pearl culture operations at Mikimoto."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[42,49],"pp":"75","s":"Covers timber shortages, Kyushu pulp and paper production, coal mine strikes and output, the dismissal of female and child miners, and Pacific phosphate rock surveys for fertilizer manufacture."},{"t":"Industry and Industrial Reparations","lv":2,"p":[50,51],"pp":"89","s":"Reports slight industrial production gains, relaxed government business restrictions, establishment of the Japanese Reparation Council, and SCAP inspections of 152 university and government scientific institutions."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[52,59],"pp":"93","s":"Details output of steel, aluminum, copper, and petroleum, merchant shipyard repair activities, chemical and fertilizer production rates, and machinery maintenance inspections for potential reparations."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[60,71],"pp":"108","s":"Covers production and prices in food processing, pulp and paper, glass, electrical equipment, truck chassis, rubber goods, and leather goods facing fuel and raw material shortages."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[72,77],"pp":"122","s":"Reports production and mill inventories across cotton, silk, rayon, wool, and hard fibers, including preparations to receive 50,000 short tons of American raw cotton."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[78,86],"pp":"145","s":"Covers railway passenger and freight increases, severe automotive fuel shortages, expanding electric power generation, and proposed 60-cycle frequency unification across Japan."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,91],"pp":"161","s":"Details rehabilitation of domestic wire lines, overseas radiotelephone traffic, communications equipment manufacture, and the Board of Communications' request for elevation to cabinet ministry status."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[92,96],"pp":"171","s":"Reports enforcement of the Labor Union Law, establishment of the Central Labor Relations Committee, reinstatement of protective mining labor standards, and elimination of cut-rate convict labor."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[97,98],"pp":"179","s":"Details the shipment of 3,000 bales of raw silk to New York to establish commodity credits, salt imports from China, and the visit of the U.S. Food Survey Board."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[99,106],"pp":"182","s":"Covers revised staple food collection quotas and price increases, the release of imported American flour for Tokyo bread rations, and official price ceilings on fertilizers, rubber, and leather."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[107,110],"pp":"201","s":"Reports a sharp contraction in Bank of Japan note circulation following emergency currency conversion, external asset declarations, and liquidation procedures for closed colonial banking institutions."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[111,114],"pp":"207","s":"Analyzes the national debt, additions of 180 companies to the Schedule of Restricted Concerns, wartime expansion of the Big Four Zaibatsu, and pending industrial war damage indemnity claims."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[115,131],"pp":"217","s":"Public health, welfare, education, religion, and information media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[116,124],"pp":"219","s":"Details typhus control through Japanese DDT dusting teams, widespread smallpox vaccination, urban population entry controls, repatriation statistics, and the organization of the Japanese Council on Medical Education."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[125,131],"pp":"227","s":"Covers the arrival of the U.S. Education Mission, compliance with the State Shinto separation directive, women's political participation, political broadcast regulations, and theatrical labor unions."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-06","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-06-1946-03","s":"K","no":6,"d":"1946-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 6 (March 1946)","v":"","pg":28,"o":"Covering March 1946, this monthly summation documents the administration of the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) during a crucial phase of post-liberation occupation. Major political developments include the opening of the first formal sessions of the United States–Soviet Joint Commission at Duk Soo Palace on 20 March to discuss Korean independence, the dissolution of Japanese-era local councils under Ordinance No. 60, the reorganization of the Oriental Development Company into the New Korea Company Limited (Ordinance No. 52), and massive, orderly rightist celebrations on Korean Independence Day (Sam Il Day). Economically, the occupation government struggled with critical shortages of food, fertilizer, and timber. It enforced a controversial rice seizure and urban rationing program in Seoul, banned opium poppy cultivation, received emergency shipments of industrial chemicals and coal from Japan, and restarted damaged textile and industrial mills. In social affairs, extensive smallpox and typhus vaccination programs were executed, educational planning was instituted alongside bans on student political activism, and relief supplies were distributed. For historians, this report offers vital empirical data on repatriation flows, vested Japanese property management, interzone relations, and the administrative mechanics of early American military rule in South Korea.","h":["The US-Soviet Joint Commission convened on 20 March at Duk Soo Palace to discuss establishing a unified provisional Korean democratic government.","Ordinance No. 60 dissolved all Japanese-era provincial and local government councils and impounded their official records and funds.","The New Korea Company Limited was established under Ordinance No. 52, taking over the former Japanese Oriental Development Company's extensive assets.","Military Government announced plans to sell formerly Japanese-owned agricultural lands to landless Korean tenant farmers on long-term credit.","USAMGIK enforced a strict rice collection program and prepared urban rationing in Seoul amidst severe food shortages and public criticism.","Korean civil police were granted authority to arrest US military personnel caught committing serious felonies, while third-degree interrogation methods were prohibited.","Repatriation of Koreans from Japan reached 831,387 cumulative returnees, while Japanese nationals remaining in South Korea dropped below 1,200."],"k":["USAMGIK","US-Soviet Joint Commission","New Korea Company","Oriental Development Company","Repatriation","Rice rationing","Land reform","Ordinance No. 60","Representative Democratic Council","Syngman Rhee","Kim Kyuh Sik","38th parallel"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,6],"pp":"","s":"Title page, publication details, and printed table of contents with chart listing."},{"t":"Section 1: General","lv":1,"p":[7,10],"pp":"1","s":"Summarizes monthly developments in administration, political parties, repatriation, rice collection, industry, transportation, public health, and educational programs across South Korea."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities","lv":1,"p":[11,16],"pp":"5","s":"Covers the dissolution of local councils, creation of the New Korea Company, political party registrations, the convening of the US-Soviet Joint Commission, and ongoing repatriation."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities","lv":1,"p":[17,24],"pp":"11","s":"Details critical food and rice rationing measures, timber shortages, chemical imports from Japan, currency controls, reopening of textile mills, and railway and shipping operations."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities","lv":1,"p":[25,28],"pp":"19","s":"Reports on educational reforms and bans on school politicking, smallpox and typhus inoculation campaigns, distribution of US medical and food relief supplies, and public information."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-07","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-07-1946-04","s":"J","no":7,"d":"1946-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 7 (April 1946)","v":"","pg":142,"o":"This seventh monthly report covers non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan during April 1946, a pivotal month marked by the nation's first post-war general election on 10 April. The election witnessed widespread popular participation, the debut of female suffrage with thirty-nine women elected to the Diet, and a moderate conservative victory that precipitated the en bloc resignation of the Shidehara Cabinet on 22 April. The report documents major political and legal milestones, notably the publication of the simplified draft constitution, the expansion and amendment of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East charter, and the filing of formal indictments against twenty-eight major war criminals. Economically, SCAP monitored persistent urban food deficits, post-conversion currency circulation, the establishment of the Economic Stabilization Board, and the dissolution of wartime control companies alongside rapid trade union growth. In social affairs, the report highlights the United States Education Mission recommendations, public health campaigns containing typhus and cholera, and the repatriation of over two million Japanese nationals. For historians, this summation provides invaluable statistical data, government directives, and institutional records detailing Japan’s early post-war democratization and economic restructuring.","h":["Japan held its first post-war general election on 10 April with 72.1% turnout, electing thirty-nine women to the Diet under newly granted suffrage.","Premier Kijuro Shidehara and his Cabinet resigned en bloc on 22 April amid inter-party pressure following the general election.","An indictment containing 55 counts against 28 major Japanese war criminals was filed with the International Military Tribunal for the Far East on 29 April.","The United States Education Mission submitted its report recommending administrative decentralization, curriculum democratization, language simplification, and tuition-free coeducational schooling.","General Orders No. 18 established the Diplomatic Section as a Special Staff Section under SCAP on 18 April 1946.","An Imperial Ordinance establishing the Holding Company Liquidation Commission was approved by SCAP and promulgated by the Japanese Government on 20 April.","The first restitution of identifiable looted property occurred on 12 April when two Hong Kong fire engines were returned to British authorities."],"k":["General Election","Women's Suffrage","Shidehara Cabinet","Constitution Revision","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","War Crimes","United States Education Mission","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","Repatriation","Rationing","Labor Unions","Black Markets"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover page, errata sheet, general table of contents, and complete lists of charts, graphs, and maps for the April 1946 monthly report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly overview summarizing non-military activities across political, economic, and social sectors in Japan during April 1946."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"Reports the establishment of the Diplomatic Section as a Special Staff Section under SCAP and amendments expanding IMTFE tribunal membership."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the 10 April general election, political party campaigns, the Shidehara Cabinet resignation, constitutional draft revisions, civil service reforms, and war crime trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"Outlines gradual industrial production increases, food collection and fishery developments, emergency financial controls, currency circulation fluctuations, and holding company subsidiary restrictions."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"15","s":"Summarizes national public assistance expenditures, epidemic disease control, urban nutrition surveys, the US Education Mission report, and information programs educating voters on democratic election procedures."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,44],"pp":"21","s":"Covers Japanese government administration, the post-war general election, public safety, legal developments, and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,28],"pp":"22","s":"Covers the 10 April general election, post-election inter-party negotiations following the Shidehara Cabinet's resignation, the revised popular-style draft constitution, purge directive enforcement, and civil service reforms."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"21","s":"Reports black-market investigations, poisonous alcohol fatalities, military occupation court convictions, police and prison inspections, demobilization intelligence, and press and radio censorship operations."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[33,44],"pp":"29","s":"Details judicial system reorganizations, apprehension of 72 war crime suspects, trials in Yokohama and the Philippines, and the filing of a 55-count indictment against 28 major war criminals."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[45,119],"pp":"83","s":"Covers Japanese agriculture, forestry, mining, heavy industries, manufacturing, textiles, utilities, communications, labor, foreign trade, rationing, finance, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[46,50],"pp":"85","s":"Reports 1946 tea crop deterioration, fruit industry conditions, pyrethrum output, crop disease fungicides, government rice collections reaching 80.7% of quota, Bonin Island whaling, and heavy Hokkaido herring catches."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[51,54],"pp":"95","s":"Details veneer and plywood stockpiles, charcoal production goals, pulp shortages caused by the loss of Karafuto, coal production trends, and lead and zinc mining operations."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[55,63],"pp":"102","s":"Reports pig iron and steel output, aluminum reduction gains, nonferrous metal refining, petroleum production, cement manufacturing, merchant ship repairs, chemical fertilizer supplies, and industrial machinery surveys."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[64,74],"pp":"121","s":"Details seasonal peak sake brewing and beer output gains, pulp and paper production, doubled truck chassis assembly, tractor manufacturing, rubber goods production, and raw hide and leather shortages."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[75,81],"pp":"143","s":"Reviews yarn spinning and cloth weaving, cotton spindle expansion ahead of expected US raw cotton shipments, raw silk exports, wool weaving gains, and sundry goods production."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[82,86],"pp":"157","s":"Details government railway passenger increases and operating deficits, rolling stock repairs, coastwise shipping traffic, electric power demand reaching 99% of 1944 levels, and gas utility coal shortages."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,94],"pp":"165","s":"Covers occupation wire communications installations, long-distance telephone service delays, overseas radiotelegraph traffic, postal service operations, radio station inventories, and equipment manufacturing shortages."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[95,99],"pp":"181","s":"Reports the drafting of the Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill, union membership reaching 877,014, formation of national industrial federations, Central Labor Relations Committee rulings, and a multi-billion yen public works program."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[100,103],"pp":"191","s":"Details centralization of customs under the Ministry of Finance, designation of Boeki Cho as sole foreign trade agency, raw silk exports totaling 8,300 bales, and imports of US surplus food and Chinese salt."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[104,111],"pp":"199","s":"Covers staple food stock levels and reduction of household rations, distribution of imported foodstuffs, fresh vegetable and fish rationing in major cities, expiration of textile coupons, and official coal price schedules."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[112,115],"pp":"215","s":"Reports termination of compulsory bank lending, deposit withdrawal restrictions to curb currency circulation, Bank of Japan balance sheet changes, capital levy and tax revision proposals, and local government budget subsidies."},{"t":"Property Controls and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[116,119],"pp":"222","s":"Details custody of 6,146 United Nations properties, German assets, first restitution of looted Hong Kong fire engines to British authorities, addition of 65 subsidiaries to the restricted list, and Holding Company Liquidation Commission establishment."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[120,142],"pp":"281","s":"Covers Japanese public health, welfare, education, religion, cultural monuments, and media of expression during April 1946."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[121,133],"pp":"233","s":"Details national public assistance disbursements, child protection committees, repatriation progress of over 2.3 million Japanese, typhus containment through DDT dusting in Osaka, smallpox vaccinations, port quarantine of cholera cases, and urban nutrition surveys."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[134,142],"pp":"257","s":"Reports the US Education Mission recommendations, school textbook purges, procedures for returning Christian missionaries, civic education campaigns surrounding the general election, BCJ radio reorganization, and theatrical and library activities."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-07","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-07-1946-04","s":"K","no":7,"d":"1946-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 7 (April 1946)","v":"","pg":54,"o":"Number 7 of the Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea covers non-military operations during April 1946. Significant administrative reorganizations took place under Ordinance No. 64, renaming bureaus into departments, establishing the Police Department as an independent agency, devolving fire protection to municipalities, and completing the removal of Japanese personnel from civil service. Politically, the report details fifth and sixth communiqués of the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission regarding Korean party consultations under the Moscow Agreement, right-wing party unification under Kim Koo, and an assassination plot against Syngman Rhee. Economically, military authorities addressed severe food distress by legalizing open-market rice purchases in Seoul, appointing a National Food Administrator, and importing vital bituminous coal and raw materials from Japan, while grappling with critical fertilizer shortages. Social developments included record school enrollments, planned transitions to compulsory schooling and horizontal Korean script, and extensive DDT dusting and vaccination campaigns that curbed typhus and smallpox. For historians, this issue offers invaluable documentation on early American state-building, bureaucratic decolonization, interzone logistical frictions, food policy interventions, and the emerging Cold War diplomacy of the joint commission.","h":["Ordinance No. 64 redesignated all government bureaus as departments and sections as offices to streamline administration and expand responsibilities for Korean personnel.","All remaining Japanese personnel, who numbered 70,000 in September 1945, were completely removed from the South Korean civil service.","The U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission issued Communiqués 5 and 6, agreeing to consult democratic parties that sign declarations upholding the Moscow Decision.","A plot to assassinate right-wing leader Dr. Syngman Rhee was foiled when Korean police arrested seven armed members of a rival political party.","Open-market rice purchasing was legalized in Seoul, causing market prices to drop from 450 yen to between 200 and 225 yen per small marl.","The first espionage case against Military Government resulted in Kim Kei Cho's conviction, a 3,100,000 yen fine, and five years at hard labor.","Educational authorities scheduled compulsory schooling for September 1946 and initiated the adoption of the horizontal Korean alphabet for newly printed textbooks."],"k":["USAMGIK","U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission","Ordinance No. 64","Korean Civil Service","Rice rationing","Repatriation","Korean Constabulary","Korean Coast Guard","Syngman Rhee","Kim Koo","New Korea Company","Typhus control"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,12],"pp":"","s":"Covers the title pages, publication details, table of contents, and list of charts for Summation No. 7."},{"t":"Section 1. General","lv":1,"p":[13,18],"pp":"1","s":"Summarizes political, economic, and social developments in South Korea for April 1946, highlighting military government reorganization, open-market rice purchasing in Seoul, coal receipts from Japan, and disease control efforts."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":1,"p":[19,30],"pp":"7","s":"Details national administration reorganization under Ordinance No. 64, civil service localization, the 1946-47 budget, an espionage trial, police work, right-hand traffic rules, U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission communiqués, and Korean and Japanese repatriation."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":1,"p":[31,42],"pp":"19","s":"Reports rice distribution adjustments, land reclamation contracts, critical fertilizer shortages, commodity shipments from Japan, appointment of a National Food Administrator, and improvements in rail and coastal shipping operations."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":1,"p":[43,54],"pp":"29","s":"Covers record school attendance, planned educational reforms and Korean alphabet adoption, adult leadership courses, typhus vaccination and DDT dusting, public health clinics, and refugee and orphanage welfare programs."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-08","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-08-1946-05","s":"J","no":8,"d":"1946-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 8 (May 1946)","v":"","pg":128,"o":"Summation No. 8 covers non-military activities across occupied Japan during May 1946, documenting a pivotal month dominated by political transition and an acute food crisis. Following the resignation of the Shidehara Cabinet and SCAP's dramatic 4 May directive barring Liberal Party leader Ichiro Hatoyama from public office, protracted inter-party negotiations culminated in the installation of Shigeru Yoshida's coalition cabinet on 22 May. Widespread popular restlessness culminated in historic May Day demonstrations drawing 1,000,000 participants and the massive 'Food May Day' rally in Tokyo, prompting a stern warning from General MacArthur against political violence and an unprecedented radio appeal by the Emperor. Critical staple food shortages in metropolitan centers caused ration delivery delays of up to 11 days, requiring emergency releases of imported American flour. Meanwhile, the International Military Tribunal for the Far East commenced proceedings with the formal arraignment of 28 major war criminals. Other notable developments include the registration of 1.7 million trade unionists, the 26 April census adjusting Japan's population to 74 million, and comprehensive screening regulations to purge militarists from the educational system.","h":["SCAP barred Liberal Party leader Ichiro Hatoyama from public office on 4 May, opening the way for Shigeru Yoshida to form a coalition cabinet.","Arraignment proceedings opened on 3 May before the International Military Tribunal for the Far East against 28 major Japanese war criminals, including Hideki Tojo.","Approximately 1,000,000 citizens participated in nationwide May Day rallies, followed by the massive 'Food May Day' demonstration outside Tokyo's Imperial Palace on 19 May.","General MacArthur issued a stern warning on 20 May against undisciplined physical violence and mass intimidation by disorderly minority groups.","Severe urban food shortages caused an 11-day delay in Tokyo rice rationing, prompting emergency SCAP releases of 8,705 tons of imported wheat flour.","Imperial Ordinance 262 established screening committees to eliminate militarists and ultranationalists from teaching posts and education administration.","The 26 April national population census recorded 73,110,995 persons in Japan Proper, adjusted to 74,000,000 when accounting for uncounted foreign nationals and transients."],"k":["Yoshida Cabinet","Ichiro Hatoyama","Purge Directive","Food May Day","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","food shortage","repatriation","rice rationing","labor unions","population census","textile inventory","educational reform"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Contains the report title pages, general table of contents across four parts, and list of 68 charts, graphs, and maps."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly overview of non-military activities in Japan, summarizing SCAP administrative changes, political developments, economic trends, and social affairs."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"Reports General Orders No. 20 of 3 May discontinuing the Civil Intelligence Section as a Special Staff Section and transferring its duties to G-2."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the month's political affairs, including the purge of Ichiro Hatoyama, installation of the Yoshida Cabinet, May Day rallies, and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"Summarizes natural resources, industrial production, labor union registrations, critical food shortages, note circulation of ¥29.5 billion, and restricted concerns additions."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"15","s":"Summarizes the decline in typhus and smallpox, progress in repatriation, 26 April census results showing an adjusted population of 74,000,000, and educational screening."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,32],"pp":"19","s":"Covers Japanese government and civil administration, public safety and intelligence, and legal affairs and war crimes prosecutions."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,21],"pp":"21","s":"Details the cabinet crisis resulting in Shigeru Yoshida's coalition cabinet, SCAP's directive purging Ichiro Hatoyama, May Day mass rallies, and Diet convocation."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[22,24],"pp":"27","s":"Reports on mass food demonstrations, crimes against occupation forces, warnings against methyl alcohol in liquor, metropolitan police reorganization surveys, and media censorship."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,32],"pp":"42","s":"Covers SCAP legal opinions, district court convictions, orders apprehending 96 war crime suspects, trials in Japan and the Philippines, and IMTFE arraignments."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[33,110],"pp":"57","s":"Details economic developments across resource extraction, heavy and light manufacturing, textiles, transportation, communications, labor relations, foreign trade, rationing, finance, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[33,37],"pp":"59","s":"Reports declining rice collections at 83.4 percent of quota, emergency food and fertilizer measures, experimental aerial DDT spraying, and authorization of 416 steel fishing vessels."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[38,42],"pp":"69","s":"Covers timber and pulp production, coal mining difficulties and accidents under 'production control', coal stockpiles falling to a five-day supply, and petroleum output."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[43,52],"pp":"79","s":"Reports pig iron furnaces operating at only 3.5 percent capacity, nonferrous metal refining limited by coal shortages, cement and petroleum production, and shipyard repairs."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[53,64],"pp":"90","s":"Details food processing trends and the cabinet order halting beer and sake brewing for two months to conserve grain, alongside production statistics for diverse consumer goods."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[65,76],"pp":"123","s":"Covers the completion of a nationwide textile inventory revealing 258,000,000 square yards of fabric, cotton yarn price revisions, preparations for incoming American cotton, and silk reeling."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[77,82],"pp":"147","s":"Reviews registered motor vehicles, railroad traffic increases, coastal shipping, adequate hydroelectric power generation, and gas distribution system repairs reducing leakage from 80 to 40 percent."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"155","s":"Covers increases in domestic postal and telephone rates, expansion of overseas radiotelegraph links to Hawaii and France, telecommunications equipment manufacturing, and overseas assets."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[89,93],"pp":"167","s":"Reports registration of 3,789 unions with 1,690,985 members, May Day demonstrations involving 1,000,000 workers, draft Labor Relations Adjustment legislation, and wage adjustments."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[94,95],"pp":"177","s":"Details export shipments including 10,986 bales of raw silk to the United States and coal to Korea, imports of wheat and salt, and Hoover's relief mission."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[96,103],"pp":"181","s":"Reports critical urban food shortages with Tokyo-Yokohama ration delays of 10 to 11 days, emergency release of 8,705 tons of imported flour, and price control enforcement."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[104,106],"pp":"197","s":"Details Bank of Japan note circulation rising to ¥33.4 billion, deposit and loan trends under the currency conversion and deposit freeze program, and termination of war damage claims interest."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[107,110],"pp":"203","s":"Reports on the administration of 1,057 seized United Nations properties, custody of $255 million in precious metals, additions to restricted concerns, and reparations inventory plans."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[111,128],"pp":"211","s":"Covers public health, welfare relief, repatriation, census tabulations, educational purging and textbook screening, religious directives, and media of expression."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[112,121],"pp":"213","s":"Details public assistance expenditures, repatriation reaching over 3,000,000 Japanese, control of cholera at Uraga, DDT dusting and vaccine production, and the 26 April census."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[122,128],"pp":"223","s":"Reports Imperial Ordinance 262 establishing teacher screening committees, approval of revised school textbooks, directives prohibiting Shinto symbols on stamps and currency, and new radio programming."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-08","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-08-1946-05","s":"K","no":8,"d":"1946-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 8 (May 1946)","v":"","pg":112,"o":"Summation No. 8 covers May 1946 in South Korea under USAMGIK, with Lt. Gen. Hodge commanding USAFIK and Maj. Gen. Archer Lerch as Military Governor. It opens with a three-section digest (Part I) and then gives fuller Political, Economic and Social parts. The central political event is the 6 May adjournment sine die of the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission over whether anti-trusteeship groups could be consulted. The report prints the American statements in full and surveys Korean press and public reaction. Other developments include the abolition of the Offices of Planning and Provincial Affairs, decentralized property custody, railway consolidation, and the FY 1946-47 budget (¥11.8 billion expenditure against ¥8.0 billion income). The economic sections stress the worsening rice shortage, summer grain collection and barter plans, and a late-May cholera outbreak at Pusan. Other topics are repatriation and the refugee flow across the 38th parallel, police reform and public-order incidents, and a long background study of Korean postal, savings and insurance systems. Historians will find ordinance lists, judicial appointments, statistical tables and press summaries useful.","h":["On 6 May the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission adjourned sine die after the Soviets sought to exclude parties that had criticized trusteeship; General Shtikov left for North Korea on 8 May.","The FY 1946-47 national budget was approved: expenditure ¥11,800,212,360 against income ¥8,013,393,996, leaving a deficit of about ¥3.79 billion.","Cholera broke out at Pusan at the end of May (24 deaths in 74 cases by the 31st); the city was quarantined and its 400,000 residents were inoculated.","Staple foods under government control fell to 30,000 metric tons on 1 May; the daily ration was cut to 150 grams. A 1946 rice crop 23 percent below 1945 is forecast.","By 2 June, 736,527 Japanese had been repatriated and 973,923 Koreans had returned to South Korea. Refugees crossing the 38th parallel rose to 8,314 in the week ending 18 May.","Ordinances 70-90 were promulgated, including the ban on sale of females (No. 70), abolition of the Offices of Planning (No. 81) and Provincial Affairs (No. 74), and the railway merger (No. 75).","A rightist anti-trusteeship rally of about 50,000 in Seoul on 12 May ended in an unauthorized parade and attacks on Communist headquarters and three left-wing newspapers.","Counterfeiters who circulated ¥9,000,000 in bogus notes were arrested; their equipment was found in the building housing Korean Communist Party headquarters."],"k":["USAMGIK","Joint Commission","trusteeship","Moscow Decision","rice shortage","repatriation","38th parallel","cholera","national budget","property custody","police","political parties","Korean Broadcasting Corporation","postal savings","Hodge"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library flyleaves, the cover and title pages for Summation No. 8 (May 1946), the printed table of contents (p. 11) and the list of 17 charts, graphs and maps (p. 13)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month in three short sections (Political, Economic, Social Activities), each with numbered paragraphs summarizing the detailed Parts II-IV."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,18],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the abolition of two offices, new provincial property custody and information offices, the budget, new parties, the 6 May Joint Commission breakdown, Chinese refugee repatriation, ordinances, May Day and anti-trusteeship demonstrations, police and prisons."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"7","s":"Notes the acute rice shortage and poor crop outlook, the Samchok coal output, railway consolidation and the Korean Liberator train, 30,000 tons of controlled staples, summer grain collection, and Materials Control Corporation sales of ¥76 million."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"9","s":"Covers dissolution of Japanese labor relief associations, enlargement of the Seoul refugee camp, the Pusan cholera epidemic, education committees and teacher shortages, the folk dance revival, and press reaction to the Commission's adjournment."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,42],"pp":"11","s":"Detailed account of government structure, parties, interzone relations, the legal system and public safety."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"13","s":"Details administrative reorganization, new ordinances, the FY 1946-47 budget, new political parties and leaders, the full story and American statements on the Joint Commission breakdown, public reaction, and Chinese repatriation."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"13","s":"Provincial governors' meeting on food (2 May); abolition of the Offices of Planning and Provincial Affairs; provincial property custody offices; customs, foreign trade and railway ordinances; budget of ¥11.8 billion; Chonju and Chunchon made cities."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"15","s":"New groups (Pak Ei Won, Independent Labor-Farmers Party, youth alliances), Lyuh Woon Hong's split from the People's Party, Chosen Democratic Party's move to Seoul, registration totals (160 registered), and profiles of Rhee and Kim Koo."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":3,"p":[28,33],"pp":"16","s":"Account of the 6 May Joint Commission adjournment over consultation with anti-trusteeship groups, the full American statements of 27 April, 11 March and the breakdown, and Korean reactions of disappointment and rising support for continued Military Government."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"22","s":"228 Chinese refugees left Inchon on 1 May, completely clearing the Seoul-Inchon area; a vessel was sent to Tangku to repatriate 2,000 Koreans."},{"t":"Section 2: Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"23","s":"Covers the Department of Justice reorganization and judicial appointments, ordinances 70-90, May Day and anti-trusteeship demonstrations, counterfeiting and black-market arrests, police training and discipline, prisons, and Coast Guard and Constabulary matters."},{"t":"Legal","lv":3,"p":[35,37],"pp":"23","s":"Justice Department takes over the Legal Division; Koreans fill all posts but the Director; lists of appointments and resignations; ordinances on limitations, sale of females and 82 offenses; repatriation of 66 Japanese criminals."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[38,42],"pp":"26","s":"Demonstrations (including the 12 May rally of 50,000), Pusan civilian guards tried, burglaries and counterfeiting, traffic campaign, police academy graduates, awards, women's police, railroad police, prisons (12,150 inmates), and Coast Guard HQ move to Chinhae."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,82],"pp":"31","s":"Detailed account of natural resources, commerce and industry, finance and property control."},{"t":"Section 1: Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,56],"pp":"33","s":"Surveys agriculture and food crops, fisheries and fishing zones, forestry and reforestation, and coal and mineral production, with charts of staple food, fishing areas and lumber output."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[45,47],"pp":"33","s":"Rice shortage after the 1945 crop of 2,167,000 tons; 1946 rice forecast 23 percent lower; summer grain estimates; cotton acreage cut; 6,000 Army horses sold to farmers; new agricultural and sericulture schools."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[47,51],"pp":"35","s":"Fishing gear is one third of need; 3,500 tons of salt allocated; licensing system to 1948; plan to store 15,000 tons of dried fish; fishing area map and table; coastal features."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[51,53],"pp":"39","s":"6,420,000 hectares of forest; timber output far above sustainable cut; ten-year reforestation program; curbs on firewood cutting; first LST of Kangwon logs reached Inchon on 3 May."},{"t":"Mining and Geology","lv":3,"p":[53,55],"pp":"41","s":"Samchok anthracite reached 53,000 tons in April; ¥100 million credit for coal; table of 1944 mineral production; talc mill, mining law revision and survey of hidden Japanese ownership."},{"t":"Section 2: Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[57,78],"pp":"45","s":"Covers industry and textiles, rail, shipping and utilities, a long background study of communications (postal, savings, insurance), labor, imports and exports, and food rationing and price control."},{"t":"Industry and Textiles","lv":3,"p":[57,59],"pp":"45","s":"Inchon factories at 46 percent capacity; chart of factories; paper output of 550,000 pounds; flour, noodles and salt regulations and prices; fertilizer plants; textile technician shortage; silk industry at 15 percent."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"47","s":"Ordinance 75 merged three private railways into the national system with a one-third pay rise; Korean Liberator train debuted 20 May; ships returning from Japan; Chung Pyung plant producing 40,000 kW per day."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[61,71],"pp":"49","s":"Background on the Department of Communications, telephone and telegraph service, equipment shortages and Japanese-supplied materials, postal history and operations, postal savings plans and insurance, with 1945 and 1946 insurance statistics."},{"t":"Labor, Public Works and Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[71,75],"pp":"59","s":"Provincial labor chiefs' conference and a printing strike; highway equipment schools; tables of SCAP-authorized imports from Japan, coal shipments, graphite, talc and pyrophyllite exports, and Ordinance 82 foreign trade licensing."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":3,"p":[75,78],"pp":"63","s":"Food survey given to Herbert Hoover on 4 May; ration cut to 150 grams; summer grain collection plan and distribution table; barter of consumer goods for rice; fish, feed, silkworm egg and cotton price controls."},{"t":"Section 3: Finance and Property Control","lv":2,"p":[79,82],"pp":"67","s":"Covers impounding of coins, tax training, the FY 1946-47 budget table, provincial property custody offices under Ordinance 73, zaibatsu property, and Materials Control Corporation sales and warehouse thefts."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[83,104],"pp":"71","s":"Detailed account of public health and welfare, education and culture, and public information."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[85,94],"pp":"73","s":"Describes welfare organization and relief, repatriation figures, refugees from the north, hospitals, nursing, veterinary and medical supply matters, the Pusan cholera epidemic, disease statistics and vital statistics."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":3,"p":[85,89],"pp":"73","s":"Welfare Bureau structure, liquidation of Japanese labor relief societies, orphanages and housing, food relief of 11,000 tons, repatriation totals, 702 Japanese remaining, northern refugee flow, enlarged refugee camps, planned Korean Red Cross."},{"t":"Public Health, Hospitals and Nursing","lv":3,"p":[90,91],"pp":"78","s":"Health motto contest, hospital payment for emergency cases, return of runaway lepers to Sorokdo, Masan sanitarium, 40 public health nurses, nursing registry and textbooks."},{"t":"Medical Education, Veterinary Affairs and Medical Supply","lv":3,"p":[91,92],"pp":"79","s":"Fact-finding board on merging Seoul medical schools; veterinary reporting forms cut from 18 to 7; first commercial ether, cholera vaccine, 610 tons of medical supplies distributed, drug price schedule."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[92,93],"pp":"80","s":"Pusan cholera epidemic (24 deaths in 74 cases) with quarantine and inoculation; communicable disease table (smallpox 2,294 cases in late April); child health contest; mosquito control; vital statistics course."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[95,98],"pp":"83","s":"Covers dissolution of the Korean Committee on Education, curriculum revision, teacher training figures, school endowments, special schools, the library and museum openings, concerts, festivals and Korea's soccer tournament appearance."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[95,97],"pp":"83","s":"Higher school curriculum committee; 15,000 Japanese teachers replaced; Pusan raised over ¥5 million for schools; nautical schools reopened; Seoul School of Law and Politics closed; compulsory schooling planned for September."},{"t":"Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[97,98],"pp":"85","s":"Law library move suspended; National Museum of Anthropology opened 25 April; Kyongju excavations; symphony concerts; Admiral Yi monument returned to Yosu; Children's Day and folk dance festival."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[99,104],"pp":"87","s":"Covers provincial public information sections, the Mobile Education Unit, motion picture control, radio (Hodge and Eisenhower broadcasts, schedule charts), newspaper licensing and suspension, and press and public opinion on the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Public Information and Motion Pictures","lv":3,"p":[99,100],"pp":"87","s":"Ordinance 71 provincial information sections; 16-member Mobile Education Unit; political, health, industry and farm information programs; Ordinance 68 moved film control from Police to Information; newsreels and educational films."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[100,103],"pp":"88","s":"Hodge's statement broadcast 9-11 May and Eisenhower's speech 15-16 May; Korea Broadcasting Corporation weekly schedule and program chart; summer schedule of 10 hours 15 minutes; popular children's and music programs."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[103,104],"pp":"91","s":"Ordinance 88 newspaper licensing; Dai Dong Ilbo suspended three weeks; newsprint rationing; Korean newspaper reactions to the Joint Commission adjournment; tenant farmers' views on Japanese farmland; waning support for a separate southern government."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[105,105],"pp":"93","s":"Fold-out geographic map of Korea, printed page 93."},{"t":"Back Matter","lv":1,"p":[106,112],"pp":"","s":"Blank pages, University of Virginia Alderman Library due-date slip, library barcode page and end covers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet-5-5-high"},{"id":"J-09","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-09-1946-06","s":"J","no":9,"d":"1946-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 9 (June 1946)","v":"","pg":146,"o":"Summation No. 9 covers non-military activities across occupied Japan during June 1946, highlighting political democratization, acute economic distress, and critical public health interventions. Politically, the 90th Imperial Diet opened under the new Yoshida Cabinet, with Emperor Hirohito delivering an unprecedented opening address in colloquial Japanese and formally submitting the draft revised constitution. SCAP and the Japanese government intensified the political purge, disqualifying prominent Diet members, while the International Military Tribunal for the Far East commenced its substantive prosecution phase. Economically, Japan faced a severe food crisis characterized by depleted urban rice stocks and widespread absenteeism, prompting emergency SCAP releases of 47,807 long tons of imported food. Industrial recovery was inaugurated by the arrival of the first postwar shipments of American raw cotton, even as critical shortages of coal and raw materials hindered heavy manufacturing. Meanwhile, union membership expanded to 2.7 million workers, and major holding company properties were impounded. Socially, authorities contained cholera outbreaks in Kyushu through strict maritime quarantine and launched nationwide teacher screening. For historians, the report provides invaluable data on constitutional deliberations, labor unrest, zaibatsu dissolution, and food relief logistics.","h":["Emperor Hirohito formally opened the 90th Imperial Diet speaking in colloquial Japanese for the first time, submitting the government's draft revised constitution for deliberation.","The Japanese Government notified eight newly elected members of the House of Representatives, including prominent party leaders, that they were disqualified under the Purge Directive.","At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, Chief Prosecutor Joseph B. Keenan delivered opening arguments, and defendant Yosuke Matsuoka died of tuberculosis.","To alleviate desperate urban starvation, SCAP authorized the emergency distribution of 47,807 long tons of imported cereals and U.S. Army excess food stocks.","The first postwar shipments of American raw cotton arrived in Japan, initiating the processing and manufacture of cotton goods for export.","SCAP ordered the dissolution of the Japan Lumber Company and local lumber monopolies, restoring distribution functions to voluntary democratic associations.","Strict quarantine measures and naval patrols were instituted to contain a 25-case cholera outbreak on Tsushima traced to illicit shipping from Korea."],"k":["Diet","Constitution","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Food crisis","Repatriation","Raw cotton","Coal production","Labor unions","Zaibatsu dissolution","Cholera quarantine","Teachers' Manual"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, maps, and graphs for Summation No. 9."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[4,12],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly summary of political, economic, and social developments in Japan for June 1946."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"3","s":"SCAP reported no changes in its non-military organization during June 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"The 90th Imperial Diet opened with the Emperor speaking in colloquial Japanese and receiving the draft constitution. SCAP conferred with 31 women Diet members, eight Diet members were purged, and the Demobilization Board was created under Shidehara."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"SCAP extended authorized fishing grounds, while coal and lumber production remained below target. The first shipment of American raw cotton arrived, staple food reserves fell to 45.3 days, and currency circulation reached ¥40,644,000,000."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"15","s":"Public relief assisted 2,182,662 individuals, and cumulative Japanese repatriates reached 3,685,378. Teacher screening began in Chiba Prefecture, the Ministry of Education published the new Teachers' Manual, and cholera outbreaks in Kyushu were quarantined."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,43],"pp":"19","s":"Covers Japanese government administration, political parties, Diet proceedings, public safety, and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,27],"pp":"21","s":"The 90th Diet convened as the Emperor submitted the draft constitution and Prime Minister Yoshida outlined government policies. Eight Diet members and several peers were purged, the Demobilization Board was established, and the Emperor toured Chiba and Shizuoka."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[28,31],"pp":"49","s":"Mass demonstrations subsided following SCAP warnings, police raided Tokyo's Ueno black market, and crackdowns targeted lethal bootleg liquor. Recommendations were submitted for police decentralization, while prison death rates remained critically elevated."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[32,43],"pp":"57","s":"Military occupation courts heard 1,550 cases, and SCAP ordered the arrest of 56 war crime suspects. At the IMTFE, Chief Prosecutor Keenan presented opening statements, key witnesses testified on Japanese militarism, and defendant Yosuke Matsuoka died."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[44,128],"pp":"61","s":"Covers natural resources, industry, commerce, labor, finance, trade, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[45,49],"pp":"83","s":"A reconnaissance soil survey of Kyushu was completed, and SCAP doubled authorized offshore fishing and whaling areas. Hokkaido herring catches reached 207,947 metric tons, while live ko-ayu fry were transplanted from Lake Biwa to nationwide streams."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[50,55],"pp":"93","s":"SCAP ordered dissolution of the monopolistic Japan Lumber Company. May coal output reached 1,695,000 metric tons, failing minimum quotas due to food shortages, while a newly formed petroleum committee estimated national reserves at 10,000,000 kiloliters."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[56,64],"pp":"105","s":"Production gains occurred in pig iron, steel, aluminum, and cement, but severe coal shortages restricted metal refining and chemical output. Commercial nitrogenous fertilizer production reached half of prewar peak levels, and 250 merchant vessels were repaired."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[65,77],"pp":"123","s":"Canned fish and flour milling production increased, but shortages of fuel and containers constrained food processing. Output rose for paper, sheet glass, and bicycles, while 1,272 truck chassis and expanding supplies of farm implements were manufactured."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[78,91],"pp":"149","s":"The first post-war American raw cotton arrived for processing, boosting cotton cloth weaving by 50 percent for export. Raw silk production rose to 7,540 bales, rayon pulp manufacturing resumed, and silk fabric output more than doubled."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[92,95],"pp":"177","s":"Rail freight increased to 8,527,200 metric tons and rolling stock additions improved operations, while coastal shipping declined slightly. Hydroelectric generation supplied ample electric power, but 70 percent of municipal gas capacity remained idle for lack of coal."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[96,102],"pp":"195","s":"An Imperial ordinance elevated the Board of Communications to a Ministry effective 1 July. Higher domestic postal and telephone rates were introduced, radio receiver production jumped 40 percent, and the 400,000-member All Japan Communications Workers' Union was inaugurated."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[103,108],"pp":"199","s":"The Cabinet approved the Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill, and registered union membership reached 2,695,988 across 7,207 unions. The April census revealed 5,600,000 totally or partially unemployed persons, and plans were made to establish a separate Ministry of Labor."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"211","s":"Arrival of 21,712 bales of American raw cotton initiated Japan's textile export-import program. June exports included 11,053 bales of raw silk and 70,000 metric tons of coal, while imports provided 60,000 tons of food grains and Chinese wheat bran."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[112,118],"pp":"217","s":"Critical urban food deficits required emergency SCAP releases of 47,807 long tons of imported cereals and flour. New official vegetable ceilings eliminated subsidies, rubber goods prices were adjusted upward, and economic police recorded 144,353 rationing and price violations."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[119,122],"pp":"231","s":"Bank of Japan note issue reached ¥40,644,000,000, though the daily expansion rate slowed. The government operated under temporary budget authorizations and short-term loans, local tax reforms were enacted to raise ¥3,327,000,000, and national debt reached ¥203,064,000,000."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"239","s":"SCAP impounded properties of 10 zaibatsu families, placed four second-line holding companies under surveillance, and added 17 holding companies to the restricted schedule. Custody was reported over 594 potential reparations facilities and ¥63,024,347 in war criminal assets."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[129,146],"pp":"251","s":"Covers public health, welfare, repatriation, education, religion, arts, monuments, and media of expression."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[130,137],"pp":"253","s":"Welfare relief assisted 2,182,493 individuals, while repatriates returned to Japan reached 3,685,378. Rigorous port quarantines and sanitary cordons contained cholera outbreaks in Tsushima and Kyushu, 16,000,000 people were revaccinated against smallpox, and DDT duster production expanded."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[138,146],"pp":"269","s":"Teacher screening was launched in Chiba Prefecture, the Ministry of Education distributed the new democratic Teachers' Manual, and Imperial portrait depositories were removed from schools. The Tokyo war crimes trials and Diet constitution debates dominated public media."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-09","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-09-1946-06","s":"K","no":9,"d":"1946-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 9 (June 1946)","v":"","pg":100,"o":"Summation No. 9 chronicles United States Army Military Government activities in South Korea during June 1946, a month characterized by acute administrative reorganization, environmental crises, and political realignment following the adjournment of the Joint American-Soviet Commission. Major political developments include Ambassador Edwin W. Pauley's reparations inspection tour of North Korea, the closure of the Soviet Consulate in Seoul, moderate coalition initiatives led by Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung, and the creation of the National Economic Board. Catastrophic late June flooding along the Han River caused 88 deaths and devastated crops and infrastructure, compounding severe food shortages that necessitated the emergency distribution of 55,000 tons of imported American wheat. Meanwhile, military authorities vigorously combated a deadly cholera epidemic that claimed 651 lives by administering over 1.6 million inoculations. For historians, this report offers indispensable primary data, featuring detailed structural surveys of the Korean fishing industry under Japanese colonial rule, comprehensive cost-of-living and family expenditure analyses showing extreme inflation, thorough descriptions of the state monopoly bureau's operations in ginseng and tobacco, and cumulative statistics on postwar cross-border repatriation and rail rehabilitation.","h":["Ambassador Edwin W. Pauley inspected industrial centers in North Korea, finding no evidence of substantial equipment removals by Soviet occupation authorities.","The Soviet Consulate in Seoul was officially closed, and its diplomatic personnel headed by Vice-Consul Alexander S. Poliansky returned to Moscow.","Moderate political leaders Dr. Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung met to negotiate a center coalition between moderate Left and Right political factions.","The National Economic Board was established under Ordinance No. 90 to coordinate government economic policy, price stabilization, commodity allocations, and rationing.","Disastrous late June flooding along the Han River caused 88 deaths, destroyed 13,686 homes, and severely aggravated South Korea's critical summer food deficit.","Public health authorities contained a major cholera epidemic that caused 651 deaths, inoculating more than 1.6 million residents across Seoul, Pusan, and Taegu.","Approximately 55,000 tons of American wheat arrived in South Korea to supplement depleted government rice stocks in critical deficit areas."],"k":["USAMGIK","Edwin W. Pauley","Kim Kyu-sik","Lyuh Woon-hyung","Han River flood","cholera epidemic","National Economic Board","Monopoly Bureau","grain collection","repatriation","Soviet Consulate Seoul","Korean Constabulary"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the June 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[11,22],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly summary of political, economic, and social developments in South Korea during June 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[13,16],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes political developments including moderate unification talks between Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung, Ambassador Pauley's North Korea mission, closing of the Soviet consulate, civil service rules, and the suspension of Ordinance No. 72."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes economic affairs including severe flood damage to crops, arrival of 55,000 tons of American wheat, summer grain collection plans, establishment of the National Economic Board and Price Administration, and currency circulation trends."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"11","s":"Summarizes public health measures against a cholera outbreak, opening of Masan Tuberculosis Sanatorium, extension of college courses to four years, teacher summer schools, adult literacy initiatives, and repatriation movements."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,32],"pp":"13","s":"Detailed reports on government administration, political parties, interzone relations, legal affairs, and public safety."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,28],"pp":"15","s":"Details Korean Civil Service organization, National Economic Board creation, moderate coalition efforts between Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung, Chungchong-pukto local elections, Ambassador Pauley's reparations tour of North Korea, and the closing of the Soviet consulate in Seoul."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"19","s":"Covers the first post-liberation meeting of chief judges, suspension of Ordinance No. 72, disastrous Han River floods causing widespread destruction, scientific crime laboratory establishment, Railroad Police graduations, and renaming the Department of Internal Security."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[33,72],"pp":"23","s":"Detailed reports on agriculture, fisheries, forestry, commerce, transportation, communications, labor, price controls, and government monopolies."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[35,44],"pp":"25","s":"Reports flood damage to summer grain crops, rinderpest cattle vaccinations, extensive structural reorganization and licensing in the fishing industry, forest fire prevention measures, and a census of provincial sawmills."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[45,66],"pp":"35","s":"Covers industrial reconversion of war material, railroad operations and communications networks, submarine cables to Japan, postal exchange with North Korea, a ¥300,000,000 public work relief program, wheat imports, and cost-of-living inflation surveys."},{"t":"Finance and Government Monopolies","lv":2,"p":[67,72],"pp":"57","s":"Surveys Bank of Chosen note circulation and details the Monopoly Bureau's operations, organization, and revenues, covering state licensing, production, and distribution of red and white ginseng, leaf tobacco, and evaporated sea salt."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[73,92],"pp":"63","s":"Detailed reports on public health and welfare, educational reforms, cultural events, and public information activities."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[75,82],"pp":"65","s":"Documents control of a major cholera epidemic through mass inoculations and vaccine production, cumulative repatriation of nearly one million Koreans, the opening of Masan Tuberculosis Sanatorium, and medical licensing examinations."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"73","s":"Reports structural reorganization of the Department of Education, extension of college programs to four years, distribution of new Korean history textbooks, adult literacy campaigns, civic university fundraising, cultural preservation, and Tan Oh Day festivals."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[89,92],"pp":"79","s":"Details public health and grain collection information campaigns via radio and mobile units, motion picture licensing, Korean Broadcasting Corporation coverage, and press commentary on North-South unification and the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[93,100],"pp":"83","s":"Full-page reference map of Korea depicting provincial administrative boundaries, the 38th parallel division, and principal cities."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-10","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-10-1946-07","s":"J","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"","pg":133,"o":"Summation No. 10 covers non-military activities in Japan during July 1946, a critical transitional month marked by severe food shortages and historic institutional reforms. The report documents the acute food crisis that forced SCAP to release over 174,000 metric tons of imported cereals and canned goods across 20 deficit prefectures to prevent widespread starvation. In the Diet, intense debates centered on the draft constitution, particularly the concepts of popular sovereignty, the symbolic status of the Emperor, and the renunciation of war. Concurrently, the government substantially completed the purge of militarists from public office and established the Ministry of Communications. In the economic realm, coal shortages severely curtailed industrial output, prompting the concentration of all steelmaking at Yawata, while the arrival of the first 30,000 bales of American raw cotton began revitalizing the export textile sector. At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, prosecution witnesses provided harrowing eyewitness testimony regarding the Mukden Incident and the Rape of Nanking. For historians, this issue provides invaluable empirical data on May nutrition surveys, black-market gang warfare in Tokyo, labor union reorganization, and early Occupation governance.","h":["The Diet extended its session to debate the draft constitution, focusing on popular sovereignty, imperial prerogatives, and the renunciation of war.","Severe food shortages forced SCAP to release 174,708 metric tons of imported cereals and canned goods to 20 deficit prefectures.","At the IMTFE, eyewitnesses including Dr. Robert Wilson and Miner Searle Bates provided harrowing testimony on the 1937 Rape of Nanking.","Screening under the 4 January Purge Directive was substantially completed, barring prominent figures including Mitsu Kono and Haruo Matsui from public office.","Violent armed clashes between rival black-market gangs and police in Tokyo's Shibuya and Shimbashi districts left six dead, prompting stall shutdowns.","The Board of Communications was elevated to the Ministry of Communications on 1 July, with Sadakichi Hitotsumatsu installed as minister.","The first shipments of American raw cotton totaling 30,000 bales arrived in Japanese mills to begin manufacturing textiles for export."],"k":["Draft Constitution","Food Crisis","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Rape of Nanking","Black Market","Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill","Raw Cotton Imports","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","Repatriation","Ministry of Communications","Tanzan Ishibashi"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page, table of contents, and list of 77 charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly summary of political, economic, and social developments in Japan during July 1946."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Under SCAP General Orders No. 30 on 26 July, the Office of Civilian Personnel was discontinued as a special staff section and its functions assumed by USAFPAC."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"The Diet debated the draft constitution, focusing on popular sovereignty and the Emperor. The government substantially completed screening officials under the Purge Directive, while the Social Democrats rejected communist collaboration."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,11],"pp":"7","s":"Finance Minister Ishibashi outlined a recovery program emphasizing coal and fertilizer. Critical staple food shortages forced emergency releases of imported foodstuffs to 20 prefectures, while note circulation expanded rapidly."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Public assistance aided 2.19 million people, while cholera outbreaks occurred in Fukuyama and Yokosuka. The Ministry of Education established Citizens' Public Halls for adult education and banned deleted wartime textbooks starting 1 August."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,37],"pp":"17","s":"Political developments, public safety, legal reforms, and war crimes prosecution during July 1946."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,24],"pp":"19","s":"The Diet extended its session to 28 August to deliberate the draft constitution and Ishibashi's economic policies. Screening under the Purge Directive removed prominent figures like Mitsu Kono, and the Board of Communications became a ministry."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[25,28],"pp":"37","s":"Clashes between rival black-market gangs in Shibuya and Shimbashi resulted in gun battles, prompting police shutdowns of open-air stalls. Tokyo crime nearly doubled since January, and a major fire destroyed 400 houses in Iida."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[29,37],"pp":"45","s":"SCAP ordered 65 suspected war criminals apprehended, while the IMTFE heard testimony on the 1928 assassination of Chang Tso-lin and Japanese atrocities during the Rape of Nanking. General Myron C. Cramer replaced Justice Higgins on the Tribunal."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[38,115],"pp":"63","s":"Economic developments in industry, agriculture, commerce, finance, and resources during July 1946."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[39,42],"pp":"65","s":"Crop forecasts indicated improved 1946 harvests despite poor wheat and barley yields. June fish landings dropped due to the close of Hokkaido's herring season, while 263 newly converted fishing vessels operated by late July."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,48],"pp":"72","s":"Coal production reached 1.66 million metric tons in June, but severe shortages hampered metallurgy and chemicals. Seventeen of 25 tracked minerals increased output in May, while Imperial Oil struggled with high costs."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[49,57],"pp":"85","s":"To conserve coal, iron and steel production was concentrated at the Yawata plant. June chemical output met only 32 percent of minimum needs, while 12 steel ships and four launched vessels were completed."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[58,65],"pp":"103","s":"Machine tool production fell as manufacturers feared reparations, but truck chassis output rose to 1,483 units in June. Food processing fluctuated seasonally, and watch and clock manufacturing steadily expanded to 62,193 units."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,80],"pp":"119","s":"First imports of American raw cotton totaling 30,000 bales arrived for export yarn production. Raw silk output dropped slightly due to coal and food shortages, while rayon pulp output tripled."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[81,84],"pp":"149","s":"Rail freight decreased to 8.14 million tons in June, and coal shortages left 72.5 percent of gas manufacturing capacity idle. Electric power generation remained adequate with the completion of the Nagamatsu hydroelectric plant."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[85,90],"pp":"157","s":"The Board of Communications was elevated to ministry status on 1 July, and mail rates rose 200 percent. Critical shortages of vacuum tubes persisted, while long-distance telephone service was expanded for Occupation Forces."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[91,95],"pp":"169","s":"The Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill was submitted to the Diet amid union protests. Government workers secured a 50 percent wage increase, while SCAP established a 40-hour work week and paid leave for Occupation-employed laborers."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,98],"pp":"177","s":"Commercial imports surged in July with 50 ships delivering 326,000 tons of goods, primarily food, cotton, and phosphate. Exports included coal to Hong Kong and Korea, alongside shipments of raw silk and metals to the United States."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[99,108],"pp":"193","s":"A severe staple food crisis forced SCAP to release 174,708 tons of imported cereals and canned foods across 20 prefectures. Vegetable deliveries to urban legal markets plummeted after subsidies were removed."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"203","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation climbed to ¥47.23 billion by 20 July due to government deficit financing. SCAP permitted the dissolution of the Yokohama Specie Bank and reviewed proposed national tax revisions."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[112,115],"pp":"209","s":"SCAP approved articles for the Holding Company Liquidation Commission, restricted additional concerns including Titan Industrial, and revised the interim reparations list by deleting 60 plants and adding 21. Blocked precious metals were analyzed."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[116,133],"pp":"217","s":"Public health, welfare, education, religion, and information media activities during July 1946."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[117,124],"pp":"219","s":"Cholera outbreaks in Hiroshima and Kanagawa Prefectures were brought under control, while typhus incidence fell sharply. Nutrition surveys revealed an average daily intake of only 1,352 calories in Tokyo, and 524,005 Japanese repatriated."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[125,133],"pp":"235","s":"The Ministry of Education initiated Citizens' Public Halls, reinstated geography instruction with new textbooks, and screened teachers in Chiba. The Japan Newspaper Publishers and Editors Association adopted democratic canons of journalism."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-10","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-10-1946-07","s":"K","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"","pg":85,"o":"Summation No. 10 documents United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) activities during July 1946, reflecting an occupation administration grappling with natural disasters, epidemic disease, and acute economic distress. The month was dominated by the catastrophic aftermath of the late June floods, which disrupted rail and telegraph communications, aggravated severe grain shortages in urban centers like Seoul and Inchon, and spurred popular food demonstrations. Concurrently, USAMGIK combated a devastating cholera epidemic claiming nearly 5,000 lives, leading to widespread quarantines, port closures, and the suspension of mail exchanges with Soviet-occupied North Korea. Administratively, Military Government established an independent Department of Labor (Ordinance No. 97), designated Cheju-do as a separate province (Ordinance No. 94), and proposed an interim Korean legislative body. To rein in runaway inflation, the Military Governor enacted strict price ceilings on essential consumer goods and demonetized type A occupational currency. For historians, this issue provides indispensable quantitative data on retail prices, factory employment, and repatriation flows, alongside an extensive institutional retrospective on the reorganization and training of the South Korean National Police.","h":["The Military Governor proposed the establishment of a Korean legislative body composed of party representatives and elected provincial delegates.","Ordinance No. 97 created an independent Department of Labor and affirmed workers' rights to form unions and engage in collective bargaining.","Ordinance No. 94 established Cheju-do and the Chuja Archipelago as a separate province, restoring administrative autonomy lost in 1906.","The National Police Academy graduated its first class of 55 policewomen on 12 July and its first commissioned officer class on 19 July.","The Korean Red Cross Society adopted its new charter under International Red Cross principles, electing Dr. Kim Kyu Sic as its first president.","A major cholera epidemic reached 7,642 reported cases and 4,878 deaths, forcing quarantines, travel restrictions, and mass public inoculations across South Korea.","Price ceilings were instituted on key commodities, including cloth, shoes, soap, and matches, in an effort to combat severe post-flood retail inflation.","The trial of alleged Communist counterfeiters commenced in Seoul on 29 July, where police repulsed an attempted rescue by sympathizers."],"k":["USAMGIK","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Department of Labor","Cheju-do","National Police Academy","Korean Red Cross Society","Kim Kyu Sic","cholera epidemic","price controls","flood relief","counterfeiting trial","Bank of Chosen","Korean Constabulary","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,15],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for Summation No. 10."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[16,23],"pp":"1","s":"Summary of monthly political, economic, and social developments in South Korea for July 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[18,19],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the creation of the Department of Labor, the proposal for a Korean legislative body, and police academy graduations."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,21],"pp":"5","s":"Summarizes flood damage to railroads and communications, crop shortages, arrival of American food imports, and new consumer price ceilings."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[22,23],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes Korean repatriation progress, the Korean Red Cross charter, distribution of 3 million textbooks, and cholera public education campaigns."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[24,37],"pp":"9","s":"Administrative reorganization, political party developments, legal enactments, and internal security activities in South Korea during July 1946."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[26,27],"pp":"11","s":"Covers the reorganization of Commerce and Labor departments, separation of Cheju Province, foreign trade licensing regulations, and the proposed interim legislative assembly."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[28,37],"pp":"13","s":"Details Ordinances 91-99, the Seoul counterfeiters trial, a comprehensive review of National Police organization and training, and Constabulary flood relief operations."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[38,61],"pp":"23","s":"Reports on agriculture, fisheries, forestry, mining, manufacturing, transportation, communications, labor, foreign trade, rationing, and finance in July 1946."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[40,41],"pp":"25","s":"Reports on stunted grain crops in Kyonggi-do, cholera-related fishing bans at Inchon, halted forestry operations, and surveys of pyrophyllite and talc mines."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[42,61],"pp":"27","s":"Covers factory employment surveys, Kwangju textile output, flood repairs to rail lines, mail service resumption with the US, price ceilings, and foreign exchange restrictions."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[62,77],"pp":"47","s":"Reports on public health, epidemic control, welfare institutions, educational institutes, cultural properties, and public information media in July 1946."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[64,69],"pp":"49","s":"Details institutional child welfare, adoption of the Korean Red Cross charter, repatriation statistics, rinderpest containment at the 38th parallel, and the cholera epidemic response."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[70,73],"pp":"55","s":"Reports on summer teacher institutes, school attendance figures, the return of the US educational mission, restoration of national art treasures, and incoming missionaries."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[74,77],"pp":"59","s":"Details public health radio campaigns, mobile visual education film-strips, licensing of 242 periodicals, and press coverage of price ceilings and political unification."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[78,85],"pp":"63","s":"Full-page reference map of Korea showing provincial borders, the 38th parallel demarcation line, and major transportation routes."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-10-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-10-1946-07-v2","s":"K","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"Second copy","pg":84,"o":"Number 10 of the USAMGIK Summation covers July 1946, documenting a critical juncture in the American occupation of South Korea characterized by administrative institutionalization amid acute socioeconomic distress. Key political and administrative developments include the proposal by the Military Governor for an interim Korean legislative body, the separation of Cheju Island into an independent province, and the promulgation of Ordinances 97 and 98, which established the Department of Labor—recognizing collective bargaining rights—and reorganized the Department of Commerce into separate foreign and domestic commerce bureaus. The occupation faced compounding crises: late June floods severely crippled the national railway network and coal transport, while a virulent cholera epidemic claimed over 4,800 lives, prompting travel bans and the suspension of mail exchanges with Soviet-occupied North Korea. Acute food shortages in Seoul and Inchon forced increased reliance on imported American grain as domestic summer grain collections fell far behind targets. To combat spiraling inflation, USAMGIK imposed price ceilings on staple consumer goods and demonetized Type A military currency. For historians, this report provides invaluable quantitative data on commodity price inflation, police professionalization, prison industries, and massive cross-border repatriation movements.","h":["The Military Governor proposed establishing a representative Korean interim legislative body to enact ordinances and screen governmental appointments.","Ordinance No. 97 established the Department of Labor, officially recognizing the right of Korean workers to organize unions and engage in collective bargaining.","Cheju-do and the Chuja Archipelago were separated from Cholla-namdo and established as an independent province under Ordinance No. 94.","A nationwide cholera epidemic reached 7,642 reported cases and 4,878 deaths by 31 July, prompting strict travel quarantines and mass vaccinations.","Price ceilings were instituted on 15 July for essential consumer goods, including cotton cloth, rubber shoes, soap, matches, and electric light bulbs.","Type A occupational currency was demonetized and required to be deposited into financial institutions between 1 and 10 July.","The National Police Academy graduated its first class of 55 policewomen on 12 July and its first commissioned officer class on 19 July."],"k":["USAMGIK","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Department of Labor","Price control","Cholera epidemic","Repatriation","Cheju Province","National Police Academy","Bank of Chosen","Summer grain collection","Foreign exchange control","Korean Red Cross"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Contains the report title pages, library markings, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for July 1946."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly general summary covering political, economic, and social developments in South Korea during July 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the reorganization of the Department of Commerce, establishment of the Department of Labor, the proposal for a Korean legislative assembly, and police training milestones."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Summarizes agricultural conditions, flood damage to rail and wire communications, the ¥200,000,000 road relief program, lagging summer grain collection, price ceilings, and currency changes."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Highlights cholera containment, the adoption of the Korean Red Cross charter, teacher training institutes, educational missions, and press commentary on price controls and national independence."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,36],"pp":"9","s":"Reports on Korean administration, political party movements, statutory enactments, police reorganization, prison industries, and internal security operations."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Details the creation of the Department of Labor, establishment of Cheju Province, regulation of foreign trade licensing, and the Military Governor's proposal for an interim Korean legislative body."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[27,36],"pp":"13","s":"Lists Ordinances 92-99, describes food protests and the counterfeiting trial, and details the reorganization, training, and communications of the National Police, alongside prison administration and flood relief."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,60],"pp":"23","s":"Reports on natural resources, agricultural and mineral production, industrial activities, transportation repairs, communications, labor regulations, foreign trade, price controls, and financial conditions."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[39,40],"pp":"25","s":"Reports poor grain germination in Kyonggi-do, closure of Inchon fisheries due to cholera, halted forestry operations, Kyongsang-pukto coal output, and surveys of pyrophyllite and talc deposits."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[41,60],"pp":"27","s":"Covers textile manufacturing, flood repairs to railways, reopening of U.S. mail, Ordinance 97 on labor, food and coal imports, consumer price ceilings, and foreign exchange restrictions under Ordinance 93."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[61,76],"pp":"47","s":"Covers public health, welfare institutions, disease control, repatriation figures, educational programs, cultural property recovery, and public information media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[63,68],"pp":"49","s":"Reports increased welfare subsidies, Korean Red Cross reorganization under Kim Kyu Sic, cumulative repatriation figures, veterinary rinderpest cordons, cholera outbreak containment, and medical supply imports."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[69,72],"pp":"55","s":"Details school attendance data, teacher summer institutes, normal school sessions, return of the Korean Educational Mission, recovery of temple art treasures, and missionary arrivals."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[73,76],"pp":"59","s":"Reviews public education campaigns on food and cholera, visual education film strips, new radio programming, periodical licensing, and press reactions to price controls and Right-Left unification."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[77,84],"pp":"63","s":"Full-page administrative map of Korea showing provincial borders, principal cities, and the 38th parallel boundary."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-11","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-11-1946-08","s":"J","no":11,"d":"1946-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 11 (August 1946)","v":"","pg":149,"o":"This eleventh monthly report covers August 1946 non-military activities of the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers (SCAP) across occupied Japan, marking the close of the first full year of occupation. The month witnessed major constitutional and political milestones, notably the House of Representatives passing the amended draft constitution by 421 to 8 after intensive subcommittee debates, and the resignation of Speaker Senso Higai over political impartiality concerns. In the economic realm, acute food shortages prompted massive emergency distributions of imported American foodstuffs across 23 prefectures, while the Cabinet established the Price Board and submitted a second comprehensive land reform bill. U.S. cotton imports revitalized the textile industry, and SCAP placed 505 industrial facilities into reparations custody. At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, prosecution presented evidence on aggression in China, featuring key testimony from Henry Pu Yi regarding Kwantung Army domination of Manchukuo. Historians will find especially valuable the special 33-page first-anniversary appendix reviewing occupation policies, administrative mechanisms, and statistical progress from August 1945 through August 1946.","h":["The House of Representatives approved the amended draft constitution by a 421-to-8 vote on 24 August, transmitting it to the House of Peers.","House Speaker Senso Higai resigned following bipartisan criticism of his political intervention in the constitutional drafting process.","Former puppet Emperor Henry Pu Yi testified before the International Military Tribunal regarding the Kwantung Army's complete control over Manchukuo.","The Japanese Government submitted a second land reform bill to the Diet, limiting tenant holdings and enabling tenant farmer land purchases.","SCAP directives designated 505 industrial plants across eight categories, including 273 munitions facilities, for reparations custody.","Arrivals of imported American raw cotton stimulated a threefold increase in cotton yarn production over June levels.","The National Federation of Labor Unions and the National Congress of Industrial Unions held their inaugural national conventions in Tokyo.","A special first-anniversary appendix reviewed the occupation's achievements, demobilization, and economic recovery since MacArthur's landing at Atsugi on 30 August 1945."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","Land reform","Reparations","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Henry Pu Yi","Economic Stabilization Board","Price Board","Textile industry","Labor unions","Antarctic whaling","Food crisis","Review of the Occupation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"General monthly summary of political, economic, and social developments in Japan during August 1946."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"2","s":"Reports General Orders creating two executive offices under the Deputy Chief of Staff and re-establishing the Civil Intelligence Section as a Special Staff Section."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,9],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the House of Representatives' approval of the new constitution, the resignation of House Speaker Senso Higai, police reforms, and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,11],"pp":"9","s":"Summarizes agricultural land reform proposals, peak daily coal output, textile expansion spurred by U.S. cotton, Price Board establishment, and reparations plant designations."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Summarizes welfare relief, repatriation and cholera control, Cabinet-level Educational Reform Committee establishment, Shinto restrictions, and public information media programs."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,34],"pp":"17","s":"Detailed reports on government administration, public safety, legal developments, and war crimes prosecutions."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,21],"pp":"19","s":"Details House passage (421-8) of the amended draft constitution, the resignation of Speaker Senso Higai, Diet attendance, purge screening, and the Economic Stabilization Board."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[22,24],"pp":"33","s":"Reports on labor demonstrations in Tokyo, tighter controls against illegal Korean entry, black-market raids, proposed police reorganization, prison instructions, and fire and crime statistics."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"39","s":"Covers property rulings, family code revisions, provost court cases, war criminal apprehensions and trials, and IMTFE proceedings including Henry Pu Yi's testimony on Manchukuo."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[35,117],"pp":"59","s":"Detailed reporting on natural resources, industry, utilities, communications, labor, commerce, finance, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,40],"pp":"61","s":"Details the second land reform bill submitted to the Diet, the five-year sericulture plan, chemical fertilizer allocations, fish catches, and SCAP authorization of Antarctic whaling."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[41,46],"pp":"71","s":"Covers lumber and plywood production, wood pulp output, coal production reaching a daily postwar peak of 72,800 metric tons, mineral extraction, and oil exploration delays."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[47,55],"pp":"83","s":"Reports on coke production, fuel-constrained iron and steel output, nonferrous metals, petroleum refining, cement production, highway construction expenditures, and chemical and machinery manufacturing."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,64],"pp":"101","s":"Covers food processing and brewing, paper production, optical and medical instruments, refractories, electrical manufacturing, motor vehicles, tractors, and agricultural equipment."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[65,76],"pp":"119","s":"Details a threefold increase in cotton yarn output following American cotton arrivals, raw silk and rayon production, and manufacture of knit goods, clothing, and fish netting."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[77,80],"pp":"143","s":"Covers motor vehicle operations, rail traffic and new rolling stock, shipping, electric power generation and hydroelectric construction, and coal-shortage impacts on the gas industry."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[81,89],"pp":"151","s":"Reports Ministry of Communications reorganization and legislation, telephone and radiotelegraph expansion, radio station frequency assignments, postal services, and communications equipment manufacturing."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[90,93],"pp":"169","s":"Reports inaugural conventions of the NFLU and NCIU federations, Diet consideration of the Labor Relations Adjustment Bill, disputes over impending mass dismissals, and U.S. Labor Advisory Committee recommendations."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[94,95],"pp":"177","s":"Details incoming shipments of U.S. food, cotton, and phosphate rock, visits by Burma and Hong Kong trade missions, and exports of raw silk, tea, lead, and rubber."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[96,105],"pp":"181","s":"Details critical food shortages requiring imported food releases across 23 prefectures, establishment of the Cabinet-level Price Board, produce price controls, fertilizer link-distribution, and recovered hoarded goods."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[106,108],"pp":"201","s":"Reports currency circulation rising to ¥55.36 billion, commercial and savings bank statistics, liquidation of closed institutions, national budget and debt figures, and Imperial Household finances."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[109,117],"pp":"207","s":"Reports administration of Allied and Axis property, custody of precious metals and gems, Schedule of Restricted Concerns revisions, dissolution of control associations, and 505 plants designated for reparations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[118,131],"pp":"223","s":"Detailed reporting on public health, welfare, education, religion, cultural preservation, and media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[119,125],"pp":"225","s":"Covers public relief grants, the Daily Life Security Bill, repatriation and cholera quarantine for illegal Korean entrants, hospital and veterinary affairs, supply distribution, and nutrition surveys."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[126,131],"pp":"237","s":"Reports a Diet resolution on education, creation of the Cabinet Educational Reform Committee, Shinto financial restrictions, cultural property protection, and press, radio, motion picture, and theatrical activities."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of the Occupation (30 August 1945 - 31 August 1946)","lv":1,"p":[132,149],"pp":"249","s":"Special comprehensive review of policies, organization, and achievements during the first full year of the Allied Occupation of Japan."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[133,134],"pp":"251","s":"Reviews General MacArthur's landing, the surrender signing, activation of 14 special staff sections, and indirect administration through the Japanese Government."},{"t":"Actions and Accomplishments of the Occupation","lv":2,"p":[135,142],"pp":"255","s":"Summarizes the first year's progress in demobilization, repatriation, food relief, economic controls, public health, democratization of family and political life, education, police reform, and war crimes prosecution."},{"t":"Economic Progress","lv":2,"p":[143,149],"pp":"271","s":"Surveys one year of economic recovery across coal and fuel production, heavy industries, manufacturing, textiles, transportation, labor, public works, communications, finance, and reparations."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-11","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-11-1946-08","s":"K","no":11,"d":"1946-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 11 (August 1946)","v":"","pg":126,"o":"This eleventh monthly summation covers United States Army Military Government activities in South Korea during August 1946, marking the first anniversary of liberation from Japanese colonial rule. Significant political events include the mass celebration of Liberation Day on 15 August, the ceremonial return of the 1910 Annexation Treaty and nine historic Korean royal seals, and published correspondence between Lieutenant General John R. Hodge and Soviet Colonel General I. M. Chistiakov regarding deadlock in the Joint Commission. The administration established the Department of Public Works (Ordinance 104) and the Property Claims Commission (Ordinance 103), while General Hodge directed the transfer of operational administration to Korean personnel. Economic reporting focuses on post-flood agricultural recovery, National Food Regulation No. 2 instituting the 1946-1947 rice collection system, and the extensive holdings of the New Korea Company. Social affairs highlight containment of a severe cholera epidemic that caused nearly 7,000 deaths, the founding of Seoul National University (Ordinance 102), and mass repatriation. A special one-year appendix comprehensively reviews occupation policies since September 1945. For historians, the issue provides critical primary documentation on Korean civil administration, political factionalism, educational reform, and public health management.","h":["Over one million Koreans celebrated the first anniversary of liberation on 15 August in Seoul, marked by the return of historic royal seals.","Lt. Gen. John R. Hodge urged the Soviet command to reconvene the Joint Commission, rejecting ideological restrictions on Korean democratic participation.","General Hodge ordered the transfer of operational civil administration to Korean officials, leaving American personnel in largely advisory roles.","Ordinance No. 102 formally established Seoul National University, merging nine existing colleges and institutes under a unified Board of Regents.","Military Government promulgated National Food Regulation No. 2, establishing local quotas and administrative procedures for the 1946-1947 rice collection program.","Ordinance No. 103 established a seven-member Property Claims Commission with final judicial authority to settle disputed Japanese property titles.","Public health officials battled a major cholera epidemic, recording 10,648 cases and 6,894 deaths while distributing nearly ten million vaccine units."],"k":["USAMGIK","Joint Commission","Liberation Day","John R. Hodge","Seoul National University","Cholera Epidemic","Rice Collection","Property Claims Commission","New Korea Company","Repatriation","National Police","Korean Constabulary"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the August 1946 monthly summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,26],"pp":"1","s":"Summary overview of political, economic, and social activities in South Korea during August 1946."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes party leader resignations, Liberation Day observances, Hodge-Chistiakov Joint Commission letters, the creation of the Property Claims Commission, and public safety."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes post-flood crop rehabilitation, factory openings, sericulture goals, National Food Regulation No. 2, currency expansion, and New Korea Company asset custody."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Covers cholera containment efforts, the establishment of Seoul National University under Ordinance No. 102, Korean textbook distribution, and repatriation progress."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[27,42],"pp":"13","s":"Reports on civil government administration, political developments, legal reforms, and public safety operations."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[29,34],"pp":"15","s":"Details the establishment of the Department of Public Works, political party resignations, the massive 15 August Liberation Day parade, and correspondence between Hodge and Chistiakov."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"21","s":"Reports Ordinance No. 103 creating the Property Claims Commission, an assassination plot against Syngman Rhee, naval coastal patrols, crime trends, and Coast Guard pay scales."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,84],"pp":"29","s":"Comprehensive reports and statistics on agriculture, forestry, mining, manufacturing, transportation, labor, prices, and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,56],"pp":"31","s":"Reports flood recovery on arable land, 1944-1945 crop production statistics, fishery bans due to cholera, fuel wood shortages, and subsidized coal mining."},{"t":"Section 2. Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[57,84],"pp":"43","s":"Covers industrial reopenings, sericulture plans, railroad flood repairs, National Food Regulation No. 2 for grain collections, rising living costs, and New Korea Company management."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[85,106],"pp":"71","s":"Reports on public health and welfare, educational reorganization, cultural programs, and public information media."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[87,92],"pp":"73","s":"Documents the severe cholera epidemic and vaccination campaigns, public welfare subsidies, repatriation of Japanese nationals, and Korea's admission as an associate member of the World Health Assembly."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[93,100],"pp":"79","s":"Details Ordinance No. 102 creating Seoul National University, the publication of new Korean-script textbooks, the return of the Education Mission, and the recovery of historic royal seals."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[101,106],"pp":"87","s":"Covers political education lecture teams, public health radio programming, motion picture attendance, Military Governor press conferences, and Korean press debates over political unification."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of Military Government Activities 7 September 1945 - 31 August 1946","lv":1,"p":[107,118],"pp":"93","s":"A comprehensive historical review of the first year of American military government in South Korea."},{"t":"Section 1. Problems of the Occupation","lv":2,"p":[109,112],"pp":"95","s":"Reviews initial postwar administrative difficulties, the repatriation of Japanese military and civilians, the elimination of Japanese influence, and responses to floods and cholera."},{"t":"Section 2. Civil Administration and Politics","lv":2,"p":[113,116],"pp":"99","s":"Chronicles the transfer of governmental operations to Korean officials, civil service and police restructuring, internal security forces development, and efforts toward a legislative assembly."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Reforms","lv":2,"p":[117,118],"pp":"103","s":"Summarizes one year of achievements in public health, epidemic prevention, and the expansion of democratic education serving 1.7 million students with Korean textbooks."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[119,126],"pp":"105","s":"Reference map of the Korean peninsula showing provincial boundaries, major cities, transport lines, and the 38th parallel division."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-12","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-12-1946-09","s":"J","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"","pg":120,"o":"Summation No. 12 covers non-military activities in Japan during September 1946 (with statistical data primarily from July and August 1946). Significant political developments include the House of Peers' line-by-line review of the draft democratic constitution, the Cabinet's announcement purging 899 high-ranking officials, and the Diet's passage of four historic local government reform bills establishing popular election of governors and mayors. In the economic realm, the Diet enacted the Labor Relations Adjustment Law amidst intense labor strife, notably a 10-day maritime strike and a narrowly averted nationwide railway strike. SCAP and the Japanese Government accelerated Zaibatsu dissolution, designating the Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Sumitomo, Yasuda, and Fuji holding companies, while introducing major tax bills to cancel war indemnities and impose a progressive capital levy. Food shortages remained severe, requiring substantial releases of imported United States grains and canned rations. International postal service resumed, and the Tokyo war crimes trials advanced with extensive evidence on state-sponsored opium operations in China. For historians, the report provides invaluable quantitative data on industrial reconversion, hyperinflationary note circulation, labor union expansion past 3.7 million members, and local government democratization.","h":["The Diet passed four local government reform bills providing for the direct popular election of prefectural governors, city mayors, and local headmen.","The Cabinet announced that 899 high-ranking government and political figures were forced to resign under the SCAP Purge Directive.","The Diet enacted the Labor Relations Adjustment Law, and a 10-day general strike by the All-Japan Seamen's Union was successfully arbitrated.","The Holding Company Liquidation Commission designated the top five Zaibatsu holding companies—Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Sumitomo, Yasuda, and Fuji Industrial—for dissolution.","International postal service from Japan was officially resumed on 10 September to all nations except Germany.","The International Military Tribunal for the Far East heard detailed prosecution evidence exposing Japanese state-sponsored opium monopolies and narcotics trafficking in occupied China."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","Local Government Reform","Purge Directive","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Labor Relations Adjustment Law","All-Japan Seamen's Union","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","War Indemnities Tax","Repatriation","Daily Life Security Law","Black Markets"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Title page, table of contents, and list of charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of non-military activities in Japan during September 1946."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"General Orders No. 30 of 7 September 1946 established the Civil Transportation Section as a Special Staff Section of GHQ, SCAP."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the House of Peers' review of the draft constitution, party reorganizations, Diet legislation, local government reform bills, and war crimes proceedings."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"7","s":"Summarizes economic developments, including agricultural forecasts, industrial output, resumption of international mail, passage of the Labor Relations Adjustment Law, and Zaibatsu dissolution actions."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,12],"pp":"11","s":"Summarizes social conditions, including passage of the Daily Life Security Bill, repatriation of over 248,000 Japanese, meetings of the Educational Reform Committee, and public health programs."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,28],"pp":"15","s":"Political activities in Japan during September 1946, covering civil administration, public safety, and legal affairs."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,16],"pp":"17","s":"Reports the House of Peers' deliberations on the draft constitution, political party realignments, the purge of 899 officials, and the Diet's passage of four major local government reform bills."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"23","s":"Details black-market crackdowns, rising juvenile delinquency, police organizational adjustments, Tokyo fire prevention measures, and July fire losses totaling over ¥294 million."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[19,28],"pp":"27","s":"Covers Japanese judicial code revisions, occupation court trials, apprehension and sentencing of war criminals, and IMTFE testimony regarding Japanese-administered narcotics trade, economic exploitation in China, and Axis treaty negotiations."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[29,107],"pp":"47","s":"Economic activities in Japan during August and September 1946, spanning natural resources, commerce, industry, finance, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[30,32],"pp":"49","s":"Reports favorable rice harvest estimates, August fish catches reaching 79,368 metric tons, plans for the Antarctic whaling expedition, and pearl oyster harvesting in Ago Bay."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[33,40],"pp":"55","s":"Details August lumber production, coal production rising to 1,770,200 metric tons, a five-year coal production goal, and crude petroleum output trends and labor disputes."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[41,47],"pp":"67","s":"Covers declines in iron, steel, and coke production due to coal shortages, civilian shipyard repairs, fertilizer manufacturing progress, and reparations designations of 396 machinery plants."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[48,57],"pp":"81","s":"Reports production across food processing, paper, glass, optical instruments, medical supplies, electrical equipment, motor vehicles, bicycles, rubber products, tanned leather, and agricultural implements."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[58,69],"pp":"99","s":"Reports a 50 percent increase in cotton yarn production, receipt of 19 American cotton cargoes, raw silk output trends, rayon and wool manufacturing, and clothing deliveries."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[70,73],"pp":"123","s":"Covers slight increases in railway freight traffic and coastwise shipping, adequate hydroelectric power generation, and urban gas utility operations severely curtailed by coal shortages."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[74,81],"pp":"131","s":"Details telephone and telegraph rehabilitation, the resumption of international postal service on 10 September, radio broadcasting expansion, equipment manufacture, and postal and communications labor negotiations."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[82,87],"pp":"147","s":"Reports Diet passage of the Labor Relations Adjustment Law, hearings on the Labor Standards Bill, the 10-day maritime strike, an averted railway strike, and trade union growth past 3.7 million members."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[88,90],"pp":"161","s":"Details foreign trade planning with Allied nations, shipments of raw silk, tea, and minerals, and incoming imports of United States food, raw cotton, and phosphate rock."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[91,99],"pp":"167","s":"Reports staple food collections, releases of imported cereals and canned goods, new price schedules for meat and vegetables, recovery of hoarded goods, and licensing controls over street stalls."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[100,102],"pp":"185","s":"Covers record Bank of Japan note circulation of ¥64.4 billion, introduction of the 100 percent war indemnities tax and capital levy bills, the national debt, and local government budgets."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[103,107],"pp":"191","s":"Details impounding of German assets, inventories of looted foreign films and vessels, custody of precious metals, designation of five major Zaibatsu for dissolution, and reparations plant inspections."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[108,120],"pp":"199","s":"Social activities in Japan during August and September 1946, covering public health, welfare, education, religion, and information media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[109,115],"pp":"201","s":"Reports relief disbursements exceeding ¥101 million under the new Daily Life Security Law, repatriation of 248,677 Japanese, medical supply production, and declining cholera incidence."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[116,120],"pp":"215","s":"Covers weekly meetings of the Japanese Educational Reform Committee, adult education programs, screening of over 63,000 teachers, return of Christian missionaries, and public information campaigns."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"","pg":105,"o":"This September 1946 monthly report documents a pivotal period of administrative transition and escalating civil unrest in the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Markedly, the Military Governor ordered Korean directors to assume full operational control of government departments, relegating American personnel to advisory roles, while preparations advanced for an interim Korean legislative assembly. However, governance was severely disrupted by the outbreak of the nationwide railroad strike on 22 September, which paralyzed ground transportation, stranded repatriates, hampered coal distribution, and precipitated an acute food crisis with skyrocketing black-market rice prices. Authorities responded with emergency airlifts of medical supplies, arrests of top Communist leaders including Pak Heun Young, and suspensions of leftist newspapers. Other significant developments include the promulgation of the Child Labor Law (Ordinance No. 112), commencement of government-to-government barter trade with Japan, structural reorganization of public education into a 6-6 system, opening of Seoul National University colleges, and ongoing efforts to control a lethal cholera epidemic that claimed over 9,400 lives. For historians, the report provides invaluable quantitative data on labor unrest, industrial stagnation, disease mortality, currency expansion, and the administrative mechanics of early South Korean state formation.","h":["The Military Governor ordered Korean directors to assume full executive charge of all government departments on 13 September, reducing Americans to advisory roles.","A general railway strike began on 22 September, halting rail transit across South Korea and escalating into wider labor unrest and food distribution crises.","Military authorities ordered the arrest of Communist Party leaders Pak Heun Young, Lee Kang Kook, and Lee Chu Ha for activities prejudicial to order.","Dr. Syngman Rhee escaped unharmed when assassins fired bullets into his automobile en route to an independence rally in Seoul on 12 September.","Ordinance No. 112 established Korea's first comprehensive Child Labor Law, prohibiting industrial employment for children under fourteen and regulating maximum hours.","Government-to-government barter trade with Japan commenced with shipments of 24 million yen of minerals and fish in exchange for coal and industrial chemicals.","The cumulative cholera epidemic in South Korea reached 14,614 cases and 9,474 deaths by 30 September, prompting emergency medical flights during the transport strike.","A Women's Bureau was established within the Department of Public Health and Welfare to advance legal rights, working conditions, and social welfare for women."],"k":["USAMGIK","September railway strike","Pak Heun Young","Syngman Rhee","Koreanization of administration","Child Labor Law","cholera epidemic","barter trade with Japan","grain collection program","Seoul National University","repatriation","Bank of Chosen notes"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,17],"pp":"","s":"Front matter containing the title page, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for Report No. 12 (September 1946)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[18,25],"pp":"1","s":"A general summary of political, economic, and social activities in South Korea during September 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,21],"pp":"3","s":"Korean directors took full charge of government departments as American personnel shifted to advisory roles, while General Hodge condemned the illegal September railway strike. Police academy classes graduated, and arrest orders were issued for three Communist leaders."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[22,23],"pp":"5","s":"The September railway strike paralyzed rail transport, disrupting coal movement and precipitating an urban food crisis. Government-to-government barter trade with Japan commenced with mineral exports, and Bank of Chosen note circulation expanded to over 11.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[24,25],"pp":"7","s":"A Women's Bureau was established, UNRRA relief clothing arrived for distribution across Korea, and cholera incidence climbed. Seoul National University opened its colleges under unified reorganization, and three leftist newspapers were suspended for inflammatory articles."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[26,39],"pp":"9","s":"Surveys political developments, government administration, civil service reforms, legal affairs, police reorganization, and public safety across South Korea."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[28,33],"pp":"11","s":"Korean directors assumed executive charge of Military Government departments, and plans advanced for a Korean legislative assembly. General Hodge addressed the political motivations behind the rail strike, and arrest orders were issued for top Communist leaders including Pak Heun Young."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[34,39],"pp":"17","s":"Provost courts sentenced editors of suspended leftist newspapers, while Dr. Syngman Rhee survived an assassination attempt in Seoul. Police forces were reorganized into three provincial zones, and U.S. Coast Guard advisers arrived to assist the expanding Korean Coast Guard."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[40,73],"pp":"23","s":"Details agriculture, fisheries, forestry, mining, manufacturing, transportation, communications, labor strikes, foreign trade, rationing, prices, and financial institutions."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[42,51],"pp":"25","s":"Summer grain collections suffered delays from the rail strike, while the Korea Land Development Company was dissolved and its assets transferred to government reclamation. Coal output fell due to strikes at the Hwasun mine, and mineral operations focused on copper smelting at Changhang."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[52,73],"pp":"33","s":"A massive railroad strike halted rail transport, interrupted mail services, and intensified an urban food shortage that pushed black-market rice prices upward. Government barter trade with Japan commenced, child labor regulations were promulgated, and currency circulation exceeded 11.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[74,97],"pp":"55","s":"Reports on public health measures, refugee relief, educational system reorganization, text distributions, cultural events, religious missions, and public information campaigns."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[76,85],"pp":"57","s":"A Women's Bureau was established, UNRRA relief supplies were received at Inchon, and over 812,000 Japanese nationals were repatriated to date. A severe cholera epidemic reached 14,614 cases with 9,474 deaths, requiring emergency air shipments of medical supplies during the rail strike."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[86,91],"pp":"67","s":"Seoul National University inaugurated its colleges under a unified system despite student protests, and secondary schools adopted a new 6-6 curriculum. Kyonggi-do households voted democratically to establish 4-H clubs, and American and Australian Christian missionaries returned to Korea."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[92,97],"pp":"73","s":"Information campaigns publicized the new child labor law, rice collection, and cholera prevention, while 'The Culture Hour' debuted on the radio. The Military Governor suspended three leftist newspapers for inciting disorder, provoking widespread editorial debate on press freedom."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[98,105],"pp":"79","s":"Full-page reference map of Korea outlining provincial borders, provincial capitals, principal ports and cities, and the 38th parallel demarcation line."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12-econ","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09-economic","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946): Economic","v":"Economic section","pg":35,"o":"This September 1946 monthly report (Part III: Economic of Summation No. 12) documents the severe economic crisis confronting the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Economic activity was paralyzed by the general railroad strike of 22 September, which involved over 30,000 railway employees protesting acute food shortages and runaway inflation, quickly spreading to telegraph, municipal, and newspaper workers. Transportation stoppages exacerbated a critical food supply emergency, leaving 6.5 million citizens dependent on official rations while summer grain collections attained only 52 percent of pre-flood goals and black-market rice hit record highs. The report details efforts to revive basic industry and mining, including copper smelting at Changhang, barter agreements exchanging Korean minerals for Japanese coal and chemicals, and the passage of Ordinance 112 (the Child Labor Law). For historians, this report is invaluable for understanding the explosive socio-economic conditions preceding the Autumn Uprising of 1946, illustrating the friction between military government policies, severe industrial bottlenecks, food distribution failures, and emerging Korean organized labor.","h":["Over 30,000 railroad workers staged a general strike on 22 September protesting living costs and food shortages, halting all rail traffic across South Korea.","Ordinance 112 (Child Labor Law) was signed on Labor Day, barring children under 14 from mercantile and industrial work and regulating youth working hours.","Government grain collections reached only 88,650 metric tons (52 percent of pre-flood estimates), leaving 6,500,000 citizens dependent on public rations.","Black-market rice prices spiked to 43 yen per pound in Seoul and 49 yen in Pusan despite emergency grain transport permits.","A government-to-government barter agreement with Japan commenced, trading 24,000,000 yen of Korean minerals and marine products for coal, parts, and industrial chemicals.","The Korea Land Development Company was formally dissolved on 6 September, transferring its assets to the Department of Agriculture's Reclamation and Irrigation Section.","Seoul established a joint Public Utilities Board composed of four Koreans and three Americans to oversee city transit and gas service."],"k":["railroad strike","food rationing","black-market rice","Child Labor Law","Ordinance 112","barter trade","coal mining","Changhang Smelter","Korea Land Development Company","fisheries","Public Utilities Board","grain collections"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Includes the War Department cover sheet and publication details for this industrial report supplement."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[4,35],"pp":"23","s":"Covers South Korean economic conditions in September 1946, comprising sections on natural resources and commerce and industry."},{"t":"Section 1: Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[6,13],"pp":"25","s":"Reports grain collection delays, dissolution of the Korea Land Development Company, August fisheries production, and mining output constraints."},{"t":"Section 2: Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[14,35],"pp":"33","s":"Details the September 22 general railroad strike, Child Labor Ordinance 112, critical food rationing shortages, and barter trade with Japan."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09-v2","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"Second copy","pg":104,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-13","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-13-1946-10","s":"J","no":13,"d":"1946-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 13 (October 1946)","v":"","pg":128,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-13","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-13-1946-10","s":"K","no":13,"d":"1946-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 13 (October 1946)","v":"","pg":108,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-14","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-14-1946-11","s":"J","no":14,"d":"1946-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 14 (November 1946)","v":"","pg":155,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-14","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-14-1946-11","s":"K","no":14,"d":"1946-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 14 (November 1946)","v":"","pg":310,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-15","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-15-1946-12","s":"J","no":15,"d":"1946-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 15 (December 1946)","v":"","pg":134,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-15","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-15-1946-12","s":"K","no":15,"d":"1946-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 15 (December 1946)","v":"","pg":98,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-16","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-16-1947-01","s":"J","no":16,"d":"1947-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 16 (January 1947)","v":"","pg":137,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-16","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-16-1947-01","s":"K","no":16,"d":"1947-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 16 (January 1947)","v":"","pg":116,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-17","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-17-1947-02","s":"J","no":17,"d":"1947-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 17 (February 1947)","v":"","pg":139,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-17","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-17-1947-02","s":"K","no":17,"d":"1947-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 17 (February 1947)","v":"","pg":108,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-18","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-18-1947-03","s":"J","no":18,"d":"1947-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 18 (March 1947)","v":"","pg":144,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-18","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-18-1947-03","s":"K","no":18,"d":"1947-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 18 (March 1947)","v":"","pg":92,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-19","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-19-1947-04","s":"J","no":19,"d":"1947-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 19 (April 1947)","v":"","pg":150,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-19","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-19-1947-04","s":"K","no":19,"d":"1947-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 19 (April 1947)","v":"","pg":92,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-20","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-20-1947-05","s":"J","no":20,"d":"1947-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 20 (May 1947)","v":"","pg":130,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-20","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-20-1947-05","s":"K","no":20,"d":"1947-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 20 (May 1947)","v":"","pg":104,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-21","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-21-1947-06","s":"K","no":21,"d":"1947-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 21 (June 1947)","v":"","pg":117,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-21-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-21-1947-06-v2","s":"K","no":21,"d":"1947-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 21 (June 1947)","v":"Second copy","pg":116,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-22","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-22-1947-07","s":"J","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 22 (July 1947)","v":"","pg":149,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-22","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-22-1947-07","s":"K","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 22 (July 1947)","v":"","pg":233,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-22-in-K-32","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-32-1948-05","s":"K","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 22 (July 1947): misbound pages","v":"Misbound pages in K-32","host":"K-32","rg":[[47,113],[134,137]],"kind":"misbound","found":"Pages stamped 'NND 785084' (the rest of the volume is 'NND 785083'); content dated to June-July 1947","pg":71,"o":"Pages 47-113 and 134-137 of the host PDF are stray pages of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 22 (July 1947), misbound into the May 1948 issue; pages 114-133 are absent. The text is the tail of Section 1 (paragraphs 22-57) and fragments of Section 2 (Legal and Public Safety). Contents: Marshall's speech and Acting Military Governor Helmick's statements on American aid and food-export rumors; Korea Civil Service; the Interim Legislative Assembly's passage of the Anti-Collaborator Bill as the Special Law on National Traitors, Collaborators and Profiteers and its second reading of a Temporary Constitution; the dispute over transferring governors; Rightist and Leftist trends and the assassination of Lyuh Woon Hyung; verbatim American and Soviet statements on the Joint Commission deadlock over consultation with anti-trusteeship parties; mail exchanges, repatriation, courts, police installations and prison population. The identification as No. 22 / July 1947 is confirmed by the text: all dated events fall in July 1947 and the statistics refer to June 1947.","h":["Anti-Collaborator Bill completed second reading 2 July as the Special Law Dealing with National Traitors, Collaborators, and Profiteers; third reading omitted.","Temporary Constitution of Korea (Chosun): second reading of all seven chapters concluded 29-31 July, local-government Part D begun 31 July.","Assembly voted 39-22 on 9 July to return the transfer of four officials, ruling it had no jurisdiction under Ordinance 118.","Lyuh Woon Hyung, Laboring People's Party leader, shot dead in Seoul on 19 July; suspect Hahn Chi Kun.","Joint Commission deadlocked over admitting Anti-Trusteeship Committee parties; Soviet side noted 70 million claimed members across applicants.","US count of parties: 71 rightist (19.3 million claimed members) against 75 leftist (32.3 million) under the Soviet list.","Civilian Supply Program aid May 1946-March 1947 stated as 91 million dollars, with 395,836 metric tons of grain.","June 1947 prison population in South Korea totalled 19,833, of whom 17,216 were sentenced."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","July 1947","Interim Legislative Assembly","Anti-Collaborator Bill","Temporary Constitution","US-USSR Joint Commission","Anti-Trusteeship Committee","Lyuh Woon Hyung","Civilian Supply Program","Helmick","Brown","repatriation","prisons","Ordinance 118","franchise law"],"toc":[{"t":"Section 1 (continued): Government and Politics, paragraphs 22-57","lv":1,"p":[47,111],"pp":"41","s":"Continuation of the July 1947 general section (opening pages and section title not in the excerpt): aid statements, civil service, Assembly business, politics, Joint Commission, interzone mail and foreign affairs."},{"t":"American aid to Korea and food-export rumors","lv":2,"p":[47,52],"pp":"41","s":"Secretary Marshall's 1 July speech rejecting charges of American imperialism was translated and released in Korean on 8 July. On 9 July the Acting Military Governor reported 91 million dollars of civilian supply aid, May 1946 to March 1947, including 395,836 tons of grain. On 19 July he denounced rumors of food export as malicious lies."},{"t":"Korea Civil Service","lv":2,"p":[53,53],"pp":"47","s":"The Training Division of the Office of Korea Civil Service completed procedures for Performance Rating Reports, required for the first time of provincial and national employees. They are due in the first fifteen days of August from staff with at least six months' service. Clerical examinations continued in provincial cities and larger towns."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly: Anti-Collaborator Bill","lv":2,"p":[54,59],"pp":"48","s":"After resuming on 30 June, the Assembly passed the remaining collaborator paragraphs (votes such as 32-14 and 42-6) and the profiteer chapter, mostly by common consent. On 2 July it retitled the measure the Special Law Dealing with National Traitors, Collaborators, and Profiteers, adopted a three-year limit, and omitted a third reading. On 7 July it assigned drafting of special-court rules to the original drafters."},{"t":"The Temporary Constitution of Democratic Korea (Chosun)","lv":2,"p":[60,77],"pp":"54","s":"A compromise revised draft of the Temporary Constitution was presented on 7 July and, despite Leftist protests, passed to second reading on 11 July. The word \"democratic\" was dropped from the title, 31 to 8. Chapters on rights and duties (including labor management participation), the Assembly, executive, bureaus, courts, finance and supplementary rules were then adopted, mostly by common consent. Local government (Section 36) began 31 July."},{"t":"Assignment of officials","lv":2,"p":[78,79],"pp":"72","s":"On 8 July the Assembly debated the Civil Administrator's proposed transfer of governors Park Keun Hon, Sunn Min Ho and Park Chong Man, and Civil Service director Chung Il Hyung. The debate turned on whether Ordinance 118 required confirmation. On 9 July the Assembly voted 39 to 22 to return the communication, for lack of jurisdiction."},{"t":"Clarification of Assembly authority (Ordinance 118)","lv":2,"p":[80,80],"pp":"74","s":"On 28 July Civil Administrator Ahn and Justice Director Kim Pyong No appeared at the Assembly's request. Kim held that Ordinance 118 leaves Military Government supreme and able to legislate without Assembly approval. Suh U Sok and Yang Che Pak disputed this and invoked the Assembly's power to review appointments. Kim urged the Assembly to adopt a constitution."},{"t":"The Franchise Law","lv":2,"p":[81,82],"pp":"75","s":"On 31 July the Assembly took up the Acting Military Governor's 21 July letter asking it to reconsider franchise provisions: the age limit of twenty-three, exclusion of ex-prisoners, traitors and senior Japanese-era officials, and votes for recent arrivals from North Korea. Members defended the age limit; no formal action followed, and the matter went to the Legislative and Judicial Committee, 44 to 0."},{"t":"Attendance of representatives, July 1947","lv":2,"p":[83,83],"pp":"77","s":"Table of the Assembly's July 1947 sessions 102 to 120, showing the date, the number of registered members present (from 55 on 15 July to 76 on 9 July, of 90 authorized) and the presiding official, either Vice-Speaker Choe Tong O or Yun Ki Sop. Quorum is 45 to hold session and 60 to consider measures."},{"t":"Political Activities: the Right","lv":2,"p":[84,84],"pp":"78","s":"Rightist expectations of the Joint Commission declined in July because of the Soviet delegation's attitude. On 4 July, 150 representatives of about seventy Rightist organizations rejected General Brown's criticism and General Hodge's letter to Rhee, demanded a general election, called for Ahn's resignation, and demanded suspension of the Labor Party's newspaper."},{"t":"Political Activities: the Left","lv":2,"p":[85,85],"pp":"79","s":"The Left continued to brand Rightists as pro-Japanese traitors and sought their exclusion from consultation. Five parties issued a joint statement on 10 July and four more on 19 July, the latter demanding consultation only with parties supporting the Moscow Decision. Lyuh Woon Hyung was shot dead on 19 July by Hahn Chi Kun, and the Nation Saving Society was ordered dissolved."},{"t":"Political Activities: General (Presidential envoy)","lv":2,"p":[86,86],"pp":"80","s":"The State Department's announcement that Lt. Gen. Albert C. Wedemeyer would go to Korea as President Truman's special envoy brightened the Rightist outlook. Rightist leaders welcomed the news and shifted their attention from the Joint Commission to the expected survey. The Leftist press barely mentioned it, and Leftist leaders offered no comment."},{"t":"US-USSR Joint Commission","lv":2,"p":[87,107],"pp":"81","s":"The Joint Commission met in Pyongyang on 2-3 July, then deadlocked in Seoul over which parties to consult. Brown (15 July) and Soviet chief Shtikov (21 July) set out positions on local groups and Anti-Trusteeship Committee members. Koreans in Japan sent support. Brown spoke on 27 and 31 July, with party tables showing 71 Rightist and 75 Leftist parties."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[108,108],"pp":"102","s":"Mail was exchanged with North Korea twice in July. On 12 July, 10,643 letters, 1,703 cards and 537 registered items went north, and 3,502 letters, 634 cards and 387 registered items came south. On 26 July, 10,108 letters, 1,568 cards and 542 registered items went north; 4,908, 1,378 and 369 came south."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"103","s":"As of 26 July, 18,672 Koreans caught entering Japan illegally had been apprehended in Japan and 416 in Korean waters; 300 permits went to Chinese for repatriation. Dr. Moon Chang Wook left on 22 July to seek sponsors for at least fifty students in American colleges. A table lists July repatriation ships at Pusan."},{"t":"Section 2 (fragment): Legal and Public Safety","lv":1,"p":[112,137],"pp":"105","s":"Opening of Section 2 plus detached later pages (police installations and prisons); pages 114-133 are absent from the excerpt."},{"t":"Legal","lv":2,"p":[112,113],"pp":"106","s":"Chief Justice Kim Young Moo's directive brought criminal dockets current by 20 July, though civil cases remain backlogged. Juvenile courts tried 154 cases in 1945, 1,700 in 1946 and 1,240 in the first five months of 1947. Twenty-nine law probationers became judges and prosecutors, and 2,500 law books were loaned to the National Law Library."},{"t":"Public Safety: police installations and prisons","lv":2,"p":[134,137],"pp":"128","s":"A table gives 1,977 South Korean police installations as of 1 Feb. 1947: 156 stations, 414 boxes, 1,495 sub-stations and 68 branches. Kwangju Prison had about 500 prisoners working its farm and workshops; 10,000 blankets reached the Justice Department by 19 July. June 1947 prisons held 19,833 (17,216 sentenced; 181 women)."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-23","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-23-1947-08","s":"J","no":23,"d":"1947-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 23 (August 1947)","v":"","pg":174,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-23-1","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-23-part-1-1947-08","s":"K","no":23,"d":"1947-08","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 23 (Part 1) (August 1947)","v":"","pg":220,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"J-24","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-24-1947-09","s":"J","no":24,"d":"1947-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 24 (September 1947)","v":"","pg":147,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-24","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-24-1947-09","s":"K","no":24,"d":"1947-09","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 24 (September 1947)","v":"","pg":333,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1,"emb":["K-25-in-K-24"]},{"id":"J-25","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-25-1947-10","s":"J","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"","pg":158,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-25","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-25-1947-10","s":"K","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"","pg":174,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-25-in-K-24","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-24-1947-09","s":"K","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"Bound in K-24","host":"K-24","rg":[[152,333]],"kind":"bound","found":"Library call-number title page 'M 105.13:25' on PDF p. 152","pg":182,"o":"This is South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 25 for October 1947, prepared in Korea by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK. It opens with an editorial on poor integration among SKIG agencies and covers population and repatriation, vested property, food supply, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, trade, labor, public services, finance, government and politics, and social matters. The dominant concerns are the 1947 rice collection and the contested rice price, with a falling rice stock, a 30 October National Economic Board session and legislative wrangling over collection law. Politically it records the appointment of Maj. Gen. William F. Dean as Military Governor, the Russian troop withdrawal proposal, the general election and UN representation debates, leftist and rightist clashes, and the Korean National Youth anniversary. Historians will find dense statistical tables, import and allocation detail from the Civilian Supply Program, financial data, and records of Assembly proceedings and press opinion.","h":["Maj. Gen. William F. Dean announced as Military Governor of Korea on 30 October 1947.","Summer grain collection reached 97.1 percent of quota: 1,237,576 hectoliters.","Rice selling price raised from 85 to 120 won per small mal; projected deficit 1.06 billion won.","Final 1947 rice estimate: 24,930,000 hectoliters brown rice.","Japanese alien registration counted 458,359 Koreans by 15 September; about 600,000 expected.","Korean National Youth membership reached 290,333 on 31 October.","Executive Order No. 8 of 20 October restricted rice transportation.","Prison population stood at 20,652 on 30 September 1947."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","rice collection","price control","vested property","Civilian Supply Program","Korean National Youth","Legislative Assembly","repatriation","National Economic Board","Military Governor Dean","coal","North-South interzone","finance","Cheju-do"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[152,161],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 25 (October 1947), prepared by the National Economic Board for the Civil Affairs Division, with blank and image-only pages. The contents list covers Part I Basic Aspects, II Production and Supply, III Public Services, IV Finance, V Government and Politics, and VI Social."},{"t":"Explanations and Implications","lv":1,"p":[162,163],"pp":"1","s":"The editors call this the second monthly report produced wholly in Korea, with the format now settling and no charts yet pending drawings from SCAP. They say poor integration among SKIG departments, such as competing claims on electric power and bituminous coal, limits the report, and add that non-economic social controls strongly shape Korean behavior."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[164,175],"pp":"3","s":"Population and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population","lv":2,"p":[164,169],"pp":"3","s":"Cumulative movement since 15 October 1945 shows 885,667 outbound and 2,342,852 inbound; 21,363 Koreans were caught trying to enter Japan illegally. Northern refugees continued at 9,661 in October. SCAP limited repatriation from Japan to illegal entrants, deportees and approved cases; 458,359 Koreans registered in Japan; Sakhalin Koreans petitioned for return."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[170,175],"pp":"9","s":"The Office of Property Custody completed a Kangwon-do inventory of 56,730 pieces of former Japanese property. It surveyed textile plants for a 150 million won rehabilitation of 12 units, guaranteed six loans totaling 361,850,000 won, took in 21,967,085 won in October, advanced operating funds to three departments, and handled 161 Operations Division cases."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[176,239],"pp":"15","s":"Food, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, distribution, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[176,180],"pp":"15","s":"Summer grain collection finished at 1,237,576 hectoliters, 97.1 percent of quota, with Cheju-do at only 16.4 percent. Polished rice stocks fell from 1,851,049 hl in June to 434,955 in October. The 1947 rice quota was 5,156,000 suk; only 3.2 percent was collected by 5 November. Executive Order No. 8 restricted rice transport, and rationed population reached 9,476,856."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[181,191],"pp":"20","s":"Rice prices set 15 September were published in October. The 30 October National Economic Board minutes defended the 640 won per bag purchase price and 120 won per small mal selling price, projecting a 1.06 billion won deficit. Seoul price indices, gold, and National Price Administration actions on coal, tobacco, tires and bicycles are also reported."},{"t":"Section 3: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[192,198],"pp":"31","s":"The final 1947 rice estimate is 24,930,000 hectoliters of brown rice, about 15 percent above 1946. Cotton fell to 42,125,574 kg after cool rains, and cocoon collection reached only 717,000 kg, 45.6 percent of goal. Also covered are livestock, a 967 million won irrigation budget, fertilizer sales at 82 percent of receipts, forestry and fisheries reporting."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[199,207],"pp":"38","s":"Anthracite output rose to 32,395 tons in September against 21,043 in August, but stockpiles kept falling. The Yong Wol mine could reach 100,000 tons monthly with a 24-mile railroad. Bituminous coal from Japan was 64,588 tons in October, with sub-allocations set by the Production Board. Pitch is short, and tables give metals output."},{"t":"Section 5: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[208,216],"pp":"47","s":"Output data mostly cover September: flour at 430.4 tons, up 83 percent, cotton yarn and cloth declining, and machine-made paper at 375.1 tons, the highest since November 1946. The Inchon welding plant began making nails. Onada cement fell 44 percent, firebrick fell 72 percent, and Monopoly Bureau ginseng, salt and tobacco are reported."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocation and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[217,227],"pp":"56","s":"Explains how Commerce allocates Korean-produced goods by province (cloth, shoes, soap, matches, tires, beer, cottonseed oil), with 367 civilian supply allocations in September and 837 in October, including trucks, clothing, cement and dynamite. FFA spent 66,697,000 won in October and FDA sales to provinces reached 269,575,779 won. Petroleum drum deposits rose to 1,000 won; salt kimchi ration cut to 2.7 kg."},{"t":"Section 7: Import and Export","lv":2,"p":[228,235],"pp":"67","s":"Private trade nearly balanced in October on permit estimates (270 million won imports, 283 million exports); September imports rose 8.45 times and exports 4.9 times over August. Covers barter and shipments with the U.S., Japan (SCAP open account), Hong Kong and Egypt, a new commercial shipping agency, Civilian Supply Program IV, and imports of 64,588 tons of coal, salt, fertilizer and foodstuffs."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[236,239],"pp":"75","s":"National Labor Week opened 20 October with lectures, contests and radio broadcasts; the Tai Han No Chong Labor College at Inchon was inspected, and the first Labor News and a union-meeting booklet appeared. Maximum-hours violations by two employers went to court; rightist and leftist unions contested Pusan seamen; a Seoul employee survey counted 59,009 workers; industrial health inspections covered about 15 plants."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[240,263],"pp":"79","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities, public works and housing."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[240,248],"pp":"79","s":"Department revenue rose 7 percent in September, helped by the 20 August freight-rate rise of 50 percent, while expenditures rose 18 percent. Railroad receipts rose 6 percent, passengers hit a fiscal-year high, freight tonnage rose 14 percent, and 55 percent of locomotives stayed inoperative. A Korean LST sailed to Shanghai; a three-day Pusan stevedore strike won a 29 percent handling-charge increase."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[249,257],"pp":"88","s":"Departmental revenue rose from 31.9 to 35.8 million won in September, with 12 million won misclassified as miscellaneous income, while expenditures rose 72 percent to 74 million won on winter provisioning. Staff fell 4,466 since April as insurance collectors went onto commission. Covers postal savings, the Postal Book Transfer System, insurance, telephone and telegraph, and a doubling of postal rates on 1 October."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[258,259],"pp":"97","s":"Electric power delivered from North Korea rose 25 percent in October, to 55 million kWh of 80 million total. Seoul had 130 streetcars carrying about 500,000 passengers daily after repairs using Civilian Supply parts; 23 trucks were converted to busses in 1947. Power plants ran at reduced capacity for lack of water, and a welfare section was set up for Seoul Electric Company hospitals."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[260,261],"pp":"99","s":"Public works man-days (150,839) and wage expenditures (26.75 million won) in October reached their highest since May. Resurfacing of the Yong Dong Po to Si Hung highway began with asphalt; bridge designs for Kotong and Taehwang were finished. Han River to Ascom City pipeline repairs found 290 leaks; a Cheju-do water survey proposed wells and reservoirs; fire service chiefs met 19 October."},{"t":"Section 5: Housing","lv":2,"p":[262,263],"pp":"101","s":"The National Housing Administration finished 11 prefabricated experimental low-cost houses at its Yong Dong Po factory. Findings shifted the design from 8 to 7 pyung with a kitchen and two sleeping rooms, and a forty-house project with Seoul City is planned. Refugee housing and tent winterization fall to Public Health and Welfare; the City of Seoul completed its 400-house project in October."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[264,283],"pp":"103","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[264,271],"pp":"103","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue rose almost 1.4 billion won in October to 21.8 billion, driven by rice collection and deficit spending; cumulative excess government withdrawals reached 19,859 million won. Collections ran at 75 and 72 percent of projected quarterly revenue. The 1947/48 budget stood at 18.79 billion won with 3.3 billion more requested; Civilian Supply receipts of 1.22 billion left a balance near 4.0 billion."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[272,283],"pp":"111","s":"Cash balances in financial institutions rose 427 million won in September owing to rice-collection preparations, and deposit balances rose 3.6 billion to 34.4 billion won, with the Bank of Chosun holding 53 percent. Loans outstanding reached 14.3 billion won. Covers life and damage insurance, 12 billion won in credit lines for rice collection, silk credit, a November-December deposit drive and insurance company developments."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[284,305],"pp":"123","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, security, police, interzone activities and legal affairs."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[284,288],"pp":"123","s":"Hodge announced on 30 October that Maj. Gen. William F. Dean would become Military Governor. The section reprints Hodge's and Helmick's speeches at the Korean National Youth anniversary, reviews Civil Service divisions and training, notes Executive Orders 6 and 7, and records National Economic Board budget approvals and the 640-won rice price session."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[289,292],"pp":"128","s":"In 16 sessions averaging 66 members, the Assembly passed parts of the Temporary Administrative Organizational Law and an Ordinance No. 118 amendment, and forwarded Rhee's UN delegate petition 34 to 7. It debated rival rice collection laws, voted to rescind Executive Order No. 8, approved Lee Choon Ho as Seoul National University president, and read the entertainment-suspension bill."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[293,295],"pp":"132","s":"Politics centered on Rhee as UN representative, the timing of a general election, and the Russian withdrawal proposal. Rightists pressed for immediate South Korean elections; moderates formed the National Independence Federation under Kimm Kiu Sic. Release of the South Korea Labor Party's 15 August riot plan prompted demands to disband leftist groups, while the Laboring People's Party reorganized."},{"t":"Section 4: Internal Security","lv":2,"p":[296,296],"pp":"135","s":"The Department of Internal Security comprises the Coast Guard and Constabulary. Constabulary men guarded supply areas, including the Pusan coal pile, and the Coast Guard's Pusan harbor block boarded 436 vessels in one week and 672 the next. Conferences addressed police friction with both forces, Constabulary recruiting screening, cost-of-living payments and terrorist youth groups."},{"t":"Section 5: Police","lv":2,"p":[297,299],"pp":"136","s":"Police Director Chough marked the force's second anniversary and warned of the difficult rice collection. Rice law changes, including Executive Order No. 8 and citizen quota committees, aimed to curb past abuses. Other items covered Order No. 6 limits on business inspections, solicitation permits, women prisoners' clinics, Kangwon border shots, labor party arrests, rightist-leftist clashes and three murders."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[300,301],"pp":"139","s":"The US-USSR Electric Power Section handles goods paid for North Korean power; use from 16 August 1945 to 31 May 1947 was 837,678,737 KWH. At a 21-24 October Pyongyang conference payment was fixed in goods at controlled ceiling prices and future agreements to be signed by Koreans. Mail exchanged 18 October included 6,348 letters from the South."},{"t":"Section 7: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[302,305],"pp":"141","s":"The Department of Justice section covers air mail Order No. 19, Ordinance No. 150 on gold transactions and Executive Order No. 8 on rice transport. Legal opinions addressed press criticism of the Civil Administrator, Assembly vetting of appointees and legalizing a gold mining program. Prisoners numbered 20,652, and 54 of 700 applicants passed the bar."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[306,327],"pp":"145","s":"Health, education, information, press and youth."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[306,313],"pp":"145","s":"A cholera outbreak in Sanchong had 14 cases and 6 deaths; September communicable disease and vaccine stock tables are given, with typhus control and rat-control plans. Other items: six leper clinics, nursing and dental figures, swine plague treated with sulfanilamide, refugee camp winterization, 19,707 refugee dwellings, milk plans, welfare training, and Women's Bureau work including ex-prostitute committees."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[314,320],"pp":"153","s":"Korean Alphabet Day was marked on 9 October. South Korea had 12,452 schools and 3,122,140 pupils, while textbook printing fell to 339,754 for lack of paper. The section covers Rockefeller and U.S. scholarships, Technological Training Board projects, nursing responsibilities, the Korea University incident, Seoul National University changes, and Cheju-do's severe teacher shortage."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[321,323],"pp":"160","s":"The Department of Public Information released 95 news items in October and printed one issue of Farmers Weekly because of newsprint shortage. Rice collection posters were prominent, and 1,060,000 copies of Hodge's letter on the UN proposal were dropped by air. It also reports seven information centers, 11 radio stations, film distribution to 65 theaters, and photographic panels."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[324,325],"pp":"163","s":"The press debated the Russian troop withdrawal proposal, with moderate papers backing the U.S. line and leftist Dok Lib Sinbo praising the Soviet plan. Rhee's 18 October call for a South Korean election was quoted. Papers of most shades attacked the rice transport ban, and rightist papers publicized the 26 October Nam San rally and the 15 August plot report."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[326,327],"pp":"165","s":"Helmick's 20 October memorandum described Korean National Youth as an official non-political, non-military USAMGIK activity for ages 18 to 35, led by Lee Bum Suk. Membership rose from 157,218 on 31 July to 290,333 on 31 October. The 7-11 October anniversary drew over 300,000, with Seoul parades, and Taedong terrorism was reported."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[328,333],"pp":"","s":"Pages 328 to 333 are blank or nearly so. They hold only Google digitization notices, a library barcode number and a due-date slip stamped June 23 2010, with no report text."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-26","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-26-1947-11","s":"J","no":26,"d":"1947-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 26 (November 1947)","v":"","pg":155,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-26","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-26-1947-11","s":"K","no":26,"d":"1947-11","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 26 (November 1947)","v":"","pg":495,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1,"emb":["K-27-in-K-26"]},{"id":"J-27","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-27-1947-12","s":"J","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"","pg":183,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-27","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-27-1947-12","s":"K","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"","pg":218,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-27-in-K-26","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-26-1947-11","s":"K","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"Bound in K-26","host":"K-26","rg":[[268,495]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M 105.13:27' (OCR: 108/13:27) on PDF p. 268","pg":228,"o":"Report No. 27 of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, covering December 1947 (with November data for transportation, communications, finance and several other sections). It records a month dominated by an acute electric power shortage after North Korea cut supply on 25 November, falling coal, textile and paper output, and food supply concerns despite rice collection reaching 96.2 per cent of quota. Politically it covers the assassination of Chang Duk Soo, the founding of the National Independence Federation, Rhee and Kim Koo's rightist merger, preparation for the UN Commission, and Legislative Assembly disputes with the executive. Tables give prices, trade, finance, refugees (163,253 entered in 1947), and vested property disposal, while the interzone section summarizes North Korean and Soviet radio. Historians will find it useful for the economic statistics and the occupation's own view of politics.","h":["North Korea cut electric power on 25 November 1947, forcing an Emergency Electric Power Board and executive orders.","Rice collection reached 96.2 per cent of quota by late December 1947.","Retail price index stood at 1,936, about 19 times the August 1945 base.","Chang Duk Soo, Korean Democratic Party head, was assassinated on 3 December by a police sergeant.","National Independence Federation held its first meeting on 20 December.","Agricultural Improvement Service was created on 15 December under Ordinance No. 160.","163,253 refugees from North Korea were counted in 1947; 185,441 in 1946.","Legislative Assembly marked its first anniversary on 12 December 1947."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","December 1947","power shortage","rice collection","vested property","prices","refugees","National Economic Board","Legislative Assembly","Rhee Syngman","Kim Koo","UN Commission","Korean National Youth","imports and exports"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[268,277],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 27 (December 1947), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK and filed by the Army Civil Affairs Division, with a map of Korea showing the U.S. and U.S.S.R. zones. The contents list Parts I-VI: population and property custody; production, supply and labor; public services; finance; government and politics; and social affairs including health, education, culture, public information and Korean National Youth."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[278,279],"pp":"1","s":"Explains that the data are mainly for December, but Transportation, Communications, Industrial Production, Mining and Imports and Exports carry November figures. Stresses the difficulty of accurate provincial statistics and the National Economic Board's Statistics Division role. Admits non-economic data are thin, tables repeat two years of figures, and the report is largely American-authored, with Koreans to assume more responsibility."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[280,293],"pp":"1","s":"Population and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population","lv":2,"p":[280,287],"pp":"3","s":"Concentrates on refugees from North Korea: December's 4,487 was the lowest since February 1947, though about seven times December 1946, and the 1947 total was 163,253 (185,441 in 1946). About 60 per cent went to Seoul or Kyonggi-do, mostly farmers and merchants. Also covers 21,916 illegal Japan entrants, Japanese property shipments, Chinese travel permits and 111 Koreans studying in the U.S."},{"t":"Section 2: Office of Property Custody (Vested Property)","lv":2,"p":[288,293],"pp":"11","s":"Reports on the Office of Property Custody: a new Corporate Reorganization Sub-section began work, with 285 companies under investigation and 113 preliminary studies done. Loans guaranteed reached 235 million won, mainly through the Chosun Bank. Sales totaled 16 transactions worth 5,205 thousand won, including the first residences. Royal Family estates passed to SKIG control from 1 January 1948; inventories are due by 30 June 1948."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[294,371],"pp":"17","s":"Food, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, allocations, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[294,301],"pp":"17","s":"Covers the grain year's December flows: 232,213 metric tons of rice collected, 35,038 distributed, and 586,419 on hand. Rice collection reached 96.2 per cent of quota, with Cholla-pukto at 77 per cent; Chungchong-namdo small farmers and a Seoul ration-store fraud are described. Imports totaled 452,473 tons for 1947. The Agriculture memorandum warns that imports of 10,000-34,000 tons monthly are needed to avoid exhausting stocks by July 1948."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[302,312],"pp":"25","s":"Seoul's composite retail price index stood at 1,936 in December 1947, over 19 times August 1945, pushed by note issue increases and North Korea's currency conversion. Wholesale index rose to 7,066, driven by coal and power shortages and rice-collection loans, with cotton yarn, raw cotton and silk rising most. Also gives nine-city prices, controlled prices, tobacco increases of 50-233 per cent on 15 December, and civilian supply prices."},{"t":"Section 3: Agriculture, Forestry, and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[313,321],"pp":"36","s":"Sets out the 1948 production goals: 3,211,844 chungbo cropped, 97 per cent of 1940-44, with 1947 rice at 81.6 per cent of goal. Covers livestock goals, seed cotton collection, sericulture cut by power shortages, irrigation, charcoal and fuelwood for Seoul, canned fish, and 220,953 tons of fertilizer shipped. Ordinance 160 created the Agricultural Improvement Service on 15 December, plus 4-H clubs and 325 vested companies."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[322,333],"pp":"45","s":"November anthracite output fell 5.3 per cent from October while lignite edged up; mines produced about 18 per cent under quota, hit by a late-November power shortage and truck and tire shortages. Only 64,823 tons of bituminous came from Japan in December. The Coal Committee allocated anthracite and emergency coal and studied the pitch shortage. Taichong gold mine was repossessed, copper mines owed 42,208,455 won, and tungsten operations restarted."},{"t":"Section 5: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[334,342],"pp":"57","s":"Reports cover under 10 per cent of 2,012 South Korean factories. November flour output fell 24.3 per cent in the two vested mills, noodles recovered, cotton yarn dropped 13.2 per cent and cloth 15.7 per cent, and paper fell 22.2 per cent. Nails held near the high. Briquettes, coke, cement, brick and rubber goods stayed low for lack of pitch, coal and power. Cigarettes fell 15 per cent."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[343,355],"pp":"66","s":"Describes how supplies are allocated and distributed in December. Surplus Army clothing is to be dyed and re-marked at the Chosun Boseki plant at Pusanjin; scrap metals and raw materials went to the Bureau of Industry; Kobayashi Mining stockpiles were liquidated. The Federation of Financial Associations distributed 89,820,257 won of goods. An emergency kerosene import of about 2,000,000 gallons was ordered, and Kimchi salt was nearly all distributed."},{"t":"Section 7: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[356,367],"pp":"79","s":"Private imports in November were 463,123,673 won against exports of 107,700,696 won, with 80 to 90 per cent of imports coming via Hong Kong; Pusan handled 48.2 per cent of imports. December government exports to Japan were worth about $134,389. Around 570 tons of scheelite sailed for the United States. Civilian Supply imports in 1947 totaled 988,344 tons, with salt, foodstuffs, fertilizer and bituminous coal detailed."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[368,371],"pp":"91","s":"The Department of Labor planned reorganization into four bureaus, pending KILA approval. Power-shortage orders suspended child labor and maximum-hours laws, and part-time vested plants pay a four-hour daily minimum. The Kyongsang Textile dispute over seven discharged union men stalled when the union's ultimatum was rejected by the Mediation Board. 193 unions with 58,556 members were reported. Radio programs ran, and the Industrial Health Association reached 55 doctors."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[372,405],"pp":"95","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities, public works and housing."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[372,382],"pp":"95","s":"Transportation concerns centered on railroads, where lack of bituminous coal forced removal of 58 trains in November. Department receipts fell 21 per cent from October and expenditures 12 per cent. Forty-nine passenger schedules were cancelled, freight tonnage fell 25,000 tons, and under 38 per cent of locomotives were operable. Marine income rose 44 per cent and ships were to carry 45 days of food."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[383,392],"pp":"106","s":"Department of Communications receipts rose 18 per cent in November, led by a 7.3 million won rise in telephone income after the 1 October rate increase, while stamp and postal fees fell. Expenditures rose 8 million won and employment 17. Postal deposits dipped slightly and insurance in force rose 3 per cent. Emergency power at Seoul exchanges was inspected, and about 41,000,000 Japanese stamps were ordered destroyed."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[393,398],"pp":"116","s":"After North Korea's 25 November notice capping South Korea's draw at 80,000 KW, power delivered fell to 68 million KWH in December (52 million from the north). An Emergency Electric Power Board with legal powers set priorities and bans on heating and cooking. Generating plants ran low; 130-134 Seoul streetcars operated."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[399,402],"pp":"122","s":"December was the low point for Public Works: man-days fell to 72,633 and wage outlay to 14.5 million won. Andong and Namchang bridge projects were inaugurated, over 15 miles of the Seoul-Suwon paving were done, Han River-Ascom City water work finished, and 248 fires cost 244.7 million won."},{"t":"Section 5: Housing","lv":2,"p":[403,405],"pp":"126","s":"The 40-house low-cost Seoul project was finished and fully occupied, with wood sheathing used against the cold; a site for 1,000 houses is planned. Refugee houses completed under the Welfare Housing Plan reached 5,736 of 15,730 planned, and 200 winterized tents in Seoul were occupied."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[406,423],"pp":"129","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[406,412],"pp":"129","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue reached 33,388 million won, driven by rice-collection loans of 11,772 million and deficit spending. Eight supplemental appropriations of 629 million won raised the budget to 20,089 million. November revenue was 1,225 million won; tax collection lagged, and Civilian Supply deposits stood at 4,463 million won."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[413,423],"pp":"136","s":"November deposits rose 2,569 million won and loans 16.2 billion, mostly for rice collection and straw and tobacco purchases. Directives tightened loan renewals and watched North Korean currency; a deposit drive added 2.5 billion won. Insurance in force grew, scheelite shipment valued near US$750,000 was sent, and December loans were listed."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[424,451],"pp":"147","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, legal affairs, police and interzone matters."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[424,426],"pp":"147","s":"The month opened with stormy relations between the Legislative Assembly and Deputy Military Governor Helmick over his vetoes, soon smoothed. Civil Service held training courses, 4,108 clerical examinations, and abolished 383 Seoul positions. The National Economic Board approved supplemental budgets, planned cloth distribution to civil servants, and continued staff studies."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[427,433],"pp":"150","s":"KILA completed passage of the theatrical performances law, settled its dispute with General Helmick over the vetoed bills, and approved governors of Kangwon-do and Chungchong-namdo. Members criticized rice quotas, the Land Reform Bill (3 chungbo ceiling) had its first reading, a no-confidence motion failed 35 to 27, and the first anniversary was held 12 December."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[434,439],"pp":"157","s":"A quiet month: the moderate National Independence Federation of 15 parties was founded 20 December under Kimm Kiu Sic, and Rhee Syngman and Kim Koo announced a merger in principle that stalled. Parties split over a South-only election and the UN Commission, the Council of 12 Parties lapsed, and Rhee kept attacking General Hodge."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[440,443],"pp":"163","s":"Ordinances 158-160 and Executive Order No. 9 were published. The Opinions Bureau ruled on Labor Mediation Board powers, civil service appointments and the Housing Administration. Prison population stood at 20,718. A rightist terrorism case went to a military commission, the Chang Duk Soo assassination was referred, and judges' resignations were not accepted."},{"t":"Section 5: Police","lv":2,"p":[444,446],"pp":"167","s":"The section reviews the centralized National Police, set up after 1945 and slowly cut back to police functions. After Chang Duk Soo's assassination on 3 December, police were ordered to stay out of politics and youth groups were restrained. Rice-collection resisters were arrested, and fingerprint requests fell from about 5,000 weekly to 1,760-2,889."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[447,451],"pp":"170","s":"Mail was exchanged at Tosang on 13 and 27 December, with thousands of letters each way. Radio Pyongyang hailed North Korea's currency conversion, a provisional constitution and rejected the UN decision; it announced Ahn Kil's death and coal quotas above 100 per cent. Radio Moscow denounced the UN Commission and MacArthur's Japan policy."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[452,488],"pp":"175","s":"Health, education, culture, public information, press and youth."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[452,460],"pp":"175","s":"December saw the National Venereal Disease Center open in Seoul on 3 December with 65 beds, 191 patients treated, and a 1,420,000-won rat-control fund for plague prevention. Typhus fell from 117 cases to 4 and typhoid from 239 to 24 against December 1946. Biological output was cut by power shortage; a nutrition survey covered 4,676 families; refugee camps were winterized; the Red Cross lacked trucks."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[461,465],"pp":"184","s":"The Department of Education completed its 1948-49 budget request of 3,128,979,081 won, nearly 80 percent for common schools, against 1,660,242,480 won the year before. Provincial advisors met 16-17 December on local school taxation. Other items: blind and deaf school enrollment of 370, teacher certification rules from 1 January 1948, Technological Board training projects, the American Language Institute, and research and testing work."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[466,471],"pp":"189","s":"The section opens with a survey of Korean literature, poetry and history, then reports on literary societies and the National Library, which logged 291,285 books used and 265,275 readers in 1947. Seven Koreans left to study in the United States and four in Nanking. December also brought Symphony Orchestra concerts, the Korean Broadcasting System's 337 broadcast hours, and the reorganized Royal Asiatic Society."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[472,477],"pp":"195","s":"The Department of Public Information issued 74 press releases in December, including Military Governor Dean's and General Hodge's statements on transferring a terrorism case to an American court and on the murder of Chang Duk Soo. Releases also covered the UN Commission, food, health and education. The Public Opinion Bureau registered 4 parties and 9 publications; a Nonsan field trip found only 35.8 percent thought Americans benefited Korea."},{"t":"Section 5: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[478,485],"pp":"201","s":"The Korean press covered the Helmick-KILA dispute over his vetoes of the anti-collaborators bill and temporary constitution, Dean's transfer of the Dai Han Min Chung terror case to a military court, and the failed Rhee-Kim Koo accord. The 2 December murder of Chang Duk Soo overshadowed all, prompting police arrests and press curbs. A women's alliance poll found 106 favoring a North-South leaders' convention, 80 opposed."},{"t":"Section 6: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[486,488],"pp":"209","s":"Korean National Youth, Inc. grew from 19,270 members in January to 404,585 in December 1947, with committees in the 9 provinces and 108 guns and 15,304 leaders. Leader Lee Bum Suk and Dai Dong's leader issued an 11 December appeal to end mutual slander. Activities included a 11,843,113-seedling planting project, civic work, a Women's Section speakers bureau, and rejection of terrorism charges."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[489,495],"pp":"","s":"Pages 489-493 are blank scanned pages marked only as digitized by Google. Page 494 shows a library barcode and a date-due slip stamped 23 June 2020, with an image of the back cover. Page 495 holds only a stray caption line, the following table provides the information in English, with no table text. There is no report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-28","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-28-1948-01","s":"J","no":28,"d":"1948-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 28 (January 1948)","v":"","pg":367,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-28","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-28-1948-01","s":"K","no":28,"d":"1948-01","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 28 (January 1948)","v":"","pg":499,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1,"emb":["K-29-in-K-28"]},{"id":"J-29","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-29-1948-02","s":"J","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"","pg":161,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-29","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-29-1948-02","s":"K","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"","pg":235,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-29-in-K-28","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-28-1948-01","s":"K","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"Bound in K-28","host":"K-28","rg":[[256,499]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M 105.13:29' on PDF p. 256","pg":244,"o":"Number 29 of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, covers February 1948. Cold-war tension dominates: leftist sabotage and strikes from 7 February, the UN Interim Committee's 27 February decision to observe elections in South Korea alone, the split between Rhee, Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, and the walkout of Speaker Kimm and 30 legislators that crippled the Interim Legislative Assembly. The report also records completion of rice collection at 97.6 per cent of quota, continued industrial shortages of bituminous coal, power and parts, record revenues exceeding expenditures for the first time since liberation, price indexes still rising, vested property disposal and land reform, police and civil-liberties reforms ahead of the May elections, and a 58 per cent rise in northern refugees. Detailed statistical tables on population, prices, production, trade, finance and labor, plus feature studies on the Korean National Youth, women and the flag, make it a rich source for historians of the occupation.","h":["UN Interim Committee decided on 27 February to witness elections in South Korea alone, overshadowing all other developments.","Leftists began a revolt on 7 February; sabotage and riots occurred but no nation-wide general strike developed.","Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and 30 legislators resigned from KILA over an 'Urgent Proposal' to the UN, halting the Assembly.","Rice collection ended 15 February with 97.6 per cent of the quota collected.","February revenues exceeded expenditures for the first time since liberation; note circulation fell 722 million won.","Refugee inflow from north of the 38th parallel rose 58 per cent over January; 4,074 processed in February.","Seoul retail price index reached 2,310, over 23 times the August 1945 level, rising for the ninth straight month.","Police administrative authority was transferred from national to provincial control as a safeguard against a police state."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","UN Temporary Commission on Korea","May 1948 elections","Kimm Kiu-sic","Kim Koo","Rhee Syngman","KILA","rice collection","vested property","inflation and prices","bituminous coal","refugees from North Korea","National Police","Korean National Youth","February 1948"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[256,265],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 29 (February 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK (call number M 105.13:29), followed by Google scan pages and the printed contents (pp. i-v). The contents list Parts I-VI: basic aspects, production and supply, public services, finance, government and politics, and social affairs, with paragraph-level page references."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[266,267],"pp":"1","s":"Reviews February 1948 under cold-war tension: the leftist revolt and sabotage from 7 February, the UN Interim Committee's 27 February decision to observe South-only elections, and the walkout of Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic with 30 legislators from KILA. Also notes a 58 per cent refugee increase, civil-rights measures, vested property leasing, industrial shortages, first revenue surplus and women's activities."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[268,283],"pp":"3","s":"Population and foreign affairs, and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[268,277],"pp":"3","s":"Refugees from the North rose 58 per cent over January (4,074 processed in February), and cumulative movement tables are given. The 1947 Seoul census counts 779,890 adults by occupation, schooling and marital status. Also covers Koreans in Manchuria (about 800,000), plans to close low-standard Osaka Korean schools, SKIG liaison problems in Tokyo, 192 illegal entrants, and draft immigration regulations."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[278,283],"pp":"13","s":"Vested property sales were held back by scarce reputable Korean buyers and nationalization and land-reform uncertainty, so leasing (299 plants leased), liquidation and corporate reorganization (409 companies examined) were tried; February sales totaled 35 for 4,600,800 won. Also covers 423 audits, the Buddhist property report, textile loans over 200 million won, and 33 million won in new loan applications."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[284,365],"pp":"19","s":"Food, agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[284,292],"pp":"19","s":"The 1947/48 rice collection ended 15 February at 97.6 per cent of quota (754,579 metric tons), with Cholla-pukto the only province well short. Covers stocks, rationing releases of 96,634 tons, grain imports of only 4,102 tons, ration cuts in Seoul, and projections showing need for 225,000 tons of imported cereals in 1948/49."},{"t":"Section 2: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[293,298],"pp":"28","s":"Covers FAO 1950 census planning, training of 123 crop-reporting employees, and the New Korea Company's status and land evaluation plan for its 324,604 chungbo, to be sold at three times annual crop value over 15 years. Also livestock, straw bags, hemp, sericulture, fuelwood, fisheries training for 350 students, laver exports, the Korean Agricultural Association, and Kyonggi-do 4-H clubs growing to 1,072."},{"t":"Section 3: Prices","lv":2,"p":[299,309],"pp":"34","s":"Seoul retail prices rose for the ninth straight month: the February index of 2,310 was nearly four per cent above January, while the wholesale index fell to 6,910, more than six per cent below January's record. Grain and food rose, textiles and fuel fell. Tables give prices, controlled prices (rice steady since November 1945) and civilian-supply prices."},{"t":"Section 4: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[310,322],"pp":"45","s":"Industrial output stayed low, with data mostly for January 1948. Flour, noodles, cotton yarn and cloth, paper, nails, briquettes, cement, rubber items and tobacco are reported, with shortages of wheat, pulp, pitch, coal and power; Puksan paper mill resumed after 10 months. Government salterns were idle, and 1947 annual production tables from the Department of Commerce are given."},{"t":"Section 5: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[323,334],"pp":"58","s":"January anthracite output reached 41,357 metric tons, 32 per cent above December, though Samchuk and Tanyang missed quotas through power failures, snow and poor transport. Bituminous imports from Japan hit a record 83,330 tons in February, with 50,000 extra tons allotted for power. Pitch for briquettes stayed scarce; gold, silver, tungsten, zinc and lead rose, copper fell."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[335,348],"pp":"70","s":"The new Consumer Goods Allocation Board and National Consumer Goods Office confined February to organization; about 22,000,000 won of Civil Service Relief Committee goods were inventoried. Petroleum allocations were reported, and prices were raised effective 1 April. Salt use, ex-Japanese metal stocks and distribution of the 223,000,000 won year-end bonus goods through the FFA and FDA were covered."},{"t":"Section 7: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[349,360],"pp":"84","s":"Private trade estimates for February were imports of 260 million won against exports of 421 million; January imports were 352 million and exports 348 million, mostly via Hong Kong. New Trustee Shipment and export-loan rules were issued. Government cargoes went to the United States and Japan (laver exports), plus FLC purchases from Hawaii, coal, fertilizer, rice and salt imports."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[361,365],"pp":"96","s":"The Seoul Electric Company's union contract with the Tai Han federation was extended pending talks, and a seamen's bargaining election was set for 5-7 March in six ports. Pungkuk Flour Mills agreed to pay back wages. Unions reported 84,363 members; labor-law inspections, pamphlets, radio programs, a labor kiosk and an industrial medical plan followed."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[366,395],"pp":"101","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[366,376],"pp":"101","s":"New rail, marine and highway rates took effect in February, rail fares rising 25 per cent (first and second class) and 50 per cent (third). Railroad sabotage from 7 February damaged 52 locomotives and ended night passenger trains. January rail income, freight, passengers and coal use fell; locomotives operative dropped by 26. Marine income rose, a Pusan pier warehouse burned, and a 75-ton crane arrived."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[377,384],"pp":"112","s":"Use of communications grew markedly in 1947, with about 7,500 would-be telephone subscribers unserved. January revenue fell from December, though Pusan and Kwangju returns were missing; expenditures rose 1,000,000 won and employees rose for a third month. Postal deposits, insurance, telephone traffic gained; an emergency transmitter was installed, and 20,000 radio receivers were repaired."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[385,388],"pp":"120","s":"Power delivered to distributors in February was 72,000,000 KWH, of which 24,000,000 were produced in South Korea, improving on December and January. The Emergency Power Board extended the curfew from 2200 to 2300 and planned year-round control. Chung Pyung and Tang In Ri failures were repaired; sabotage was minor. Seoul tram fares were approved at five won from 5 March."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[389,395],"pp":"124","s":"Mild February weather let road and bridge repairs begin early. Highway man-days reached 171,451. The Bureau of Highways reported 236,753,123.85 won spent on the Seoul-Pusan road, and a Seoul-East Coast road was surveyed. An irrigation and flood program was submitted, 50,000,000 won flood funds allocated, and volunteer fire brigades and the Seoul Firemen's School were started; 149 fires caused 219,143,550 won loss."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[396,421],"pp":"131","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[396,408],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun notes outstanding fell 722 million won in February, and for the first time revenue deposits exceeded withdrawals, a net 386 million won. Supplementary 1947/48 appropriations of 627 million won were approved. The Military Governor ordered a balanced 1948/49 budget, set at about 22,000 million won of revenue; taxes, guaranteed loans (13,710 million won contingent) and Civilian Supply cash (5,083 million won) are also reported."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[409,421],"pp":"144","s":"Data cover January only. Cash balances rose over 614 million won and total deposits rose 6,882 million won, over 15 per cent, to 52,208 million. Loans fell 1,062 million won. The section describes the eight financial institutions, gives life and damage insurance figures, and reports the Foreign Exchange Bank's first trade loan, a 352 million won claim on past-due guaranteed loans, and new borrower registration rules."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[422,453],"pp":"157","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, law, police and interzone affairs."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[422,425],"pp":"157","s":"Maj.-Gen. Helmick, Deputy Military Governor, was in Washington 19 January to 4 February to justify GARIOA and rehabilitation funds for 1948/49 and discussed the North Korean power agreement. Civil service ratings, examinations and personnel actions are listed. The National Economic Board approved supplemental budgets, decontrolled silk and raised coal prices. A reorganization plan would cut 40 per cent of bureaus; a vested-land sale ordinance and Military Government personnel figures are included."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[426,434],"pp":"161","s":"KILA approved the 1947 budget 38 to one on 2 February, and debated the Kukmin College eviction, Koje fishing rights, a nationality law, absentees, rice rationing and a prostitution-bribery rumor. The rightists' Urgent Proposal asking the UN Commission for a South Korean election split the Assembly. It passed amended 40 to 0 on 23 February; Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and Vice-Speaker Choe Tong-O resigned."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[435,438],"pp":"170","s":"Politics centered on the UN Commission and whether to hold elections in South Korea alone. Rhee Syngman's rightists favored a separate election, while Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo wanted a North-South leaders' conference and an all-Korea vote. Menon described the parties on 19 February; the three leaders met 10 February. The UN Interim Committee approved a South Korean election 31 to 2 on 27 February."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[439,442],"pp":"174","s":"The Department of Justice published ordinances including an order barring separate Sam Il Day demonstrations, and issued opinions on overtime pay, vested property damage, rice confiscation and transferring vested farm land. Penal items include 191 guards graduated and prison security inspections. Korean courts admitted five northern lawyers, drafted arrest-protection rules and handled a prosecutor-beating complaint; a claims program is being prepared."},{"t":"Section 5: National Police","lv":2,"p":[443,447],"pp":"178","s":"Police Department recommendations on civil liberties included release after three days without charge, court-appointed jail inspectors, and curbs on graft and brutality. The Military Governor reaffirmed that provincial police chiefs answer to governors, except on organization, administration, finances and personnel. A 7-12 February strike and riot wave brought 1,489 arrests, with 6 police and 29 rioters killed. General Dean commended the arrest of 12 counterfeiters."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[448,453],"pp":"183","s":"More than 45,000 pieces of mail crossed the 38th parallel at Tosong. Radio Pyongyang attacked General Hodge, the UN Commission and American imperialism, and covered the 8 February inauguration of the Korean People's Army. Kim Il-sung announced 1948 production quotas. The draft North Korean constitution (102 articles, ten chapters) was broadcast 16 February. The section also reports riots and an alleged 12 February border clash."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[454,493],"pp":"189","s":"Health, education, culture, press, youth and women."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[454,460],"pp":"189","s":"A study found 62,198 of 173,305 deaths in April-December 1946 were children under five, about 40 per cent preventable with penicillin or sulfonamides. Typhus, typhoid and diphtheria were the main diseases; the National Venereal Disease Center and the 14 February prostitution ban were covered. Also reported: penicillin shipment, hospital conferences, nursing and veterinary courses, drug supplies, narcotics training, diphtheria toxoid and refugee camp welfare."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[461,466],"pp":"196","s":"February education report: provincial education directors met 18-19 February; middle school tuition rises from 600 to 1,800 won from 1 April, elementary tuition abolished; elementary budget cut to 1,600 million won subsidy. Teacher certification standards set, 500,000 surplus textbooks obtained, 182 English teachers finished American Language Institute course, and schooling figures since liberation tabulated."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[467,472],"pp":"202","s":"Culture: essay explains the Tai Guk symbol on the Korean flag and its yin-yang philosophy; year 4281 celebrated 10 February. Museum director Kim Che-won and Kim Won-yong win Rockefeller fellowships. Monument to Admiral Li Sun-sin planned; Mozart concerts, opera, first New Korean Dictionary volume, HLKA's 21st anniversary, new HL amateur radio prefix, exhibits and a Chunchon library opened."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[473,482],"pp":"208","s":"Press centered on the UN Temporary Commission, with calls for unity after Gandhi's murder. Papers covered the 7-10 February strikes (8,479 arrests by 26 February), clashed over police reform, and followed the prostitution-abolition law and the 7,000,000 won bribery scandal. Inflation and falling prices were debated; opinion polls and Department of Public Information outputs are reported."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[483,489],"pp":"218","s":"Study of Korean National Youth, Inc.: the 22-23 February council of 227 delegates re-elected Lee Bum-suk as president. Leftist infiltration during the 7 February riots was blocked and membership re-screened; membership reached 519,938 against a 600,000 goal. Reports cover training schools (curriculum of 209 hours), community service, new women's and student committees, and opposition."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[490,493],"pp":"225","s":"Women's activities: Korean women served as hostesses for the UN Commission, breaking custom, and hold posts in legislature, police, nursing and universities. Women's Bureau (founded September 1946, Director Koh Whang-kyung) published \"New Homemaking\" and moved to Hokuksa Temple on 27 February. Altrusa Club heard Mrs. Nixon and awarded a nursing fellowship; YWCA began English, night school and dressmaking classes."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[494,499],"pp":"","s":"Pages 494-499 hold no report content: blank Google-digitized pages, a back-cover image, a library date-due slip (stamped June 2010) and a final page of repeated meaningless OCR math symbols."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-30","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-30-1948-03","s":"J","no":30,"d":"1948-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 30 (March 1948)","v":"","pg":167,"o":"","h":[],"k":[],"toc":[],"sk":1},{"id":"K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":30,"d":"1948-03","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 30 (March 1948)","v":"","pg":655,"o":"Number 30 of South Korean Interim Government Activities covers March 1948, bound in a Google Books volume with Nos. 31 (April) and 32 (May). It reports a turbulent month: Korean politics divided over the UN-observed 10 May election in the south, communist-inspired riots under a South Korea Labor Party plan captured by police, a wildcat Seoul Electric strike, and Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic's exchange with Pyongyang leaders over a joint conference. Administrative items include Ordinance No. 176 (protection from unlawful arrest), creation of the National Land Administration to sell former Japanese farmland, and formation of the National Election Committee. Economic sections give detailed tables on prices, industrial production, coal, trade, finance, transport and labor, with prices softening and imports exceeding exports. Historians will find useful the National Economic Board statistics, FY 1948/49 aid planning, refugee flows, Mukden repatriation planning, and press and youth-group reports.","h":["Ordinance No. 176 gave Koreans protection against unlawful arrest, effective 1 April, with prisoner time allowances.","Ordinance No. 173/174 of 22 March created the National Land Administration and dissolved the New Korea Company.","Seoul Electric Company wildcat strike from 13 March; Declaration No. 6 declared it unlawful on 15 March.","Southbound refugee flow rose nearly 200 percent over February; Mukden repatriation of 2,000 Koreans arranged.","Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic wrote to Pyongyang; North Korea accepted a joint conference, proposed for 14 April.","Flour output rose 123 percent in February; bituminous coal imports reached 75,243 tons in March.","Private imports of 625.9 million won exceeded exports of 384.4 million; about 95 percent of exports went to Hong Kong.","Korean National Youth reached 646,331 members and 75 million volunteer hours in 15 months."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","National Economic Board","May 1948 election","National Election Committee","Ordinance No. 176","National Land Administration","vested property","refugees from North Korea","Mukden repatriates","Seoul Electric strike","Korean National Youth","price and production statistics","coal and electric power","North-South leaders' conference","Radio Pyongyang"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,19],"pp":"","s":"Google Books cover pages, cover of No. 30 (March 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board), National Economic Board membership list at 31 March, foreword by Arthur G. Peterson on statistics, and full table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[20,23],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Wilhelm Anderson's overview: factional split over the UN-observed May election, communist riots under the South Korea Labor Party plan, Ordinance No. 176, land reform, rising flour and paper output, falling prices, and $125 million FY 1948/49 aid program."},{"t":"PART I. BASIC ASPECTS","lv":1,"p":[24,35],"pp":"","s":"Population, foreign affairs and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[24,31],"pp":"5","s":"Refugee influx from the north up nearly 200 percent, Koreans in Mukden, and Koreans in Japan: repatriation complaints, suffrage, protest meeting, schools and organizations."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"5","s":"Southbound refugee flow jumped about 200 percent over February; refugees distributed among eight provinces; those bypassing camps lack ration cards, fueling crime."},{"t":"Koreans in Manchuria","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"6","s":"58,558 Koreans came from Manchuria since the surrender; plan to fly 2,000 leaders from Mukden to Tientsin and ship them to Inchon, with 15,000 more under study."},{"t":"Repatriation from Japan","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"9","s":"Osaka Koreans petition against slow repatriation processing (three months minimum) and the 1,000-yen exchange limit."},{"t":"Korean Suffrage Ruling","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"9","s":"Koreans remaining in Japan keep Japanese status since 20 November 1946, so cannot vote; Japan-based factions nevertheless split on the election."},{"t":"Korean Protest Meeting in Japan","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"10","s":"About 15,000 Koreans at Osaka 1 March rally organized by the communist League adopted ten resolutions, including simultaneous US-Soviet withdrawal."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"11","s":"Japanese vice-minister rejects Korean school autonomy; school consolidation in Osaka and Kyoto; pupils' form letters to General Mullins."},{"t":"Other Korean Affairs in Japan","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"12","s":"Korea Foreign Trade Association showrooms in Tokyo and Osaka, the Tamkyo mediation club, and Japanese press reaction to North Korea's draft constitution."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[32,35],"pp":"13","s":"Office of Property Custody activity: sales and appraisals, decentralized audits, 714 company investigations, and policy rulings on Korean directors and stolen-goods claims."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"13","s":"Four OPC divisions active; provincial affairs, disposal, audit and operations summarized."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"13","s":"Unusually large March sales of moveables and properties, including agar agar, coins and Datsun cars; vested company counts by province."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"14","s":"Corporate reorganization sub-section finished 714 investigations; 54 sales of vested shares ordered; 3,505 financial statements received."},{"t":"Audits and Investigations","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"14","s":"Audit Division decentralizes desk audits to nine provincial OPC branches."},{"t":"Provincial Affairs","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"15","s":"Nine Korean liaison men appointed, alternating weeks between field and national office; more inspection trips."},{"t":"Policies and Decisions","lv":3,"p":[34,35],"pp":"15","s":"Rulings on Korean board representation in firms with small Korean holdings and on claims to proceeds of stolen property."},{"t":"PART II. PRODUCTION AND SUPPLY","lv":1,"p":[36,115],"pp":"","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,48],"pp":"17","s":"Creation of the National Land Administration, completed straw-products collection, silk decontrol, fertilizer imports over 277,000 tons, and seed, forestry and fisheries items."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics Bureau","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"17","s":"Farm household tables for 1945-46 show fewer large farms; provincial authority to adjust seed rice prices."},{"t":"Agricultural Production Bureau","lv":3,"p":[38,39],"pp":"19","s":"Livestock feed, 14 million chicks hatched in 1947, cotton goal of 182,000 chungbo, silk decontrol of 27 February, 38 million mulberry seedlings, irrigation ponds."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"21","s":"104 million seedlings available for spring planting; 7 million won for planting vested forest; 1947 forest product output."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"21","s":"Exports to Japan of 517 tons dried anchovy-type fish and 656,760 bundles of laver completed contracts."},{"t":"Korean Agriculture Association","lv":3,"p":[40,44],"pp":"21","s":"Fertilizer imports exceed 277,000 tons, 67 percent sold; straw bag and rope collection finished; seed potato shipments."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[45,46],"pp":"26","s":"Experiment station 1948 project lists, sericulture technicians' meeting, and Extension Service school; 1,100 4-H clubs with 25,000 members."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"28","s":"Ordinance No. 174 of 22 March dissolves the New Korea Company; its land passes to the new National Land Administration, remainder to OPC."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[49,56],"pp":"30","s":"Seoul prices softened in March, rice down nearly seven percent; unweighted indexes dropped; wholesale metals, building materials and textiles up."},{"t":"Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[49,49],"pp":"30","s":"Most retail prices fell; cleaned rice down nearly seven percent, suggesting inflation halted."},{"t":"Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[49,56],"pp":"30","s":"Metals, building materials and textiles rose; fuel unchanged; grains and fish fell."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[57,68],"pp":"38","s":"February output recovered: flour up 123 percent, paper 73.6 percent, cotton yarn 10.6 percent; cement halted by power shortage; data coverage criticized."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[57,57],"pp":"38","s":"Flour 2,315 tons versus 1,039 in January; noodles down 39.5 percent seasonally."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[58,58],"pp":"39","s":"Cotton yarn up 10.6 percent, cloth up 11.8 percent, both still below 1947 monthly average."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"40","s":"Production 392 tons; Pukun Mill setting records but facing pulp and coal shortages."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[60,60],"pp":"41","s":"Nail output down 17 percent because of New Year holiday and coal and power shortages."},{"t":"Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[61,64],"pp":"42","s":"Briquettes nearly stopped, no cement in February, rubber tires mixed, first data on oxygen, carbide, matches, soap."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[65,65],"pp":"46","s":"Electric insulators and pencils."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"47","s":"Government salterns idle fifth month; private plants 148 tons salt; cigarettes up 19.1 percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[69,80],"pp":"50","s":"Record bituminous coal imports (75,243 tons), rising anthracite output and quotas, coal tar pitch shortage, and tungsten, copper and zinc notes."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[69,73],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite exceeded January record; quota raised to 76,000 tons; stockpiles 182,000 tons; March allocations by Coal Committee."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal","lv":3,"p":[74,74],"pp":"55","s":"Japanese imports 75,243 tons in March against record 83,330 in February; Commerce allotted 7,500 tons."},{"t":"Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"56","s":"Consumer receipts lag production owing to transit losses and rail limits."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"56","s":"Coal tar pitch shortage cut briquette output 40 percent; US supply on order."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[76,80],"pp":"57","s":"Copper, tungsten (Sangdong flotation cells) and zinc concentrates (300 tons from Mitsubishi) reported."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[81,95],"pp":"62","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board, civilian supply and petroleum allocations, salt, ex-Japanese metals, and distribution of Korean-produced goods and year-end bonuses."},{"t":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board","lv":3,"p":[81,81],"pp":"62","s":"New unified distribution procedure for government and vested-industry employees; publicity efforts."},{"t":"Allocation of Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[82,82],"pp":"62","s":"March allocations to Commerce, Agriculture and Finance; 273 miscellaneous Honolulu FLC items."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[83,85],"pp":"64","s":"Petroleum allocations and sales; pre-1 April price rise rush; drum deposit doubled; fuel oil to Pusan power barge."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[86,86],"pp":"67","s":"29,516 tons distributed in FY 1947/48; spring soy-sauce distribution began 1 March."},{"t":"Allocation of Ex-Japanese Materials","lv":3,"p":[87,87],"pp":"68","s":"Brass, copper coins, scrap iron, steel and minerals from captured Japanese stocks allocated; zinc exhausted."},{"t":"Allocation of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[88,91],"pp":"69","s":"Board met 18 March; goods worth 3.5 million won to employees of 13 departments."},{"t":"Distribution of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[92,95],"pp":"73","s":"1947 year-end bonus goods distributed through the Federation of Financial Associations."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,107],"pp":"77","s":"Private imports 625.9 million won against exports 384.4 million; 95 percent of exports to Hong Kong; first Korean merchant ship to Hong Kong; coal, fertilizer, grain imports."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[96,100],"pp":"77","s":"Imports 625,941,711 won, exports 384,449,388 won (agar agar, eel, ginseng, furs); Pusan handled most trade."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[101,101],"pp":"82","s":"Korea Steamship's Aeng Do Wan sailed for Hong Kong 31 March; ship arrivals and cargoes at Pusan."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[101,102],"pp":"82","s":"Trade with United States, China and Japan; activities at Pusan."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"84","s":"Sales to the Army Exchange Service in March highest since program began August 1947."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[104,107],"pp":"85","s":"75,243 tons coal, 49,605 tons fertilizer (37,336 ammonium nitrate), wheat, rice, sugar, 34,619 tons salt, petroleum figures first included."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[108,115],"pp":"89","s":"Department of Labor field training conference, Seoul Electric Company wildcat strike and Declaration No. 6, collective bargaining, child labor, and labor education."},{"t":"Organization and Organizational Training","lv":3,"p":[108,109],"pp":"89","s":"New National Labor Mediation Board terms; 25-27 March conference on lectures, reports, bargaining quotas, police interference and cost-of-living survey."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[110,112],"pp":"91","s":"Seoul Electric strike from 13 March, declared unlawful 15 March; Marine Bureau election won by Tai Han; conciliation cases."},{"t":"Labor Laws","lv":3,"p":[113,113],"pp":"94","s":"Employment of minors in government offices and enforcement of the Maximum Working Hours Law; child labor investigation."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"95","s":"Three booklets published, 7,000 copies of Labor News, and HLKA weekly radio programs."},{"t":"PART III. PUBLIC SERVICES","lv":1,"p":[116,145],"pp":"","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[116,126],"pp":"97","s":"Rainbow Coastal Service launched, no repeat of February rail sabotage, February rail receipts up 12 percent, freight up 14 percent, LST recalls."},{"t":"General and Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[116,116],"pp":"97","s":"Five-schedule Rainbow coastal passenger and freight service; department revenue up 24.5 million won."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[117,121],"pp":"98","s":"Railroad receipts and traffic up; three more locomotives operating; employment down seventh month; coal use slightly lower."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[122,125],"pp":"102","s":"Marine receipts down 4 million won, staff down 10 percent, two LSTs recalled to SCAP, shipping demand exceeds supply."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"107","s":"Slightly higher expenditures and fewer employees."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[127,135],"pp":"108","s":"Leftist sabotage interrupts lines; revenue fell 16 million won; postal savings, telephone, telegraph, power-carrier survey and broadcasting repairs."},{"t":"General, Revenue and Expenditures, Employment, Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[127,128],"pp":"108","s":"Sabotage campaign, revenue decline, spending cuts, first staff decrease since October 1947."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[129,132],"pp":"110","s":"Depositors up 1,819 but deposits down 22 million won; insurance policies slightly fewer."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"114","s":"Messages and revenue fell; Korean engineers to survey power-carrier systems."},{"t":"Communications Bureau","lv":3,"p":[134,134],"pp":"115","s":"Weather-station space at Inchon for radio equipment; related technical work."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting Corporation","lv":3,"p":[135,135],"pp":"116","s":"Public address systems repaired by month end; auxiliary transmission line under construction."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[136,139],"pp":"117","s":"Electricity distribution 73.8 million KWH, Seoul streetcars halted 12-17 March by strike, Pusan fare increases."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"117","s":"Distribution 73.8 million KWH; generation 26.8 million KWH."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Actions","lv":3,"p":[137,137],"pp":"118","s":"Capability estimates and conservation; illegal floor heating a major loss."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[137,138],"pp":"118","s":"High thermal and hydro output; 13 trouble calls repaired."},{"t":"Street Railways","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"120","s":"Seoul service halted by strike; Pusan fares raised on Committee advice."},{"t":"Permanent Public Utilities Committee","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"120","s":"Sessions with Utilities Bureau and Finance on rates."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[140,145],"pp":"121","s":"Seoul-Pusan highway returned to provincial control 31 March, volunteer road labor, bridge projects, Han River water works completed, 180 fires."},{"t":"General and Roads and Bridges","lv":3,"p":[140,142],"pp":"121","s":"Seoul-Samchok highway survey; volunteer maintenance; Hyopchong, Andong and Chonan bridges."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"124","s":"Water Resources Bureau tour; Chungchong-namdo completed four projects."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply System","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"124","s":"Han River-Ascom City reservoir project completed; Japanese chlorinators installed."},{"t":"Geodetic Surveys","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"125","s":"New topographic map plates; triangulation of Sosa area."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"125","s":"180 fires, 397 million won damage, five killed."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[144,145],"pp":"125","s":"86 graduates of Heavy Equipment Operators' Training School at Ascom City."},{"t":"PART IV. FINANCE","lv":1,"p":[146,163],"pp":"","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[146,153],"pp":"127","s":"Revenue exceeded expenditure; revenue-to-expenditure ratio 75 percent; supplemental appropriations; Bank of Chosun note issue and overdraft; Civilian Supply Program accounts."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[146,148],"pp":"127","s":"Note issue and currency circulation trend through March."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[149,149],"pp":"130","s":"Cumulative overdraft versus revenue deposit balances."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[149,149],"pp":"130","s":"Supplemental appropriations including 60 million won for the election; 1.25 billion won pending."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[150,150],"pp":"131","s":"February revenue up 152 million won; expenditures down 715 million, lowest since May 1947."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[151,151],"pp":"132","s":"Revenue was 75 percent of expenditure and 62 percent of budget over 11 months."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[152,153],"pp":"133","s":"Civilian supply financial activity; 12.2 billion won receivable."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[154,163],"pp":"135","s":"February cash balances fell 1.5 billion won, deposits down 3.18 billion, loans down 7 percent; insurance activity; rice-loan rediscount rate raised; ban on political credit."},{"t":"Cash Balances and Deposits","lv":3,"p":[154,156],"pp":"135","s":"Largest monthly cash decline since liberation; deposit trends of government, banks and public."},{"t":"Deposit Balances in Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"138","s":"Deposits fell at each institution; government deposits slightly higher."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[158,159],"pp":"139","s":"Net loan decrease of 2.34 billion won; Bank of Chosun held 32 percent of loans."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"141","s":"Insurance in force rose 83.8 million won."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[161,161],"pp":"142","s":"9,920 new policies, over 3 billion won liabilities."},{"t":"Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[161,161],"pp":"142","s":"Han Kook Fire and Marine report, FY 1948/49 budgets, uniform bank examination form."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[162,163],"pp":"143","s":"Rediscount rate on rice loans raised to 4.02 percent; directive barring credit to finance politics."},{"t":"PART V. GOVERNMENT POLITICS","lv":1,"p":[164,187],"pp":"","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, legal affairs, police and interzone activities."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[164,168],"pp":"145","s":"FY 1948/49 budget review, GARIOA import program, Personnel Ceiling Board, civil service exam, National Election Committee, MG personnel."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[164,164],"pp":"145","s":"Supplemental budgets and agency funding limited to one-twelfth for April."},{"t":"Requirements Program","lv":3,"p":[165,165],"pp":"146","s":"GARIOA import-export program for FY 1948/49 sent to Joint Chiefs of Staff; separate Japan-sourced program."},{"t":"Personnel Ceiling Board","lv":3,"p":[166,166],"pp":"147","s":"Board organized 9 January sets maximum personnel for agencies."},{"t":"Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"148","s":"31 March clerk examination, grades CAF 10-13, about 1,200 applicants."},{"t":"National Election Committee","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"148","s":"Executive Order No. 14 created 15-member committee; four resigned 30 March; over 50 percent registration."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[168,168],"pp":"149","s":"Personnel numbers at end of March; civilian turnover high from one-year contracts."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[169,170],"pp":"150","s":"Almost no legislation; Kimm Kiu-sic's resignation accepted 2 March; 29 more resignations; nationality law reading; bribery allegations."},{"t":"General and Resignations","lv":3,"p":[169,169],"pp":"150","s":"Rightist Urgent Proposal split the Assembly; Speaker and Vice Speaker resigned."},{"t":"Action of the Assembly","lv":3,"p":[170,170],"pp":"151","s":"Second reading of Temporary Provisions on Nationality; prostitution-abolition bribery charges."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[171,175],"pp":"152","s":"Politics split over the UN election; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic's letter to Kim Il-sung and Kim Doo-bong; Rhee's Hankook Democratic Party dispute."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[171,171],"pp":"152","s":"Division between supporters led by Rhee and opponents among moderates, leftists and Kim Koo."},{"t":"North-South Leaders' Conference","lv":3,"p":[172,173],"pp":"153","s":"Letters to Pyongyang; North Korean acceptance and suggested date of 14 April."},{"t":"Organizations","lv":3,"p":[174,175],"pp":"155","s":"National Representative Group formed 7 March; friction within Rhee's coalition; moderate unification and women's parties."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[176,182],"pp":"157","s":"Ordinance No. 176 protects against unlawful arrest, time allowances for prisoners, legal opinions, prison population 22,446, guard graduation, court liaison."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[176,179],"pp":"157","s":"Ordinances in the Official Gazette and drafts; Ordinance No. 176 on criminal procedures effective 1 April."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"161","s":"Opinions issued to departments."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"162","s":"Time-allowance ordinance, 142 guards graduated 29 March, prison population 22,446, camp expansion."},{"t":"Court Liaison","lv":3,"p":[182,182],"pp":"163","s":"Taegu judges dispute settled; 26 March judges' and prosecutors' meeting on election law."},{"t":"Section 5. National Police","lv":2,"p":[183,185],"pp":"164","s":"Riot plan and disturbed-area reports from Kyongsang-namdo, Chungchong-namdo, Cholla-namdo and Kyongsang-pukto."},{"t":"Disturbances, Riot Plan and Disturbed Areas","lv":3,"p":[183,185],"pp":"164","s":"Master plan seized; attacks on police boxes, Chinhae city hall burned, killings and 41 arrests in Kyongsang-namdo."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[186,187],"pp":"167","s":"Mail exchange and Radio Pyongyang propaganda analysis."},{"t":"Mail Exchanges","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"167","s":"More than 37,000 pieces of mail exchanged between North and South."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[186,187],"pp":"167","s":"Propaganda against the election, UN Commission and Menon and Arranz; promotion of Kim Il-sung and the joint conference."},{"t":"PART VI. SOCIAL","lv":1,"p":[188,221],"pp":"","s":"Health, education, culture, press, youth and women."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[188,192],"pp":"169","s":"Cholera prevention program, 2,000 Mukden repatriates, leprosarium transfer to Presbyterian missions, nursing training, refugee camps, relief rates raised."},{"t":"General, Preventive Medicine and Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[188,189],"pp":"169","s":"Typhus, typhoid and diphtheria main diseases; 25 doctors licensed; internships in Army hospitals."},{"t":"Nursing, Veterinary and Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"171","s":"Nursing capping, refresher courses, Eastgate slaughterhouse reopened, Cheju dog rabies vaccination."},{"t":"Narcotics, Laboratories and Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[191,192],"pp":"172","s":"LARA relief goods, Uijongbu camp inspection, new children's homes, increased home relief rates."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[193,197],"pp":"174","s":"Teachers' workshops spread to provinces, technician training survey, textbook output, medical and nursing education, American Language Institute."},{"t":"Bureaus of Common Schools and Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[193,193],"pp":"174","s":"Home economics workshop for 60 teachers, English pronunciation drills, women teachers' meeting at Taegu."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[194,194],"pp":"175","s":"Nurse refresher courses, library objectives, medical education goals."},{"t":"Textbooks, Research and Adult Education, American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[195,197],"pp":"175","s":"New textbooks, skilled-worker survey, 300-woman election familiarization conference, language institute enrollment."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[198,204],"pp":"179","s":"Sam Il Day history, Korean alphabet, Confucian festival, puppet shows, sunrise service, HLKA broadcasts, art and music, and sports including Winter Olympics."},{"t":"Sam Il Day and Korean Alphabet","lv":3,"p":[198,199],"pp":"179","s":"29th anniversary of 1919 Declaration of Independence; King Se-jong's 1443 alphabet."},{"t":"Festival, Puppet Show and Sunrise Service","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"181","s":"Confucian Spring Festival 13 March; Kyonggi puppet shows; 20,000 at Nam San Easter service."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting System and Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[201,202],"pp":"182","s":"HLKA programs, art exhibitions, symphony concert, theater rates."},{"t":"Sports","lv":3,"p":[203,204],"pp":"184","s":"Seoul-Suwon relay and soccer schedule, Winter Olympics results."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[205,211],"pp":"186","s":"Press reaction to election, Pyongyang conference and reforms; procurator-police dispute; Ordinance No. 176; strike and riot coverage; fear of Japan."},{"t":"The Election and Reforms","lv":3,"p":[205,208],"pp":"186","s":"Factional attitudes, free atmosphere debate, land sale and Ordinance No. 176 reactions."},{"t":"Youth Groups, Police, Strikes, Foreign Affairs and Public Information","lv":3,"p":[209,211],"pp":"190","s":"Political murder trials, 55 arrests, streetcar strike poll, Philippine base report, cost-of-living survey of 288 families."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[212,216],"pp":"193","s":"Membership 646,331, 75 million volunteer hours in 15 months, training and election work; opposition from rightist and leftist groups."},{"t":"General, Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[212,213],"pp":"193","s":"Membership 646,331; 36,599 volunteer leaders trained; refused merger of rightist youth group."},{"t":"Community Service","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"195","s":"2,151,740 members worked 75,289,176 hours; classes, playgrounds, tree planting."},{"t":"Other Projects, Election and Opposition","lv":3,"p":[215,216],"pp":"196","s":"Speakers' bureaus, election awareness work, and attacks from rival groups."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[217,221],"pp":"198","s":"Women's Bureau election drive, English classes, Salvation Army Home, nurses, policewomen, YWCA, Red Cross, and Cheju Island women."},{"t":"Women's Bureau and Pioneer Institution","lv":3,"p":[217,217],"pp":"198","s":"Seven Seoul women selected as candidates; election pamphlets; first home for unmarried mothers."},{"t":"Nurses, Policewomen, YWCA, Red Cross and Cheju Island","lv":3,"p":[218,221],"pp":"199","s":"Candle-light capping at Severance, YWCA leadership conference, Cheju women divers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-31-in-K-30","K-32-in-K-30"]},{"id":"J-31","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-31-1948-04","s":"J","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"","pg":168,"o":"Summation No. 31 covers April 1948 (statistics mostly for February-March). Politically, government employees' unions accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale on 16 April after SCAP forbade a communications strike; the Ashida coalition was strained by the Social Democratic demand to suspend war bond interest, cabinet disagreement over labor law revision, and the Tsuji hoarded-goods inquiry. Korean protests against school closures in Osaka and Kobe required Occupation troops. The Diet created the Maritime Safety Board and restored parliamentary vice-ministers; the purge continued and the IMTFE heard final defense summations. Economically, March output hit postwar highs in steel, chemicals, rayon, cotton and silk fabrics, with coal at 2.86 million tons, while the February trade deficit was $60.9 million. Finance, rationing, labor, health, education and media statistics follow. Historians will find detailed tables on production, trade, repatriation and the war crimes trials.","h":["Government unions accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale 16 April after SCAP barred a communications workers' general strike.","Social Democrats' proposal to suspend war bond interest split the coalition; the council reported both sides.","Koreans protested school closures in Osaka and Kobe 23-26 April; Occupation troops restored order in Kobe.","Maritime Safety Authorities Bill passed 15 April, authorizing up to 10,000 men and 125 vessels.","IMTFE defense summations completed 15 April; the court adjourned 16 April to prepare judgment on 25 accused.","February trade deficit was $60.9 million; 73 percent of imports came from the United States.","By 17 April 711,036 persons were screened in the purge; 4,880 removed and 2,876 barred.","764,900 persons were still awaiting repatriation to Japan; 396 returned in April."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","government employees wage dispute","war bond interest","Tsuji case","purge","Korean schools","Maritime Safety Board","IMTFE","coal production","industrial production","foreign trade","rationing","repatriation","textbooks","reparations"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Summation No. 31 (April 1948), a table of contents for Parts I to IV (General, Political, Economic, Social) and a list of charts, maps and graphs, from the industrial production indexes through coal, textiles, finance, health and publishing tables to the Map of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Brief digest of the month's developments across all fields, paragraph-numbered."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"The Restitution Advisory Committee was established 13 April to advise the Supreme Commander on disposing of looted property found in Japan. The Civil Property Custodian chairs it ex officio, with one representative from each Reparations and Restitution Delegation of the 11 Far Eastern Commission nations wishing to take part."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Government employees accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale; Cabinet members disagreed on labor-law changes; the war bond interest dispute went to interparty talks; and the Cabinet failed to settle the 1948-49 budget. Also covered: Tsuji fund inquiry, party maneuvers, 18 bills, purge totals, Korean school protests in Osaka and Kobe, the Maritime Safety bill and war crimes trial."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,14],"pp":"7","s":"March output rose in coal, pig iron, steel, chemicals (record caustic soda), machinery, textiles, bicycles (47,120) and motor vehicles. February imports were $70.3 million against exports of $9.4 million, a deficit of $60.9 million. Also: new radio and telegraph links, the 220.4 billion yen note circulation, 353 looted ships, 7,805 machine tools shipped as reparations, and scientific research notes."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"Japanese B encephalitis research began at the National Institute of Health, penicillin output rose 138 percent, and 764,900 persons awaited repatriation. Education items: 647 million yen for lower secondary schools, 94,650,553 textbooks authorized, and the National Diet Library moved. Religion, art exhibition, public opinion surveys, radio, films and music are also noted."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,47],"pp":"21","s":"Government and politics, public safety, and legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,32],"pp":"23","s":"Resolution of the government-employee wage dispute, war bond interest controversy, Tsuji hoarded-goods inquiry, party positions, Diet business and the purge."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[17,26],"pp":"23","s":"The government employees' dispute over the 2,920-yen scale ended 16 April after SCAP three times barred communications strikes; Ashida and Labor Minister Kato clashed over labor-law revision. Also: the war bond interest conference, Ashida's and Kitamura's economic statements, provisional budgets, the governors' conference, Local Autonomy Law amendment, Tsuji inquiry, bribery arrests including Vice-minister Ino, and a national inventory of hoarded goods."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[27,29],"pp":"","s":"The Democratic Party defined 'modified capitalism', stayed optimistic about the coalition, and denied merger with the Democratic Liberals, losing three members to them. Social Democrats defended war bond interest suspension, the Satsuki-kai backed Kato, and the party gained to 123 lower house seats. Democratic Liberals attacked the Cabinet; the People's Cooperative Party backed it."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"","s":"SCAP urged curbs on Diet absenteeism and the House cancelled its recess. Ashida asked to extend the session to end of May. The Diet received 17 bills and enacted 18 laws including the Coal Board and Maritime Safety bills. Democratic and People's Cooperative candidates won the Hiroshima by-election, and 22 parliamentary vice-ministers were appointed 15 April."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"","s":"By 17 April 711,036 persons were screened, with 4,880 removed and 2,876 barred; 189,162 were finally designated. Media screening reached 490 final designations, with 78 pending and 331 writers provisionally designated. The Appeals Board rejected 135 appeals. Destroyed records were being replaced, and the Special Investigation Bureau was organized on 20 April with nine regional headquarters."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[33,35],"pp":"55","s":"Korean-school closure protests in Osaka (23-26 April) left 23 Koreans and 58 police injured and 183 arrested; Occupation Forces helped disperse Kobe crowds that held Hyogo officials captive. February saw 149,885 offenses and 79,220 arrests. Rural Police crime laboratory funded, Maritime Safety Authorities Bill passed, prison population 87,233, 548 rail accidents, 1,806 fires."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[36,47],"pp":"61","s":"The Supreme Court upheld capital punishment on 12 March and a High Marine Court of Inquiry opened 1 April. Atrocity investigations on hand fell to 972; 610 war criminals tried by 20 April (572 convicted, 94 sentenced to death). At the IMTFE the defense summations for all 25 accused (Minami through Umezu) were completed 15 April and the court adjourned 16 April to prepare judgment."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[48,146],"pp":"85","s":"Thirteen sections of statistics and narrative on production, trade, labor, prices, finance, property and science."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[49,51],"pp":"87","s":"Land sales to tenants reached 146,558 cho in March (427,320 cumulative), and 12,051 agricultural cooperatives finished preliminary meetings. Reclamation reached 294,522.7 hectares since 1945, with 499,725 families receiving land. February marine production totaled 110,464 tons; fishing petroleum allocations mostly fell in April. Antarctic whaling yielded 17,544.6 long tons of whale oil; whale oil shipments went to Bremen."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[52,58],"pp":"93","s":"March log production rose to 88,984,500 cubic feet, and February firewood and charcoal output increased. About 2,865,884 cho needed reforestation, with seed supply 22,768 kilograms short, and 1947 cutting outran replanting (866,170 against 430,810 cho). March coal output was 2,862,500 tons, with 29,330,000 tons for the fiscal year; coal mine employees numbered 516,897. March crude oil was 16,016 kiloliters."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[59,69],"pp":"107","s":"March coke output was 221,910 tons. Steel mills set postwar records: pig iron 44,777 tons and rolled steel 76,927 tons. Copper, zinc, lead and ferroalloys rose, aluminum fell to 374 tons, and cement reached 131,669 tons. Chemicals hit new highs (ammonium sulfate 66,186 tons, superphosphate 81,486). Machine tools numbered 327; locomotives and rolling stock increased."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[70,77],"pp":"129","s":"February flour output jumped 290 percent to 103,066 tons after larger wheat releases; sake peaked at 80,780 hectoliters while tobacco output fell. March pulp rose 10 percent and paper reached 34,492 tons. Trucks fell to 942 chassis, three-wheelers hit a postwar high of 1,093, and bicycles a record 47,120. Leather, agricultural implements, handicrafts, business machines, matches and timepieces also reported."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[78,92],"pp":"145","s":"March textile output rose generally, with postwar peaks in rayon filament, spun rayon, cotton and silk fabrics. Cotton yarn reached 23,181,000 pounds, the highest since July 1947, and cotton cloth topped 65.7 million square yards, mostly for export. Raw silk gained 1,236 bales to 10,106; first postwar acetate yarn, acetone and wood pulp imports arrived; wool, bast fiber, knit goods, clothing and dyeing figures also reported. Opening pages cover sewing machines (12,272 home units), cosmetics and toys."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[93,98],"pp":"175","s":"Registered commercial vehicles rose to 163,766, of which 67.5 percent were operable; deadlined trucks lacked fuel, tires or repairs. Government railways carried 303,921,713 passengers, up 9.5 percent, and freight reached 9,861,800 metric tons. Coastal steel-ship cargo was 955,100 tons. Electric generation was 2,607,867,000 kilowatt hours, up 17 percent, and gas output was 61,338,471 cubic meters. Coal allocation plans for 1948 are described."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[99,106],"pp":"187","s":"Telephones in service rose 1.3 percent to 1,195,238, and new radiotelephone and radiotelegraph links opened to Latin America, Europe, Asia and the Netherlands East Indies. Radio households reached 6,443,206, and a school survey found 87 percent of primary schools equipped. Mail volumes, postal savings restoration, vehicles and building projects are reported. Vacuum tube output hit a postwar high; absenteeism was 4.9 percent; a Diet appropriation of ¥3,289,493,000 and a White Paper projecting a deficit over ¥5.3 billion are noted."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[107,116],"pp":"203","s":"The Government resisted revising labor laws as the government workers' dispute ended 16 April with acceptance of the ¥2,920 wage level and a 15-grade pay classification plan. Other settlements covered coal miners, metal miners, private railways, textiles and Toho Studio, while seamen and Tokyo Shibaura talks continued. Unions numbered 29,378 with 6,243,017 members. Also covered are Economic Reconstruction Council dissolution, workmen's compensation, labor supply bans and unemployment insurance, February disputes involving 189,558 workers, and labor education."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[117,121],"pp":"223","s":"February imports were estimated at $70,261,684 and exports at $9,400,019, leaving a deficit of about $60.9 million. About 73 percent of imports came from the United States, with India and Cuba also major sources. Foodstuffs were 44 percent and textiles, chiefly raw cotton, 41 percent of imports. Textiles were 41 percent of exports, followed by nonmetallic minerals and foodstuffs. Private contracts approved were 34 import and 604 export contracts."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[122,127],"pp":"233","s":"Full April rations were met from carryover stocks after 1947 crop quotas were fulfilled in March (rice 100.1 percent), and the substitute-rice program ended. Fish prices were revised, soybean flour and copra meal released, and incentive goods to rice farmers nearly delivered. February rents, family expenditures (6,214 yen) and consumer prices rose; railway food losses and 6,646 enforcement cases were reported."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[128,136],"pp":"245","s":"Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 220.44 billion yen on 30 April, while government securities held fell 6.7 billion yen. Reconstruction Finance Bank loans stood at 59.46 billion yen, with mining and electric power taking most new lending. Also covered: securities liquidation, insurance account mergers, the new Securities and Exchange Law, 134 control organizations closed in March, 1947-48 tax receipts of 124.9 billion yen, April provisional budgets, a 360.8 billion yen national debt and local government finance."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[137,141],"pp":"263","s":"Looted property worth 3.76 million yen was newly taken into custody, with restitutions to the Netherlands, China and Britain; 353 looted ships were reported, 55 percent sunk. The SCAP Custody Account reached 265.2 million yen. SCAP approved 282 restricted-concern transactions, 73 control organizations were ordered closed, 325 concerns designated for reorganization, and Fair Trade Commission filings continued. Advance reparations shipments reached 7,805 machine tools by April."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[142,146],"pp":"273","s":"The Renewal Committee for the Organization of Science held its eighth and final meeting on 25-27 March, recommending a Science Council under the Prime Minister's office and a Cabinet-level coordinating committee. Society meetings, research on a polyvinyl alcohol fiber at Kyoto, and Honda's Comet were reported. Japanese Standards in effect reached 1,009 (1,956 of all types); 175 patents were granted; terminology standards for 20,810 terms were decided, and new publications appeared."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[147,168],"pp":"283","s":"Public health and welfare; education, religion, cultural resources and media of expression."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[148,156],"pp":"285","s":"About 36.4 million typhoid inoculations had been given by 26 April; a 98-case typhus outbreak at an Osaka hospital was contained, and March communicable disease figures were given. Medical supply production and distribution were tabulated, with penicillin at a record 157,019 vials. Welfare aid reached 3,077,031 persons; LARA shipments, Red Cross decisions, 396 repatriates in April (5,843,095 total) and February vital statistics followed."},{"t":"Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[157,168],"pp":"303","s":"April 1948: ¥647 million supplementary budget for lower secondary schools, glass and cement allocations, Korean school protests at Kobe, 12,123 new lower secondary schools reported, textbook authorization committee and 94.7 million textbooks authorized. Religion and cultural items follow. Public opinion surveys (coeducation, Central Screening Committee, tax revision), information programs, press comment, radio, films, and a Fukuoka SCAP library close the section."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-31","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-31-1948-04","s":"K","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"","pg":222,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 31, prepared by the National Economic Board, covers April 1948, the month before the 10 May election. It reports voter registration of 7,730,000 (94 percent) and 939 candidates for 200 Assembly seats, communist violence including the Cheju-do revolt, and the Pyongyang North-South Conference attended by Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic. It records 15,848 refugees entering from the north, the first month of National Land Administration farm sales (28 percent sold), vested property disposal rules, and Military Government amnesty for 3,000 political prisoners. Economic chapters give prices, industrial output (paper, cotton, anthracite), coal and fertilizer imports, private trade (exports above imports), railroads, power, public works, budgets for F/Y 1948/49, bank deposits and note issue. Later chapters cover KILA, legal ordinances and the draft Civil Code, police, interzone mail and Radio Pyongyang, health, education, culture, press, youth and women. Useful for election-eve conditions and statistics.","h":["7,730,000 registered in the 10-day period ending 9 April; 939 candidates, 17 of them women, contested 200 Assembly seats.","Pyongyang North-South Conference opened 19 April; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic joined it and it rejected the 10 May election.","Cheju-do disturbances began 3 April; communists attacked about 52 of 13,455 registration booths.","More than 28 percent of former Japanese farmland sold to farmers through the National Land Administration by 30 April.","15,848 refugees entered South Korea from the north in April, 20 percent above March.","Railroads moved over 480,000 metric tons of freight in March, the highest since October 1945.","Military Governor amnestied 3,000 political prisoners; a draft Civil Code, Korea's first, was completed by Judge Lobingier.","Hodge issued the Proclamation of the Rights of the Korean People on 5 April; election date was set for 10 May as a holiday."],"k":["10 May 1948 election","voter registration","Pyongyang conference","Cheju-do","National Land Administration","vested property","Office of Property Custody","refugees from North Korea","coal and fertilizer imports","private trade Hong Kong","KILA","Civil Code","Korean National Youth","National Economic Board","Seoul Electric strike"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,11],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 31 (April 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK; SKIG and USAMGIK organization charts as of 1 January 1948, with notes on the dual governing apparatus and the 10 May election; Board members under Chairman Dr. Wilhelm Anderson (30 April); and a six-part table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[12,13],"pp":"1","s":"Chief of the Statistics Division Arthur G. Peterson's 13-paragraph overview: 94 percent of 7,730,000 voters registered for the 10 May election despite communist terror, with Cheju revolt and the Pyongyang conference. Over 15,000 refugees came south; 28 percent of Japanese-held farmland sold; prices steady; output, coal imports and literacy (71 percent) rose."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[14,29],"pp":"3","s":"Population and foreign affairs; vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[14,17],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee arrivals rose 20 percent over March to 15,848 despite tighter North Korean restrictions; the Kaesong camp was inspected and Kyonggi-do's quota redistributed for 2,000 Mukden repatriates. The Korean League in Japan expelled anti-communist dissenters. Osaka and Hyogo ordered Korean schools closed; 73 parents were arrested and about 4,000 protesters were dispersed."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[18,29],"pp":"7","s":"Financial statements from vested firms, guaranteed bank loans, published rules for selling small businesses and homes, simplified claims procedure, audit decentralization and rental tables."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"10","s":"Property Disposal Division publicized its rules for selling former Japanese small businesses and residences: auction or sealed bid, appraisal first, direct negotiation after two rejected bids, and no sales to families already owning homes. Terms were 20 percent down, 5 to 7 percent interest and 10-year contracts, with Korean advisory committees. Table 3 shows 96 April sales worth 62.4 million won."},{"t":"Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"13","s":"On 17 April the Military Governor signed a directive simplifying claims against vested property seized under Ordinance No. 33. Of over 50,000 claims, only 50 had been processed in a year. Strong cases now follow a seven-step review by OPC Claims Section lawyers, reaching about 12 cases per day."},{"t":"Property Rentals","lv":3,"p":[26,29],"pp":"15","s":"A Provincial Affairs Division report gave the most detailed picture yet of rented vested property at 1 January 1948: 102,822 leases on 82,339 properties, 3,467 held by government employees, and rent increases in 83 percent of cases. Rents in arrears averaged 21.4 percent of collections (87.2 million won delinquent); net rental revenue since 1945 was 283.5 million won; residences numbered 56,080."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[30,107],"pp":"19","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[30,41],"pp":"19","s":"Fertilizer sales through the Korea Agricultural Association rose to 35,091 metric tons and straw-product collection exceeded its quota. Livestock estimates, silk grading, cotton seed and Agricultural Improvement Service work are covered. Under Ordinance No. 173, 28.7 percent of 587,974 former Japanese farms were sold by 30 April, priced at three times annual crop output."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[42,49],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices in Seoul and nine cities slipped seasonally in April; wholesale prices barely moved, with dried fish, hemp, lumber, cement and coal rising while rice, cotton yarn and charcoal fell. Seoul cleaned rice fell to 1,320 won per mal. The section gives National Price Administration controlled prices and a chart of price and production trends."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[50,60],"pp":"39","s":"March output rose sharply over February: cotton yarn up 28.5 percent, cotton cloth 26.2 percent, nails 19.3 percent and oxygen 80 percent. Paper reached a post-liberation record of 533.4 tons, and rubber-shoe reporting expanded to 86 plants (1,899,834 pairs). Cement stopped for lack of bags; government salterns produced 16,036 tons in April."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[61,72],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite output reached a record 62,796 tons in March, almost entirely from the Samchuk mine (40,596 tons), but under half of local coal could be moved for lack of rail transport. April bituminous imports from Japan were 83,171 tons, 58,150 to railroads. Coal allocations, the Hwasun cave-in, gold field surveys and tungsten, silver, copper and graphite output are covered."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[73,85],"pp":"62","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board, civilian supply allocations to SKIG agencies, petroleum sales and shortages, salt, and distribution of year-end bonus goods."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[75,77],"pp":"64","s":"The Petroleum Distributing Company allocated 6.4 million liters of gasoline, 3.8 million of kerosene and 2.9 million of diesel in April; April sales were below March for most products. Kerosene, diesel and lubricant shortfalls were blamed on stock and drumming limits, overestimated fisheries needs and late arrivals. Ordinances on illegal sales were drafted and pipeline work neared completion."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[86,98],"pp":"75","s":"April permits (113 import, 94 export) implied exports of about 443.8 million won against imports of 376.8 million. March private imports reached 822.7 million won, the highest since September 1946, led by rubber, cotton and newsprint; exports of 654.1 million went over 93 percent to Hong Kong. Government exports to Japan totaled $351,976, and civilian supply imports covered coal, fertilizer, rice, salt and petroleum."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[99,107],"pp":"88","s":"Labor Department work centered on the Seoul Electric streetcar dispute: General Dean's 7 April fact-finding committee recommended reorganizing company management and a union representation election. Marine Bureau and Korea Transportation contract talks continued, a Pusan stevedore rate petition was investigated, and Maximum Working Hours and Child Labor inspections were reported. Immunization and health inspections covered Kangwon-do mines; the refugee-camp labor selection project was abandoned."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[108,141],"pp":"97","s":"Transportation, communications, public utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[108,117],"pp":"97","s":"A priority system for marine shipping space was set in April, giving Pusan 48 percent. March Transportation revenue rose 119 percent (283 million won), the fiscal-year high, partly from a 161 million won Army payment. Railroads carried over 480,000 tons of freight, the most since October 1945, with more locomotives operable. Sabotage was checked, and Pohang harbor was dredged for FS-type ships."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[118,126],"pp":"107","s":"Communist sabotage on the mainland waned in April but an intensive drive hit Cheju, where 16 poles were cut and 26 wires severed. March revenue, telephone and telegraph traffic, and postal deposits rose, while employment fell under reduction-in-force orders. The radio license fee went from 40 to 50 won on 15 April, and 20-won and 50-won stamps were issued on 10 April."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[127,131],"pp":"116","s":"Electricity delivered in April totaled 75.9 million KWH, down slightly from March, with fewer stoppages. The Emergency Power Board met on 7 and 21 April on conservation and a plan for a North Korean cutoff. Plant outputs were reviewed, and the Seoul streetcar tax claim of 27,909,000 won was studied. The Pusan barge YFP-1 began production, and the barge Electra reached Inchon."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[132,141],"pp":"121","s":"April brought a comprehensive inspection of Public Works sites, but roads had virtually no spending for lack of a national appropriation, offset by volunteer labor. Bridge work continued at Andong, Waegwan and Namjong. A new Water Resources Committee met on 2 and 23 April on the Sumjingang dam. Flood control, Seoul water rates, 144 fires with 76 million won in losses, and fire volunteers were reported."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[142,161],"pp":"131","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[142,151],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue fell to 28.2 billion won, with 5,213 million won returned since December after rice collection ended. SKIG's overdraft hit a record 22,005 million won. Additional 1947/48 appropriations of 787 million were authorized, and the Budget Bureau recommended 1948/49 budgets. March revenue of 2,135 million won was a record, with Monopoly Bureau and civilian supply accounts also reported."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[152,161],"pp":"141","s":"Data covered March only. Financial institution deposits rose 6,279 million won to a record 55,305 million, while loans fell for the third month by 1,591 million. Life and damage insurance grew, and a new salary scale and investment limits were set. Ordinance No. 182 required exchange of Japanese-printed 100-won notes by 24 April, and 39 loans totaling 713 million won were approved."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[162,195],"pp":"151","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, legal affairs, police, interzone activities."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[162,164],"pp":"151","s":"Registration closed 9 April with 7,730,000 voters (over 94 percent of those eligible) for 200 districts and 942 candidates. Public Information ran press releases, Hodge, Jacobs and Dean statements, posters, 100 film shorts and radio coverage. The National Economic Board reviewed 1948/49 budgets, approved 400 million won irrigation credit and 250 million for Sumjingang Dam, and a summer grain quota of at least 20 percent. Military Government personnel rose to 3,041."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[165,166],"pp":"154","s":"KILA met only once in April, on 19th, rejecting three resignations (Yun Ki-sop, Kim Ik-tong, Lee Sun-taek), tabling pending bills 20 to 0, and adjourning 30 days by 23 to 0, effectively dissolving itself. The section reviews its history: 90 members from December 1946, 11 Public Acts, four resolutions, and three near-dissolutions over the voting age, the November 1947 outline and the UN petition."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[167,176],"pp":"156","s":"Pyongyang North-South Conference, election preparations, 939 candidates, rightist party splits, Choi Nung-chin candidacy against Rhee, reactions to land reform."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[167,173],"pp":"156","s":"North and South Korean parties reacted along moderate, communist and rightist lines to the Pyongyang conference invitations. Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended after hesitation. Sessions ran 19-23 April with unanimous votes and no real debate, a resolution signed by 16 parties and 45 organizations, and a request that US and USSR troops withdraw. A 30 April joint communique rejected the southern election. The report calls the conference a Soviet victory."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[173,176],"pp":"162","s":"Rightists formed a committee on 1 April under O Sei-chang to speed the election; Rhee called the vote necessary for a UN-recognised government. A total of 939 candidates, including 17 women, sought 200 seats, with the Rhee-aligned society fielding 239 and Dai Dong 89. Parties split over too many candidates, and a poll claimed 91 percent felt forced to register. Choi Nung-chin's challenge to Rhee was nullified on 7 May."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[177,188],"pp":"166","s":"Ordinances 182-191, Hodge's Proclamation of Rights, legal opinions, prison conditions at Kaesong, amnesty releases and the draft Civil Code syllabus."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[183,188],"pp":"172","s":"The Department of Justice finished a proposed first Korean Civil Code, being translated into Korean. Judge Lobingier, its drafter, argued Korea needed its own code instead of the untranslated Japanese one, placed corporation law under juristic persons, and drew heavily on China's code. A syllabus lists four books: Persons, Obligations, Property and Succession, with detailed subheadings."},{"t":"Section 5. National Police","lv":2,"p":[189,190],"pp":"178","s":"Police resented the new court-warrant rule, the release of over 3,000 minor political prisoners and curbs on force, though warrants worked more smoothly than expected. Communist-led violence targeted registration: about 52 of 13,455 booths were attacked. Savage incidents included poisoning arrests and burnings. Cheju-do rioting began 3 April; casualty figures for 3-19 April included 29 rioters killed. Communists courted security forces."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[191,195],"pp":"180","s":"Mail exchange with North Korea became weekly from 15 April; four exchanges moved 59,000 pieces against 37,000 in March. Radio Pyongyang used the joint conference to attack the southern election, praised the Economic Plan, denounced land sales, and broadcast adoption of a constitution on 28-30 April. Police director Chough Pyong-ok reported 21 Koreans killed and about 150 wounded since 7 February, with 74 border violations."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[196,222],"pp":"185","s":"Health, education, culture, press, youth and women."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[196,200],"pp":"185","s":"Public Health and Welfare expanded cholera immunization along coasts and the 38th parallel, and outfitted the refugee ship sailing to Tientsin on 30 April for Mukden repatriates. It received 520,000 pounds of DDT, licensed 74 physicians and 31 pharmacists for narcotics, and finished 10,053 low-cost housing units. UNRRA funds went to orphanages, and the Kaesong refugee camp's operations were described."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[201,206],"pp":"190","s":"Education held curriculum workshops, and prepared a middle school entrance achievement test. Literacy reportedly rose from 21 percent in 1945 to 71 percent by August 1947. The Personnel Ceiling Board cut the department from 177 to 143 positions, and the classroom budget was trimmed. 13,230,670 textbooks had been published since 1945, and the first 7,000 English-language textbooks arrived."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[207,211],"pp":"196","s":"Arbor Day, 5 April, became a national holiday, with 100 million trees planted. Hodge presented instruments to the Korea Symphony Orchestra on 25 April. Concerts, recitals, rain dances, court music and a UNTCOK-hosted program were held, and two commemorative stamps issued. KBS promoted the election, and 58 athletes were selected for the London Olympics after $140,000 was raised."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[212,214],"pp":"201","s":"The press centred on the 10 May election and the Pyongyang conference. 91.7 percent registration was reported, and 939 candidates sought 200 seats, 48 percent independents. Papers covered communist charges against the US, the proposed Fatherland Protection Group, police warrants, the Chang Duk-soo assassins' commuted sentences, Cheju-do disorders, and indignation at the closing of Korean schools in Japan."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[215,217],"pp":"204","s":"The government-sponsored Korean National Youth mobilized 67,475 members on Arbor Day to plant 3,451,300 trees, and ran forest patrols and spring clean-ups. Membership reached 774,949 by 30 April, up over 370,000 since January, with seven new training centers bringing the total to 62. It expanded vocational placement and urged voting in the 10 May election without backing candidates."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[218,222],"pp":"207","s":"The Women's Bureau gave over 50 election speeches, showed the film \"The Peoples Choice\" and distributed posters. It also ran a mothers' school and published 5,000 copies of \"New Homemaking.\" The section also covers Women's Press editorials, American sewing aids, a new home economics association, YWCA feeding, Junior Red Cross, KNY women, and the Koh sisters' welfare work."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-31-in-K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"Bound in K-30","host":"K-30","rg":[[222,449]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:31' on PDF p. 222","pg":228,"o":"Report No. 31 of South Korean Interim Government Activities (USAMGIK), prepared by the National Economic Board, covers April 1948, the month before the 10 May election. It records 94% voter registration (7,730,000), 939-942 candidates for 200 seats, communist sabotage and the Cheju revolt, and the Pyongyang North-South conference rejected by American authorities. Economic sections show higher industrial and anthracite output, stable prices, a trade balance turning to export surplus, record rail freight, rising deposits and a revenue surplus. Political chapters cover KILA's de facto dissolution, amnesty for 3,000 political prisoners, police reform, the draft civil code and land sales. Vested-property policy, refugee flow of 15,848, Korean schools in Japan, youth and women's organizations and literacy gains are also documented. Historians will find dense tables, official framing of the election period and detailed reporting on Property Custody and agrarian reform.","h":["94% of eligible voters (7,730,000) registered for the 10 May election despite communist terror.","Pyongyang North-South conference opened 19 April; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended; rejected the South-only election.","Cheju-do revolt broke out, mixing communist opposition to the election with feuds against police.","About 3,000 political prisoners amnestied; police now need court warrants for arrests.","Anthracite output reached 62,796 tons, up 49.5% from February, mostly from one rehabilitated mine.","28% of vested farmland sold to Korean farmers in the first month of the program.","April exports exceeded imports by about 15% versus a 1,000% import excess a year earlier.","KILA adjourned for 30 days on 19 April, effectively dissolving itself after passing 11 Public Acts."],"k":["USAMGIK","South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","10 May 1948 election","voter registration","Pyongyang conference","Cheju-do","vested property","Office of Property Custody","land reform","refugees from North Korea","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","fertilizer","anthracite coal","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[222,235],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 31 (April 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board; a map; organization charts of USAMGIK and SKIG as of 1 January 1948; notes describing the dual American-Korean apparatus; the NEB roster headed by Dr. Wilhelm Anderson; and a six-part table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[236,237],"pp":"1","s":"Thirteen numbered paragraphs: 7,730,000 voters (94 percent) registered for the 10 May election despite communist terror; the Cheju revolt; the Pyongyang conference and a Soviet-style constitution; over 15,000 refugees; 28 percent of Japanese farmland sold; stable prices; higher output; trade balance; and literacy rising from 21 to 71 percent."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[238,253],"pp":"","s":"Population and foreign affairs; vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[238,241],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee inflow rose 20% over March; Korean League in Japan purged of dissenters; Japan closed non-conforming Korean schools."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[238,239],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee entries from North Korea in April reached 15,848, 20 percent above March, as Russians tightened travel limits. Officials inspected the Kaesong camp on 19 April, where refugees stay about three days. The Kyonggi-do quota was shared among five provinces so Inchon could process 2,000 Mukden repatriates. Cumulative inbound total was 2,384,553 against 886,203 outbound."},{"t":"Korean League in Japan","lv":3,"p":[240,240],"pp":"5","s":"The Office of Foreign Affairs reported that a minority in the Korean League in Japan, trying to end communist domination, was defeated and expelled by leftist leaders following the party line. The League's ideals were Korean nationalism, equal rights with Japanese and foreign-national privileges; its aggressive demands drove dissenters to form separate political groups."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[241,241],"pp":"6","s":"Conflict over Korean schools in Japan peaked in April. Osaka ordered 19 non-compliant schools closed by 15 April, and 14 kept teaching. In Hyogo, 73 parents petitioning the governor were arrested on 15 April; about 4,000 Koreans protesting on 18 April were dispersed by police. A USAMGIK liaison officer urged cooperation with SCAP-directed policy."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[242,253],"pp":"7","s":"Office of Property Custody: financial statements, guaranteed bank loans, disposal rules for small businesses and residences, claims procedure, audits and rentals."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[242,242],"pp":"7","s":"The Office of Property Custody had two main developments in April: operations centered on the Forms 20 and 21 financial statements arriving from supervised industries, and a standard set of rules for disposing of vested property was published widely. Provincial liaison and training improved, policy memoranda were issued, and 21 chemical plants were inspected."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[242,244],"pp":"7","s":"Operations received 895 financial forms in April, with 519 tabulated showing a net loss of 389.5 million won before taxes. Inspections found paper mills limited by fuel and parts shortages and 21 chemical plants under 30 percent of capacity. A forestry custody order was issued, 6,600 thousand won of capital spending approved, and 51 loan cases totaling 2,138 million won tabulated."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[245,246],"pp":"10","s":"Publicity restated rules for selling Japanese small businesses and residences: managers or occupants get first chance, appraisals precede sales, inadequate bids may be rejected, and all-Korean advisory committees review buyers. Terms required 20 percent down, 5-7 percent interest and contracts up to 10 years. April sales completed numbered 96, worth 62.4 million won."},{"t":"Real Estate Section","lv":3,"p":[247,247],"pp":"12","s":"The Real Estate Section handled 19 transfers of Army-released property in April, two to SKIG agencies and six covering 180 Seoul houses, and processed 21 Army requisitions. It also drew up a plan for transferring Army real property to the Korean government through OPC, with provincial custodians as representatives, should USAFIK withdraw."},{"t":"Policies and Precedents","lv":3,"p":[248,248],"pp":"13","s":"The Policy Division issued a statement that property registered to Allied nationals on 9 August 1945 is not vested, but the Custodian will protect it and account for income in the owner's absence. A procedural memorandum covered management and return. Further papers addressed ending brothel use of vested properties and barring lease of critical industrial assets."},{"t":"Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[248,248],"pp":"13","s":"On 17 April the Military Governor signed a directive simplifying disposal of ownership claims against vested property. Of over 50,000 claims under Ordinance No. 33 handled by the Justice Department's commission, only 50 were processed in a year. The new eight-step expedited procedure, using Korean lawyers, sometimes reached 12 cases a day."},{"t":"Audit Division","lv":3,"p":[249,249],"pp":"14","s":"The Audit Division completed audits of seven organizations (three commercial associations, two provincial offices) plus five investigation reports and a Korean Industrial Shipbuilding Association balance sheet. It reported 922 desk audits requested and 52 completed. Audit work was decentralized to provincial offices, with balance-sheet audits replacing detailed ones and a field audit section being formed."},{"t":"Property Rentals","lv":3,"p":[250,253],"pp":"15","s":"A report on rentals as of 1 January 1948 counted 102,822 leases on 82,339 properties, 3.4 percent leased to government employees and 83 percent with rent increases. Tables showed arrears (21.4 percent delinquent, 87.2 million won), gross collections of 319.3 million won since liberation yielding net revenue of 283.5 million, and property types led by 56,080 residences."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[254,331],"pp":"","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[254,265],"pp":"19","s":"Fertilizer sales through KAA rose to 35,091 tons; livestock recovery; silk grading; forest, fisheries and land-sale activities."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics Bureau","lv":3,"p":[254,254],"pp":"19","s":"The new Crop Reporting Service integrated 281 more persons in April, bringing appointments to 664 as of 20 April, with Cholla-pukto and Kyongsang-namdo complete. Training for gun agricultural statisticians began in Kyonggi-do and spread to other provinces. A grain inspectors' school at Yongdungpo graduated 200 under Order No. 4."},{"t":"Agriculture Production Bureau","lv":3,"p":[254,258],"pp":"19","s":"Livestock numbers rose steadily from 1946, with 1949 estimates higher. Seoul milk plant lacked ammonia and lost 30 percent of milk; sheep stations were dropped. USGS arranged 400 tons each of sulphur and copper sulphate from Japan. Cotton and hemp goals were set, exportable silk graded poorly, and coal and cement arrived for irrigation work."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"24","s":"Forest output for January and February 1948 was 39,660 and 36,092 cubic meters of logs, 4,423 and 3,719 tons of charcoal, and 314,150 and 198,362 cubic meters of fuelwood. With warm weather the fuel situation eased, Seoul fuelwood falling from 4,000 to 2,500 won per cubic meter. Forest nurseries received 222 tons of ammonium nitrate."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"24","s":"Processed marine products for 1947 totaled 54,870 metric tons, 3,736 below 1946 and 10,000 under the goal, blamed on shortages of tinplate, salt and transport. Salted fish (27,388 tons) and dried fish (20,874) led. A 14-vessel deep-sea expedition from Pusan landed 389 tons, bringing the program total to 615 tons worth over 26 million won."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[260,262],"pp":"25","s":"Fertilizer sales to farmers rose in April on seasonal demand and a KAA campaign: 38,464 tons imported and 35,091 sold. From July to 10 April, 306,895 tons were received and 75 percent sold. KAA also collected 839.7 million won, and its straw bag and rope collection exceeded quotas, with 22.2 million bags bought."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[263,263],"pp":"28","s":"The service investigated farmer complaints that 20 organizations in the two Kyongsang provinces levied unexplained fees, and an executive order was expected. Maj.-Gen. Dean told provincial officials AIS would unify research, teaching and extension. Experiment station projects were approved, forage land was prepared at two livestock farms, and extension agents agreed to use village discussion clubs."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[264,265],"pp":"29","s":"Sale of former Japanese farmland under Ordinance No. 173 moved quickly: by 30 April, 168,735 of 587,974 farms (28.7 percent) were sold and 8.6 percent had sales binders. Buyers were capped at two chungbo, tenants had preference, price was three times annual crop output, and payment was 20 percent of crop in kind for 15 years."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[266,273],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices in Seoul and nine centers eased slightly in April; wholesale prices nearly unchanged; controlled price lists."},{"t":"Retail and Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[266,271],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices in Seoul and nine cities slipped seasonally in April, except Korean turnip, salt, cotton shirting and coal briquettes. Wholesale prices were nearly unchanged: dried fish, yellow hemp, lumber, cement and coal rose, while cleaned rice, cotton yarn, charcoal, firewood, copper and leather fell. Tables gave indexes (August 1945=100) and won prices; Seoul rice retailed at 1,320 won per mal."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[272,273],"pp":"37","s":"The National Price Administration sets prices for goods from vested or government-controlled enterprises and for Civilian Supply imports. The March 1948 schedule listed controlled prices with effective dates, for example cleaned rice 280 won per large mal, cotton sheeting 56 won per yard, lump anthracite 3,500 won per ton, and calf leather shoes 1,660 won. A chart tracked price and production trends."},{"t":"Section 3: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[274,284],"pp":"39","s":"March output up sharply: cotton yarn 28.5%, cloth 26.2%, paper 36.1%, nails 19.3%, oxygen 80%; rubber shoe reporting widened."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[274,274],"pp":"39","s":"Processed food output: the Taihan mill and six small private mills produced 2,386 tons of flour in March, 3.1 percent above February and 28.7 percent above the 1947 monthly average, while a second large mill was being rehabilitated. Six of nine small mills made 628 tons. Noodle output was 77 tons, far below the seasonal average."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[275,275],"pp":"40","s":"Cotton yarn output in March rose 28.5 percent over February to 522,000 kg, the best since June 1947. Cotton cloth rose 26.2 percent to 2,286,000 meters despite power shortages, one mill getting 300 KW daily against 1,000 KW needed. Mill activity depended on electricity and coal, and cotton distribution to mills remained unreliable."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[276,276],"pp":"41","s":"Machine-made paper from 14 mills hit a post-liberation record of 533.4 metric tons in March, against 391.9 in February. Puksun mill exceeded its best Japanese-era month by 16 percent, and Dai Dong reached a new high. The Dai Yang mill made trial runs, and the first independent ground wood mill, near Seoul, began experiments."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[277,277],"pp":"42","s":"Nail production in March was 148.9 metric tons, 19.3 percent above February's 124.8 and close to the 1947 monthly average of 142.1. The three plants in South Korea reported fairly steady operations. The table gave monthly output from October 1946, with January 1948 at 150.4 tons."},{"t":"Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[278,280],"pp":"43","s":"Chemicals in March: briquettes rose 41.3 percent to 6,398 tons, under a third of March 1947, for lack of pitch; no cement made because storage was full, though 1,710 tons of clinker were; common brick resumed at 10,000 tons; rubber shoes reached 1,899,834 pairs with wider coverage; oxygen rose to 90,455 cubic meters; matches and soap noted."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[281,281],"pp":"46","s":"Miscellaneous output for March: electric insulators fell to 5,512 pieces from 11,760 in February because coal was scarce, while lead pencils rose to 4,521 gross, continuing an upward trend since the start of 1948 (3,089 in January and 3,684 in February)."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[282,284],"pp":"47","s":"Government salterns produced 16,036 tons in April, nearly three times April 1947, aided by weather and better control; private salterns made 154 tons. Cigarette output fell 17.6 percent in March to 288.8 tons after a week's shutdown for lack of endless tape; the tape factory moved to Seoul and began production on 18 March. Cut tobacco rose to 686.6 tons."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[285,296],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite output jumped to 62,796 tons from one rehabilitated mine; Japanese bituminous coal imports; pitch and other minerals."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[285,285],"pp":"50","s":"The Bureau of Mining was beginning to realize its coal rehabilitation goal. Anthracite output expanded sharply in March, and April bituminous imports from Japan nearly matched February's record. Total coal supply from March production and April imports was nearly double the tonnage available three months earlier."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[285,289],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite output rose 49.5 percent in March to 62,796 tons, nearly all from the Samchuk mine (40,596 tons), helped by restored power. Lignite reached 5,112 tons. Under half of the coal reached market, with 53,000 tons stranded at railheads. Hwasun mine was caving, Yong Wol made 10,250 tons in April, and April allocations went to Army, Commerce and railroads."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal","lv":3,"p":[290,290],"pp":"55","s":"Bituminous coal imports from Japan, the only source, were 83,171 tons in April, nearly equal to February's record 83,330. The railroads received 58,150 tons but reportedly needed at least 100,000 monthly. The Department of Commerce received 7,500 tons, of which 3,520 went to gas and coke plants and 1,000 to cement."},{"t":"Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[291,291],"pp":"56","s":"Tonnage delivered to consumers does not match production, import and allocation figures, mainly because of pilferage in transit and because the transport system could not haul all allocated coal. In March 36,659 tons of anthracite and 51,983 tons of bituminous coal were actually supplied."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[291,291],"pp":"56","s":"Coal tar pitch, needed as binder for briquettes, was virtually unavailable in March, so briquette output was little better than one-third of the 1947 monthly average. Pitch had been ordered from the United States but was not expected for two months. It is good for combustion but too expensive for extensive use."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[292,296],"pp":"57","s":"Gold, valued near 10 million dollars in 1942, remains far from rehabilitated; five Bureau of Mining teams visited over 30 vested gold properties and found 683 stamps in 70 mills producing about 20,000 grams monthly with 3,500 miners. Kobayashi Mining planned ferro-tungsten recovery of 10 tons monthly. Tables gave March output of gold, silver, copper, tungsten, graphite, zinc and lead."},{"t":"Section 5: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[297,309],"pp":"62","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board procedure, civilian supply allocations, petroleum, salt, and allocation and distribution of Korean-produced commodities."},{"t":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board","lv":3,"p":[297,297],"pp":"62","s":"The National Consumer Goods Office completed and distributed its Standard Operating Procedure, summarizing Executive Order No. 12 and the steps in allocating, distributing and reporting consumer goods. Conferences with G-4, XXIV Corps agreed that incentive goods for service employees go directly to unit commanders where employee co-operatives exist; otherwise the Federation of Financial Associations distributes."},{"t":"Allocation of Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[297,298],"pp":"62","s":"Civilian Supply, Commerce and Price Administration agreed that miscellaneous supplies from Pusan would be priced against inventory and reconciled later with shipping documents, which lag two to three months, easing Yongdungpo warehouse congestion. DIS surplus clothing and mess equipment was inventoried for re-allocation. April allocations included 4,555 bales raw cotton, 1,500 tons rubber, 78,980 bags cement and 500 cases each of radio batteries."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[299,301],"pp":"64","s":"Petroleum Distributing Company April allocations were 6,414,345 liters gasoline, 3,815,507 kerosene, 2,924,821 diesel and 4,242,796 Navy Special fuel oil, with departmental shares for Transportation, Coast Guard, Marine Transportation and electric power. Sales tables show 1947 gasoline at 60,977 thousand liters. Kerosene, diesel and lubricant shortfalls were explained; an anti-illegal-sales ordinance was drafted and the Inchon pipeline to Wangemiri neared completion."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[302,302],"pp":"67","s":"The Monopoly Bureau reported 25,803 metric tons of salt distributed in April 1948, with 60,812,947 kilograms in stock for May. Home soya-sauce rations of 2.7 kg per person took 21,169,282 kg, fisheries curing 3,268,020 kg, industrial uses 718,596 kg and miscellaneous users such as hotels, restaurants and police mess halls 631,287 kg."},{"t":"Allocation of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[302,305],"pp":"67","s":"On 15 April the Consumer Goods Allocation Board unanimously set a 40 per cent government and vested-industry employee share and 60 per cent public share, up from 30-70. Goods worth 12,000,000 won from the former Civil Service Welfare Committee were allocated to 33 organizations. Tables give April allocations totaling 766,082 thousand won across 446 allocations, and monthly cloth, shoe, tire, soap, match, cement, oil and nail allocations."},{"t":"Distribution of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[306,309],"pp":"71","s":"The 1947 year-end bonus distribution continued, with 41,000,000 won of goods bought, 96 per cent of the Commerce allocation; 7,828,235 won of goods could not be purchased for stated reasons. The FFA distribution contract was completed 23 April: FFA receives 15 per cent, pays FDA eight, and retailers earn 13 per cent. Tables list 462,505 eligible employees and FFA purchases of 1,628,036 thousand won since July 1946."},{"t":"Section 6: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[310,322],"pp":"75","s":"April licenses show exports (443.8 million won) exceeding imports (376.8 million won) by about 15%, against 1,000% excess of imports a year earlier."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[310,314],"pp":"75","s":"Licenses issued in April (113 import, 94 export) indicate exports of about 443.8 million won against imports of 376.8 million, a marked improvement from April 1947. March private imports reached 822.7 million won, led by rubber, cotton and newsprint; exports of 654.1 million consisted of dried fish, agar, ginseng and furs. Over 93 per cent of exports went to Hong Kong; Inchon took 62 per cent of imports."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[315,315],"pp":"80","s":"Private trade with Hong Kong in April was carried by vessels including the Coastal Buccaneer, Suchow, Kutsang, Choysang and Lenverett. Arrivals at Pusan and Inchon brought paraffin wax, caustic soda, rubber, paper, truck tires and 213 tons of wool. Outbound cargoes were cuttle fish, marine and vegetable products, dried fish and furs, such as 500 tons on the Suchow."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[315,316],"pp":"80","s":"Government trade involved a pending offer of 75 tons of lead bullion to a New York firm. Exports to Japan credited to the USAMGIK account were $351,976 for April and $4,223,237 for January-April, led by graphite, zinc concentrates, anchovies and lead. SCAP's offer of six cents per pound for hemp waste was accepted. Pusan shipped 4,242 tons; 427 tons of inferior kaolin were returned."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[317,317],"pp":"82","s":"Sales of Korean-made goods to the Army Exchange Service in Korea were $27,355 in April, bringing the total since the program began in August 1947 to $174,506. The table shows 17,434 pieces delivered in April, against 22,176 pieces worth $38,746 in March and the 1947 total of 66,065 pieces worth $64,701."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[318,322],"pp":"83","s":"April civilian supply imports included 83,171 metric tons of coal (286,196 for the year), 47,815 tons of fertilizer with a record 15,551 tons of ammonium sulphate from Japan, and rice only (6,226 tons from the U.S., 3,593 from the Philippines). Salt imports totaled 19,515 tons; petroleum dipped slightly. Cotton, wool, rubber, petroleum coke, cement and medical supplies also arrived."},{"t":"Section 7: Labor","lv":2,"p":[323,331],"pp":"88","s":"May Day permit dispute, threatened Seoul Electric streetcar strike, labor laws, labor education, industrial health, labor distribution."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[323,324],"pp":"88","s":"Chung Pyung's complaint over a May Day permit was referred to General Dean. Dean warned that no illegal Seoul Electric strike would be tolerated and appointed an eight-member fact-finding committee, which recommended reorganizing company management and a union election before June. Marine Bureau and Korea Transportation contracts were negotiated, Pusan stevedores sought rates, and Tai Han won 366 of 413 votes."},{"t":"Labor Laws","lv":3,"p":[325,325],"pp":"90","s":"The Bureau of Labor Standards found Samchok mines had violated the Maximum Working Hours Law (Ordinance No. 121) until September 1947, and Korean Broadcasting Company workers withdrew overtime claims. Seoul ration stores were exempted from the 60-hour limit during the election emergency. Child Labor Law inspections covered the Tobacco Monopoly and Pyung Chang Silk factories, and minors under 14 in government offices were ordered dismissed."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[326,326],"pp":"91","s":"The Bureau of Labor Relations displayed an exhibit on American labor conditions and distributed the November 1946 Industrial Labor Force and Wage Survey. It planned educational tours to six towns in Kangwon-do and Chungchong-pukto using films, slides and speeches. It circulated 7,500 copies of the March Labor News and presented four Monday radio broadcasts of the Laborers' Hour on HKA."},{"t":"Industrial Health","lv":3,"p":[327,330],"pp":"92","s":"The Industrial Health and Safety Section began a recreation program for 200 girl workers, studied the Japanese industrial accident compensation act, and gave 6,615 immunizations at five Seoul firms. Mine inspections in Kangwon-do (Machari, Sang Dong, Yongwol) produced 43,969 immunizations and found poor hospitals, spoiled meat rations, silicosis and tuberculosis, prompting shipments of drugs, a refrigerator and beds."},{"t":"Labor Distribution","lv":3,"p":[331,331],"pp":"96","s":"The Department of Labor withdrew its special labor officers from refugee collection camps at Uijongbu and Kaesong, where they had been selecting skilled workers for the provinces. Of 424 workers distributed during 1947, only 60 proved skilled, so the project was judged useless and abandoned in the interest of economy."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[332,365],"pp":"","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[332,341],"pp":"97","s":"Shipping priority system adopted; March revenue up 119%; railroads carried 480,000 tons, a record since October 1945."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[332,332],"pp":"97","s":"Rising demand for marine shipping led the Department of Transportation in April to set a priority system for cargo space: 48 per cent for Pusan allotments, 24 coal, 19 rice, six fertilizer from Yosu and three miscellaneous. The plan runs 60 days, after which priorities may be adjusted depending on the overall situation."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[332,332],"pp":"97","s":"Department of Transportation revenue for March rose 119 per cent, or 283,000,000 won, the highest monthly figure of F/Y 1947/48, partly through 161,000,000 won paid by the U.S. Army for freight and passenger bills and partly through greater rail and marine activity. Expenditures rose too because of fiscal year-end contract payments, reaching 464,552 thousand won against revenue of 520,065 thousand."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[332,339],"pp":"97","s":"Anti-sabotage precautions kept April damage small. March railroad receipts rose 269,000,000 won, passengers by 921,000, and freight reached over 480,000 tons, the most since October 1945. Operable locomotives rose by 17, and backshop repairs and coal consumption increased. Employment kept falling but wages were the year's highest; Ensung coal was being tested in locomotives."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[340,340],"pp":"105","s":"Marine receipts for March were 147 per cent above February because of heavier traffic and higher rates, and expenditures rose with year-end payments. Employment rose slightly to 2,540. At Pohang, dredging let FS-type ships enter the harbor, while dredging continued at Kunsan and Mokpo and work began to remove Japanese crane wreckage at Yosu."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[340,341],"pp":"105","s":"Highway Transportation expenditures fell sharply to 356 thousand won in March, partly from year-end lack of funds, while Air Transportation outlays rose nine per cent to 296 thousand won. Neither bureau reported income. Highway employment stood at 72 and air employment at 31 in March."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[342,350],"pp":"107","s":"Communist sabotage shifted to Cheju (16 poles cut, 26 wires cut); revenue, employment, postal savings, telephone and broadcasting."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"The communist sabotage campaign against communications on the mainland waned in April, but an intensive drive hit Cheju island: 16 telephone poles were cut down and 26 wires cut. Other provinces suffered slight damage. In Kwangju, telephone and telegraph employees rioted and partly interrupted service for more than 24 hours."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"Department of Communications revenue rose markedly in March: telephone fees up 14,000,000 won, insurance up 2,000,000 won from year-end bills, and stamps up 1,000,000 won with new issues. Postal, radio and telegraph fees fell 5,000,000 won because provincial reporting was incomplete. Expenditures dropped over 50 per cent to 39,282 thousand won for lack of fiscal year-end funds."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"Employment in the Department of Communications continued to decline in March, to 13,113 from 13,479 in February, in compliance with the Military Governor's reduction-in-force orders. Postal staff fell to 3,292 and insurance staff to 871, while telephone staff stood at 3,068."},{"t":"Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[342,344],"pp":"107","s":"Government income collected by Communications for the Department of Finance rose over 39 per cent in March to 726 million won, the high point since October 1945. Monopoly returns were higher because cigarette factories ran the full month rather than 20 days, and taxes on other products stayed high, with taxes reaching 704 million won."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[345,346],"pp":"110","s":"In March the Postal Deposit System gained 1,868 depositors, reaching 7,002 thousand, and deposits rose 562,170 won, holding at 843 million. The Postal Book Transfer System lost depositors (52,828) but deposits were 31,000,000 won above February at 154 million. National Life Insurance policies fell to 5,308 thousand, though insurance in force rose 6,000,000 won to 1,776 million."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[347,348],"pp":"112","s":"Telephone messages in March were 17,000 above February at 182,000, with revenue up 739,000 won to 11,464 thousand won. Telegraph traffic and revenue also rose: domestic messages sent were 182,215 and domestic revenue 6,691,343 won, while messages to Japan generated 92,739 won."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting Corporation","lv":3,"p":[349,349],"pp":"114","s":"Effective 15 April, the license fee for Korean civilian owners of entertainment radio receivers rose from 40 won to 50 won per month."},{"t":"Communications Bureau","lv":3,"p":[349,349],"pp":"114","s":"The Seoul-Pusan telephone circuit of the Korean Electric Power Company was re-aligned 20 April, and a radio station at Mukko, Kangwon-do was costed for the Bureau of Mines. Inspections of Seoul plants (Chosun Light Industry, Chosun Communications Industry, Chosun Electric Industry) surveyed capacity to make telephone plugs, switchboard parts and 20 constabulary radio transmitters."},{"t":"Postal Bureau","lv":3,"p":[350,350],"pp":"115","s":"Two new Korean postage stamps went on sale 10 April: a pink 20-won stamp showing the Independence Gate (one million printed) and a light brown 50-won stamp showing Admiral Yi's Turtle Ship (500,000 printed). Both measure 20 by 24 mm."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[351,355],"pp":"116","s":"Electric power 75.9 million KWH in April, with 33.7 million from southern sources; Emergency Power Board, plants, streetcars, power barges."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[351,352],"pp":"116","s":"Electricity delivered in April totaled 75,900,000 KWH, slightly below March, with 42.2 million from north of 38 degrees and 33.7 from the south. Lengthening daylight reduced consumption, delivery and service improved and stoppages fell. For the first time, compiled data showed production of all South Korean plants since August 1945, previously a business secret, with seasonal variation similar to U.S. patterns."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Transactions","lv":3,"p":[353,353],"pp":"118","s":"The Emergency Electric Power Board met 7 and 21 April on conservation, priorities and allocation if North Korean power were cut off. Unauthorized uses, estimated at 30 per cent of supply, were blamed on big bulbs, floor heating and bypassed cut-offs. Adviser Roames outlined Korean-run load dispatching, backfeeding for water pumps, three 5,000 KVA transformers from Japan, and an interconnected Seoul system."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[354,354],"pp":"119","s":"April plant operations were generally favorable. Chung Pyung hydro averaged 15,700 KW, Sumjingang 8,700 KW (third highest ever), Unam did not operate, Yong Wol steam averaged 7,200 KW while stockpiling 4,500 tons of coal, and the Pusan floating plant about 6,500 KW. Japanese engineers repaired a Chung Pyung transformer, and tests showed Dong In Ri can generate 18,000 KW continuously."},{"t":"Street Railways","lv":3,"p":[354,354],"pp":"119","s":"Seoul streetcars ran about 135 cars in April, with a wage study submitted to the Wage Stabilization Committee and more welfare work. Fred Simons's study settled a dispute over 27,909,000 won in claimed taxes owed by Seoul Electric to the City: traffic tax claim invalid, vehicle and rail taxes due, payable in monthly installments without penalty."},{"t":"Auxiliary Power Barges","lv":3,"p":[355,355],"pp":"120","s":"The 20,000 KW power barge YFP-1, a former 5,000-ton freighter, was berthed in Pusan Harbor in April under the 11th Medium Port and began producing at once, the first floating plant of its kind. The Navy barge Electra, with six 800 KW diesel generators and 3,000-4,000 KW expected output, reached Inchon for connection to Seoul Electric lines."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[356,365],"pp":"121","s":"Comprehensive spring inspection; no national road appropriation, so volunteer labor carried road, bridge, levee and fire-control work."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[356,356],"pp":"121","s":"April, the start of the new fiscal year, brought the most detailed inspection of Public Works construction sites yet, showing progress despite funding shortages. Volunteer labor on road, bridge and irrigation work increased, and new volunteer fire companies were installed. Long- and short-range water supply projects advanced and problems found were promptly addressed."},{"t":"Highways","lv":3,"p":[356,356],"pp":"121","s":"Employment and expenditures on highways were nearly nil in April because no national road appropriation existed, partly offset by volunteer labor. Surveys covered the 54-km Chungdong-ni to Changpyong-ni stretch of the planned Seoul-east coast highway, the Kangnung-Samchok mine road with two unsafe bridges, and the half-repaired Yongwol-Chungju-Chochiwon road."},{"t":"Bridges","lv":3,"p":[357,357],"pp":"122","s":"Bridge work proceeded well: the Andong bridge was 20 per cent complete at a cost of 4,478 thousand won, and a contract was let 22 April for a concrete deck on the Waegwan bridge, due by July. Capt. Nelson's Namjong Bridge redesign cuts load from 13 to eight tons, saving 5,000 bags of cement and 3,500,500 won. Standard culvert designs were drafted."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[357,361],"pp":"122","s":"A new Water Resources Committee under Choi Kyong-wol met 2 and 23 April to coordinate irrigation and flood plans, and began reviewing the Sum Jin Gang dam. Provincial levee projects were reported in Cholla-namdo and Chungchong-namdo, and bulldozer levee work was observed near Seoul. An adviser's flood report cited 311 rivers, the 1946 flood and 1,250,000 feet of needed levees. Tables list projects and costs."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[362,362],"pp":"127","s":"The City Planning Bureau drew up the 1948/49 water works program and minimum operating requirements for major cities. Power failures and uneven voltage were the main problems, and Seoul's Duksum pumping station was repaired quickly. A Kolang reservoir was designed, and the Utilities Commission approved Seoul rate increases up to 80 times 1937 levels, about 25 won per seven cubic meters."},{"t":"Maps and Surveys","lv":3,"p":[362,364],"pp":"127","s":"The Bureau of Geodetic Survey studied the accuracy of the 1:50,000-scale maps of South Korea for possible revision and compiled a tabulation of all triangulation points with latitude, longitude and azimuths. The pages also hold the flood control programs table (4,866 million won to complete, 69,500 hectares) and the proposed 1948/49 water works expenditure figure."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[365,365],"pp":"130","s":"April saw 144 fires with 76,018,490 won in estimated losses, one-fifth of March's; January-April totaled 693 fires and 925,350,840 won. A survey found all but five of Seoul's 32 fire lanes passable. A Yongdungpo firm produced 1,200 yards of hose, equipment programs and standardization began, and a 250-member volunteer brigade in Song Tong was recognized 4 April."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[365,365],"pp":"130","s":"On 3 April the Department of Public Works' Heavy Equipment School at Ascom City graduated its second class of 86 Korean engineers, who completed a three-month course in heavy-equipment operation and management held 12 February to 3 April under the Technological Training Board."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[366,385],"pp":"","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[366,375],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue fell 5,213 million won from December's 33,389 million; revenue exceeded expenditure second month running."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[366,367],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue peaked at 33,389 million won in December 1947, mostly from the rice collection, and had fallen 5,213 million by the end of April (28.2 billion won in the table). Causes included completed rice collection and distribution, record tax collections and tobacco sales, little government spending, and 1,132 million won of deposit transfers during April."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[368,368],"pp":"133","s":"Cumulative excess withdrawals by the South Korean Interim Government reached a new high of 22,005 million won at the end of April, though only 2,146 million above six months earlier. April withdrawals were 2,340 million against 1,555 million deposits. Spending controls and higher utility, transport, tax and tobacco rates limited growth; total overdrafts were 39,325 million."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[369,370],"pp":"134","s":"The approved F/Y 1947/48 budget was 21,407 million won, with 787,000,000 won added in April and 1,177 million pending (Electra Barge, Communications, Police, Transportation, Justice), totaling 23,370 million. The Budget Bureau's F/Y 1948/49 recommendations total 28,704,824 thousand won expenditure against 27,821,485 thousand revenue; agencies may spend one-twelfth in April while the National Economic Board reviews."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[371,371],"pp":"136","s":"March revenues of 2,135 million won set a monthly record, 507 million above February. Monopoly Bureau receipts reached 1,004 million won, tax collections 432 million, and departmental revenue nearly 700 million, helped by 520 million from Transportation. Expenditures rose 601 million won over February but stayed below the six-month average; Table 5 gives monthly series."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[372,373],"pp":"137","s":"Cumulative F/Y 1947/48 revenue (15,336 million won, led by the Monopoly Bureau at 7,294 million) was 79 per cent of expenditure payments (19,319 million), 69 per cent of the proposed expenditure budget, while payments equalled 87 per cent of that budget. Tables 6 and 7 list revenues and expenditures by agency."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau","lv":3,"p":[374,374],"pp":"139","s":"Monopoly Bureau expenditures for March were 195.5 million won and 3,539 million for the fiscal year, mostly tobacco (2,698 million). Revenue for the year was 7,294 million won. Tobacco supplies about 10,000 of 28,000 million won estimated revenue (36 per cent); salt and ginseng add six and two per cent."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[374,375],"pp":"139","s":"The Civilian Supply Program sells goods bought by Military Government from Japan and the United States through South Korean Interim Government channels. Table 8 shows receipts and disbursements from May 1946; the balance stood at 5,319 million won at end of April 1948. Accounts receivable of 13,686 million won were also outstanding."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[376,385],"pp":"141","s":"Deposits in financial institutions rose 11% in April; loans, insurance and institution activities."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[376,376],"pp":"141","s":"Cash balances in financial institutions rose 1,088 million won in March to 3,508.9 million, partly from reduced loans and higher deposits and partly as a rebound from February's fall. South Korea has no securities markets and banks face no legal liquid-reserve requirement. Table 1 gives balances from December 1946."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[377,378],"pp":"142","s":"Deposit balances in financial institutions rose 6,279 million won, nearly 13 per cent, to a record 55,305 million in March; deposits were up 7,961 million and withdrawals down 1,500 million from February. Government balances grew nearly 1,900 million to 28,926 million, and inter-bank balances rose 1,159 million to a new high of 4,151 million."},{"t":"Deposit Balances in Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[379,379],"pp":"144","s":"Public deposits were the largest since December 1947, with balances up 3,221 million won to a record 22,228 million. Every type of deposit rose at every institution in March. Government deposits gained mainly at the Bank of Chosun, and inter-bank gains were at Chosun Industrial, Savings and Mutual Aid banks. Table 3 gives totals of 55,305 million."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[380,381],"pp":"145","s":"March was the third straight month in which loans retired exceeded new loans; outstanding loans fell 1,591 million won, nearly five per cent, to 32,120 million (guaranteed down 863 million, others 728 million). The Bank of Chosun held 30 per cent of loans; total loans equalled 58 per cent of deposits, with ratios per bank given."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[382,382],"pp":"147","s":"Life insurance continued steady growth: insurance in force rose 74.4 million won, nine per cent, in March to 814.6 million. Premiums collected were 68 per cent above February and equalled 21 per cent of annual premiums due, while claims paid were the highest since January 1948. Four companies reported; a postal life insurance system also operates."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[382,382],"pp":"147","s":"Damage insurance companies wrote 9,265 new policies in March, fewer than January or February but well above the 1947 monthly average. Liabilities assumed of nearly 4,400 million won set a record, 42 per cent above February, and premiums written reached 51.6 million. Claims paid were exceeded only in January 1948."},{"t":"Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[383,383],"pp":"148","s":"In April damage insurance companies were instructed to limit investments to first mortgages, real estate and corporate bonds and stocks, bank deposits and Korean Government obligations. A new life insurance salary scale, agreed after conferences between employees and companies, raised maximum salaries about 50 per cent; employees agreed to help improve operating efficiency."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[383,385],"pp":"148","s":"The new Bank Examination Section of the Department of Finance examined its first Korean bank and plans annual examinations on U.S. methods. Controller of Commodities proposed repaying the 12,502 million won rice loan by 31 October (4,540 million repaid). Ordinance 182 replaced Japanese-printed 100 won notes, about 5,000 million exchanged at 75 million cost. Thirty-nine loans totalling 713 million won were approved, including 400 million for the Irrigation Union."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[386,419],"pp":"","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, legal affairs, police and interzone activities."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[386,388],"pp":"151","s":"Election preparations (7,730,000 registered, 942 candidates), National Economic Board and Military Government personnel."},{"t":"Preparation for National Election","lv":3,"p":[386,386],"pp":"151","s":"Registration closed 9 April with 7,730,000 voters, over 94 per cent of those eligible (51 per cent men); 200 districts and 13,407 polling places were set, and 942 candidates registered. Public Information publicised the election via press releases, Hodge, Jacobs and Dean statements, directives prioritising trials of election interference, posed pictures, 100 film shorts and radio coverage."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[387,387],"pp":"152","s":"The National Economic Board met seven times and reviewed F/Y 1948/49 budgets. It discussed an agricultural production tax, authorised 400 million won credit for the Korean Irrigation Union, passed 250 million for the Sumjingang Dam power project, and approved summer grain collection of at least 20 per cent of output. It centralised funds in the Bank of Chosun and listed its publications."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[388,388],"pp":"153","s":"Military Government personnel numbered 3,041 in April 1948 (547 officers, 1,469 enlisted men, 1,025 civilians), up from 2,740 in March. Table 1 shows military strength steady and civilians rising for seven months, while civilian turnover was high because of one-year contracts. The total was 3,721 in October 1946."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[389,390],"pp":"154","s":"KILA met once on 19 April, enacted nothing, and voted 30-day adjournment amounting to dissolution; summary of legislation."},{"t":"April Session","lv":3,"p":[389,389],"pp":"154","s":"The Korean Interim Legislative Assembly met only once in April, on the 19th, and enacted no legislation. It rejected resignations of Yun Ki-sop, Kim Ik-tong and Lee Sun-taek to keep membership at 49, tabled pending bills until the National Assembly convenes, and adjourned 30 days, tantamount to dissolution."},{"t":"Summary of KILA Legislation","lv":3,"p":[389,390],"pp":"154","s":"KILA, first proposed June 1946 and convened 12 December 1946 with 45 elected and 45 appointed members, passed 11 Public Acts (including child labor, rice and grain collection, abolition of licensed prostitution, Arbor Day) and four resolutions. It nearly dissolved three times: the May 1947 voting-age dispute, the November 1947 outline of measures, and the January-February 1948 UN petition."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[391,400],"pp":"156","s":"North-South Conference at Pyongyang from 19 April, election candidacies and land reform."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[391,391],"pp":"156","s":"Two matters absorbed South Korean parties in April: the Pyongyang North-South conference opening 19 April, and the first general election. Over 90 per cent of eligible voters registered, and 939 candidates sought 200 National Assembly seats when registration closed 16 April. Moderates and communists focused on the conference, rightists on the election."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[391,396],"pp":"156","s":"Party reactions split: moderates and leftists backed the conference, while Hankook Democratic Party and Rhee opposed it. Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, hesitant at first, went to Pyongyang. Sessions from 19 April were scripted; a 23 April resolution demanded foreign troop withdrawal, and a 30 April communique rejected the separate election. The report judges it a Soviet victory."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[397,399],"pp":"162","s":"Rightists formed an election acceleration committee under O Sei-chang; 939 candidates, 17 of them women, contested 200 seats allotted by province, with Rhee's society fielding 239 and Dai Dong 89. Party splits and a poll claiming 91 per cent were forced to register appeared. Choi Nung-chin's challenge to Rhee in Tongdaimoon A was nullified 7 May."},{"t":"Land Reform","lv":3,"p":[400,400],"pp":"163","s":"Extreme rightists, backed largely by landlords, opposed the National Land Administration's sale of New Korea Company land to tenants, saying the property was Korean and should be disposed of by a Korean government. Communists accused U.S. authorities of keeping proceeds and wanted free distribution as in North Korea. Non-extremists generally favored the sales."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[401,412],"pp":"166","s":"Executive Orders including election date moved to 10 May; legal opinions, penal institutions, codification of civil code."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[401,402],"pp":"166","s":"April ordinances included Executive Order 20 moving the election to 10 May because of an eclipse, Order 21 on demonstrations and liquor, and Ordinances 182-191 (banknote exchange, repeal of obsolete laws, port fees, income tax exemption raised to 25,000 won). Hodge proclaimed 11 rights of Koreans on 5 April and a 10 May holiday; KILA's last act was Public Act 11."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[403,404],"pp":"168","s":"Department of Justice opinions covered: no licence needed for foreign firms such as Jardine, Matheson to trade; English as official language of the National Land Administration; implied licensing power over film distribution; informal adoption under customary law allowing a Seoul adoption agency; and National Food Administration being independent of the National Economic Board."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[405,406],"pp":"170","s":"Prisons were guarded against election-time release attempts, and six were found in good condition. Kunchon Prison became an adult institution taking about 200 overflow prisoners, and prisoners worked for Army units. The Kaesong Boys' Reformatory, holding 1,100 boys aged 14 to 20, was praised for its buildings, discipline rules, schooling, five trade shops and Warden Kwon Yung-jun."},{"t":"Court Liaison","lv":3,"p":[407,407],"pp":"172","s":"Release of prisoners under the Military Governor's amnesty was completed in April, except for some transferred prisoners who were being traced. Delivery of pardon-order copies to many paroled or suspended-sentence persons was impossible because their addresses were unknown."},{"t":"Property Claims Commission","lv":3,"p":[407,407],"pp":"172","s":"The Department of Justice adviser and the Property Custodian recommended transferring to the Custodian pending claims with abundant proof of Korean ownership, and the Property Claims Commission concurred. The Custodian will release such property administratively, speeding relief for Korean claimants suffering hardship from loss of their property."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[407,412],"pp":"172","s":"A proposed Civil Code, drafted by Judge C. Sumner Lobingier, was finished and being translated; it will be Korea's first, replacing the untranslated Japanese code. Lobingier's reasons, the placing of corporation law under juristic persons, his Chinese and European sources, and the four-book syllabus (Persons, Obligations, Property, Succession) are given."},{"t":"Section 5: National Police","lv":2,"p":[413,414],"pp":"178","s":"Court-warrant rule worked; communist violence aimed at registration, with about 52 attacks on 13,455 booths."},{"t":"Reform Measures","lv":3,"p":[413,413],"pp":"178","s":"A court-warrant requirement for arrests began to work more smoothly than expected, though police had feared it would block precautionary arrests. Police also felt they lost face when over 3,000 minor political offenders were released and resented limits on harshness and property destruction, balking at signing statements justifying force."},{"t":"Violence","lv":3,"p":[413,413],"pp":"178","s":"Communist-inspired violence aimed to block registration: about 52 of 13,455 booths were attacked, led by Kyongsang-pukto with 21. Attacks on police and sabotage fell, but bystander injuries and disorders rose, with savage killings. Cheju-do's outbreak from 3 April involved about 2,000 rioters; to 19 April, 22 civilians and seven police were killed."},{"t":"Warning","lv":3,"p":[414,414],"pp":"179","s":"There were signs that communists were renewing efforts to win adherents among security forces. Police were told that the Americans were on their way out and the communists coming in, so it would be wise to join the winning side."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[415,419],"pp":"180","s":"Weekly North-South mail exchange (59,000 pieces), Radio Pyongyang intercepts and border incidents."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[415,415],"pp":"180","s":"Mail exchange between North and South Korea became weekly from 15 April. Four exchanges in April handled 59,000 pieces versus 37,000 in March, counted as regular mail, postcards and registered items. The largest was 3 April, with 9,768 letters, 3,313 postcards and 703 registered items from South Korea."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[415,416],"pp":"180","s":"Radio Pyongyang used the Joint Conference to attack the South's separate election, praise the 1947 People's Economic Plan, and denounce Rhee, American colonisation and South Korean land sales. Kim Il-sung's opening speech was the month's key broadcast. The People's Assembly adopted a constitution 28-30 April, subject to an all-Korean assembly's approval."},{"t":"Border Incidents","lv":3,"p":[417,419],"pp":"182","s":"Police Director Chough Pyong-ok said 38th parallel incidents reached a new high, with uniformed Soviet soldiers accompanying communist raids in at least two cases. Since 7 February, 21 Koreans were killed and about 150 wounded, and North Korean marauders violated the border 74 times. Small-arms fire echoes across the parallel nightly."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[420,449],"pp":"","s":"Public health, education, culture, press, youth and women."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[420,424],"pp":"185","s":"Mukden repatriate teams, cholera vaccine distribution, nurse training, lepers, narcotics, laboratories and public assistance."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[420,420],"pp":"185","s":"Public Health and Welfare expanded training and disease control, outfitting the refugee ship that repatriated Koreans from Mukden via Tientsin with welfare and preventive medicine teams. Medical Services ran training courses and Nursing launched a graduate nurse program. Cholera vaccine and control supplies went to the provinces for the three-phase vaccination."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[420,420],"pp":"185","s":"Communicable disease stayed low, with typhus and typhoid declining. Cholera and smallpox immunisation covered seacoast cities, border refugee camp towns and border townships. The National Health Center milk station served 120 infants daily. The Mukden refugee ship sailed 30 April with a 14-day quarantine planned. A Pusan quarantine property dispute was settled and a vital statistics course held."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[421,421],"pp":"186","s":"The last UNRRA medical shipment, worth about $2,000, was divided among four leprosaria at Yosu, Sorakto, Taegu and Pusan. The tenth class for doctors and x-ray technicians at the Lasan Tuberculosis Sanitorium finished, bringing the total completing courses to 50."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[421,421],"pp":"186","s":"A three-month training program for graduate nurses began 1 April at three U.S. Army hospitals under the 80th Medical Group. Surplus Army nurses' clothing was arranged for sale to Korean hospitals. Inspections found Pusan Transportation Hospital poor, Pusan City Hospital much improved, and Seoul City Hospital lacking adequate nursing care."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"Completed statistics for 1946 and 1947 showed more animals treated in 1947, with cattle rising from 26,302 to 34,591 and recoveries 97 per cent, helped by imported supplies. The fifth veterinary bulletin on horse breeding was distributed, 400 copies via the Department of Agriculture and 160 by Agriculture Improvement Service."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"Military Government received 520,000 pounds of DDT in April, distributed through Army Malaria Control Units in each province. The first large shipment of 100,000 bars of laundry and toilet soap went to hospitals, doctors and dentists. Of 44 candidates in the spring pharmacists' examination, only two completed all three parts."},{"t":"Narcotics Section","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"After investigation, the Narcotics Section licensed 74 physicians and 31 pharmacists to handle narcotics in April. The Narcotics Adviser and section chief inspected the narcotics ward at Sun Hwa Hospital, Seoul, and interviewed six addicts under treatment."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[423,423],"pp":"188","s":"The National Vaccine Laboratory in Seoul kept production of biologicals at requirements, and increased cholera, typhoid and rabies vaccine output. Smallpox vaccine production was delayed because of budgetary difficulties."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[423,424],"pp":"188","s":"A flood-season disaster relief plan relies on local community organisations for food, clothing and shelter. Welfare officers completed 10,053 low-cost housing units, and five child welfare institutions were licensed; UNRRA aid of 300,000 won went to White Lily Orphanage. The Kaesong Refugee Camp processes 500 to 1,000 North Korean refugees, with relief agencies supplying clothing."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[425,430],"pp":"190","s":"Adult literacy rose, curriculum workshops, first model hospital ward, and bureau activities."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[425,425],"pp":"190","s":"Curriculum workshops in art, music, science, home economics and English grew in number and drew many teachers. A nursing radio program began, and the Medical Section arranged South Korea's first model hospital ward. Surveys indicated the adult education program greatly reduced adult illiteracy through local schools, radio and press."},{"t":"Bureau of Research and Special Subjects","lv":3,"p":[425,426],"pp":"190","s":"The program stressed democratic community education, and tables showed literacy rising from 21 per cent in August 1945 to 71 per cent in August 1947. Higher education grew from 19 to 26 schools and 3,039 to 19,241 students. Adult schools reached 15,502 by September 1947. A middle-school entrance achievement test with four forms was prepared."},{"t":"Special Activities","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"The Department studied the acute situation of Korean schools in Japan; a Directorate sub-committee proposed reopening them with approved Korean textbooks and sending an investigating committee. A Personnel Ceiling Board cut the Department's table of organization from 177 to 143, prompting reorganization and a proposal to merge the Bureau of Culture into Social Education."},{"t":"Bureau of Administration","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"A 3,200,000 won budget request was cut to 2,200,000 won, excluding secondary schools, prompting redrafting of sub-agency budgets. A statistical summary of children, teachers, classrooms and funds showed 2,041 new classrooms built, yet classes average 95 children. Per-classroom cost was estimated at 710,000 won; 650,000 was allotted, and middle schools got 140,000."},{"t":"Bureau of Common Schools","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"Curriculum workshops for social studies (Taegu), language and art (Taegu) and science (Seoul) trained certified teachers to run provincial workshops. Plans included 10 English vacation institutes and a textbook workshop for 15 Seoul English teachers. Half of Seoul's middle schools had American volunteer English teachers. The exceptional children section held parents' classes and studied a Lighthouse project for the blind."},{"t":"Bureau of Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[428,428],"pp":"193","s":"The Bureau prepared a booklet on teaching United Nations facts and social studies materials, and sent a draft Educators' Guide to Korea to Washington. A Chunju teachers' meeting drew 600 against the 200 expected. Preliminary plans for a normal-school summer session were estimated to train 10,000 elementary teachers."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[428,428],"pp":"193","s":"Advisers helped develop a curriculum for Taegu Agriculture College. A Seoul National University Medical School committee decided on a model ward of about 30 beds. Two thousand nursing ethics textbooks were printed, and 19 nurses from the Seoul Electric Company hospital, the first run to American standards, completed refresher training."},{"t":"Bureau of Adult Education and Culture","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"The Bureau of Adult Education and Culture prepared a manuscript on the schools' responsibility in connection with the 10 May election and submitted it to the Department of Public Information for publication."},{"t":"Bureau of Textbooks","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"A total of 13,230,670 textbooks had been published since 15 August 1945, with April printings including Korean readers, Grade 6 social studies and Grade 6 science (678,500 copies). Work began to standardise mathematics, government and science works, and the first 7,000 English-language textbooks arrived, with sets for each middle school."},{"t":"American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"The American Language Institute began a new term on 26 April. At student request, the evening class for middle school English teachers will meet four times a week instead of twice. One thousand surplus library books from the Inchon salvage yard went to the Institute and normal schools."},{"t":"Bureau of Vocational Education","lv":3,"p":[430,430],"pp":"195","s":"The adviser prepared a supplementary budget to set up a Korean Bureau of Vocational Education and considered converting the Department of Communications school into a model Seoul technical institute. A Korean Home Economics Association was organised, and field trips to Taegu and Kaesong found equipment shortages, broken sewing machines and food-cost problems."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[431,435],"pp":"196","s":"Arbor Day tree planting (100 million trees), Symphony Orchestra instrument gift, music, dance, broadcasting and sports."},{"t":"Arbor Day","lv":3,"p":[431,431],"pp":"196","s":"Arbor Day, 5 April, made a national holiday by Public Act No. 10, was marked on Nam San with about 10,000 participants. Military Governor Dean planted the first of 100,000,000 trees planted nationwide, five per person, and 26,000 trees covering 19 acres were planted on Nam San that afternoon."},{"t":"Korea Symphony Orchestra","lv":3,"p":[431,431],"pp":"196","s":"On 25 April Lt.-Gen. Hodge presented musical instruments from U.S. personnel to the Korea Symphony Orchestra, a public corporation founded September 1945 with 30 musicians and now 60. A concert under Lim Won-sik included the first Korean performance of an American orchestral work, Douglas Moore's Village Music."},{"t":"Music and Dances","lv":3,"p":[432,432],"pp":"197","s":"Music and dance events included a 28-29 April election publicity program by Seoul pupils, the 22d symphony concert under Kim In-soo, and recitals. Farmers performed the rain ceremonial dance on Nam San 4-10 April, an Oriental dance troupe performed 10-11 April, and a 17 April court music program for UNTCOK featured a mask dance."},{"t":"Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[433,433],"pp":"198","s":"Thousands viewed cherry blossoms in mid-April, with 10,000 at the Zoological Gardens on 11 April. A \"Night of Poets\" at the YMCA on 25 April supported the Liberation of Korean Culture in Japan. Two commemorative stamps issued 10 April showed Independence Arch (20 won) and the turtle ship (50 won)."},{"t":"Korea Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[434,434],"pp":"199","s":"The Korea Broadcasting System publicised the election through spot announcements and programs such as the Adult Culture Hour, and ran a 52-student radio class at Ewha College. It recorded Korean songs for pressing in the United States, gave scripts to the Hoover Library, made two remote opera broadcasts, and favored Beethoven in evening music."},{"t":"Sports","lv":3,"p":[435,435],"pp":"200","s":"Fifty-eight athletes were selected for Korea's team to the London Olympics, with 10 track and field men, 16 soccer players and nine basketball players. A lottery of 560,000 tickets raised funds to $140,000 by 24 April, averting a 35-man cut. Weightlifters broke six records, and Suh Yun-bok won the Boston-memorial marathon on 18 April."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[436,438],"pp":"201","s":"Press focused on election and Pyongyang conference; riots, law enforcement, amnesty of 3,140 and fear of Japan."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"The 10 May election and the 19-24 April Pyongyang Conference dominated the press. Voter registration, amnesty and disturbances were treated as phases of the election struggle. The closing of Korean schools in Japan was read by Koreans as a plot to re-enslave Korea and by American authorities as a communist scheme, and judicial and police reform were discussed in election terms."},{"t":"Pyongyang Conference","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"Pyongyang Conference coverage filled most newspaper space in early April. Rightist reporters visiting the Soviet-controlled city found liberties completely suppressed in North Korea, while leftist reporters found conditions perfect."},{"t":"Registration for Election","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"U.S. authorities worked to create a free atmosphere: papers printed Hodge's 2 April secret-ballot statement, the civil rights ordinance took effect 1 April, and an amnesty freed 3,140 political offenders. The UN Commission found people wanted unification. Both sides alleged intimidation; 52 of 13,455 centers were attacked. Kyeng Hyang Sin Mun reported 91.7 per cent registered."},{"t":"Political Campaign","lv":3,"p":[437,437],"pp":"202","s":"Rightist papers complained of 939 candidates for 200 seats, with independents (including moderates and leftists whose parties boycotted the vote) making up 48 per cent; some non-recommended candidates withdrew under party commendation. Military Governor Dean's 8 April press conference denied High Commissioner, US-printed Bank of Chosun notes and land-sale charges; communists alleged colonization and puppet government."},{"t":"Law Enforcement","lv":3,"p":[437,437],"pp":"202","s":"A police-initiated, Military Government-approved Fatherland Protection Group enlisted men aged 18 to 55 to curb election terrorism, with safeguards against a revival of the Japanese-era namesake. The Korean Supreme Court rejected American police advisers' 3 April petition on police-issued arrest warrants. Sentences of Chang Duk-soo's assassins were commuted on 23 April."},{"t":"Riots and Disorders","lv":3,"p":[438,438],"pp":"203","s":"Disturbances in the southern provinces continued through April, with violent outbreaks on Cheju-do beginning 3-7 April. A police report cited 15 killed and 30 wounded; a rightist paper said the riots were worsening and that Constabulary Ninth Regiment troops were sent. A 29 April report described 2,000 armed men in mountain dugouts and martial law."},{"t":"Fear of Japan","lv":3,"p":[438,438],"pp":"203","s":"Korean papers of all views protested the 31 March closing of Korean schools in Japan as an attack on Korean culture and independence, while printing General Dean's statement that the schools were communist training centers. Korean Education and Foreign Affairs offices urged SKIG-approved textbooks and punishment of Kobe rioters. The press also feared Japanese travel permits would revive militarism."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[439,441],"pp":"204","s":"67,475 members planted trees on Arbor Day; cleanup, training, vocational work, election preparation."},{"t":"Community Service","lv":3,"p":[439,439],"pp":"204","s":"Government-sponsored Korean National Youth mobilized 67,475 members in all nine provinces on Arbor Day, 5 April, planting 3,451,300 trees in 1,086,880 working hours. The effort is part of an ongoing project: 1,246,480 trees planted in early 1948 versus 175,000 in all of 1947. Members also patrol forests and ran an April cleanup campaign, including 7,000 members at Inchon."},{"t":"Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[439,440],"pp":"204","s":"KNY membership reached 774,949 on 30 April 1948, including 108,326 women, growing by over 3,000 daily; tables give monthly totals since January 1947 and provincial breakdowns. Over 370,000 members joined in four months, with 1,000,000 forecast by 30 June. Seven new training centers brought the total to 62, and 36,599 leaders were trained in the first quarter."},{"t":"Vocational Training","lv":3,"p":[441,441],"pp":"206","s":"KNY emphasized recruiting employed workers and training apprentices, countering the tradition of unemployed youths joining for strong-arm gain. On-the-job classes were organized in about six communication and transportation industries, and the national Department of Organization expanded job placement in April. At Yongdongpo, apprentices manufactured and assembled prefabricated homes to ease the provincial housing shortage."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[441,441],"pp":"206","s":"During April thousands of KNY members across the American zone urged neighbors to vote in the 10 May election without backing any candidate or party. They held neighborhood sessions explaining the democratic concept of suffrage. In areas lacking government funds for transportation and salaries, members also distributed ballots and ballot boxes."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[442,449],"pp":"207","s":"Women's Bureau election education, press, sewing aids, YWCA, Junior Red Cross, KNY women, professional women; blank pages follow."},{"t":"The Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[442,442],"pp":"207","s":"Election preparation was the Women's Bureau's main April work: over 50 speeches to girls' schools, colleges and community groups on suffrage, and a provincial tour with Civil Information staff showing the film \"The Peoples Choice\". Posters and leaflets urged registration. Its mothers' school averaged 80 attendees, 5,000 copies of \"New Homemaking\" were distributed, and two teachers assisted a hospital kitchen training program."},{"t":"The Women's Press","lv":3,"p":[443,443],"pp":"208","s":"The \"Women's Press\", Korea's only women's newspaper, was published by Park Sun Chun, a Legislative Assembly candidate, and founded 21 April 1947 with 27 staff and 8,000 daily circulation. A two-page paper sponsored by the Women's Patriotic Society, its April editorials stressed the election; the 8 April editorial urged women to vote, citing 15,000,000 eligible women."},{"t":"Sewing Aids","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"Church women's groups in the United States sent several hundred dollars' worth of sewing aids to Korean women in April, handed to Korean welfare agencies for needy families. One family newly arrived from Manchuria used needles and thread to alter clothing supplied by Licensed Agencies for Relief of Asia, while others made quilts with LARA batting."},{"t":"Domestic Economy Science Association","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"The Domestic Economy Science Association, Korea's first home economics association, was organized in April by American adviser Mrs. Lyda Heffron at Shangshin Girls' Middle School, Seoul. It has 30 charter members, restricted to middle-school-level teachers and up, with only Seoul active. Its program covers publications, teaching-method research, and literature and reference collections on domestic economy."},{"t":"The YWCA","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"The National YWCA received $57,000 from American Overseas Aid for Adolescents and the UN Appeal for Children to feed 2,000 undernourished South Korean adolescents. An executive committee was appointed and principals of three Seoul primary schools consulted. Each school gets a feeding room and nurse, food and menus come from the YWCA, and Dr. Y.Y. Kim is examining physician."},{"t":"Junior Red Cross","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"The first members of the Korean Junior Red Cross were initiated at a Seoul ceremony in April led by chief Miss Kim On-hie and Seoul chapter chief Mrs. Chai Sun Pok. Its first project is spreading health knowledge through literature and hygiene aids, supported by 50,000 boxes of toilet articles from the American Junior Red Cross."},{"t":"Women of Korean National Youth","lv":3,"p":[445,445],"pp":"210","s":"The KNY women's section, headed by former champion athlete Kim Hyun Suk and backed by the Aegug Puin, trains women for citizenship; 108,326 of 774,949 KNY members were women on 30 April. Training covers first aid, home nursing, child care and crafts under the principle \"train before organizing\"; 121 women graduated at Suwon in early 1948."},{"t":"Professional Women","lv":3,"p":[445,449],"pp":"210","s":"Profiles highlight the Koh sisters: Evelyn (law degree from Doshisha, M.A. in economics at Michigan), Gladys (piano diploma, Ph.D. in absentia) and Dr. Nan (M.D., studying public health at Michigan). They ran the Chamawon \"Sisters' Garden\" settlement near Seoul. Evelyn now heads the Women's Bureau and Gladys directs Red Cross women's volunteer services."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-32","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-32-1948-05","s":"J","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"","pg":206,"o":"Summation No. 32 reports on Occupation non-military activities in Japan for May 1948, with most economic statistics running through March or April. Politically the month was dominated by the Ashida coalition's dispute over suspending war-bond interest, settled by a one-year deferral, and by Cabinet struggles over the 1948-49 budget and local finance. The report reprints MacArthur's message and other statements on the first anniversary of the Constitution, the Cabinet's Second Economic White Paper and the Economic Stabilization Board's five-year rehabilitation plan. It records party conventions, the Diet's investigation of political donations, the dissolution of the purge committees with 301,815 persons purged, and war crimes trial totals. Economic sections give detailed output, trade, price, finance and labor series, including postwar highs in steel, paper and textiles. The social sections cover health, nutrition, repatriation, education, religion and the press. Historians will find the primary-source texts and monthly statistical series especially useful.","h":["Cabinet accepted the Tomabechi plan deferring war-bond interest payments for one year from June, dropping 460 million yen from the provisional budget.","MacArthur's message on the Constitution's first anniversary, 3 May, warned against despotism of the left or right and urged local autonomy.","Cabinet issued the Second Economic White Paper on 28 May; the Economic Stabilization Board published a five-year rehabilitation plan draft on 17 May.","Purge machinery dissolved 10 May; total persons purged announced as 301,815.","Maritime Safety Board activated 1 May; Impeachment Court established with Gisai Oniarau as first president.","April pig iron (55,072 tons) and steel ingots (118,557 tons) set new postwar records; paper reached a postwar high of 37,983 tons.","Ministry of Education policy of 7 May made Korean language and history optional in private Korean schools.","Of 33,635 Japanese repatriated 30 April to 27 May, 97 percent came from Soviet and Soviet-controlled areas."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","war bond interest","Constitution anniversary","Economic White Paper","Economic Rehabilitation Plan","purge","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","budget 1948-49","coal production","industrial production","labor relations","repatriation","Maritime Safety Board","war crimes trials","Korean schools"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover and front matter of Summation No. 32 for May 1948: table of contents for Parts I to IV (General, Political, Economic, Social, with 109 numbered charts), plus separate lists of charts, maps and graphs by subject, from industrial production indexes to repatriation, textbooks and film production, ending with Men of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no SCAP reorganization; political, economic and social paragraphs summarizing the detailed Parts."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"A single paragraph states that the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, underwent no change during May 1948. It is the shortest section of the Part I summary, and the page introduces the Political Activities section that follows."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes May politics: the 3 May Constitution anniversary, the war-bond interest dispute ending in a one-year deferral compromise, Cabinet administrative reorganization, demobilization placed under the Welfare Ministry, and 16 laws enacted. Party conventions, dissolution of the purge committees with 301,815 purged, the Maritime Safety Board, the Impeachment Court and war crimes trial results (53 suspects, 44 convicted) are also noted."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,13],"pp":"7","s":"April production rose in coke (233,940 tons), pig iron, steel ingots, cement, paper and textiles, with silk and cotton fabric at postwar highs. Also covered: first-quarter trade deficit of $151.8 million, ration needs met from carryover stocks, calmer labor relations, tax receipts above estimate, antitrust rulings, machine-tool reparations shipments and eclipse observations."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"Reports 3,237 communicable disease cases and 326 deaths in April, rising medical supply output, 123,511 orphans, and 33,635 repatriates, 97 percent from Soviet areas. Education covers the 7 May Korean school policy; religion notes the new National Christian Council; culture notes 379 Meiji historic sites delisted; media covers newspaper circulation, translation rights and broadcasts."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,80],"pp":"21","s":"Detailed political section: government and politics, public safety, legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,74],"pp":"23","s":"First anniversary of the Constitution, war-bond interest deferral, Second Economic White Paper, five-year rehabilitation plan, budget wrangling, Diet, party conventions and the purge."},{"t":"Anniversary of Promulgation of the Constitution","lv":3,"p":[17,21],"pp":"23","s":"Marks the 3 May anniversary of the Constitution. MacArthur's message urges vigilance, local autonomy, free enterprise, responsible press and labor. Prime Minister Ashida, Speaker Matsuoka, Councillors' President Matsudaira and the Chief Justice spoke at a Diet ceremony before the Emperor. Fourteen first-year reforms are listed, from popular sovereignty and police decentralization to land reform and purges."},{"t":"War Bond Interest Payment Issue","lv":3,"p":[21,24],"pp":"30","s":"The three coalition parties split over suspending war-bond interest, with ¥460 million due in June. The council issued two opposing reports on 30 April. Chief Cabinet Secretary Tomabechi proposed a one-year deferral on 8 May; Social Democrats accepted it 12 May with conditions, the Cabinet adopted it 14 May and formally announced it 22 May, while financial circles organized opposition."},{"t":"National Government","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"36","s":"On 22 May the Cabinet set ten principles for administrative readjustment, including shifting authority to local governments and paying allowances to discharged staff. A 24 May rally of local-government bodies demanded abolition of about 20 central agency branches and new local taxes. Remaining demobilization work passed to civilian ministries on 31 May, with about 500,000 servicemen still in Soviet areas."},{"t":"Second Economic \"White Paper\"","lv":3,"p":[25,47],"pp":"38","s":"The Ashida cabinet's 28 May report, reviewing ten months since the July 1947 paper, finds living conditions still hard but prospects brighter: coal reached 29.32 million tons (97.7 percent of target), rice delivery quota was met, and note issue fell early in 1948. It surveys war losses, production, trade, inflation, public and industrial finance, wages, rations, labor and construction, and urges self-support."},{"t":"Foreword and Basic Conditions of Postwar Economy","lv":4,"p":[25,25],"pp":"38","s":"The foreword says hard living conditions persist, but coal output, expected Allied aid and full rice delivery brighten the outlook. Three basic handicaps are set out: war losses of about 20 percent of prewar domestic assets (45.7 billion yen at surrender prices), trade far below the 2 billion dollar level needed, and rapid population growth."},{"t":"General Survey of Present Economic Conditions","lv":4,"p":[26,27],"pp":"39","s":"Population reached 78.62 million (October 1947 census) through repatriation of 5.77 million. Industrial production averaged 43.1 percent of 1930-34 in fiscal 1947, up 21 percent, with coal at 29.32 million tons. Rice crop was 59.67 million koku; trade deficit totaled 520 million dollars; note issue rose 103 billion yen but fell in early 1948; transport remained the bottleneck."},{"t":"Coal Production Increase and Power Situation","lv":4,"p":[28,29],"pp":"44","s":"Priority for coal mines in materials, funds, rice and housing, plus the Emergency Coal Production Increase Program, produced a record 2.95 million tons in December. Coal allocation rose 30 percent, industrial allocation 46 percent, though quality fell. Drought and typhoon damage caused power shortages; November measures and a new allocation system helped, with 30.5 billion kWh generated in 1947."},{"t":"Situation in Manufacturing Industry","lv":4,"p":[30,30],"pp":"48","s":"Output rose in every category except food processing and crude oil, led by metals (71 percent) and chemicals (31 percent). Finished steel reached 569,727 tons and pig iron 411,462 tons, short of the 675,000-ton program because of power shortage and unrealized North China coking coal imports. Cement reached 1.28 million tons, against a 1.67 million target; Occupation Forces took about 20 percent of steel and 50 percent of cement."},{"t":"Production in Agriculture, Forestry and Aquatic Industries","lv":4,"p":[31,31],"pp":"50","s":"Fertilizer for rice rose to 4.3 kan per tan, about 60 percent of demand. Rice (59.67 million koku) and wheat and barley stayed below 1930-34 levels; the autumn typhoon cost 2.5 million koku of rice. Land reclamation lags, timber and charcoal pile up for lack of transport, and fish output was 62 percent of the prewar average."},{"t":"Transportation and Communication","lv":4,"p":[32,33],"pp":"52","s":"Government railways carried 112 million tons (85 percent of requirements), and a January 1948 cabinet decision gave rail priority similar to coal. Coastal shipping handled over 10.68 million tons, up 88 percent, though ports operate at 40 to 60 percent of prewar capacity. Trucks lack tires and fuel; Tokyo telephones fail in 54 of 100 calls."},{"t":"Foreign Trade Returns","lv":4,"p":[34,34],"pp":"56","s":"In 1947 imports were 526 million dollars and exports 174 million, leaving a 352 million deficit made good by United States relief funds. Imports remained mainly food, fertilizer and petroleum, with 68.2 percent for hunger and disease prevention. Textiles supplied over half of exports; 80 percent of imported cotton went into export goods, and surplus stocks of cloth and yarn accumulated."},{"t":"Prospects for Foreign Trade","lv":4,"p":[35,35],"pp":"58","s":"Trade shifted toward East Asia (66.8 percent of exports) while 88.3 percent of imports came from the United States. Barter deals were made with the Soviet Union and an American firm; civilian trade opened in September 1947. Prospects improve with a 60 million dollar cotton loan, but dollar shortage and unsettled exchange rates remain obstacles."},{"t":"Trend of Inflation","lv":4,"p":[36,36],"pp":"60","s":"The report defines official, black-market and effective prices and tabulates Tokyo indexes. Black-market prices rose faster than official ones until June 1947; after the price revision official prices jumped while black-market and effective prices slowed. Black-market retail prices rose only 16 percent from November to February, against 68 percent a year earlier."},{"t":"Currency Situation","lv":4,"p":[37,37],"pp":"62","s":"Bank of Japan note issue grew 103 billion yen in fiscal 1947, ending at 218.7 billion in March, with a 40 billion rise in December. January and February 1948 showed declines through tax collection and curbed public payments. Deposits (128.5 billion yen net) fell short of the 232.3 billion yen needed by treasury and industry."},{"t":"Balance of Government Finance","lv":4,"p":[37,38],"pp":"63","s":"The General Account was near balance after a pay-tax campaign lifted collections to 124.9 billion yen by March. The report notes unequal burdens on wage earners, income-tax payers rising to 28.6 percent of the population, tobacco monopoly sales shortfalls, and expenditures dominated by postwar costs. Special Account borrowing reached 66.8 billion yen, mostly taken by the Bank of Japan."},{"t":"Recent Condition of Industrial Enterprise Finance and Savings Accounts","lv":4,"p":[39,39],"pp":"66","s":"Industrial financing grew 167.5 billion yen in the year, 32 percent through the Reconstruction Finance Bank; coal took 53 percent of its general-industry advances. Bank loans largely followed the priority table. The RFB sold only 22 percent of its bonds publicly, the Bank of Japan absorbing 75 percent. Savings campaigns raised deposits, but time deposits were only 21 percent of the total."},{"t":"Recent Conditions of Enterprises","lv":4,"p":[40,40],"pp":"68","s":"Enterprises suffer money shortage and deficits because power shortages cut operations, wages rose (January 1948 averaging 1.6 times July 1947), and allocation procedures remained inefficient. Labor productivity was only 28 to 33 percent of 1935-37, but the report argues overemployment is smaller than commonly claimed. It urges efficiency, less price subsidy, and support for small enterprises through a new Board."},{"t":"Wage and Living Cost","lv":4,"p":[41,42],"pp":"70","s":"Average manufacturing wages rose from 964 yen in January 1947 to 3,224 yen in March 1948; government salaries were raised to 3,930 yen in January. Wage differentials narrowed between male and female and skilled and unskilled. Food took 67 percent of family spending; real wages stood near 30 percent of prewar, and household deficits averaged about 7 percent in 1947."},{"t":"Demand and Supply of Living Necessities","lv":4,"p":[43,43],"pp":"74","s":"Staple rations were fulfilled since August 1947 after emergency imported-food releases, and the rice quota was fully delivered by mid-March. Fresh food rations rose to about 70 percent of purchases by February under December emergency measures. Clothing supply for 1948 is at most two pounds per capita; household fuel is about 35 percent of prewar."},{"t":"Labor Situation","lv":4,"p":[44,44],"pp":"76","s":"The 1 October 1947 census gives 33.88 million gainfully employed and only 672,000 unemployed, a figure believed greatly underestimated, with agriculture absorbing much of the postwar increase. Additional rations went to about 6.15 million essential workers. Unions numbered 36,471 with 5,993,613 members at end of November 1947, and dispute statistics are tabulated."},{"t":"Construction Works","lv":4,"p":[45,46],"pp":"79","s":"Public facilities lag: flood restoration cost far exceeded the 5 billion yen appropriated for fiscal 1947, and river control protecting 1.04 million chobu needs about 150 billion yen. Overcutting threatens forests, with 1.68 million chobu awaiting afforestation. Only 145 kilometers of road were repaired, and housing losses equal a third of urban floor area."},{"t":"\"Japanese Economic Rehabilitation Plan\"","lv":3,"p":[47,59],"pp":"54","s":"The Economic Stabilization Board's first draft five-year plan, issued 17 May, aims to restore the 1930-34 living standard by 1952. Targets include mining and manufacturing at 130 percent of base, coal 44,000,000 tons, exports of $1,646 million, national income of 121 percent, and 2,000,000 houses. It conditions success on ending inflation and securing foreign aid."},{"t":"Conclusion","lv":4,"p":[47,47],"pp":"82","s":"The report calls 1948 a crossroads for beginning real reconstruction, with the aim of a self-supporting economy that lessens the Allied burden. It stresses maximum production recovery, curbing inflation, efficiency in administration, enterprise and labor, a guaranteed minimum living standard, restricted luxuries and mobilized domestic capital. Constructive outside aid will come only if Japan uses its own resources fully."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"60","s":"A national conference of town and village heads opened in Tokyo 13 May, the first since the war, with Prime Minister Ashida pledging the Government would resign if it lost popular support. Its six resolutions sought 10 percent more food output, funds for the 6-3-3 school system and a Ministry of Construction. A 27 May compromise settled the local finance dispute at 199,700,000,000 yen."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"60","s":"Fourteen bills were introduced 25 April to 25 May, including the Political Contributions bill, the National Government Organization bill and the Juvenile Guidance bill. Sixteen laws were enacted, among them Daylight Savings Time and Minor Offense. Committee chairmanships went to Social Democrats, Democrats and People's Cooperatives, and Oishi and Takahashi won the 21 May Miyagi by-election."},{"t":"Budget Action","lv":3,"p":[60,62],"pp":"62","s":"A June provisional budget of 25,800,000,000 yen passed 28 May while the Cabinet wrangled over the 1948-49 general budget. Disputes covered rail and postal rates (3.5 times), a 3,731 yen wage base, war bond interest deferral, local finance and school funds. The Cabinet approved a 399,300,000,000 yen budget 30 May."},{"t":"Civil Service Institute","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"67","s":"On 14 May the Civil Service Institute graduated its first class from a 100-day course in scientific personnel administration, an outgrowth of the Civil Service Mission and the National Public Servants Law of October 1947. Of 354 graduates, 126 return to ministries and 226 join the National Personnel Commission. A second class runs 2 August to 1 October."},{"t":"Hoarded Goods and Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","lv":3,"p":[63,66],"pp":"68","s":"The Diet's Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee, in its 30 April interim report, urged laws curbing secret political funds after 16 sessions and 45 witnesses on the Tsuji case. Four perjury indictments followed 10 and 12 May. Construction firms' donations touched Deputy Prime Minister Nishio, the Showa Denko case continued, and Chairman Muto defended the committee's methods against Democratic criticism."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[67,72],"pp":"72","s":"Party conventions and maneuvers dominated May. Democrats held their national convention 4-5 May and accepted the war bond interest compromise. Social Democrats moved to unify the party and set a policy on the Labor-Farmer Liaison Council. Democratic Liberals attacked the Cabinet, with three small groups joining them 25 May. People's Cooperatives met 15 May and lost their Osaka branch. Ashida proposed barring Communists from office."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[72,74],"pp":"85","s":"The Public Office Qualifications Committee and Appeal Board were dissolved 10 May under Cabinet Order No. 62. Of 717,415 persons screened since 1946, 201,815 remained purged after 129 reinstatements on 22 May. Statistical tables follow. A new advisory group (11 May) and the Attorney-General's Investigation Bureau, with eight regional offices, took over continuing checks and enforcement."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[75,78],"pp":"139","s":"Hoarded goods recovered in April were worth 123,296,141 yen, up 36 percent, mostly metals. March saw 180,850 offenses and 101,402 arrests; Tokyo's rate was highest. Recruitment of 36,000 new police was completed. Prison population was 89,234. April rail accidents numbered 596, March fires 1,806 with 28 deaths, and the Maritime Safety Board was activated 1 May."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[78,80],"pp":"145","s":"The Diet elected the first Impeachment Court president, and passed the Administrative Execution by Proxy Law (6 May); the Minor Offenses Law was enacted 30 April. Seventy-nine atrocity investigations closed, leaving 897. By 20 May 666 war criminals had been tried, 617 convicted including 95 sentenced to death; Okada was sentenced to hang. The IMTFE held no session."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[81,183],"pp":"151","s":"Detailed economic section covering thirteen subjects from agriculture to science and technology."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[82,85],"pp":"153","s":"Tenant land sales were 104,014 cho in April, cumulative 531,334 cho; livestock-farm retention criteria were set for about 158 farms. Agricultural cooperatives approved by governors reached 11,774. Livestock slaughter fell in February; milk output was 10,185,000 liters in March; 280 goats arrived from the Brethren Service Committee. March marine production was 111,575 tons; Bonin whaling landed 243 whales."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[86,92],"pp":"161","s":"April log output fell to 63,490,300 cubic feet, while 1947 fiscal-year logs totaled 752,916,200 cubic feet, 18 percent over goal. Firewood, charcoal and gasumaki figures are given. April coal production was 2,572,400 tons, nine percent under quota; fiscal 1947 coal was revised to 29,335,500 tons. Lignite, crude oil (15,630 kiloliters), drilling and March mineral gains follow."},{"t":"Section 3: Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[92,102],"pp":"173","s":"April coke reached 233,940 tons, a fifth consecutive postwar record, and pig iron (55,072 tons) and steel ingots set postwar highs, though labor disputes cut rolled steel. Nonferrous metals, rubber stocks, petroleum, cement (169,050 tons) and construction are covered, as are 10 tugs and 13 barges for the USSR, fertilizers, chemicals, rolling stock, machine tools and machinery."},{"t":"Section 4: Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[103,112],"pp":"195","s":"Covers March food processing (flour 103,664 tons), sake output of 747,060 hectoliters and tobacco recovery after a strike. April paper reached a postwar high of 37,963 tons. Glass, ceramics, electrical goods (hurt by labor trouble), trucks, rubber, leather, agricultural implements, toys, buttons, watches and sewing machines are covered, with record alarm clock and phonograph record output."},{"t":"Section 5: Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[113,127],"pp":"215","s":"April set postwar highs in spun silk yarn, rayon staple and filament, and cotton and silk fabrics; raw silk stayed above the 10,000-bale goal at 10,310 bales. Raw cotton stocks fell 29,250,000 pounds with a 100,000-bale minimum backlog. Wool, hard and bast fibers, knit goods, clothing, dyeing and finishing follow, including GARIOA cotton yarn for fish netting."},{"t":"Section 6: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[128,133],"pp":"245","s":"Registered commercial vehicles fell to 161,320 in April, with about 67 percent operable; deadlining was blamed on fuel, tire and repair shortages. Government railways carried 312,127,898 passengers and 9,787,700 metric tons of freight. Coastal steel-ship cargo rose six percent. Power generation reached 3,878,000,000 kWh, mostly hydroelectric, while gas available for distribution rose two percent to 64,363,146 cubic meters. Gas curbs on industry were extended to 1 August."},{"t":"Section 7: Communications","lv":2,"p":[134,140],"pp":"257","s":"The Cabinet approved a bill on 22 May creating a Ministry of Communications and abolishing ten local bureaus. April brought 1,303,346 telephones, record toll calls in nine cities, expanded overseas radiotelephone and radiotelegraph service, and 6,535,531 radio households. Postal mail, savings and money-order volumes rose. Equipment output dipped amid strikes, with 438,836 ministry employees, a March profit of 355,966,254 yen, and research on voltage regulators and tubes."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[141,151],"pp":"271","s":"Labor relations were calmer than at any time since mid-1947 despite wage talks in nearly every industry. Prime Minister Ashida opposed revising labor laws; May Day rallies drew 300,000 in Tokyo. The Diet unanimously backed rejoining the ILO, over NCIU objections. Unions numbered 30,199 with 6,390,227 members. Strikes, production-control trials and grievance-machinery disputes continued, alongside labor education, wage and compensation data, and employment measures including a coal-mine freeze."},{"t":"Section 9: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[152,158],"pp":"293","s":"March imports were valued at $40,160,265, with about 77 percent from the United States, followed by Cuba and the Philippines. Exports were $11,606,301, two thirds to Asia, led by the Netherlands East Indies. Foodstuffs were 47 percent of imports and textiles 21 percent; textiles made up 69 percent of exports, mostly cotton goods. First-quarter trade with the United States showed a deficit of $133,121,288."},{"t":"Section 10: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[158,163],"pp":"305","s":"Staple collections were negligible in May, the second month rations came from carryover stocks, causing local delays; 137,620 tons of imported food were authorized, including 97,620 tons of sugar. Vegetable and fish per-capita supplies, fats, oil, salt, spinning and railway-car prices were set. Incentive goods reached farmers and coal miners; rents, family expenditures and the consumer price index rose."},{"t":"Section 11: Finance","lv":2,"p":[164,170],"pp":"317","s":"Bank of Japan notes stood at 223.5 billion yen on 31 May. A nationwide financial reorganization of 15 May wrote off worthless assets, with about 18 billion yen budgeted for bank losses. Reconstruction Finance Bank loans reached 64.3 billion yen; 67 control bodies were closed; April tax receipts passed budget estimates; national debt was 358.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Section 12: Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[171,175],"pp":"331","s":"Looted property worth 278,781 yen was taken into custody and restitution of 737,044 yen effected. The SCAP custody account reached 368.4 million yen, with a new German External Property Commission account opened. Rehabilitation borrowing was cleared for restricted concerns, 80 control organizations were ordered liquidated, and 50 holding companies were released; 9,967 machine tools had been shipped to reparations claimants."},{"t":"Section 13: Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[176,183],"pp":"341","s":"Twenty-one observation groups studied the 9 May annular eclipse, mainly at Rebun Island, with Japanese-American and Chinese-Japanese cooperation. Japan Academy prizes went to Koga, Fujiwara, Kotani and Tomonaga. Polyvinyl alcohol fiber, bojuntan coke and sea-water caustic soda were reported, along with society meetings, 2,353 standards in effect, and 346 patents granted in April."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[183,206],"pp":"355","s":"Detailed social section: public health and welfare, and education, religion, cultural resources and media of expression."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[184,196],"pp":"357","s":"April recorded 3,237 cases and 328 deaths from 11 communicable diseases, with no cholera. Medical and dental supply output reached about ¥1.68 billion, up sharply on March. LARA relief arrivals reached 4,625 metric tons; 123,511 orphans were counted. Unemployment insurance replaced allowances on 1 May. Births, deaths and nutrition surveys followed, and population reached 79,690,953; 33,635 persons were repatriated."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[197,206],"pp":"383","s":"The Ministry of Education announced a Korean schools policy (7 May), allotted cement, and set secondary and university standards, correspondence education rules and 107 million authorized textbooks. The National Christian Council was organized 17 May, and 379 Meiji historic sites were declassified. Media coverage covered opinion surveys, a 22-newspaper circulation survey, a 98-book translation list, radio, films, theater and three new SCAP libraries."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-32","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-32-1948-05","s":"K","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"","pg":199,"o":"Report No. 32 covers May 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board. Its Summary calls the month historic: the 10 May general election (over 91 per cent of registrants voting despite communist terror) and the 31 May opening of the National Assembly, which elected Syngman Rhee Speaker. North Korea cut electricity to the South on 14 May, discrediting Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic after the Pyongyang conference and cutting industrial output sharply. The text also covers Cheju-do guerrilla fighting, 38th Parallel incidents, Mukden repatriation, vested property sales, farmland transfers, drought, finance, public works, police, education, culture and youth/women's groups. Caution for researchers: the scan is misbound. PDF pages 47-113 and 134-137 are pages of a July 1947 report (Legislative Assembly debates, constitution draft, Joint Commission, police and prison tables), replacing May 1948 material on industry, mining, allocations, trade, labor, transportation, communications and loans.","h":["10 May 1948: first general election; over 91 per cent of registrants voted, 198 Assembly members elected, Cheju-do's Puk Cheju A and B districts postponed.","31 May: National Assembly opened with all 198 members; Rhee Syngman elected Speaker with 188 votes.","14 May: North Korea cut electric power, which supplied about 60 per cent of South Korea's electricity; overall production fell 75 per cent.","Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic lost political standing after their Pyongyang promise that power would continue proved false.","448,513 farms, 76 per cent of former Japanese farmland to be sold, had been transferred to Koreans by 31 May.","First Mukden repatriates reached Inchon from Tientsin on 10-11 May; 3,831 repatriated by month's end.","Election violence: press tally of 202 dead and 643 wounded; Cheju-do rebellion intensified, with 3,126 held.","Ordinance No. 192 separated the courts from the Department of Justice, effective 1 June 1948."],"k":["May 1948 election","National Assembly","Syngman Rhee","power shut-off","Pyongyang conference","Kim Koo","Cheju-do","vested property","National Land Administration","Mukden repatriates","National Police","public works","Korean National Youth","fertilizer","National Economic Board"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,7],"pp":"","s":"Cover (No. 32, May 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board), blank page, list of Board members at 31 May 1948, and a four-page table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[8,11],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of May: the 10 May election, power cutoff of 14 May, falling output, drought, fertilizer, population growth, border and Cheju-do violence, fear of Japan, public works, vested property, trade and finance."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[12,27],"pp":"5","s":"Population, migration, repatriation, immigration rules and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[12,21],"pp":"5","s":"South Korea's population rose one-third since 1940 to 20.0 million by January 1948; 2.4 million entered against 886,000 leaving. Covers Mukden repatriates, Korean schools in Japan and draft immigration ordinance."},{"t":"Census Report","lv":3,"p":[12,13],"pp":"5","s":"Compares 1940, 1944, 1946 and 1948 population counts; natural increase revised to 1.8 per cent; urban population up 72.7 per cent since 1940."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[14,17],"pp":"7","s":"Table 1 by province, population charts, and tables of cumulative movement; refugee arrivals from the North fell about 20 per cent from April to May (12,391)."},{"t":"Manchurian Repatriates","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"11","s":"First Mukden repatriates reached Inchon by LST from Tientsin on 11 May; 3,831 repatriated by month's end, 2,651 still awaiting; LARA aid."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[19,19],"pp":"12","s":"Dispute between the Korean League and the Japanese government over Korean schools; CI&E proposals on textbooks and teachers; Osaka and Kobe disturbances halted talks."},{"t":"Repatriation of Korean Businessmen from Japan","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"13","s":"Korean businessmen in Japan increasingly applied to return; the 1,000-won allowance and property-shipment limits had slowed repatriation."},{"t":"Immigration and Emigration Regulations","lv":3,"p":[20,21],"pp":"13","s":"Digest of a new ordinance replacing Ordinance No. 49 (due about 1 August 1948): entry documents, Home Affairs Bureaus as agents, definition of Korean citizen, exempt aliens."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[22,27],"pp":"15","s":"Office of Property Custody prepared for Koreanization: a record 2,334 financial statements, reorganization studies, rising sales applications after the election, rent collection and decentralized accounting. Page 27 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"15","s":"Took over Korean Residence Management Corporation 6 May; loan guarantees status; investigation of 1,841 companies for reorganization, 484 unlocated."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"17","s":"Post-election rise in purchase applications (497 cases in May); tables of appraisals and sales, May total 111 million won."},{"t":"Real Estate Section","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"18","s":"63 dependent houses and 11 Army parcels released; condition surveys arranged with Camp Seoul."},{"t":"Comptroller Division","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"18","s":"Training Korean auditors, handbook, bilingual reports, new tax forms, Budget Section, bank-deposit reconciliation."},{"t":"Rental Properties","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"19","s":"Full lease report as of 31 December 1947; table of new leases and rents collected January-April 1948 (2,252 new leases in April)."},{"t":"Property of Allied Nationals","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"19","s":"Japanese Custodian records checked for Allied-owned property; US-owned cases go to the State Department or claims against Japan."},{"t":"Executive Division","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"19","s":"Collected synopses and narrative reports from all SKIG agencies toward a general inventory of vested property."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[28,46],"pp":"21","s":"Surviving pages cover agriculture, forestry, fisheries and prices; later sections of this Part are missing from the scan."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[28,38],"pp":"21","s":"Drought threatens summer grain on a forecast 1,956,227 acres; fertilizer receipts and straw-product purchases; reforestation collapse since 1944; fishery exports to Japan; 448,513 farms sold by NLA."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"21","s":"Insufficient spring rainfall likely to cut summer grain yields; final estimates redone in May."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Economics","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"21","s":"5 May summer-grain forecast tables 1930-1948; 1,847 Crop Reporting Service staff appointed, 37 resignations over pay."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Production","lv":3,"p":[29,30],"pp":"22","s":"Livestock, poultry feed, fertilizer allocations, cotton stocks of 7,578 tons, sericulture, power-pump irrigation (94,153 acres), sweet and Irish potatoes."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"24","s":"16 million acres of forestland; annual cut exceeds growth; reforestation fell from 358 million trees (1942) to 61.6 million (1947); charcoal, fuelwood and log tables."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"26","s":"Exports to Japan Sept 1947-May 1948 worth $4.9 million (laver, anchovies, seaweed); landings expected near pre-war levels after fleet reorganization."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"28","s":"Fertilizer receipts and sales (156,444 and 106,717 tons in 1948), imports from US replacing Japan, 10,000 tons ammonium sulphate from North by barter; straw bag and rope purchases."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"30","s":"448,513 of 587,974 farms sold or transferred by 31 May (76 per cent), table by regional office; surveys ordered for deeds."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"31","s":"Rice experiment projects seeded; Pusan Fisheries College placed under AIS for administration."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[39,46],"pp":"32","s":"Wholesale and retail price indexes; Seoul rice up nearly 10 per cent in May; tables of controlled prices and a chart of price and production trends. Page 45 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Wholesale and Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[39,42],"pp":"32","s":"Indexes (August 1945 = 100) for 27 wholesale and 26 retail commodities to May 1948; grain shortage lifted food prices."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[43,46],"pp":"37","s":"National Price Administration prices of controlled domestic goods; nine-city open market retail prices; price and production trend charts."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[114,123],"pp":"91","s":"Surviving pages cover public utilities and public works; Transportation and Communications are missing from the scan."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[114,117],"pp":"107","s":"May power distribution fell to 56.1 million KWH from 74.5 million after the 14 May cutoff; emergency board, bulb substitution and 24-hour monitoring."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[114,114],"pp":"107","s":"South Korea produced 41.5 million KWH, plus 15.1 million from the North before the cutoff; production up 8.5 million over April."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Transactions","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"107","s":"Board met 12, 19, 24 May; emergency plans worked; 30-watt bulbs replace 60-watt, saving 28,000 KW; new priorities set."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[115,116],"pp":"108","s":"Table of supply by source; record output at Sum Jin Gang; Yongwol coal draw; power barges at Pusan and Inchon; national load dispatcher."},{"t":"Street Transit Systems","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"110","s":"Seoul streetcars stayed at 136, out only 45 minutes on 14 May; buses rose from 21 to 31."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[118,123],"pp":"111","s":"Two-and-a-half-year stocktaking of roads, bridges, levees, water supply, surveys and fire service; bridge tables, flood-control subsidies and 181 fires in May."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"111","s":"Seoul-Pusan Highway rebuilt with 37 bridges; 162 km of levees; 27 water plants; 2,750 map plates recovered from Japan; fire service reorganized."},{"t":"Roads and Bridges","lv":3,"p":[120,121],"pp":"113","s":"Volunteer road-maintenance contest won by Cholla-namdo; map of roads and bridges May 1948; progress table of major bridges."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[122,122],"pp":"115","s":"153 million won in flood-control subsidies; Ilsan levee and Kaesong road levee on the Han completed."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[122,122],"pp":"115","s":"Power cutoff and drought hurt water pumping at Inchon and elsewhere; emergency measures taken."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[123,123],"pp":"","s":"181 fires in May, 37 by arson, loss of 251.7 million won; industrial fire survey; extinguishing supplies received."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[124,139],"pp":"117","s":"Government finance and non-government finance, with four misbound July 1947 pages."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[124,129],"pp":"117","s":"Note issue rose 469 million won in May; SKIG overdraft 20,232 million won; F/Y 1948/49 budgets approaching 29 billion won; revenue-to-expenditure ratio 73 per cent."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[124,124],"pp":"117","s":"Note issue reached 33,389 million won by end-December 1947, driven by the rice collection program."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[124,125],"pp":"117","s":"Cumulative excess withdrawals 20,232 million won at end of May; tables of note issue and Bank of Chosun deposits and withdrawals."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"119","s":"F/Y 1947/48 authorizations 22,320 million won; table of F/Y 1948/49 agency budgets approved by 31 May."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"119","s":"April revenue 1,930 million won, tobacco sales 702 million; expenditures 119 million above March."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[127,127],"pp":"120","s":"Revenue was 73 per cent of expenditure paid and 69 per cent of the expenditure budget in F/Y 1947/48; monthly table and revenues."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[128,129],"pp":"121","s":"Assets summary of imported civilian supply goods; table of departmental expenditures for F/Y 1947/48."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[130,139],"pp":"123","s":"April data: cash balances down 1,080 million won, deposit balances at a record 56,421 million, insurance and loan activity. Pages 134-137 are misbound July 1947 police and prison tables."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[130,130],"pp":"123","s":"Cash balances fell 1,080 million won in April to about the February level."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[131,132],"pp":"124","s":"Deposits up 11 per cent and balances up over 2 per cent; 55 per cent government accounts, 40 per cent public; deposit and withdrawal chart."},{"t":"Deposit Balance of Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"126","s":"Inter-bank and general public deposit tables; Bank of Chosun balances up from government deposits."},{"t":"Damage Insurance and Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[138,138],"pp":"131","s":"Life insurance table; 8,294 damage policies written in April; collateral insurance instructions and past-due agency balances."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"132","s":"Damage insurance table; Hyup Dong Life wins sales trophy; 95 loans totaling 1,839 million won approved, including 900 million for filatures; claims against Japan."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[140,171],"pp":"133","s":"Administration, election and Assembly, legal affairs, police, interzone activities."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[140,143],"pp":"133","s":"Chief Adviser Johnson's Washington mission; NEB actions; civil service convention and 25-130 per cent pay rise from 1 June; Military Government personnel table."},{"t":"Chief Adviser's Mission","lv":3,"p":[140,140],"pp":"133","s":"Dr. Edgar Johnson in Washington 29 March-25 May: budget, supplies, post-transfer legislation, Foreign Liquidation Commission, power shortage."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[141,141],"pp":"134","s":"Four weekly meetings in May and a list of items acted on, including daylight saving time from 31 May and telephone rates."},{"t":"Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"135","s":"Four-day provincial officers' convention from 16 May; warning about abuse of eligible lists; general pay raise averaging 50 per cent."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[142,143],"pp":"135","s":"Table of personnel October 1946-May 1948; civilian turnover high due to one-year contracts."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[144,155],"pp":"137","s":"The 10 May election and 198-member Assembly (tables by province, party, occupation), Rhee's statements, 31 May opening, North-South Conference, the 14 May power shut-off and Koreans in Japan."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"137","s":"Over 91 per cent of registrants voted; nearly half the winners avowed rightists; power cutoff angered public."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[144,150],"pp":"137","s":"Results by province with names, ages and parties of the 198 members; party and occupation tables; reactions of rightist, moderate parties and Kim Koo; average age 47."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[151,152],"pp":"144","s":"Rhee's statements on the government; preliminary meeting 27 May; 31 May opening, Rhee elected Speaker 188 votes, formal ceremony and his speech."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[153,153],"pp":"146","s":"Hodge and Dean speeches at the opening; Kimm and Kim Koo's return from Pyongyang on 5 May, their joint statement and rightist disapproval."},{"t":"Power Shut-off","lv":3,"p":[154,154],"pp":"147","s":"Cutoff of 14 May after Kim and Kimm said power would continue; Kim Koo denies knowing the contract; rightists call promise false."},{"t":"Koreans in Japan","lv":3,"p":[155,155],"pp":"148","s":"South Korean parties protest what they call Japanese imperialism being re-established."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[156,160],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinances Nos. 192-197 (court reorganization first), daylight saving proclamation, four legal opinions on vested property, prisons (22,141 inmates), courts and Cheju-do trial team."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[156,156],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinance 192 Court Reorganization to Ordinance 197 (Coast Guard searches); daylight saving from 31 May; proclamation on the National Assembly; summer grain collection."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"150","s":"Opinions on SKIG claims for vested property damage, Chosun Aircraft board election, Seoul city property powers, and free silk market."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[158,158],"pp":"151","s":"Guard target practice, prison labor for constabulary uniforms, raincoats, prison population 22,141 at 30 April, sports equipment."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[159,159],"pp":"152","s":"Court team sent to Cheju-do; NLA suits; courts separated from Department of Justice effective 1 June."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Air Carriers section of the proposed Civil Code completed."},{"t":"Property Claims Commission","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Simplified procedure directive of 17 April 1948 for vested property claims."},{"t":"Section 4: National Police","lv":2,"p":[161,166],"pp":"154","s":"Police largely kept order on election day (68 of 13,407 booths attacked) but not on Cheju-do; also decentralization, police finances and Pusan police."},{"t":"General and National Election","lv":3,"p":[161,162],"pp":"154","s":"Propaganda, 14 labor and nine school strikes, sabotage of rail and power lines, four candidates killed, attacks on police and towns."},{"t":"Cheju-do","lv":3,"p":[163,163],"pp":"156","s":"Reasons for election success; fighting intensified despite appeals; 3,126 persons held; voting postponed in two districts."},{"t":"Provincial Police","lv":3,"p":[164,164],"pp":"157","s":"Decentralization aims, provincial chiefs and governors, and unofficial finances through the Police Protective Association."},{"t":"Pusan Police","lv":3,"p":[165,166],"pp":"158","s":"Profile of Kyongsang-namdo police chief Pak Myun-jae and adviser Hackett; stations, property, cooperative stores, discipline."},{"t":"Section 5: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[167,171],"pp":"160","s":"31 border incidents, over 43,000 pieces of mail exchanged, and Radio Pyongyang propaganda on the North constitution, election and power cutoff. Page 171 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Border Incidents","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"160","s":"Nightly small-arms fire; dam fight near Oe-Dong; a Soviet Yak-9 landed near Yosu on 19 May."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"160","s":"South sent 28,810 pieces to the North and received 14,707."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[168,171],"pp":"161","s":"North Korean constitution of 28 April, attacks on the South election, Soviet-line commentary and the radio campaign after the power cutoff."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[172,199],"pp":"165","s":"Health and welfare, education, culture, press, youth and women's activities."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[172,175],"pp":"165","s":"Mukden refugees arriving by ship, smallpox cases, low-cost housing in Kyongsang-namdo, UNRRA grants to child welfare institutions, nursing, veterinary, pharmaceutical, narcotics and laboratory items."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[172,172],"pp":"165","s":"Smallpox among Cheju constabulary; first Mukden refugee ship (1,091) and later shipments with measles cases."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[173,173],"pp":"167","s":"Relief staff devoted to Mukden refugees; 2,106 housing units built in Kyongsang-namdo; UNRRA grants to orphanages and boys' schools."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"167","s":"38 doctors examined for Army hospital internships; leper settlement of 100 in caves visited."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"168","s":"Qualification exams for nurses and midwives, three-month training course, Nurses' Association meeting."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[176,182],"pp":"169","s":"Three ordinances drafted to create school districts and Boards of Education financed by family income tax; teacher certification, summer training for 845 teachers, textbooks and Korean typewriter."},{"t":"Bureau of Administration","lv":3,"p":[176,176],"pp":"169","s":"F/Y 1948/49 budget 1.5 billion won; three ordinances on school districts, boards and school financing."},{"t":"Bureau of Common Schools","lv":3,"p":[177,177],"pp":"170","s":"Curriculum workshops, civics book for middle schools; special education clinics for handicapped children."},{"t":"Bureau of Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[178,178],"pp":"171","s":"Unified certification standards effective 15 June; summer sessions; Teacher Training Center committees."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[179,179],"pp":"171","s":"Nursing education, 23 Severance graduates, first postwar nursing textbook, Seoul National University Hospital rehabilitation board, veterinary hospital."},{"t":"American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"174","s":"26 English teachers and 15 interpreters; fees introduced after the budget was eliminated; 13 of 32 students qualified for US study."},{"t":"Bureau of Vocational and Technical Education","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"174","s":"New bureau budget 2,000,000 won; Korean typewriter demonstration, typist training and accounting course."},{"t":"Bureau of Adult and Social Education","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Plans for a national dramatic contest and an Adult Education Association conference."},{"t":"Bureau of Textbooks","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Nearly 14,000,000 textbooks published from August 1945 to May 1948."},{"t":"Bureau of Research","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Manual on objective tests; primary school achievement tests approved 14 May."},{"t":"Provincial Education","lv":3,"p":[182,182],"pp":"175","s":"Home economics association meeting; 200 Kyonggi-do teachers visit an American dependent school."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[183,186],"pp":"176","s":"Election campaign teams gave 803 performances to 1.33 million; music festival and orchestra scholarships; US information centers; Rhee portrait exhibition; election stamps; KBS election coverage."},{"t":"Election Campaign Teams","lv":3,"p":[183,183],"pp":"176","s":"Stage Arts Association teams toured from 7 April: 803 performances, 1,334,464 attendees, plays and songs on voting."},{"t":"Music","lv":3,"p":[184,184],"pp":"177","s":"Musical Festival 15-17 May; Seoul Philharmonic Beethoven concerts; Korea Symphony filmed; scholarships for three musicians."},{"t":"Information Centers and Art Exhibits","lv":3,"p":[185,185],"pp":"178","s":"US Information Center in Seoul opened 29 April, Kaesong center 6 May; about 100,000 viewed a Rhee Syngman exhibition."},{"t":"Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"179","s":"Children's Day ceremony 5 May; election commemorative stamps issued 10 May."},{"t":"Korea Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"179","s":"Election publicity campaign, shortwave relay on election day, BBC-style procedures, Olympic and Assembly coverage plans."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[187,191],"pp":"180","s":"Press fights over the Pyongyang conference, election disorder tallies (202 dead), Rhee's 24 May press conference, power cutoff blame and a resurgent fear of Japan."},{"t":"Pyongyang Conference","lv":3,"p":[187,187],"pp":"180","s":"Press battle over the conference; alleged promises to the two Kims that power would continue; one promise broken."},{"t":"Election","lv":3,"p":[188,189],"pp":"181","s":"Precautions, UN Commission trips, casualty tallies, congratulations, Country Guard Corps criticism, Rhee's press conference."},{"t":"Electric Power","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"183","s":"Papers divided on whether the cutoff was political or economic."},{"t":"Fear of Japan","lv":3,"p":[190,191],"pp":"183","s":"Closure of Korean schools in Japan and rumors of Japanese officials returning provoke denunciations; press objects to UN report in Tokyo."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[192,194],"pp":"185","s":"33 KNY members elected to the Assembly; membership 859,208 at end May; reserve leader training; F/Y 1948/49 budget 20.6 million won; community service projects."},{"t":"Election Results","lv":3,"p":[192,192],"pp":"185","s":"33 members elected as private citizens; KNY judged the strongest of ten dozen youth groups; two-month turnout drive."},{"t":"Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[192,193],"pp":"185","s":"Membership 859,208, with screening to weed out extremists; tables by month and by province; 65 training centers."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[194,194],"pp":"187","s":"Budget of 20,600,000 won; discussion groups, anti-illiteracy classes, road repair, scouting, clean-up, reforestation."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[195,199],"pp":"188","s":"About 3,000,000 women voted; 19 women candidates, none elected; Women's Bureau, volunteer service, Junior Red Cross, YWCA fashion show and Ewha University's 62nd anniversary."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[195,195],"pp":"188","s":"Women voted 3,000,000 strong; 19 of 594 candidates were women, none elected; table of registered women voters."},{"t":"Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Two members on the National Election Committee; Mothers' School certificates; Korean group accepted by US women's clubs federation."},{"t":"Women's Special Volunteer Service","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Over 1,200 volunteer women daily on welfare and community projects as of 30 May."},{"t":"Junior Red Cross","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Aims, gift boxes to schools and orphanages, 4,714 members, planned publications and training."},{"t":"The YWCA","lv":3,"p":[197,197],"pp":"190","s":"American-Korean fashion show on 15 May drew over 500 spectators and 33 models."},{"t":"Ewha Women's University","lv":3,"p":[197,199],"pp":"190","s":"62nd anniversary; history since 1886 under Dr. Helen Kim; dormitories; list of colleges and departments."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-22-in-K-32"]},{"id":"K-32-in-K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"Bound in K-30","host":"K-30","rg":[[450,655]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:32' on PDF p. 450","pg":206,"o":"This issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities (No. 32, prepared by the National Economic Board) covers May 1948, a month dominated by the 10 May general election, the first in Korean history, with about 91 percent of registrants voting despite communist terror. Nearly half of the 198 elected Assemblymen were rightists, Rhee Syngman became Assembly chairman, and the Assembly convened on 31 May. North Korea's 14 May electricity cutoff discredited Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic after the Pyongyang conference and cut industrial output about 75 percent, while drought threatened summer grain. The report also covers the Cheju rebellion, border incidents, sabotage, Mukden repatriates, vested property Koreanization, court reorganization, education ordinances and women's participation. Detailed tables on prices, coal, trade, petroleum, transport, power, finance and insurance give historians a month-by-month statistical baseline for the last weeks of the Interim Government.","h":["10 May 1948: first free general election; more than 91 percent of registrants voted despite communist violence.","North Korea cut electricity to the South on 14 May, ending roughly 60 percent of supply.","Overall industrial production fell about 75 percent by end of May due to power shortage.","Elections in two of three Cheju districts were invalid; virtual civil war continued on the island.","Ordinance No. 192 separated Korean courts from the Department of Justice.","Three education ordinances drafted to create school districts and Boards of Education.","First Mukden repatriates (3,800 in four ships) arrived at Inchon.","Thirty-one 38th parallel border incidents with North Korean Constabulary reported."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","general election 1948","National Assembly","power shut-off","Pyongyang Conference","Cheju-do","Rhee Syngman","vested property","Mukden repatriates","private trade Hong Kong","coal production","Bank of Chosun","National Police","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[450,457],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 32 (May 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, followed by blank and image pages. Lists Board members at 31 May 1948, including Chairman Wilhelm Anderson, Executive Secretary Otis T. Weaver and directors for Agriculture, Commerce, Finance and Transportation, plus a six-part table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[458,461],"pp":"1","s":"Reviews May 1948 as historic: the 10 May election drew 91 per cent of registrants despite communist terror, and the Assembly sat 31 May. North Korea's 14 May power cutoff cut production about 75 per cent and discredited Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic. Also covers drought, Cheju rebellion, border incidents, rising trade, 469,000,000 won note issue and rice prices."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[462,477],"pp":"5","s":"Population, repatriation and foreign affairs, plus vested property administration."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[462,471],"pp":"5","s":"South Korea's population rose one-third since 1940, reaching about 20,041,000 by January 1948; net migration added roughly 1,534,000. Refugees from the north fell about 20 per cent from April; the first Mukden repatriates reached Inchon on 11 May (3,831 by month end). Covers Korean schooling disputes in Japan, repatriating businessmen, and a new immigration ordinance expected about 1 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[472,477],"pp":"15","s":"The Office of Property Custody prepared for Koreanization by decentralizing operations and training auditors. A record 2,334 financial statements arrived, and the Korean Residence Management Corporation was taken over on 6 May. Purchase applications rose after the election (497 cases), sales totaled 111,225 thousand won, and rent collections and loan guarantees continued; Allied nationals' property was reviewed."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[478,547],"pp":"21","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[478,488],"pp":"21","s":"Summer grain forecast of 673,982 metric tons on 1,956,227 acres (104.1 per cent of 1947) faced reduced yields from drought, and 94,153 acres of rice land depended on power pumps. Reports covered livestock, fertilizer, forestry tables, fisheries exports to Japan worth $4,936,162, KAA fertilizer sales, 448,513 farms sold by the National Land Administration, and the Agricultural Improvement Service."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[489,496],"pp":"32","s":"Open-market wholesale prices in Seoul rose in May: rice up nearly 10 per cent to about the January level, with barley, red beans, building materials, metals and raw silk higher. Retail prices increased after a March-April dip, while sugar, salt, dried fish, laundry soap and work shoes fell. Nine-city rice averaged 734 won by 25 May; National Price Administration controlled prices are tabulated."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[497,507],"pp":"40","s":"Power cutoff from North Korea on 14 May cut overall production about 75 per cent by month end, reversing a strong April (rubber shoes from 154 plants, 2,261,906 pairs; cigarettes up 66.2 per cent). May hit flour, paper (166.9 tons), cement, bricks, briquettes and cotton goods, while salt rose to 28,056 tons. Production statistics cover under 100 larger plants."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[508,518],"pp":"51","s":"Anthracite output reached 68,612 metric tons in April, up 9.3 per cent and more than double the 1947 average, with Samchuk exceeding quota; the May power cutoff then reduced mining, flooded the Ongjin and Haeju mines, and Yong Wol fell to 6,639 tons. Bituminous imports from Japan dropped to 61,068 tons from 83,171. The Coal Industry Fund sought a 200,000,000 won loan."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[519,528],"pp":"62","s":"The Consumer Goods Allocation Board dealt with delayed cotton cloth deliveries over price differences. Civilian supply allocations went to Commerce, Agriculture and other departments; May petroleum allocations included 6,936,921 liters of gasoline for elections. Salt distribution reached 27,434 tons, ex-Japanese metals were allocated, and Federation of Financial Associations goods reached employees."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[529,541],"pp":"72","s":"May licenses (95 import, 83 export) pointed to exports near 548 million won against imports near 323 million. April private trade: imports 746 million won (paper products, rubber, drugs), exports 391 million (dried fish over half), 99 percent to Hong Kong, 87 percent of imports via Inchon. Government sales of ore and graphite, Civilian Supply coal, fertilizer, flour, sugar and petroleum are also covered."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[542,547],"pp":"85","s":"Strike threats hit two industries in May. At Seoul Electric the Tai Han union denied a communist-inspired strike, the Military Governor approved Maj.-Gen. Dean's fact-finding report, and a 29 May conference followed. At Pusan, after wildcat stoppages of 450 stevedores on 11-12 May, contractors fell to seven or eight, a joint hiring hall replaced the honcho system, and the union deferred strikes. Child labor and overtime inspections, a renamed labor paper, and radio and health items also appear."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[548,573],"pp":"91","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[548,556],"pp":"91","s":"Communist sabotage around the 10 May election wrecked train No. 36, pulled rails and spikes, and damaged locomotives at several cities. Marine shipping priorities were readjusted, coal first at 42.9 percent. April Transportation revenue fell 28 percent to 374.6 million won. Rail employment rose 2.3 percent, 17 more locomotives became operable, and coal moved from Mukko. Refugees were repatriated from Tientsin."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[557,563],"pp":"100","s":"Saboteurs burned the Inchon telephone exchange (repairs of two to three months, about 7 million won) and arsonists destroyed the Yongdungpo repeater station on 23 May, killing two employees and a policeman. April revenue fell 30.6 percent from March, staff declined to 12,761, and postal deposits, book transfers and life insurance are tabulated. Phone and telegraph rates, about 85 times 1937 levels, rose at the end of May."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[564,567],"pp":"107","s":"May power distribution fell to 56.1 million KWH from 74.5 million in April; South Korea produced 41.5 million and the North delivered 15.1 million before the 14 May shut-off. The Emergency Power Board set Daylight Saving Time from 31 May, 30-watt home bulbs and new priorities, and US monitoring of dispatch began 26 May. Sun Jin Gang hit a record output; Seoul streetcars resumed within 45 minutes."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[568,573],"pp":"111","s":"A stocktaking of two and a half years credited Public Works with rebuilding the Seoul-Pusan Highway (37 bridges, 497 km, about 236.8 million won), 162 km of levees, water plants, fire service reorganization and 515 pieces of heavy equipment. May brought a road competition won by Cholla-namdo, bridge progress, 153 million won in flood subsidies, power-cut water problems, and 181 fires."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[574,589],"pp":"117","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[574,579],"pp":"117","s":"The Bank of Chosun note issue peaked at 33,389 million won in December 1947 and was down 4,745 million by May, though May rose 469 million. The overdraft stood at 20,232 million won after a 4,424 million credit. F/Y 1947/48 authorizations reached 22,320 million; F/Y 1948/49 budgets approximate 29 billion. April revenue was 1,930 million (tobacco 702 million), expenditure 2,010 million, revenue-to-spending ratio 73 percent; Civilian Supply assets 22,995 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[580,589],"pp":"123","s":"April data only. Cash balances fell 1,080 million won to 2,428.7 million, and deposits reached a record 56,421 million (55 percent government). Loans outstanding fell 2,096 million to 30,025 million, the fourth monthly net retirement. Life insurance in force rose 68 million won; damage insurers wrote 8,294 policies. May loans approved totaled 1,839 million, and banks were told to compile claims against Japan."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[590,621],"pp":"133","s":"Administration, politics, legal affairs, police and interzone matters."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[590,593],"pp":"133","s":"Chief Adviser Dr. Edgar Johnson returned on 25 May from Washington after 72 conferences on budgets, supplies, Japan trade and post-transfer planning. The National Economic Board met four times on budgets, rates and grain prices. Provincial civil service officers met in Seoul; pay rises of 25 to 130 percent were approved from 1 June, tied to staff cuts. Military Government personnel were 3,003 in May."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[594,605],"pp":"137","s":"10 May general election (91% turnout, 198 seats), National Assembly opening, Pyongyang North-South conference outcome, power shut-off effects on Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, Koreans in Japan."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[594,600],"pp":"137","s":"On 10 May over 91 percent of registrants voted in Korea's first general election with little sabotage. Of 198 members, nearly half were rightists, none communist, and Hankook Democratic Party was strongest; two Cheju districts were voided. Party and occupation tables, rightist praise, moderate and Kim Koo objections that the vote was unfree, and an average age of 47 are included."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[601,602],"pp":"144","s":"Rhee Syngman said after the election that the Assembly's decisions would be state law and preferred the American presidential system. A preliminary meeting on 27 May saw 149 members elect him temporary chairman. On 31 May all 198 attended, Rhee won the speakership with 188 votes, Shin Ik-hi and Kim Tong-won became vice-speakers, and Hodge and Dean spoke at the opening."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[603,603],"pp":"146","s":"Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo returned from Pyongyang on 5 May after the 19-25 April conference. They reported a four-Kim agreement to oppose a separate southern government, demand withdrawal of US and Soviet forces, and hold a later North-South conference. Their 5 May statement said the North promised no separate government; the text breaks off in the electricity assurance."},{"t":"Power Shut-off","lv":3,"p":[604,604],"pp":"147","s":"North Korea cut off electricity to the South on 14 May, citing unpaid bills and the refusal of USAFIK, which does not recognize the North Korean People's Committee, to send a delegation. Rightists blamed the Soviets and communists blamed the United States. Kim Koo disclaimed knowledge of the contract, and some saw benefit in forcing self-sufficiency."},{"t":"Koreans in Japan","lv":3,"p":[605,605],"pp":"148","s":"Several South Korean parties had for months protested treatment of Koreans in Japan, calling it a revival of Japanese imperialism. Reports that a number of Korean schools in Japan had been closed were read as an infringement of Korean rights. Protests came from all political factions, as already noted in the April report."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[606,610],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinance No. 192 reorganized the courts, separating them from the Department of Justice from 1 June, alongside Ordinances 193-197, two USAFIK proclamations (daylight-saving time, National Assembly's first meeting). Opinions covered vested property and silk profiteering. Prison population was 22,141; Cheju trials were expedited; the Civil Code gained an air-carriers section; 49 vested-property claims were screened administratively."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[611,616],"pp":"154","s":"Violence around the 10 May election killed four candidates, 15 election officials and 32 policemen, with about 275 civilian deaths; only 68 of 13,407 booths were attacked. Fighting on Cheju-do intensified, with 3,126 held and two districts unable to vote. The section also describes police financing through contributions and the effective Pusan police under Chief Pak."},{"t":"Section 5. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[617,621],"pp":"160","s":"Thirty-one border incidents with North Korean Constabulary occurred, mostly near the Oe-dong dam, and an American civilian was wounded on 26 May. A Soviet Yak-9 landed near Yosu. About 43,000 pieces of mail were exchanged, down from 59,000 in April. Radio Pyongyang attacked the election, the Americans and the power issue."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[622,649],"pp":"165","s":"Health, education, culture, press opinion, youth and women."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[622,625],"pp":"165","s":"Repatriation of Mukden refugees began, with four ships carrying about 3,800 people through Inchon quarantine camps and a low disease rate; smallpox appeared on Cheju. Kyongsang-namdo completed 2,106 low-cost housing units, UNRRA grants aided orphanages and training schools, and nursing, veterinary, pharmaceutical, narcotics and laboratory matters were reported."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[626,632],"pp":"169","s":"Three ordinances were drafted creating school districts, Boards of Education and a household-income-tax basis for school finance, replacing land-value taxes eroded by inflation. The FY 1948/49 budget was 1.5 billion won. Unified teacher certification starts 15 June; 845 teachers will attend summer sessions. Bureau reports covered textbooks (13.9 million printed), tests, language institute and vocational education."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[633,636],"pp":"176","s":"Thirty-organization election campaign teams gave 803 performances to 1,334,464 people from 7 April. Music news covered the first Musical Festival, Beethoven concerts and scholarships for three Korean musicians. The Seoul U.S. Information Center opened 29 April; art exhibits, Children's Day, election stamps issued 10 May, and KBS election coverage were also reported."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[637,641],"pp":"180","s":"The press debated the Pyongyang Conference and the broken power promise, then covered the 10 May election. A police report listed 202 dead and 643 wounded from 31 March to 17 May; moderate and rightist papers praised the election, while the Country Guard Corps was ordered disbanded on 22 May. Rhee's press conference, power cutoff and fear of Japan also drew comment."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[642,644],"pp":"185","s":"Thirty-three KNY members were elected to the National Assembly, mostly as rightist-backed candidates or independents, far outnumbering Dai Dong's 13. Audited membership reached 859,208 (605,316 men, 109,899 women, 93,910 maritime, 50,083 students), with a fifth screening ordered. The FY 1948/49 allocation was 20,600,000 won; May projects included literacy classes and road repair."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[645,649],"pp":"188","s":"About 3,000,000 women voted on 10 May, roughly half of 7,908,892 registered voters (3,903,293 women); 19 of 594 candidates were women and none was elected. The Women's Bureau ran a Mothers' School, and the Korean women's federation was admitted to the American federation. Volunteer service, Junior Red Cross gift boxes, a YWCA fashion show and Ewha University's curriculum followed."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[650,655],"pp":"","s":"Back matter consists of blank pages, a library date-due slip (stamped June 2010) with a barcode number, and a closing source note giving the UCSF Industry Documents Library URL. It contains no report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-33","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-33-1948-06","s":"J","no":33,"d":"1948-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 33 (June 1948)","v":"","pg":170,"o":"Summation No. 33 covers non-military Occupation activities in Japan for June 1948 (data mostly for April-May). Politically the month was dominated by the Nishio political-funds scandal, the Diet budget deadlock, and the Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee's probes of donations and hoarded military supplies, along with rifts in the Democratic Party. The 28 June Fukui earthquake devastated Fukui city and threatened textile rehabilitation. Economic sections report postwar peaks in coke, vacuum tubes, watches and silk fabric, a growing coal output, and new trade machinery: Boeki Cho's direct dealing authority, a $60 million raw-cotton credit and a sterling payment agreement. Labor reports union membership (6.55 million), left-right struggle and wage standards. Social sections cover new pharmaceutical and narcotics laws, repatriation, the Diet's rescinding of the Imperial Rescript on Education, and information programs. Historians will find detailed statistics, war crimes trial figures, land reform totals, and party and Diet legislative detail.","h":["House of Representatives rejected nonconfidence in Deputy Prime Minister Nishio 209 to 178 over unreported political funds.","Earthquake of 28 June destroyed about 70 percent of Fukui city; 3,195 died there and 9,858 were injured.","Diet enacted the National Property Law 28 June governing acquisition, management and disposal of all public property.","House of Councillors resolved 18 June that the 1890 Imperial Rescript on Education is invalid.","Board of Trade authorized 28 May to deal directly with foreign firms; $60,000,000 raw-cotton credit signed 6 June.","Cabinet approved a Broadcast Bill 18 June creating a Broadcasting Commission and non-profit Broadcasting Corporation of Japan.","By 20 June 673 war crimes suspects tried: 623 convicted, 95 death sentences; 1,136 interned at Sugamo.","Labor unions numbered 31,075 with 6,553,816 members; government wage standard raised to 3,791 yen."],"k":["Nishio scandal","budget deadlock","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","Fukui earthquake","National Property Law","war crimes trials","land reform","coal production","Broadcast Bill","labor unions","Boeki Cho","sterling payment agreement","Imperial Rescript on Education","Ashida cabinet","Pharmaceutical Law","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,6],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages and tables of contents for Summation No. 33 (June 1948): the four-part outline (General, Political, Economic, Social), a list of 80 charts, maps and graphs by topic with page numbers, and a topographic map of Japan listed at the end."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[7,17],"pp":"1","s":"Condensed digest of the month's political, economic and social developments."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[8,8],"pp":"3","s":"A single sentence reports that there was no change in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during June 1948. The page also opens Section 2 and carries its contents list."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"3","s":"Part I digest of political events: the Nishio political-funds charge, a 209 to 178 nonconfidence vote, budget delays and a 30.1 billion yen revision, Ashida on Zaibatsu, the National Property Law, Democratic Party defections, the 28 June Fukui earthquake (3,195 dead), Korean school disorders, judges' pay laws and war crimes figures (Sugamo 1,136)."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,14],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of May and April economic data. Land sales reached 101,498 cho; coal output rose to 2,788,500 tons and coke hit a postwar peak; textiles, motor vehicles, power and gas were reported. Also covered are the Broadcasting Commission bill, union membership of 6.55 million, a 3,700 yen wage level, trade contracts and a 200 million dollar four-month deficit. Finance, reparations and science follow."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,17],"pp":"17","s":"Social digest: first model health center at Morioka, new Pharmaceutical, Narcotics and Marihuana laws, vital statistics and 59,196 repatriates. Education items include the 18 June resolution nullifying the Imperial Rescript on Education, boards of education and 124.9 million textbooks. Also Cardinal Spellman's visit, shrine land transfers, opinion surveys, press comment, book rights, film and music."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[17,43],"pp":"21","s":"Government and politics, public safety, legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[18,35],"pp":"23","s":"Nishio scandal, budget controversy, illegal transactions probes, National Property Law, local finance, Diet bills and party developments."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"23","s":"The antiadministration alliance formally resolved to overthrow the Ashida cabinet, while the Nishio donation case and budget disagreements dominated politics. At month's end the government parties united to reject the opposition's nonconfidence vote against Nishio and moved gradually toward agreement on the main budget issues. The page introduces the subsections that follow."},{"t":"Nishio Controversy","lv":3,"p":[18,19],"pp":"23","s":"Deputy Prime Minister Nishio's failure to report 500,000 yen in political funds threatened the cabinet and split the Social Democrats, whose left wing demanded his ouster. The 24 June nonconfidence motion lost 209 to 178, with 12 left-wingers absent or abstaining. Attorney General Suzuki said Nishio would resign after the budget passed and then be prosecuted."},{"t":"Budget Controversy","lv":3,"p":[19,23],"pp":"25","s":"The budget stayed deadlocked until a 30 June compromise. Proposals included a 330 percent rail fare rise, war bond interest deferral on 78.1 billion yen and a transactions tax; Kitamura addressed the Diet 4 June. The 399 billion yen General Account was presented 8 June. Final terms set fares at 255 percent, changed 30.1 billion yen, and a 3,791 yen wage base."},{"t":"Zaibatsu Dissolution and Foreign Capital","lv":3,"p":[23,23],"pp":"","s":"Prime Minister Ashida told the Diet on 22 June that Zaibatsu dissolution was virtually complete: leaders replaced, stocks in the Holding Company Liquidation Commission's hands and purgees restricted. Economic Stabilization Board official Horigoshi said little private foreign capital could be expected soon, citing investor caution, occupation status, taxes, labor unrest and undetermined exchange rates."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions","lv":3,"p":[23,28],"pp":"","s":"Procurators uncovered hoarded military goods (Nakajima, Tohoku Metals, Fuji Sanpro) and indicted metal-company officials. The Diet committee probed Tsuji and Kamei funds, Nishio's 500,000 yen and his disbursements, Democratic Party funds via Chizaki, the Gunma coffee case (governor resigned), the Sasebo company and Finance Minister Kitamura, and Ono's perjury case."},{"t":"Government Activities","lv":3,"p":[28,30],"pp":"","s":"The National Property Law, passed 28 June, creates five classes of public property, a classification commission and Diet control over Imperial and park property. Three local finance measures seek 199.4 billion yen with new local taxes, though loan sums are disputed. Also reported are 34 prefectural conferences and the planned closing of many national local offices."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[30,32],"pp":"","s":"Diet bills introduced 26 May-25 June are tabulated by ministry and sponsor, from Diet-member bills to Government bills on agriculture, finance, welfare and construction. The enacted list includes the political contributions bill (19 June). Both houses reaffirmed the invalidity of the 1890 Imperial Rescript on Education (18-19 June); Democrat Takahashi and Social Democrat Fujieda won by-elections."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[33,35],"pp":"","s":"Eight Democratic Diet members announced secession on 6 June over war bond interest deferment, and Secretary General Kimura offered to resign before staying on; Harahashi became adviser. Social Democrats approved a plan to nationalize electric power; the People's Cooperative Party offered ten budget revisions; Democratic Liberals launched an anti-Ashida campaign; the Ryokufu-kai moved toward becoming a party."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[36,39],"pp":"59","s":"The 28 June Fukui earthquake destroyed about 70 percent of Fukui city and killed 3,195 there, with relief by police, prisons, fire officials and tugboats. A dynamite blast at the Mitsubishi-Katsuda mine killed 62. Also covered are rail accidents, hoarded goods recovered, 196,951 April offenses, police strength, a prison population of 92,607, and April fires costing ¥950 million."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[40,43],"pp":"67","s":"The Diet passed the administrative litigation law and pay increases for judges and procurators on 25 June, and the Supreme Court set expedited rules for purge-violation cases. Atrocity investigations on hand fell to 796. Sugamo held 1,136 internees; 673 suspects had been tried by 20 June, 623 convicted and 95 sentenced to death. The Tokyo tribunal stayed adjourned since 16 April."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,149],"pp":"73","s":"Thirteen sections on production, trade, prices, finance and science."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[44,52],"pp":"75","s":"Tenants bought 101,498 cho in May (632,632 cho cumulative); reclamation lands 567,873 cho; land use, cooperatives, insurance, livestock slaughter, fisheries, equipment and whaling."},{"t":"Agrarian Reform","lv":3,"p":[44,45],"pp":"","s":"Tenant farmers bought 101,498 cho in May, bringing cumulative sales under the agrarian reform to 632,632 cho. The Finance Ministry stopped accepting land for taxes on 31 March, paying in land bonds instead; 200,383 cho had been transferred to the Agriculture Ministry. Reclamation holdings reached 567,873 cho, and land commissions took over reclaimed-land sales. Demonstration farms of 2,767 cho were to be sold."},{"t":"Arable Land by Prefectures","lv":3,"p":[45,49],"pp":"76","s":"Arable land was 12.49 percent of Japan Proper's 36,048,047 hectares at 31 August 1947, and Hokkaido only 9.40 percent. Saga had the highest share of irrigated rice land, 20.28 percent. The farm population of 36,509,458 on 4,970,023 hectares averaged 7.345 persons per hectare. Pages 46-49 are prefectural charts and maps with no text."},{"t":"Livestock, Fisheries and Whaling","lv":3,"p":[50,52],"pp":"88","s":"Agricultural cooperative federations were allowed to organize, with 75,473 directors and auditors elected by 5,470 cooperatives. Crop insurance paid out for 1947 damages, and 3,674 mutual relief organizations were registered. April slaughter fell to 4,064 tons; marine output nearly doubled to 222,509 tons. Fishing received sisal, abaca, cement and petroleum allocations. Antarctic whaling was authorized 21 June; 104 whales were taken."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[53,58],"pp":"93","s":"May log production was 60,355,400 cubic feet, with stockpiles down to 245,988,400; forests were overcut, especially coniferous saw timber. Charcoal and gasumaki rose. Coal output in June reached 2,788,500 tons, below quota, with May deliveries at 2,756,000 tons. Crude oil output was 15,399 kiloliters, with one new Niigata gas well; 13 of 33 minerals rose in April."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[59,69],"pp":"105","s":"Coke output reached a postwar peak of 254,819 tons in May. Rolled steel rose 5.6 percent while pig iron and steel ingots fell; aluminum, copper, zinc and ferroalloys rose. Ammonium sulfate hit a peak of 104,765 tons. Cement fell to 141,883 tons. Shipyards completed 21 steel vessels, and tugs and barges went to Russia. Rolling stock, machine tools, bearings and textile machinery are reported."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[70,78],"pp":"127","s":"Food processing was hit by depleted grain stocks, with wheat flour down sharply in April, while edible oils and fish rose. Beer rose 20 percent; tobacco, pulp and glass are covered. Electrical goods, rubber, leather and agricultural implements are reported. Standard trucks reached 1,211 and small trucks a postwar peak of 243. Typewriters rose 361 percent; watches and clocks hit a postwar peak of 197,039."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[79,94],"pp":"145","s":"Fukui earthquake threatens rayon rehabilitation; raw silk 10,536 bales; cotton yarn held down while fabrics rise; rayon, wool, bast fibers, rope, garments and dyeing."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[79,82],"pp":"146","s":"Raw cotton stocks fell as May imports dropped 3,347,000 pounds; Edward T. Robertson and Son was licensed as cotton controllers. Pure yarn output held near April at 23.9 million pounds, with spinners' stocks under a month's supply. Fabric output rose as independent weavers gained 4,678,000 square yards; allocation tickets were recalled from 36 plants, and operating looms reached 105,277."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[82,85],"pp":"151","s":"Raw silk output was 10,536 bales in May, with 20/22 denier rising to 6,155 bales; 11,856 bales were tested for export. Distributor stocks fell to 11,537 bales' worth of deliveries, one reeling mill closed, and the spring cocoon crop was estimated at 77,379,000 pounds. Silk fabric reached a postwar record of 10,631,000 square yards of filament; an 8,700,000-yard weaving program was announced."},{"t":"Artificial Fibers","lv":3,"p":[85,87],"pp":"159","s":"Rayon staple and filament output set postwar records, up 589,000 and 380,000 pounds over April, while spun yarn dipped slightly. Imports included 1,700 tons of rayon pulp and the first nylon waste shipped to Japan; Kurashiki began commercial polyvinyl alcohol fiber (300 pounds). Filament fabric reached 3,368,000 square yards and spun rayon fabric 4,756,000; mills consolidated spindles and looms."},{"t":"Wool","lv":3,"p":[87,89],"pp":"162","s":"May wool imports totaled 3,183 bales, but little of the 9,605 bales received since 1 April reached mills because of inspection and distribution delays. Woolen and worsted yarn output edged up on continuing imports, and fabric production rose slightly despite reduced yarn supplies, with woolen fabric at 1,571,000 square yards. Operating worsted spindles fell as mills shifted to pure yarns, and looms were consolidated."},{"t":"Hard and Bast Fibers, Garments and Finishing","lv":3,"p":[89,94],"pp":"","s":"The seventh postwar abaca shipment, 6,558 bales, arrived, lifting mill receipts of hard and bast fibers 4,112,000 pounds. Flax yarn rose 10 percent, but ramie, hemp and jute fell after a strike at the largest ramie and hemp mill. Throstle-spun materials had only about 10 days' stocks. Garment output fell except workmen's gloves; fish netting dropped as spinners withheld yarn awaiting price rises; cotton printing set a postwar record."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[95,101],"pp":"177","s":"Registered motor vehicles numbered 209,002 in May, 67.3 percent operable, with freight tonnage up to 13,732,047. Railways carried 565,601,143 passengers and government rail freight reached 10,419,900 metric tons. Steel-ship coastal cargo rose 11.4 percent to 1,129,958 tons; three foreign shipping agents were licensed. Power generation was 2,974,000,000 kWh, with coal shortfalls worrying the dry season; gas output was 64.2 million cubic meters."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[102,110],"pp":"191","s":"The Cabinet approved a Broadcast Bill on 18 June creating an independent Broadcasting Commission and a nonprofit Broadcasting Corporation. Telephones reached 1,322,017; new radiotelephone links opened with Trinidad, Barbados, Italy and Kobe. Radio licensees numbered 6,650,046, and BCJ sought a higher listening fee. Postal savings, life insurance, mailbag and equipment figures were reported, including 988,505 vacuum tubes and 433,279 ministry employees."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[111,119],"pp":"209","s":"Right-left struggle in unions, Labor-Farmer Liaison Council, 31,075 unions with 6.55 million members, disputes, labor relations committees, wages, employment and labor education."},{"t":"Labor and Employer Organizations","lv":3,"p":[111,114],"pp":"","s":"Left-right struggle sharpened in unions. The Labor-Farmer Liaison Council (9 June) and Tokyo Council of Independent Labor Unions (12 June, 125,000 members) were founded, and the NCIU Democratization League held its first convention. Government Railway Workers' Union elections shifted left; electric power, teachers and women communications workers met. Employers backed managerial rights. SCAP did not object to ILO observers; unions numbered 31,075 with 6,553,816 members."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[114,117],"pp":"","s":"June labor relations stayed calm as grievance machinery was debated. Coal unions ratified it; Tokyo-Yokohama rail locals struck for 24 hours; the CLRC set a base wage of ¥3,923 for private railways and settled the All-Japan Express dispute at ¥4,854. Aikado Printing union leaders were convicted over production control, Toho Studio cut 1,019 jobs, and April saw 79 stoppage disputes. Labor education and publications were also reported."},{"t":"Wages, Working Conditions and Employment","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"","s":"Government wage stabilization set ¥3,700 as the base for 23 June price revisions, while government workers sought ¥5,200 and private settlements ran higher, such as ¥6,300 at All-Japan Iron and Steel. April manufacturing wages rose 14 percent. Labor standards inspections numbered 9,955, with 19,019 violations. Illegal labor supply projects, coal mine employment freeze, prison labor in Hokkaido and unemployment insurance were addressed."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[120,126],"pp":"227","s":"Boeki Cho was authorized 28 May to deal directly with foreign firms, subject to SCAP validation. A $60 million bank credit for U.S. cotton, a 31 May sterling payment agreement and a 9 June China trade agreement were concluded. Limited tourism resumed 1 July. April imports were $60.3 million against $12.0 million of exports; the four-month deficit reached $200,186,092."},{"t":"Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[127,132],"pp":"241","s":"Full ration needs were met from rice stocks plus imported sugar substituted for five days, though consumers complained. Collections of 1947 staple crops met or exceeded quotas by 30 June. The Cabinet approved fish and vegetable control plans 1 June, and milk, vegetable and grain prices rose. Tokyo water rates doubled, consumer prices climbed, incentive goods reached rice farmers, and 12,500 ration \"ghosts\" were caught."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[133,140],"pp":"253","s":"Licenses of nine foreign banks were broadened 14 June. Bank of Japan notes stood at 230.6 billion yen on 30 June. Reconstruction Finance Bank loans rose to 70.4 billion yen, mainly to mining and power. Fifty-one control organizations were closed. Tax collections reached 147.2 billion yen (108.7 percent of estimate); interim June budgets passed 28 May; national debt totaled 365.6 billion yen."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[140,144],"pp":"267","s":"Looted property taken into custody, mainly 39.5 million yen of cinchona bark from the Netherlands East Indies, and 246 vessels were deleted from looted lists. The SCAP Custody Account balance reached 284,957,133 yen, and foreign properties were restored. SCAP raised no objection to 775 restricted-concern applications; antitrust orders went to holding companies, and 14,122 machine tools had shipped to claimants by 30 June."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[144,149],"pp":"275","s":"The Cabinet approved the Science Council Bill 8 June. The Japan Invention Association publicized inventions, and learned societies met. Japan sent astronomy papers to the International Astronomical Union, and Professor Honda discovered a new comet 3 June. Osaka's research institute reported an ephedrine pilot plant; scientific publications were issued; 1,327 Japanese Standards were in effect and patent registrations totaled 123."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[149,169],"pp":"285","s":"Public health and welfare; education, religion, cultural resources and media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[150,159],"pp":"287","s":"Communicable diseases rise to 4,399 cases and 420 deaths in May, veterinary affairs, medical supplies, narcotics laws, public assistance, vital statistics and repatriation of 59,196."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[150,153],"pp":"","s":"A model health center opened at Morioka 15 June. The House of Councillors passed the Preventive Vaccination Law and Venereal Disease Prevention Law. Eleven principal communicable diseases caused 4,399 cases and 430 deaths in May, up from 3,337 and 338. Veterinary items included fowl cholera in Akita, April meat inspection with rising condemnations, and dairy inspection of 8,588 farms."},{"t":"Medical Administration and Supply","lv":3,"p":[153,156],"pp":"","s":"The Pharmaceutical Affairs Law passed 30 June, requiring accredited pharmacists and setting penalties up to three years' imprisonment. Japan had 3,451 hospitals with 310,562 beds. Nursing courses were held for public health nurses and midwives. May medical supply output was 1.94 billion yen, with record sanitary materials; distribution fell. Narcotics laws passed 28 June; 82,324 handlers were registered."},{"t":"Public Assistance, Vital Statistics and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[156,159],"pp":"","s":"May public assistance reached 1,911,666 persons, with welfare payments of 377 million yen. A Tokyo institution for the handicapped opened, LARA shipments arrived, and the Welfare Commissioner Law passed. Mennonite service centers and Red Cross campaign plans were approved. National Health Insurance was amended. April births were 324,393 and deaths 79,756; 59,198 persons were repatriated, mostly from Soviet areas."},{"t":"Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[160,169],"pp":"307","s":"Rescript on Education invalidated, 1948 education budget of 26.7 billion yen, boards of education, textbooks, Cardinal Spellman visit, public opinion surveys, press, radio, film, theater, music and libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[160,163],"pp":"307","s":"The House of Councillors reaffirmed on 18 June that the 1890 Imperial Rescript on Education is invalid. The Ministry of Education budget went to the Diet with a school-building allotment of 5,381,320,000 yen, and a bill for boards of education in all communities over 10,000 was submitted. Teacher-training, social-science, adult, vocational and library programs, textbook authorization (687 manuscripts announced) and standardization of 1,850 characters are covered."},{"t":"Religion and Cultural Resources","lv":3,"p":[163,164],"pp":"","s":"Cardinal Spellman and 14 Catholic officials visited Tokyo 7 June, and clearances were approved for 46 religious workers entering Japan. Tenri, Aizen-en and Seicho no Ie reported programs and membership figures. The Ministry of Finance closed applications for transfer of state precincts (72,000 shrines, 30,000 temples), and repairs restored 28 percent of Christian churches, 16 percent of temples and 10 percent of shrines. Six Western-painting exhibitions were held, and the Government ended subsidies for contemporary art shows."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[164,169],"pp":"","s":"Public opinion work expanded with a 19-newspaper Newspaper Public Opinion League, sampling conferences, a radio listener survey and polls on land resettlement, daylight saving and police. Information programs covered rationing, coal, labor, savings, housing, civil liberties, health, farming and women. Press sections report 821 new book editions in May, 98 licensed foreign books, the first press-code prosecution (Nikkan Sports), and Tokyo and prefectural comment on the Nishio fund affair. Radio, film, theater, music, exhibits and two new SCAP libraries (Kanazawa, Kobe) close the section."},{"t":"Map of Japan - Topography","lv":1,"p":[170,170],"pp":"326","s":"Final page of the report: a topographic map of Japan, with the back cover and a printing-plant mark. It contains no narrative text, figures or developments for the month."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":33,"d":"1948-06","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 33 (June 1948)","v":"","pg":591,"o":"This file is a bound Google-digitized volume holding three consecutive economic and political reports, not only No. 33. No. 33 (June 1948) covers the monsoon-quiet month after the Cheju and May disorders, the first full month without North Korean electricity (cut 14 May), collapsing industrial output, the new weighted wholesale price index (12-fold rise since 1945), summer grain and land sales, and the new National Assembly drafting the Constitution. No. 34 (July-August 1948) is the final SKIG report, covering passage of the Constitution, Rhee's election, the 15 August proclamation of the Republic, the end of USAMGIK, with three-year retrospectives on population, vested property, labor, utilities, public works, police, press, youth, women and culture. No. 35 (September-October 1948) is the first economic-only summation under the Republic, with transfer of offices to Korean ministries, rice collection and the ration-roll 'ghost population'. Historians get dense statistics on prices, trade, power, coal, finance and land reform.","h":["June 1948 was the first full month without North Korean electric power; flour, paper, cotton and cement output fell sharply versus April.","First weighted index shows South Korean wholesale prices rose about 12-fold between August 1945 and mid-1948.","National Assembly passed its Rules 153-1 on 10 June and began reading the draft Constitution on 23 June.","On 8 June US planes bombed Dok Do fishermen, provoking a wave of Korean protest from all factions.","Constitution passed 12 July; Rhee Syngman elected President 20 July (180 votes); Republic of Korea proclaimed 15 August 1948.","By end of July 85 percent of Japanese-owned farmland, 481,957 acres, had been sold to tenants via National Land Administration.","SKIG closed with an overdraft above 22,000 million won; Property Custodian administered about 85,000 million won of vested property.","No. 35 reports summer grain collection of 107,766 tons and padded ration rolls: about 500,000 'ghost' recipients in Seoul."],"k":["USAMGIK","South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","North Korean power cutoff","wholesale price index","vested property","land sales","National Assembly","Constitution","Republic of Korea","Cheju-do rebellion","civilian supply imports","Korean National Youth","rice collection","Dok Do bombing"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter (Google Books notice)","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Google Books digitization notice, a library-stamp image and pages marked only \"Digitized by Google\", with a stub page noting that a table provides the information in English. No report content appears in these five pages."},{"t":"No. 33 (June 1948): Front matter","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 33, June 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, with a map of Korea, 1 July 1948 organization charts of USAMGIK and SKIG, the 30 June Board roster (Acting Chairman C. H. Donnelly), and a table of contents covering five parts."},{"t":"No. 33: Summary","lv":1,"p":[16,19],"pp":"1","s":"June was relatively quiet as monsoon rains suppressed Cheju and southern disorders, but the cutoff of North Korean power sharply cut output (flour down 33 percent, paper 57, cement 100 from April). The Assembly drafted the Constitution, wholesale prices stood 12-fold above 1945, over 15,000 refugees crossed the parallel, and housing and teacher schools were planned."},{"t":"No. 33, Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[20,31],"pp":"5","s":"Population movement, Koreans in Japan, and vested property."},{"t":"Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[20,22],"pp":"5","s":"More than 15,000 refugees crossed the 38th parallel in June despite Soviet orders to turn farmers back; tables give monthly refugee counts and cumulative movement (886,385 outbound, 2,424,175 inbound). In Japan, a new Korean trade association formed, and seven Kobe and Osaka school-closure rioters received prison terms from a Hyogo military court."},{"t":"Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[23,31],"pp":"8","s":"The Office of Property Custody collected 3,370 financial statements from vested enterprises, processed 69,223,437 won in capital requests, and surveyed 3,072 former Japanese corporations. It took over the Korean Residence Management Corporation, shifted delinquent rent collection to the Comptroller on 15 June, released USAFIK properties, and decided 141 title claims."},{"t":"No. 33, Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[32,111],"pp":"17","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocations, trade and labor."},{"t":"Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[32,41],"pp":"17","s":"Final summer grain estimates showed 1,889,606 acres planted, equal to 1947 but with about 15 percent drought loss, while early rains moved rice transplanting two weeks ahead. Cotton, silk cocoon credit of 1,170,368,400 won, fertilizer handled by the Korea Agricultural Association, 494,699 farms sold by NLA (84 percent), and a plan to halve experiment stations also appear."},{"t":"Prices","lv":2,"p":[42,65],"pp":"27","s":"June wholesale and retail prices rose, rice about 25 percent over May; controlled prices listed; supplement introduces first weighted wholesale price index."},{"t":"Monthly wholesale, retail and controlled prices","lv":3,"p":[42,45],"pp":"27","s":"Seoul retail prices rose in June, led by rice and red beans (cleaned rice 1,850 won per mal against 1,460 in May), while fish and vegetables eased. Tables give relative wholesale prices of eight commodities, relative retail prices of 26 goods, nine-city open-market prices, and the National Price Administration's controlled prices; a new weighted index is introduced."},{"t":"Index Numbers of Wholesale Prices in South Korea, August 1945 to July 1948","lv":3,"p":[46,65],"pp":"30","s":"Arthur G. Peterson's first weighted wholesale index (1947=100, 34 commodities) shows prices up twelve-fold since August 1945, reaching 171 in July 1948. It reviews Japanese and Military Government price policy, justifies the 1947 base, explains data, grouping and weights, and appends commodity-by-commodity weight estimates with monthly tables for each group."},{"t":"Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[66,73],"pp":"51","s":"The power cutoff from the north, in force all of June, held down output in most industries. Flour rose to 1,653 tons from 1,023, but noodles fell 24.5 percent, cotton yarn about 28 percent and cloth 27.5 percent. Paper rose 12 percent, briquettes and coke fell, cement stood idle, and carbide stopped. Government salt fell to 19,911 tons; cut tobacco hit a postwar high of 729.4 tons."},{"t":"Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[74,82],"pp":"59","s":"Coal supply was squeezed by the power shortage and a late import schedule. Anthracite output fell to 54,954 tons in May from 68,612 in April, while shipments to consumers reached a postwar high of 63,000 tons. Bituminous imports from Japan fell to 45,729 tons in June, 25 percent below May. About 4,600 tons of U.S. pitch arrived; gold stayed unprofitable at the fixed price, silver output hit an occupation high, and tungsten shipments continued."},{"t":"Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[83,92],"pp":"68","s":"The National Consumer Goods Office planned 500 million won of goods as incentives for the summer grain collection, with a province-by-province schedule. The Consumer Goods Allocation Board approved 118.9 million won of civilian supplies and 458.2 million won of Korean-produced goods for distribution through the Federation of Financial Associations. Also covered: June petroleum allocations, 6,360 tons of salt distributed, and a 1,500 million won credit for the Federation."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[93,105],"pp":"78","s":"Private trade kept growing; June licenses showed exports of about 323 million won against imports of 368 million. Officials Oh Chung-soo and John Thorn attended the ECAFE session in India and revived Hong Kong demand for agar agar, tungsten and ginseng. Government sales included 4,000 tons of zinc concentrate to the U.S. and record graphite shipments to Japan. The FLC credit expired 30 June, with $2,139,775 committed. Civilian-supply imports covered coal, fertilizer, flour and sugar."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[106,111],"pp":"91","s":"The Pusan stevedore dispute eased: contractors were cut from 24 to eight, the tonnage split moved from 80-20 to 70-30, and a hiring hall replaced the honcho system on 27 June. Inchon stevedores were investigated and an election was expected. Collective bargaining continued at Seoul Electric and the Marine Bureau. Plans were made for six labor-management councils. Child labor violations were found in 94 percent of Seoul firms inspected, plus a provincial bureau chiefs' conference and labor education broadcasts."},{"t":"No. 33, Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[112,139],"pp":"97","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Transportation","lv":2,"p":[112,118],"pp":"97","s":"Department of Transportation revenue in June was about 14 percent below May but 37 percent above the fiscal-year monthly average, while expenditures rose to 554,289,000 won after postponed bills. Railroads carried 3,069,000 passengers in June against 4,298,000 in May, and about 440,564 tons of freight. Operable locomotives stayed at 247 of 656, though repairs rose. Coal shipped by water from Mukko totaled 21,564 tons to Inchon and 13,801 to Pusan."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[119,124],"pp":"104","s":"Department of Communications revenue fell in May but rose nearly 20 million won in June, led by telephone income. Expenditures climbed as postponed bills were paid. Staff fell for a fifth straight month under a Military Governor reduction-in-force order. Postal savings deposits stood at 846 million won in June; insurance policies fell 38.5 percent on lapses. Telegraph revenue rose and a Seoul-Inchon telephone circuit was built for the power ship Electra."},{"t":"Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[125,130],"pp":"110","s":"Only 41 million KWH reached consumers in June, the first full month without northern power, down 15 million from May. The Emergency Power Board kept its priority system; U.S. Army engineers began investigating on 28 June. O. Sauter's report reviewed the history of the northern supply and the unpaid Soviet account. Plant output recovered, with the Chung Pyung transformer still out; 136 Seoul streetcars ran."},{"t":"Public Works","lv":2,"p":[131,139],"pp":"116","s":"With FY 1948/49 funds released, first-quarter road subsidies of 50,222 thousand won (of 201,888 total) went to provinces, and 11 national bridge projects stood at 30 June. The Waegwan Bridge deck began 19 June, the Masan-Chinhae tunnel opened, and the volunteer road program (over 8,000 miles) closed. The Korean Waterworks Association was founded, 133 fires cost 353 million won, and 1:50,000 map plates were duplicated."},{"t":"No. 33, Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[140,155],"pp":"125","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[140,146],"pp":"125","s":"Bank of Chosun notes rose from 28,200 million won in April to 30,000 million at end-June, tied to fishing and irrigation credits. Cumulative SKIG overdraft stood at 21,268 million won, while FY 1947/48 authorizations reached 22,320 million. May revenue was 1,736 million against expenditures of 1,644 million; Civilian Supply net assets were 25,702 million won, with 97 million won in supplemental appropriations approved."},{"t":"Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[147,155],"pp":"132","s":"May deposits reached a record 58,280 million won, with government balances at 34,315 million. Loans outstanding fell 696 million to 29,328 million, the fifth straight net retirement. Life insurance in force rose to 952.7 million won. In June the Finance Department approved 72 loans totaling 2,461 million won; Yun Ho-byung became Bank of Chosun governor, and Korean bank claims on Japan totaled 15,271 million."},{"t":"No. 33, Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[156,181],"pp":"141","s":"Administration, politics, legal affairs, police and inter-zone matters."},{"t":"Administration","lv":2,"p":[156,158],"pp":"141","s":"The Korea Civil Service processed 1,719 appointments, 1,290 promotions and 1,979 resignations in June. The National Economic Board met five times: it approved Transportation's 7,644 million won budget and a 44 million won Monopoly Bureau power supplement, tabled a 1,200 won gold price proposal, and raised postal rates. USAMGIK personnel totaled 3,178 in June."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[159,167],"pp":"144","s":"The new National Assembly adopted its rules 153 to 1, formed nine standing committees and a Constitution drafting committee, and passed a letter to North Korea 106 to 20. First reading of the Constitution ended 30 June amid dispute over presidential powers and unicameralism. Clubs formed (Hankook Democratic 83, Independent 63, March First 51); Jaisohn, Dok Do bombing and fear of Japan stirred politics."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[168,173],"pp":"153","s":"Executive Order 22 indefinitely postponed the Cheju-do by-election, and Order 23 created an Election Review Board; ordinances revised land and local taxes. Legal opinions held South Korea Labor Party membership not itself a crime and addressed consulate land and refugee cargo duty. The 19 prisons held 22,331 prisoners; Chonju clerks were arrested for leftism and Cheju trials were assisted."},{"t":"National Police","lv":2,"p":[174,176],"pp":"159","s":"June was far quieter than May: no strikes or sabotage, and two policemen were killed against over 30 in May. Arrests were mainly of suspected leftists, including 60 officials each at Taegu and Taejon. On Cheju-do the constabulary commander was murdered 18 June, about 3,000 rioters were reported arrested, the Northwest Young Men's Association was ordered out, and a police dispute over leftist policy arose."},{"t":"Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[177,181],"pp":"162","s":"Over 41,500 pieces of mail were exchanged at Tosong in four weekly exchanges, 65 percent southbound. Radio Pyongyang denounced the South Korean election as invalid, attacked nonpayment of the power bill and the Dok Do bombing and Cheju fighting, and welcomed two defecting Coast Guard cutters. It also praised Soviet troop reduction, announced North Korean price cuts on 280 items, and cited literacy gains."},{"t":"No. 33, Part VI: Social (printed as Part IV)","lv":1,"p":[182,209],"pp":"167","s":"Health, education, press, youth and women."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[182,187],"pp":"167","s":"A three-month public health nursing course began with 13 nurses, and about 100 nurses were recruited for the first Constabulary Nurses Corps. Vaccine production continued despite power shortages. About 6,100 Mukden refugees were processed at Inchon, with over 4,700 released. Welfare plans addressed 38,816 homeless families with 5,476 housing units on 100 million won, LARA flour distribution, and a UNRRA grant of 3.7 million won."},{"t":"Education","lv":2,"p":[188,192],"pp":"173","s":"Plans were completed for normal-school summer sessions (26 July to 21 August) to train about 7,520 teachers in 18 localities with 3,384,000 won in subsidies. Adult educators reported literacy rising from 20 to 70 percent. Textbook distribution since 1945 totaled 14,567,626; American Language Institute enrollment was 599; the Inchon Technical School got a 2,000,000 won program; Seoul National University received a 98 million won budget."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[193,197],"pp":"178","s":"June 1948 press coverage centered on the National Assembly's opening and the 23 June draft constitution (one or two houses, president or prime minister), with a defeated proposal to abolish torture. Papers reacted to false reports of Japanese officials in Korea, which Hodge and Dean denied, to the 8 June Dok Do bombing, to the power cutoff, and to a police-press clash over bribery charges."},{"t":"Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[198,201],"pp":"183","s":"Many of the hundred-odd youth groups began disintegrating after the May election, while government-sponsored Korean National Youth grew to 873,310 members, a June gain of only 14,087 because of tighter screening. It trained 10,573 reserve leaders, moved its national school to Oo-E-Dong, and ran community projects, 981 literacy classes, 159 speakers' bureaus and job placements."},{"t":"Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[202,209],"pp":"187","s":"Covers Korean women's dress as a marker of age and status, and women in medicine: 300 of 3,250 registered doctors, 36 of 865 specialists, Women's Medical University (404 students, certified as a college in June), and nurse numbers. Also the Nurses' Association (1,000 members), the Women's Bureau, Girl Scouts, 4-H girls, KNY women (114,370), and a home economics radio program. Pages 207-209 are blank."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-34-in-K-33","K-35-in-K-33"]},{"id":"J-34","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-34-1948-07","s":"J","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 34 (July 1948)","v":"","pg":183,"o":"Summation No. 34 covers July 1948 and the Ashida coalition's last weeks. The central political event is MacArthur's 22 July letter on National Public Service Law revision, followed by a 31 July cabinet order banning strikes and collective bargaining by government employees. The Diet's 209-day session ended 5 July after passing the budget and a wave of legal reforms: Habeas Corpus, criminal procedure, Reformatory and Juvenile laws, health and medical practice laws, boards of education, and new tax and local finance laws. Party politics show the Social Democratic split, the New Liberal Party, the Nishio indictment and graft inquiries. Economically the report tracks record coke output, the Fukui earthquake (28 June) damage to silk and rayon weaving, price stabilization, rate increases, financial liquidation, trade agreements, nutrition surveys and repatriation. Historians will find detailed statistical tables, legal chronology, labor and press reaction, and war crimes trial results.","h":["MacArthur's 22 July letter to Ashida: government employees may not strike; cabinet order of 31 July bans strikes and bargaining.","Second Diet session closed 5 July after 209 days, enacting 230 of 273 bills.","Deputy Prime Minister Nishio resigned and was indicted for unreported political funds and perjury.","Supreme Court used constitutional review for the first time, excluding a coerced confession (Imai case).","Fukui earthquake of 28 June damaged 525 of 1,253 silk and rayon mills; export fabric output expected down by one third.","Eight war criminals sentenced to death in 10 trials, including the Bataan Death March case (Kawane, Hirano).","New Liberal Party formed 15 July; 16 Social Democrats left or were expelled over the budget.","Land sold to tenants jumped to 589,491 cho in June; coke output reached a postwar record 265,707 tons."],"k":["National Public Service Law","Ashida cabinet","Supreme Commander's letter","Social Democratic Party","Nishio case","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","Fukui earthquake","land reform","price stabilization","financial reorganization","war crimes trials","Habeas Corpus Law","boards of education","public health laws","nutrition surveys"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover and title pages for Summation No. 34 (July 1948), with a table of contents for all four parts (General, Political, Economic, Social; 13 economic sections and two social ones) and a numbered list of 108 charts, maps and graphs, from industrial production indexes to textbooks, motion pictures and SCAP information libraries."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,16],"pp":"1","s":"Condensed overview of the month's non-military activities, by topic and paragraph."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"A single sentence reports that there was no change in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during July 1948. The page also opens Section 2, with its contents list for government and politics, public safety, and legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes July politics: MacArthur's 22 July letter on the National Public Service Law led to a 31 July cabinet order banning strikes and bargaining by public employees. The Diet closed 5 July, enacting 230 measures, and the Ministry of Construction was created. Nishio resigned and was indicted. A New Liberal Party formed 15 July, Social Democrats split, and floods, rail accidents, police, fire and prison laws, criminal procedure reforms, a Supreme Court ruling and war crimes trials are also covered."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,14],"pp":"7","s":"Economic summary for July: land sales to tenants rose fivefold to 589,491 cho in June; coke output hit a postwar record of 265,707 tons; the 28 June Fukui earthquake damaged 525 of 1,253 silk and rayon mills. The Diet raised postal and telephone charges (nearly 300 percent for telephone and telegraph), passed the 1948-49 budget (General Account 414.5 billion yen) and a Transactions Tax. Other items: labor, trade, food, finance, reparations and the new Industrial Science and Technology Agency."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,16],"pp":"19","s":"Social summary: the Diet passed a package of health laws (preventive vaccination, venereal disease control, medical practitioners, dentists, nurses, hot springs, eugenics protection). CARE began operating 19 July with 10-dollar food packages, and 1,841,429 people received welfare aid in June. Education items include boards of education in every locality, the end of the textbook monopoly, and a nine-day national holiday law. Postcensorship was completed 25 July, and 45,333 persons were repatriated."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[17,46],"pp":"23","s":"Government and politics, public safety, and legal affairs and war crimes. Page 17 also carries the end of Part I (media of expression, paragraphs 35-41)."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[18,38],"pp":"25","s":"Political life dominated by the Supreme Commander's letter on public service labor, the end of the Diet session, the Nishio case, coalition strain, party realignments and graft investigations."},{"t":"Public Service Law Revision","lv":3,"p":[18,23],"pp":"25","s":"MacArthur's 22 July letter to Prime Minister Ashida argues that public employees owe allegiance to the sovereign state and may not strike, and calls for revising the National Public Service Law. Newspapers largely agreed; party leaders approved, while some unionists feared misuse. The cabinet suspended wage mediation, issued a 31 July order banning strikes and bargaining, and the party chiefs planned a mid-September special Diet session."},{"t":"Governmental Activities (Diet, Wage Disputes, Laws, Party Strength)","lv":3,"p":[24,30],"pp":"","s":"The 209-day Diet session closed 5 July, the longest in Japanese history; the 1948-49 budget passed after a compromise on railway fares. Of 273 bills introduced, 230 became law and 43 failed. The wage base of 3,790 yen was fought by unions demanding 5,200 yen, with CLRB mediation halted after the MacArthur letter. Tables list the laws by ministry and party strength in both houses; the Ministry of Construction began 10 July and the Imperial Household received 58,785,000 yen."},{"t":"Political Developments (Nishio Case, Coalition Weakness)","lv":3,"p":[30,32],"pp":"","s":"Deputy Prime Minister Nishio resigned 6 July and was tried from 16 July for perjury and failing to report a 500,000-yen donation from building contractors; he pleaded not guilty, and his cabinet post was left vacant. Social Democrats, angered by the shelved war bond interest bill, threatened to leave the coalition. Katayama's 18 July call for dissolution, a poll showing Ashida's support at 16 percent, and Yoshida's demand that the cabinet resign are reported, with the New Liberal Party formed 15 July."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[32,36],"pp":"","s":"Katayama announced 18 July that the Social Democrats would demand Diet dissolution; Miki proposed a centrist federation, Ashida backed it and the Democratic Liberals and Social Democrats scorned it. The New Liberal Party formed 15 July with 12 members. Six left-wingers were expelled over the budget and 10 seceded, cutting the government margin to nine seats. A bomb injured Communist Tokuda 19 July."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions Investigation","lv":3,"p":[36,38],"pp":"","s":"The Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee and procurators pursued political graft in July. Diet members Iimura and Nishimura were arrested or indicted, and Kono, Isazaki and Miura went on trial for perjury. Former Vice-minister Ino and railway officials were tried. Hearings examined Ashida, Chizaki's Democratic Party donations and Finance Minister Kitamura's Sasebo ties, and a subcommittee began probing black-market incomes above 5,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Revised Economic Policy","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"","s":"The Economic Rehabilitation Committee published a revised program 14 July, moving the five-year plan back a year to 1949-53 and aiming to restore 1930-34 per capita real income, with the first two years for checking inflation, power and transport. Director General Kurusu saw bright prospects. The Economic Stabilization Board's ten-point stabilization draft of 17 July drew press comment."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[39,41],"pp":"67","s":"Floods from 25 July hit Fukui, Toyama, Ishikawa and Shiga, with 30 killed and 22 missing; Kagoshima landslides killed 38. June rail accidents numbered 622 with 206 killed. Procurators uncovered about 1,950,000,000 yen of hoarded goods, and 88,179,132 yen was recovered in June. Other items: Economic Investigation Board, police rules, prison population 94,700, Fire Service Law and May fire losses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[42,46],"pp":"73","s":"The Diet revised the Codes of Criminal and Civil Procedure and passed the Inquest of Prosecution, Habeas Corpus, Juvenile, Reformatory and Impeachment of Judges laws. On 19 July the Supreme Court, 13 to one, used constitutional review for the first time to bar a confession after prolonged detention. War crimes trials produced eight death sentences, including the Bataan Death March and Fukuoka prison cases; the IMTFE held no session."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[47,154],"pp":"83","s":"Thirteen sections on production, transport, labor, trade, prices, finance, property and science."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[48,51],"pp":"85","s":"The Government sold 589,491 cho to tenants in June, 1,222,323 cumulative, and bought 209,782 cho, with enforcement orders issued 19 July. Cooperative approvals, mutual relief insurance and an extension act advanced, while fertilizer allocations met 70, 59 and 1 percent of requirements. Marine production fell to 200,630 tons on gear shortages; whaling expeditions for 1948-49 were approved."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[52,57],"pp":"93","s":"June log production was 53,364,500 cubic feet, a seasonal drop, and firewood, charcoal and gasumaki fell below May as farmers turned to crops. A five-year reforestation program began and the Diet raised the bond limit to 1,500,000,000 yen. July coal output was 2,711,000 tons against a 2,850,000 goal; stockpiles reached 680,600 tons. Crude oil was 16,632 kiloliters; 22 minerals rose."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[58,68],"pp":"105","s":"Coke output hit a postwar record 265,707 tons in June. Pig iron rose 2.4 percent while power shortages cut electric-furnace steel 30 percent; nonferrous metals, rubber, petroleum and cement were reported. Coal miners' housing and a new Ministry of Construction (10 July) were covered, along with shipbuilding, chemicals (dyes at a postwar peak), rolling stock, machine tools and textile machinery."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[69,78],"pp":"127","s":"Reports May food and tobacco and June industrial output. Canned food rose to 1,924 tons on imported tin plate while wheat flour fell to 26,295 tons; cigarettes rose 11 percent with two new brands. Pulp reached 39,612 tons and paper 40,555 tons. Watches and clocks set a record 206,226 units; trucks, bicycles and typewriters rose; sewing machines gained on an Indian export order."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[79,93],"pp":"147","s":"Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damaged 525 of 1,253 mills; cotton, silk, rayon, wool, bast fiber and knit goods output, stocks and machinery; raw cotton and wool imports."},{"t":"Earthquake Damage","lv":3,"p":[79,79],"pp":"","s":"Describes damage from the 28 June earthquake in Fukui and Ishikawa Prefectures, the heart of silk and rayon weaving. A Textile Bureau survey found over a third of weaving capacity destroyed or damaged, including 547 Fukui mills with 12,904 looms. About 28 percent of bleaching and dyeing capacity is in the area; export fabric output is expected to fall by one third."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[94,100],"pp":"177","s":"Covers June transport and utilities. The Diet passed the Port Regulations, Port Area (418 ports), Pilot and Mariners' Employment Security laws. Registered motor vehicles reached 211,092, 70.4 percent operable. Rail passengers fell after a 75-percent private fare rise and strikes. Electric generation dropped 11 percent to 2,642 million kWh amid drought; coal was cut 10 percent. Gas output was 63.7 million cubic meters."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[101,110],"pp":"191","s":"Covers communications for June and July. The Diet approved a training law and roughly fourfold postal, telephone and telegraph rate increases. Telephones in service reached 1,230,872, hampered by earthquake damage. Radio authorizations, the Japan Broadcasting Corporation reorganization, doubled listening fee and 6,762,022 radio households are noted. Postal savings restrictions were lifted 20 July; equipment output, personnel (434,516) and research are reported."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[111,120],"pp":"211","s":"The Diet passed the Mariners' Employment Security Law on 3 July. Unions protested price rises and the Communications Workers demanded a $5,200 wage against the Government's $3,791. Union membership reached 6,629,222 on 31 May, and the government workers' wage dispute went to mediation until the Supreme Commander's letter halted it. Also covered: labor standards inspections, workmen's compensation, employment security, unemployment insurance and a 1948-49 public works budget of $42.5 billion."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[121,127],"pp":"231","s":"Japan concluded a trade agreement with the French Union on 6 July, including 25,000 tons of Indo-Chinese anthracite, and contracted iron ore from Hongkong and Malaya. SCAP required licensing of all commercial cargo. May imports were $28.3 million, 76 percent from the United States, against exports of $17.8 million, half to Asia. The cumulative 1948 trade deficit was $219.7 million, with commodity breakdowns led by textiles, petroleum and foodstuffs."},{"t":"Section 10. Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[128,134],"pp":"245","s":"The July staple ration was met in full, with imported food at 28.6 percent of requirements. Collections of the 1947 crops exceeded quotas, and the Food Maintenance Temporary Measures Law set preplanting quotas. A new price stabilization program raised subsidized basic materials to 110 times 1934-36 levels, with four commodity groups repriced. Staple food producer and consumer prices were revised, and incentive goods, surplus Army clothing and railway food-loss enforcement figures are reported."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[135,144],"pp":"259","s":"Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 241.4 billion yen on 31 July. Reports cover financial institution reorganization losses, bank and Reconstruction Finance Bank figures, securities liquidation (20.3 million shares sold) and 47 control organizations closed in June. The Diet passed tax, insurance, Commercial Code and accountancy laws. The 1948-49 General Account budget was 414.5 billion yen, national debt 367 billion yen, and new local finance and local tax laws were passed."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[145,149],"pp":"279","s":"Looted property in custody stood at 65.9 million yen, with 95 looted ships reported, and the SCAP Custody Account reached $286.5 million. SCAP did not object to 766 transactions by restricted concerns, chiefly shipping, chemicals, mining, textiles and power; 78 firms left the restricted list. The Holding Company Liquidation Commission issued its first structural reorganization order, to Japan Soda, and the Diet passed the Trade Association, Horse Racing and Smaller Enterprise laws."},{"t":"Section 13. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[150,154],"pp":"289","s":"A joint party of 97 Japanese seismologists and engineers investigated the 28 June Fukui earthquake, while the Diet raised earthquake research funds. The Science Council of Japan Law (210 members, about 90,000 electors) and new Industrial Science and Technology and Smaller Enterprises Agencies were created, with inspection and export-commodity laws. Also covered are new processes (amilan, fertilizer, rayon), research appropriations of 2,297,500,000 yen, standards, and patent registrations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[155,182],"pp":"299","s":"Public health and welfare, and education, religion, cultural resources and media of expression."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[156,171],"pp":"301","s":"New health laws, model health centers, communicable disease tables, medical supplies, public assistance, CARE, social security mission report, nutrition surveys, vital statistics and repatriation."},{"t":"Nutrition","lv":3,"p":[165,170],"pp":"320","s":"The May national nutrition survey, second under the revised plan, sampled 42,000 people in 11 large cities, 40 other cities and all prefectures. Per capita calories were 1,961 in Tokyo, 1,937 in the 11 cities and 2,001 in rural areas, the latter two still declining. Underweight fell (Tokyo 17.4 percent), but most deficiency symptoms rose and impaired lactation reached 27.8 percent. Tables give food consumption by group."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[172,182],"pp":"333","s":"Education budget, boards of education law, textbook monopoly abolished; religion and cultural resources; press moved to postcensorship; radio, film, theater, music, exhibits and SCAP information libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[172,176],"pp":"333","s":"The 1948 budget gave the Ministry of Education 27,171,630,000 yen, and the Board of Education Law created elective boards, in prefectures and major cities by 1 November. Fukui earthquake damage to schools, curriculum revision, and strikes at 94 of 658 higher schools over tuition increases are reported. Other items: the Textbook Publication Act, playground needs, MacArthur Cup finals, UNESCO groups, and language reform survey results."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[176,176],"pp":"","s":"The first postwar Japanese-language Year Book of Religion appeared 15 July. The National Holiday Law cut holidays from twelve to nine, dropping State Shinto-linked days. Items cover missionary travel, 1,297 foreign missionaries in Japan, and 946 shrines, temples and churches damaged by the Fukui earthquake. A Jiji survey of 4,600 adults found 60 percent professing a faith, 86 percent of them Buddhist."},{"t":"Cultural Resources","lv":3,"p":[177,177],"pp":"","s":"The Ministry of Education asked prefectural governors to cooperate in preserving scenic and historic sites, citing sanitary facilities and refuse receptacles. To remove governmental control from the fine arts, the Fourth Annual Japanese Art Exhibition, set for October and November in Tokyo, passes to the sponsorship of the Japan Art Academy. July saw eleven painting exhibitions, three of applied arts and four miscellaneous ones."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[177,182],"pp":"","s":"Postcensorship was extended to all Japanese newspapers and news services by 25 July, completing a staged relaxation begun in 1947. Sections cover public opinion, ministry information campaigns, civil liberties, rural and women's affairs, press comment on Diet politics, the budget, labor and language reform, and book output (844 new editions in June). Radio, films, theater, American music, exhibits and SCAP libraries at Shizuoka and Nagasaki are also reported."},{"t":"Map of Japan - Topography","lv":1,"p":[183,183],"pp":"354","s":"A fold-out topographic map of Japan occupies the final pages of the volume, appearing only as two image pages with no printed text in the excerpt. It adds no reported developments for the month."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-34","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-34-1948-07","s":"K","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"1948-08","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 34 (July-August 1948)","v":"","pg":271,"o":"Final issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities, covering July-August 1948 and prepared by the National Economic Board's Statistics Division. It records the 15 August 1948 establishment of the Republic of Korea, the end of USAMGIK (succeeded by the Civil Affairs Section, USAFIK) and the continuing SKIG wind-down. The Summary and the retrospective passages review three years of occupation: population movement, vested property, agriculture and fertilizer, industry under the loss of northern electric power (cut 14 May), trade, labor, transport, finance, law, police, health, education, press, youth and women. Political chapters cover the Constitution, Government Organization Law, National Assembly, Rhee's cabinet and party history. Historians will find extensive statistics (refugees, prices, production, trade, deposits, budgets), the vested-property handover, the Torture Case against the police, the floods, and candid official assessments of occupation achievements and limits.","h":["Republic of Korea proclaimed 15 August 1948; USAMGIK replaced by the Civil Affairs Section, USAFIK; Rhee Syngman president, Lee Bum-suk premier.","Northern electric power cut 14 May; local generation reached 55.6 million KWH in August versus 33 million in April.","About 950,000 refugees counted from the north since 1945; 1.2 million Koreans repatriated; roughly 900,000 Japanese left.","Vested property worth 85,000 million won under custody; 35 companies transferred to elected boards; 481,957 acres of Japanese farmland sold to farmers.","SKIG overdraft exceeded 22,000 million won at August close; F/Y 1948/49 budget about 30,000 million won.","Fertilizer imports passed 548,000 metric tons since October 1946; a bumper 1948 rice crop expected.","Torture Case in Seoul police Division M led to resignations of top National Police Detective Bureau officials in August.","Korean National Youth donated over 12.6 million eight-hour work days of labor from January 1947 to 15 August 1948."],"k":["Republic of Korea","end of USAMGIK","National Economic Board","refugees","repatriation","vested property","fertilizer","electric power shortage","foreign trade","inflation","Constitution","National Assembly","National Police","Korean National Youth","floods"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Front matter of the final SKIG Activities report (No. 34, July-August 1948): list of National Economic Board members under Chairman Wilhelm Anderson, Statistics Division contributors, and a foreword by Arthur G. Peterson. The foreword notes the Republic's establishment on 15 August, the series' history since 1946, poor Korean statistics and staff losses, followed by the table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[11,14],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Anderson reviews the three-year occupation ending 15 August 1948 with the Republic's founding. He covers refugee and repatriate population growth, near food self-sufficiency from fertilizer imports, trade deficit falling from 400 to 25 per cent, the northern power cutoff of 14 May, vested property and farmland sales, price inflation, the overdraft above 22,000 million won, and continuing ECA aid."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[15,40],"pp":"5","s":"Population and foreign affairs; vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[15,29],"pp":"5","s":"Refugee, repatriation and population statistics through August 1948, the Turk-Tartar emigration to Turkey, demographic estimates and the Population and Census Committee."},{"t":"Refugees","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"6","s":"The National Refugee Board was created on 12 May 1947 to plan refugee rehabilitation and distribution to provinces. The text traces 1945 to 1947 migration from North Korea, China and Manchuria, with seasonal peaks tied to food shortage. Table 1 counts 185,441 in 1946, 163,235 in 1947 and 86,036 to mid-August 1948. Seoul entry was barred from 1 July 1947; bypassing camps fueled crime."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[18,20],"pp":"8","s":"Table 2 gives cumulative movement since October 1945: 886,618 outbound, mostly Japanese civilians and military, against 2,454,480 inbound, including 1,115,550 Koreans from Japan and 622,044 from North Korea. Net repatriation equals about seven per cent of population. Japan's Home Affairs Bureau reports 596,780 Koreans remain there, and 21,916 stragglers were apprehended and returned by December 1947."},{"t":"Emigration of Stateless Turk-Tartars","lv":3,"p":[20,22],"pp":"10","s":"Turkey issued visas in August 1948 to 151 stateless Turk-Tartars (97 adults, 54 children) living in Seoul, Pusan and Inchon, handled by Lt. J. F. Guilfoyle. The text gives their history and fears of Soviet return, Turkish conditions, and the Office of Foreign Affairs' won-dollar exchange recommendations. The project passed to the Republic's Foreign Ministry; sailing was expected by end of October."},{"t":"Statistical Analysis of Population in South Korea","lv":3,"p":[22,28],"pp":"12","s":"George Deihl's analysis compares 1947 Seoul and national vital registrations to estimate under-reporting, deriving about 887,000 births (44.35 per thousand), 260,000 deaths and 3.1 per cent natural increase, or 627,000. Tables give age and sex distribution from food-ration registers, the estimated 1 July 1948 population of 20.3 million, rural-urban contrasts, death rates, a mortality table, and life expectancy at birth of 45 to 49 years."},{"t":"Population and Census Committee","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"19","s":"The Committee on Population and Census Statistics was set up on 4 March 1948 to coordinate SKIG agencies' population data and devised monthly tabulations of births by sex and deaths by province. It issued quarterly projections of South Korea's population from 20,100,000 on 31 March 1948 to 21,000,000 by 31 December 1949."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[30,40],"pp":"20","s":"Office of Property Custody activities, reorganization, plant inspections, disposal and real estate transfers, claims, loan guarantees and corporate affairs as the office was handed to Koreans."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[31,33],"pp":"21","s":"The Office of Property Custody prepared the handover to the Republic: Administrative Division reorganized with seven sections, a Purchase and Contract Board for transactions over 200,000 won, and gasoline use cut 50 per cent. Corporate Affairs and Records moved to the Comptroller Division, and some firms changed supervising departments. Table 1 totals vested holdings at 256,019,630 thousand won gross, net worth 85,849,975 thousand."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[34,35],"pp":"24","s":"Property disposal accelerated in July: residence sales tripled and MCC sales rose tenfold over June. Table 2 shows 199 sales worth 217,870 thousand won in July and 957 sales worth 1,918,888 thousand won since August 1947. The section also reports inspections of Taegu and Pusan textile mills, 355 vested vessels at Mokpo and Kunsan, two paper mills, and cork-substitute experiments."},{"t":"Real Estate Transfers","lv":3,"p":[35,35],"pp":"25","s":"Occupancy of five USAFIK properties, including OCI offices, CIC Headquarters in Inchon and the Sugar Bowl Building, was regularized. The largest release of army property yet undertaken followed: 149 dependent houses, 80 buildings in Area W at Camp Ascom, 117 buildings at Camp Taejon, a 19-building Pusan Navy Compound, billets, airstrips, islands and ranges."},{"t":"Policy Decisions and Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[36,36],"pp":"26","s":"OPC Policies and Precedents Memorandum No. 32 of 12 August declared property of Allied-owned companies organized in Japan to be Allied, not vested under Ordinance No. 10, and prepared it for release. The streamlined Claims Section title hearings were extended nationwide; table 3 lists 1,119 cases, 453 allowed, 233 disallowed and 531 pending at 31 August, with three provinces completed."},{"t":"Comptroller Activities and Corporate Affairs","lv":3,"p":[37,40],"pp":"27","s":"The Property Custodian guaranteed loans of 7,000,000 won to Chosun Tanning and 5,000,000 won to Korean Fisheries Development, and completed five provincial audits. Statements for 1,812 companies show net sales of 37,062,145 thousand won and net profit of 1,021,121 thousand. Corporate Affairs investigated 5,358 companies; 35 transferred to elected boards, starting with South Korea Electric Co. on 10 July."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[41,142],"pp":"31","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocations, trade and labor."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[41,58],"pp":"31","s":"Rice and summer grain crops, rationing, stocks, floods, agricultural bureaus, fertilizer imports, land administration and the sale of vested farmland."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[41,46],"pp":"31","s":"South Korea's 1948 crop estimate of almost 6,000,000 metric tons is the highest since 1941, with larger planted area, and self-sufficiency is expected from January 1949. The summer grain collection reached 95.8 per cent of quota by 31 August. Tables cover ration populations (9,489,600 in June), rice and grain releases, stocks of 244,169 tons at end of August, and imports."},{"t":"Floods","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"37","s":"The 7 July typhoon and heavy rains damaged Cholla tidal reclamation works: 801 levees broken, 28,219 acres damaged, 75 killed, and 387 boats lost. Small irrigation works lost 21,728 acres. Iri reported 68,431 acres flooded and Taegu hailstorms hit fruit land. Table 8 lists 504,541 acres flooded, mostly in Cholla-pukto; crops were replanted and forecasts stayed above 1947."},{"t":"Agricultural Bureaus and Forestry and Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[49,52],"pp":"39","s":"Rice transplanting finished two weeks early. Farm supply items include a 27,500 won copper sulphate price, vetch seed and salt for hides. Raw silk output fell to 121.5 tons for lack of coal, power and cocoons, and chick hatching reached 605,470 of 2,000,000 goal. Reports cover July rainfall, apple losses, seed self-sufficiency, North China cement tests, new irrigation, timber sales and fishing landings at 67 per cent of 1947."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[53,54],"pp":"43","s":"In July the Korea Agricultural Association distributed imported fertilizer and shipped 5,200,000 straw grain bags to Pusan, Inchon, Mokpo and Yosu for export to Japan despite floods. Military Government directives of 10 and 28 July set plant-food prices, such as 370 won per bag of ammonium sulphate, dropped straw-bag payments, and allowed a 270 won handling fee. Table 12 shows 424,970 tons sold to farmers."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"45","s":"Fertilizer imports since October 1946 exceeded 548,000 metric tons by end of August 1948 (table 13). Extension work included training over 1,000 myun agents in non-compulsory methods, a drive on disease-free potatoes and second-crop planting, and adult education planning with the Department of Education. Experimental stations reported forage self-sufficiency at Sang Whan and tomato disease; Suwon added a soils course."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[57,58],"pp":"47","s":"Ordinance No. 215 of 10 August amended the National Land Administration's founding ordinance, providing damages or return where courts favor claimants. By 31 July 85 per cent of available farms were sold: 502,224 farms and 481,957 acres. Summer grain collection for land payments reached 41 per cent and rent collection 25 per cent; OCC was to pay NLA from verified records."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[59,68],"pp":"49","s":"Wholesale, retail and controlled prices resumed rising after five months of stability; new weighted index of retail prices paid by urban workers."},{"t":"Index Numbers of Retail Prices Paid by Urban Workers","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"56","s":"A new weighted index of retail prices paid by urban workers (1947 = 100) replaced unweighted Bank of Chosun series, with foods weighted 57.5, utilities 21.9 and clothing 15.1. Total rose from 86 in January 1947 to 179 in August 1948. It uses 32 price series from nine cities, 63 per cent of urban population, with weights from nutrition and cost-of-living surveys covering 18 foods and other items."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[69,81],"pp":"59","s":"Output under the restricted power supply; processed food, textiles, paper, hardware, chemicals, miscellaneous goods and Monopoly Bureau products."},{"t":"Processed Food and Textiles","lv":3,"p":[72,73],"pp":"62","s":"Only one of two large vested flour mills had power in July-August; with small plants, flour output was 707 tons in July and 1,782 in August versus 1,653 in June. Noodles edged up from June despite rains. Cotton yarn rose 22 per cent in July and cotton cloth 34 per cent, both limited by power shortage; Suwon silk training progressed."},{"t":"Paper, Hardware and Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[74,78],"pp":"64","s":"Power shortage depressed paper, chemicals and building materials. The best paper mill stayed idle for lack of sulphite pulp, and 150 Army blankets served as felts. Nail output rose 46 per cent in July. Briquettes recovered slightly as US pitch arrived; coke hit a postliberation low. No cement was made in July; brick fell; rubber shoes, oxygen, carbide and matches were reported."},{"t":"Miscellaneous and Monopoly Bureau","lv":3,"p":[79,81],"pp":"69","s":"Electric insulators fell to 4,800 pieces in July and 1,500 in early August because of power shortage, while pencils rose 22 per cent in July to 4,400 gross. The Monopoly Bureau, chief government revenue source, runs four tobacco plants and six salterns; salt fell to 4,808 tons in July, and cigarettes to 108.3 tons before August recovery."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[82,90],"pp":"72","s":"Mining output followed electric power: after the Soviets cut power on 14 May 1948, anthracite fell from 68,612 tons in April to 38,887 in July, and Hwasun mine was nearly out. August anthracite allocations totalled 28,844 tons to Commerce, 26,000 to Army and 9,656 to railroads. Bituminous imports from Japan reached 104,055 tons in July and 97,864 in August. Pitch stockpiled; gold, copper, tungsten, graphite, zinc and lead output tabulated."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[91,106],"pp":"81","s":"National Consumer Goods Office operations since January 1948, controlled commodity distribution, petroleum, salt supply and barter trade with North Korea."},{"t":"National Consumer Goods Office","lv":3,"p":[91,94],"pp":"81","s":"The National Consumer Goods Office, created by Executive Order No. 12 of 12 January 1948 under Dr. Chyung Yil-heung, allocates domestic vested-factory goods and civilian supply imports. By 31 August it had processed 1,095 allocations worth 2,184,468,531 won, 88.7 per cent for special groups, and 28.2 per cent went to farmers as rice and grain collection incentives."},{"t":"Distribution of Controlled Commodities","lv":3,"p":[95,99],"pp":"85","s":"The Federation of Financial Associations, a cooperative of over 600 branches and nearly 2,000,000 members, distributes goods to government and vested-industry employees on a 15 per cent commission, with distributing agents serving the public. Eligible employees numbered 480,273, up 43,630 since June. Tables give FFA purchases and distributions of cloth, shoes, soap, matches and other goods for July and August."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"90","s":"Petroleum Distribution Agency allocations and deliveries to provincial agencies are listed for July and August: gasoline deliveries 4,901,333 and 6,079,399 liters against allocations above 7,000,000. Gasoline and kerosene fell short mainly because Pusan terminal could not fill drums; diesel and marine fuel shortfalls reflected Fisheries taking less. National-level deliveries and sales through August are tabulated."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[101,103],"pp":"91","s":"In F/Y 1947 government salterns produced 65,323 tons and total supply reached 182,336 tons, forcing ration cuts. For F/Y 1948 the goal was 134,631 tons; 68,774 tons (58.6 per cent) were made by 15 August despite the power cutoff and heavy rain. Stocks were 184,170 tons, allowing full rations. Purchase prices rose 15 August; Sosan saltern is under construction."},{"t":"Barter Trade with North Korea","lv":3,"p":[104,106],"pp":"94","s":"Private barter with North Korea, running since mid-1946, was hampered by slow communications, rivalry over confiscated cargo and absconding shipowners, pushing small traders overland. Department of Commerce Order No. 4 (2 August 1948) legalized overland trade. Imports from the north totalled 950,861,250 won from January to 15 August, notably dried fish; contracts worth 382,499,000 won were pending, and policy changes were feared under the new Republic."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[107,131],"pp":"97","s":"Private trade (imports 3,600 million won, exports 2,700 million in seven months of 1948), government-to-government transactions, items sold to Americans and civilian supply imports."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[111,116],"pp":"101","s":"August licenses (215 imports, 97 exports) imply imports near 722,000,000 won and exports near 361,000,000. June imports were 436,652,000 won and exports 286,673,000; July imports 431,060,000 and exports 345,916,000, led by paper products and straw hats. Inchon handled over 59 per cent of January-July trade; Hong Kong took most exports, with import origins listed by country."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[117,118],"pp":"107","s":"By August 1948 six commercial shipping companies, three American, two British and one Dutch, had regular runs; Hong Kong-Pusan freight was HK$65 per measurement ton. Fifteen businessmen were cleared to investigate trade in August. The text logs July-August voyages of Butterfield and Swire and Everett vessels carrying paper, rubber, medicines and newsprint in and sea products out."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[119,124],"pp":"109","s":"The Bureau of Foreign Commerce is sole negotiator for government trade. Exports to Japan totalled $8,377,383 since 1945, with $828,457 in July-15 August, chiefly 3,329,070 straw bags and graphite; laver was the top earner. US shipments by USCC and directly (tungsten, copper, zinc) are detailed. Agar agar and ginseng went to Hong Kong; China allowed none; Pusan exported 15,141 tons."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[125,125],"pp":"115","s":"Since 1 August 1947 the Army Exchange Service has bought Korean merchandise through the Bureau of Foreign Commerce, totalling 160,789 units worth $223,063 to 15 August 1948, mainly jewelry, brassware, lacquerware and silk. Scarce materials, costly inputs and untrained management limited volume. July sales were only $4,857, the lowest since August 1947, and early August $3,609."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[126,131],"pp":"116","s":"Imports since September 1945 totalled $328,117,607, including $24,935,669 through the Foreign Liquidation Commission in eight contracts. Coal from Japan reached a record 104,055 tons in July; GARIOA foodstuffs came to $110,446,030 by June. July imports included 19,609 tons of salt, fertilizer (8,816 tons superphosphate), pitch and gypsum; petroleum imports were 364,236 tons through June."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[132,142],"pp":"122","s":"Labor laws and Department of Labor activity, strikes, wage stabilization, labor relations disputes, union elections, standards, health and education."},{"t":"Wage Problems","lv":3,"p":[134,137],"pp":"124","s":"Traces the National Economic Board's wage policy from its 25 June 1946 hold-the-line stance through the March 1947 reversal to the Wage Stabilization Division, set up 25 March 1948. The Division surveyed living costs, recommended a 25 per cent staff cut, and drafted incentive plans. A 3 July 1948 directive restricted general raises. A critical appraisal found policy without enforcement, and occupation-force wage coordination failed."},{"t":"General Current Activities and Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[138,139],"pp":"128","s":"Covers labor relations in the 45 days to 15 August. At Pusan, stevedores threatened a strike over a 500-won daily wage demand. Talks on 22 July set a 350-won minimum and dropped the 80-20 split, but a coal pier stoppage followed on 7 August. Tai Han railway workers' wage grievance was sent to the Department of Transportation. The Marine Bureau contract for about 1,500 workers was agreed except for wage schedules."},{"t":"Unions, Standards, Health and Education","lv":3,"p":[140,142],"pp":"130","s":"Reports a Yongwol Mines election (8 July) won by Tai Han with 2,099 votes, and a Tong Wha Department Store election with intimidation charges. Tai Han's executive committee resigned after the Chun Jin-han motion failed 44 to 22. Bank overtime violations left 15,654 won owing to 158 employees. Also covered: a labor-law abstract, an industrial health tour of Seoul, a southwest labor education tour and a new labor library."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[143,177],"pp":"133","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities, public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[143,155],"pp":"133","s":"Department of Transportation income and expenditures, railroad operations and equipment, marine bureau history and highway and air transport."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[145,151],"pp":"135","s":"Describes the Japanese-built, north-south rail system taken over in September 1945 (2,145 operating miles) and its chronic shortages of equipment and fuel. Operable engines fell from 300 to 268 by August 1948. The text gives August receipts of 578 million won against 737 million won expenditures, passenger and freight tonnage, back-shop repairs, employment of 31,894, engine kilometers and coal use of 44,888 tons."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[152,152],"pp":"142","s":"Traces the Marine Bureau from Japanese days, when it moved between several departments, through its merger into the Bureau of Transportation in 1942. After liberation, abandoned ships were disputed and sham sales to Koreans voided. Navigational aids were restored, 12 LSTs and 11 Freight Supply vessels were bought, and Rainbow service began in 1948. July and August expenses rose sharply."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[153,155],"pp":"143","s":"Reviews highway transport from the 1944 Pan-Korean Trucking Union, 80 per cent Japanese-owned, to the Korean Truck Co. of December 1945. It was disbanded in July 1946 and provincial governors took over its facilities after the June 1946 floods. Table 11 shows highway and air expenditures rising sharply in July, with air still rising in August. Highway employment had fallen to 46 and air to 27."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[156,165],"pp":"145","s":"Describes the Department of Communications: a deteriorated plant at liberation, rehabilitation of telephone exchanges and 20,000 poles, and Korean training. July revenue of 88.9 million won was a post-liberation high, and expenditures doubled. It also covers tax collection, postal savings and insurance, 37,183 telephones and 10,000 unserved applications, 623 post offices, and 11 radio stations with a 100-won monthly fee from 1 August."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[166,171],"pp":"155","s":"Reviews power from Japanese neglect through Military Government rehabilitation, the Yong Wol subsidy and the 14 May 1948 northern cutoff. Deliveries were 56.2 million KWH in July and 55.7 million in August. Chung Pyung's transformer was repaired 10 July, and Yong Wol was separated from the Korea Electric Power Company on 26 July. The Giroux mission report of 23 July is quoted, and streetcar and bus figures are given."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[172,177],"pp":"161","s":"Reviews public works from the Japanese era through the 1946 Department and Koreanization, citing 517 bridges, 1,422 km of road and 162 km of levees rebuilt. The department prepared the transfer in July and August. Floods caused over 2,000 million won damage; 800 million won of road and bridge funds were sought, and an Emergency Water Conservation Board was set up 26 July. First-half fires totaled 1,006."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[178,193],"pp":"167","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[178,184],"pp":"167","s":"Examines the Bank of Chosun note issue, 30,000 million won at end-August 1948 against a 33,400 million peak, and the Interim Government's overdraft of 22,506 million won. Revenues were 2,157 million won in August against 1,924 million expenditures. Appropriations totaled 35,264 million won, including 6,260 million for civilian supply. Civilian Supply assets were 38,465 million won, with sales of 7,676 million in August."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[185,193],"pp":"174","s":"Covers financial institutions through July 1948, since August data was unavailable. Cash balances were 2,244 million won; deposits reached 43,335 million, 57 per cent from the public; loans outstanding were 30,038 million, a 70 per cent ratio to deposits. Life insurance in force was 1,067 million won with record claims of 672,000 won paid in July, and damage insurers wrote 9,243 policies in July."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[194,231],"pp":"183","s":"Administration, politics, legal affairs, police, inter-zone."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[194,198],"pp":"183","s":"Records the proclamation of the Republic (Tai Han Min Kook) on 15 August 1948 and the end of Military Government. It reviews the 10 May election, the 24 July inauguration of President Rhee, and USAMGIK achievements in industry, land sales, health and prisons. The 11-man National Economic Board's July-August actions are listed, with Military Government personnel falling to 2,870 by 15 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[199,213],"pp":"188","s":"Constitution and Government Organization Law, National Assembly actions, Rhee's cabinet, moderate groups and a review of political party development."},{"t":"Constitution and Government Organization Law","lv":3,"p":[199,203],"pp":"188","s":"Reviews the Constitution passed unanimously on 12 July (preamble, ten chapters, strong presidency, four-year Assembly, state ownership of mines and key industries, farmland to tillers, profit-sharing for laborers) and the Government Organization Law of 16 July, which creates eleven departments, four offices under the Prime Minister, an Economic Board, and Civil Service and Inspection Committees."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[204,206],"pp":"193","s":"Covers July-August Assembly actions: Rhee elected President 180 votes of 196 on 20 July and Lee Si-young Vice-President on the second ballot; inaugurated 24 July. The Assembly rejected Lee Yun-yung 132-59 but approved Lee Bum-suk as Prime Minister 110-84, elected Shin Ik-hi Speaker, confirmed Kim Byung-no as Chief Justice 117-31, and took up amnesty and pro-Japanese traitor legislation."},{"t":"Republic of Korea","lv":3,"p":[207,208],"pp":"196","s":"Reports Rhee's cabinet, completed by 6 August, and the 15 August proclamation of the Republic ending Military Government; General Hodge said Americans would only advise, and transfers began 16 August. Gives biographies of the eleven ministers, including Chang Taik-sang, Lee Bum-suk, Cho Bong-am and Louise Yim, and the press verdict that the cabinet was mediocre and partisan."},{"t":"Moderate Activities","lv":3,"p":[209,210],"pp":"198","s":"Lists vice-ministers and other appointments, notably Chough Pyung-ok as ambassador-at-large and Chang Myun as chief delegate to the UN in Paris. Moderates split three ways over the new government: one faction joined the North Korean election, some backed the South, and Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic held to unified independence, with Kimm wavering after meeting Lee Bum-suk on 10 August."},{"t":"Summary (political parties)","lv":3,"p":[211,213],"pp":"200","s":"Retrospective on party politics since liberation. Three blocs emerged: communists (Hu Hun's South Korea Labor Party, Park Heun-yung's Democratic People's Front), extreme rightists (Hankook Democratic Party, Rhee's National Society, Kim Koo's Korean Independence Party, Dai Dong youth) and moderates under Kimm Kiu-sic. Ordinance No. 55 of February 1946 produced over 400 Seoul parties; Rhee gained power as Kim Koo defected."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[214,221],"pp":"203","s":"Ordinances and orders, legal opinions of the Department of Justice, court caseloads and new branch courts, and penal institutions."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[214,216],"pp":"203","s":"Lists ordinances drafted 1 July-31 August 1948, Nos. 203-219: litigation fees, taxicabs, the legal profession and bar associations, release of seized property, rice and fall grain collection for 1948-49, prosecution reorganization, travel controls, National Land Administration suits, and school districts, boards and funding. Also lists executive and departmental orders on water conservation, interzonal barter, postal rates, and Korean court jurisdiction; 26 American lawyers admitted to the Korean bar."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[217,217],"pp":"206","s":"Gives four Department of Justice opinions: pension deposits of Japanese repatriates (No. 1627, 16 July) may be paid in Japan only if not physically in Korea before 25 September 1945; the Chosun Mining Development Corporation should not be dissolved pending the new government (No. 1612); narcotics informer awards require amending Ordinance No. 119 (No. 1650); and the metric system is official but not exclusive (No. 1633)."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[218,218],"pp":"207","s":"Cheju-do riot trials continued; a judicial team reported the island unsuitable for trials, fearing violence from leftists or rightists, with about 250 held, and the Military Governor allowed transfer of cases to Mokpo or Kwangju. Courts heard 157,293 cases in 1946-47; table gives 67,181 in 1946 and 90,112 in 1947. Kangnung branch court became three-judge; a Kunsan branch opened."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[219,221],"pp":"208","s":"Prison population: 22,141 in April, 22,331 in May, 22,033 in June and 22,321 in July, about 45 per cent for theft or burglary and 11 per cent under Proclamation No. 2. Tables give figures by prison and juvenile house of correction (541 in June, 664 in July). Conditions were reported sanitary; 174 guards graduated in June and 161 enrolled, with about four per cent turnover."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[222,227],"pp":"211","s":"Police operations, arrests of political suspects, Seoul crime statistics, the Torture Case, attack on Chief Chang and an overview summary."},{"t":"Torture Case","lv":3,"p":[224,224],"pp":"213","s":"Torture death of suspect Lim Wha (an alias) at Metropolitan Police Division M in January, allegedly by water treatment, with the body thrown in the Han River. The Detective Bureau leaked it in July; Chief Detective Ro Duk-sul was reported to have confessed and escaped on 27 July, and acting chief Kim Tai-il was briefly suspended. Politically motivated against Chang Taik-sang; no prosecutor decision by August."},{"t":"Attack on Chief Chang","lv":3,"p":[225,225],"pp":"214","s":"A fourth attempt on Metropolitan Police Chief Chang Taik-sang's life came on 24 July, when three men fired on his car in Yongdungpo, Seoul; a bodyguard was wounded and Chang unhurt. Separately, Detective Bureau chiefs Lee Man-chong and Cho Byung-sul resigned on 12 August citing interference, and Chough Pyung-ok apologized to Rhee on 13 August and warned critics of the government transfer."},{"t":"Summary (National Police)","lv":3,"p":[226,227],"pp":"215","s":"Retrospective on the National Police: the effort to build a democratic force and shed Japanese methods, with Economic and Sanitation Bureaus eliminated. It recounts separation from National Defense in April 1946, riots from the January 1946 Moscow protests to Taegu in October 1946 (over 120 policemen killed), and sabotage guarding by Railroad Police. Headquarters bureaus and ten divisions listed, plus a special Inspection Command on Cheju-do."},{"t":"Section 5. Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[228,231],"pp":"217","s":"Covers mail exchange across the 38th parallel (tabulated March 1946-August 1948; 9 trips in July-August 1948) and Commerce Order No. 4 of 2 August requiring permits and bonds for barter, with June imports such as 516 tons of fertilizer. An American soldier was killed on 19 July at the parallel. Radio Pyongyang attacked Rhee and promoted the 25 August election."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[232,271],"pp":"221","s":"Health, education, press, youth, women, culture."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[232,243],"pp":"221","s":"Preventive medicine, nursing, veterinary, pharmaceutical and narcotics work, laboratories, public assistance for flood victims and refugees, and a retrospective summary."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[239,241],"pp":"228","s":"Flood relief: ten carloads of supplies sent to provinces; damage tables report 3,604 houses washed away, 14,881 crushed and 254,664 persons made needy. Five of 19 applicants chosen for welfare scholarships abroad; 8,616 Manchurian repatriates in total; 1,149 tents allocated to refugees; new orphanages licensed; UNRRA grants of 2,700,000 won (July) and 6,135,000 won (August) funded institutions; Seoul asylum transferred."},{"t":"Summary (Public Health and Welfare)","lv":3,"p":[242,243],"pp":"231","s":"Concludes that the Department of Public Health and Welfare achieved much in three years despite shortages and untrained staff. Cholera deaths fell from over 15,000 in 1946 to 10 in 1947 through mass immunization. Credited also: sanitation, refugee quarantine, new clinics including the first venereal disease clinic, nurse and doctor training, orphanages, vaccine laboratories and veterinary and slaughterhouse improvements. The 1 July transfer of the Chung Nyang Ni asylum to medical supervision is also noted."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[244,249],"pp":"233","s":"Education: three ordinances effective 12 August created school districts, nine-member school boards and local school taxation. Also reported: 500 teachers in the Kwanju workshop among 7,500 trained, normal school graduations, a Teacher Training Center of 400 with 20 American educators, scholarship screening, 14,989,286 textbooks published since liberation, an achievement test, American Language Institute enrollments, vocational committees and adult education conferences."},{"t":"Section 3. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[250,257],"pp":"239","s":"58 dailies, 67 weeklies and 125 monthlies in July; press reaction to the new government, North Korean election, floods, Cheju-do riots and Dok Do incident; editorials and miscellany."},{"t":"Formation of New Government","lv":3,"p":[251,252],"pp":"240","s":"Press coverage of forming the government: papers followed the Constitution debate, including profit-sharing in Article 17, and feared a separate Police Department, which was defeated 108 to 88. They welcomed Assembly rejection of Lee Yun-yung and approval of Lee Bum-suk. Context given on 58 dailies, with a table of eleven leading papers and circulations, led by Seoul Shin Mun at 76,000."},{"t":"Floods, Cheju-do Riots and Dok Do Incident","lv":3,"p":[253,253],"pp":"242","s":"The press protested the North Korean election of 25 August, with underground voting organized in the South and Hu Hun and Park Heun-yung as candidates. July floods and a typhoon were estimated to cost 2,000,000,000 won, with 169 killed in Cholla-namdo. Papers blamed Cheju-do unrest on police and mainland officials, and demanded more after 2,480,000 won reparations for the accidental bombing of Dok Do."},{"t":"Foreign News and Editorials","lv":3,"p":[254,256],"pp":"243","s":"Foreign news covered the Berlin crisis, with Cha Yoo Shin Mun ruling out immediate war, and the North Korean Labor Party's endorsement of the Cominform's Yugoslav condemnation. Editorials blamed SKIG's failure on military officials and Korean corruption, praised Lee Bum-suk, criticized the Constitution as too socialistic, attacked Rhee's cabinet, police terrorism, and urged national defense and cooperation with the US and UN."},{"t":"Miscellany","lv":3,"p":[256,257],"pp":"245","s":"Miscellany: the torture row between the Detective Bureau and the Metropolitan Police ended with Maj.-Gen. Dean limiting police press statements to the police director. Papers exposed exorbitant school entrance fees in Seoul, exaggerated sentences for Koreans in the Kobe and Osaka riots, and Korean nurses refusing US hospital training over physical examinations, and criticized drinking and profiteering."},{"t":"Section 4. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[258,262],"pp":"247","s":"Korean National Youth, Inc., chartered 9 October 1945, reached 1,154,821 audited members by 15 August 1948. Members donated over 12,600,000 eight-hour work days to community projects, planted 6,770,397 trees, trained more than 100,000 group leaders, and withstood terror and police hostility. Lee Bum-suk resigned as president on becoming Prime Minister; Lee Jun-sic succeeded him, with Lt.-Col. Ernest Voss as adviser."},{"t":"Section 5. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[263,266],"pp":"252","s":"Women gained constitutional and legal equality, and now work in professions, business, education and the 500-strong policewoman force. Louise Yim became Minister of Commerce and Industry. Ordinances Nos. 70 and 112 and Executive Order No. 16 protect women and outlaw prostitution. The Women's Bureau (Ordinance No. 107, 1946) ran a magazine, English classes, provincial meetings, a Travelers' Aid and the Federation of Women's Clubs."},{"t":"Section 6. Culture","lv":2,"p":[267,271],"pp":"256","s":"Hangul was reintroduced in schools from October 1945, but Chinese characters persist, and obstacles include orthography, technical vocabulary and typewriters. The American Language Institute, with about 500 students, teaches English and wrote a short history of Korea. Korea competed under its own flag at the London Olympics (67-man team, 24th place, 15 points) after a 1946-48 preparation effort."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-34-in-K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"1948-08","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 34 (July-August 1948)","v":"Bound in K-33","host":"K-33","rg":[[210,485]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M105.13:34' on PDF p. 210","pg":276,"o":"This final issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board Statistics Division, covers July-August 1948 and the transition on 15 August 1948 from US Army Military Government to the Republic of Korea under President Rhee Syngman. It opens with a summation of three years of occupation, citing food self-sufficiency prospects, fertilizer imports, farmland sales, a narrowing trade deficit and vested property returned to Korean management. Regular sections report prices (wholesale index 184 percent of 1947), industrial output under reduced power after the 14 May North Korean cutoff, labor, transport, communications, finance with a 22,000 million won overdraft, politics, police, and social fields. Historians will find refugee and population statistics, trade figures, the Constitution and cabinet formation, and retrospective accounts of each department.","h":["Republic of Korea proclaimed 15 August 1948; USAMGIK succeeded by Civil Affairs Section of USAFIK.","Refugees from north: 185,441 in 1946, 163,235 in 1947, 86,036 by mid-August 1948.","Wholesale prices rose to 184 percent of the 1947 average in August.","SKIG overdraft exceeded 22,000 million won at the end of August.","Local power generation reached 55,600,000 KWH in August versus 33,000,000 in April.","Management of 35 vested companies transferred to elected Boards of Directors; 481,957 acres of farmland sold.","Korea competed in the London Olympics under its own flag for the first time.","Torture death case in Metropolitan Police and attack on Chief Chang reported."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","Republic of Korea","Rhee Syngman","National Economic Board","vested property","refugees","fertilizer","electric power shortage","prices","Bank of Chosun","Constitution","National Police","Korean National Youth","Hangul","Olympics 1948"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[210,223],"pp":"","s":"Cover and front matter of the final SKIG Activities report (No. 34, July-August 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board. Lists Board members under Chairman Wilhelm Anderson and Statistics Division contributors. Arthur G. Peterson's Forward records the 15 August 1948 founding of the Republic, the report's lineage since 1946, poor Korean statistics, and staff losses. Includes the full table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[224,227],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Wilhelm Anderson reviews three years of occupation as the Republic was proclaimed on 15 August 1948. Cites refugee and repatriate inflows, 500,000 tons of imported fertilizer, near food self-sufficiency, the narrowing trade gap, the power cutoff of 14 May, 85,000 million won of vested property, 481,957 acres of farmland sold, inflation, and a 22,000 million won overdraft."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[228,253],"pp":"5","s":"Population and foreign affairs, and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[228,242],"pp":"5","s":"Covers refugees, repatriation and population statistics. Refugees from the north totalled 86,036 in 1948 to mid-August, after 185,441 in 1946; cumulative inbound movement was 2,454,480 against 886,618 outbound, mostly Japanese. 151 stateless Turk-Tartars awaited emigration to Turkey. Demographic estimates give 3.1 percent natural increase, a population of 20.3 million, and the Population and Census Committee's projections."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[243,253],"pp":"20","s":"Office of Property Custody prepares for handover to the Republic, reorganizing its Administration Division and inspecting textile and paper plants. Disposal sales and the return of Army-occupied property reached new highs. Claims hearings on title disputes covered 1,119 cases nationwide, and a policy memorandum released Allied-owned property. Corporate Affairs investigated 5,358 companies; 35 passed to elected boards. Net worth of vested property totalled about 85,850 million won."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[254,355],"pp":"31","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, distribution, trade, labor."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[254,271],"pp":"31","s":"South Korea's 1948 crop is estimated near 6,000,000 metric tons, the highest since 1941, with food self-sufficiency expected by January 1949. Summer grain collection reached 95.8 percent of quota. The 7 July typhoon and floods damaged Cholla coastal levees. Fertilizer imports passed 548,000 tons and Korea Agricultural Association took over distribution. National Land Administration had sold 481,957 acres, covering 85 percent of available farms."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[272,281],"pp":"49","s":"Wholesale prices rose to 184 percent of the 1947 average in August, from 171 in July, ending five months of stability, led by grains, fish, raw cotton and salt. Controlled prices for rice, barley and wheat rose on 1 August and for salt on 15 August. Seoul retail prices roughly doubled for grains over August 1947. A new weighted urban worker index stood at 179 in August."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[282,294],"pp":"59","s":"Explains why 1947 South Korean output looks far below 1940 (incomplete reporting, home production of noodles and cloth), then reports July-August 1948 under the power shortage: flour 707 and 1,782 tons, paper mills short of pulp, cement halted, rubber shoes steady, salt hit by rains, and Monopoly Bureau tobacco revenue and cigarette paper problems."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[295,303],"pp":"72","s":"Coal output fell after the Soviets cut North Korean power on 14 May 1948: anthracite dropped from 68,612 tons in April to 38,887 in July, and Hwasun was flooded. Bituminous imports from Japan reached a record 104,055 tons in July. Covers August allocations, coal pitch stockpiling, Yong Wol, and gold, copper, tungsten, graphite, zinc and lead output."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[304,319],"pp":"81","s":"Describes the National Consumer Goods Office (est. 12 January 1948), which processed 1,095 allocations worth 2.18 billion won by 31 August, and the Federation of Financial Associations distributing goods to about 480,000 eligible employees. Also covers petroleum deliveries, salt supply for F/Y 1947-1949 (68,774 tons produced by 15 August), and barter with North Korea (950.9 million won inflow)."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[320,344],"pp":"97","s":"Private trade totaled about 3,643 million won in imports and 2,763 million in exports for January-July 1948, with paper the leading import and sea products and straw hats leading exports. Covers licensing rules, the Trustee Shipment plan, ship movements, government exports to Japan ($828,457), the US and Hong Kong, PX sales, and Civilian Supply imports (FLC, coal, food, salt, fertilizer, petroleum)."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[345,355],"pp":"122","s":"Surveys three years of labor: about 118,000 unionists in some 500 unions, mostly in rightist Tai Han, with Chun Pyung underground. Reviews labor laws, the NEB's wage policy and the Wage Stabilization Division (25 March 1948). July-August items: Pusan stevedore wage talks (350 won minimum), Yongwol election won by Tai Han (2,099 votes), Tai Han executive resignation, bank overtime violations and a labor education tour."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[356,389],"pp":"133","s":"Transportation, communications, public utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[356,367],"pp":"133","s":"Reviews the Department of Transportation since 1945: Japanese departure, shortages of parts and coal, and expenditures exceeding income. August revenue was the highest since liberation. Rail section gives 268 operable engines in August, higher freight income, passenger, tonnage, coal and employment tables. Also covers marine fleet (LSTs, Rainbow service), Japanese-era trucking, floods and rising marine expenditure."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[368,377],"pp":"145","s":"Describes the Department of Communications: Japanese-era organization, wartime neglect and the American rehabilitation of telephone exchanges. July revenue was the highest since liberation (June rate increase), while expenditures more than doubled to 196.9 million won. Covers staff cuts, postal savings and insurance, 37,183 telephones, telegraph declines, 623 post offices and 11 radio stations."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[378,383],"pp":"155","s":"Traces electric power from Japanese neglect through 1948. The North Korean shut-off of 14 May 1948 and the Chung Pyung transformer failure caused shortages, but deliveries recovered to 56.2 million KWH in July and 55.6 million in August. Covers plant output, the Yong Wol overhaul directive of 26 July, the Giroux Commission report of 23 July and Seoul and Pusan streetcar and bus service."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[384,389],"pp":"161","s":"Reviews public works since 1910 and under the Military Government: department status in August 1946, Koreanization and lessons learned. Summer 1948 brought preparation for transfer to the Korean Government and flood damage exceeding 2,000 million won. Covers road and bridge repairs and subsidy requests, the Emergency Water Conservation Board of 28 July, and fire statistics for the first half of 1948."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[390,405],"pp":"167","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[390,396],"pp":"167","s":"Reports Bank of Chosun note issue of about 30,000 million won at end of August and an SKIG overdraft of 22,506 million won. Revenues of 2,157 million won in August against expenditures of 1,924 million. Gives fiscal-year revenue and expenditure totals, F/Y 1948/49 appropriations of 35,264 million won including 6,260 million for civilian supply, and civilian supply assets of 38,465 million won."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[397,405],"pp":"174","s":"Covers private finance through July (August data unavailable). Cash balances were 2,244 million won; deposits reached 43,335 million won, 57 percent held by the public. Loans outstanding were 30,038 million won with record new lending in June and July. Life insurance in force passed 1,067 million won, and damage insurers wrote 9,243 new policies in July."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[406,443],"pp":"183","s":"Administration, political developments, legal affairs, police, inter-zone activities."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[406,410],"pp":"183","s":"The new government (Tai Han Min Kook) was proclaimed 15 August 1948, ending USAMGIK; the 10 May election drew over 90 per cent of 7,730,000 registered voters. The National Economic Board met five times, raising rice to 150 won per small mal and approving fare and per diem increases. Table shows MG personnel falling to 2,870 by 15 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[411,425],"pp":"188","s":"Covers the Constitution (12 July) and Government Organization Law, giving the President strong powers and state control of major industries. The Assembly elected Rhee Syngman President (180 votes) and Lee Si-young Vice-President, approved Lee Bum-suk as Prime Minister 110 to 84, and fought over cabinet picks. Moderates split three ways between Kim Koo, Kimm Kiu-sic and the North."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[426,433],"pp":"203","s":"The Department of Justice drafted Ordinances Nos. 203-219 and Executive Orders Nos. 24-28, covering litigation fees, the bar, rice collection, school districts and narcotics informers. Opinions addressed Japanese repatriate pensions and the Chosun Mining monopoly. Cheju-do riot trials were movable to Mokpo or Kwangju. Prisoners numbered 22,321 in July; 664 juveniles were in correction houses."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[434,439],"pp":"211","s":"Police concentrated on communist efforts to force signatures for the 25 August North Korean election, with arrests in Pusan, Chungchong and Cholla provinces and 200 officials held in Taegu. A torture death by Metropolitan Police caused Detective Bureau resignations. Chang Taik-sang became Foreign Minister, and a fourth attempt on his life failed on 24 July."},{"t":"Section 5. Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[440,443],"pp":"217","s":"North-South mail exchange continued at Tosong, with 9 trips in July-August 1948 and tabulated letter counts. Commerce Order No. 4 on overland interzonal barter took effect 2 August, and June imports included 516 tons of fertilizer. An American soldier was killed at the 38th parallel on 19 July. Radio Pyongyang attacked Rhee and promoted the 25 August election."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[444,485],"pp":"221","s":"Health, education, press, youth, women, culture."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[444,455],"pp":"221","s":"Floods and typhoons left 254,664 people needy, and hog cholera hit five provinces, with 43,428 hogs affected by mid-August. Two smallpox outbreaks were controlled, with about 420,000 vaccinated in Seoul. Reports cover nurse graduations, narcotics seizures, 28,698 kg of opium returned to Japan, vaccine stocks, tents for refugees, new orphanages and UNRRA grants."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[456,461],"pp":"233","s":"Three ordinances effective 12 August 1948 created school districts, nine-member school boards and local school taxation. The Department also reported teacher workshops reaching 7,500 teachers, a new elementary achievement test, scholarship screening, textbook output (421,660 copies in July), American Language Institute classes, vocational committees in Pusan, home economics sessions and adult education conferences."},{"t":"Section 3. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[462,469],"pp":"239","s":"The press dwelt on forming the new government: the Constitution, the police-under-Internal Affairs vote (108 to 88), rejection of Lee Yun-yung and approval of Lee Bum-suk as Premier, and attacks on Rhee's cabinet. It also covered the North Korean election, floods, Cheju-do riots, Dok Do compensation, and a table of 11 leading dailies among 58."},{"t":"Section 4. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[470,474],"pp":"247","s":"Describes Korean National Youth, Inc., chartered 9 October 1945 with Military Government backing, as a community-service alternative to terrorist youth groups. Membership reached 1,154,821 by 15 August 1948; members gave over 12,600,000 work-days of labor. Covers training, terror opposition, and the succession of Lee Jun-sic after Lee Bum-suk became Prime Minister."},{"t":"Section 5. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[475,478],"pp":"252","s":"Reports that the new Constitution and Military Government ordinances guarantee women equality, and that women now work in many professions. Notes Mrs. Rhee, Louise Yim as Minister of Commerce and Industry, teachers and students abroad, religious work, and ordinances against selling women. Describes primitive home life and the Women's Bureau's magazine, classes and Federation of Women's Clubs."},{"t":"Section 6. Culture","lv":2,"p":[479,485],"pp":"256","s":"Covers Hangul's reintroduction in schools, with problems of Chinese characters, orthography and printing, plus the Bible Society shipment and a new dictionary. Describes the American Language Institute's English courses (about 500 students). Reports Korea's first Olympic entry under its own flag at London, 67 athletes, 24th place with 15 points; pages 484-485 are blank."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-35","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-35-1948-08","s":"J","no":35,"d":"1948-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 35 (August 1948)","v":"","pg":173,"o":"Summation No. 35, the final issue of the series, covers August 1948 (statistics mostly for June-July) and carries a Review of the Third Occupation Year (1 September 1947 - 31 August 1948). Politically the month is dominated by the Ashida cabinet's cabinet order and planned revision of the National Public Service Law following MacArthur's July letter, labor unrest on the railways, argument over dissolving the Diet and a proposed centrist league, the Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee hearings (Nishio acquitted, Nakasone sentenced), the Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damage to textile mills, and MacArthur's address at the inauguration of the Republic of Korea. Economic parts cover SCAP's removal of export controls (15-18 August), a sterling-area trade plan, price stabilization, hoarded goods, the Reconstruction Finance Bank, holding-company dissolution and reparations shipments. Long statistical sections give production of coal, metals, chemicals, textiles, power, communications, labor, trade, prices, finance, science, patents, health and education, useful for quantifying recovery. The appendix surveys the third Occupation year.","h":["MacArthur addressed the 15 August inauguration of the Republic of Korea in Seoul, calling Korea's division an artificial barrier to be torn down.","July cabinet order denied government employees strikes and collective bargaining; Cabinet interpretation 11 August; Shikoku absenteeism cancelled 118 trains on 16 August.","Former deputy prime minister Nishio acquitted 27 August; Nakasone sentenced to four years 31 August in the Army Uniform Case.","SCAP authorized elimination of major Japanese Government controls on export trade, 15-18 August, letting foreign buyers deal directly with sellers.","Holding Company Liquidation Commission issued its first final reorganization order 22 August (Japan Soda Company split into six companies).","Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damaged 686 of 1,774 textile mills; Reconstruction Finance Bank supplied about 1.1 billion yen for repair.","Japanese B encephalitis hit about 4,100 people with 219 deaths by 28 August; equine encephalomyelitis killed 522 horses.","Review of third Occupation year: industrial production index rose from 43.1 to 59.9; Japan's cumulative trade deficit reached about 822.8 million dollars."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","National Public Service Law","SCAP","Republic of Korea inauguration","export controls","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","holding company dissolution","Fukui earthquake","coal production","price stabilization","war crimes trials","labor disputes","third Occupation year review","textile industry","Japanese B encephalitis"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Archive cover, notice that this is the final issue of the Summation, title page, table of contents and list of charts and tables (108 numbered charts)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Short digest of the month in four sections: SCAP organization, political, economic and social activities."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"One sentence: no change in the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP in August."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Digest of MacArthur at Seoul, constitutional revision, Public Service Law debate, Diet dissolution, centrist realignment, court decisions, dissolved ultranationalist groups, public safety figures and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,11],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of agriculture, mining, industrial output, rail unrest, power, communications, labor, trade controls lifted 15 August, food rations, finance, property control, antitrust and science items."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"15","s":"Digest of encephalitis epidemics, public assistance increases, repatriation of 44,400, Board of Education Law, university reorganization, religion and press items."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,31],"pp":"19","s":"Government and politics, public safety, legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,26],"pp":"21","s":"MacArthur and Ashida messages, constitutional revision study, Public Service Law cabinet order, dissolution disputes, party maneuvers, local elections, corruption hearings, purge cases and economic policy statements."},{"t":"Supreme Commander's Statement to Republic of Korea","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"","s":"Text of MacArthur's 15 August Seoul address to President Rhee: friendship since 1882, the artificial division of Korea, and defense of liberty."},{"t":"Prime Minister's Message on Anniversary of Defeat","lv":3,"p":[16,16],"pp":"","s":"Ashida's 15 August message: improving living conditions, falling inflation, and a call for a middle path and peace-loving reconstruction."},{"t":"Constitutional Revision Proposals","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"","s":"Diet leaders agree 20 August on a joint committee to study revision; six topics listed, including the Emperor's ceremonial role and Diet conflicts."},{"t":"Political Developments: National Public Service Law Discussion","lv":3,"p":[17,19],"pp":"","s":"Text of the cabinet order banning strikes and collective bargaining, labor and Communist reaction, Cabinet interpretation of 11 August, social democratic seven-point plan and cabinet disagreements."},{"t":"Problem of Diet Dissolution","lv":3,"p":[19,20],"pp":"","s":"Government parties split on dissolving the Diet; Ashida wants delay to end of year, Katayama and left-wing Social Democrats want a November election."},{"t":"Prime Minister's Address to Foreign Correspondents","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"","s":"Ashida's 27 August speech on food, clothing, slowing inflation, labor, political stability and middle-of-the-road policy."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"","s":"Debate over a Middle Course Political League, Democratic, Social Democratic, People's Cooperative and Democratic Liberal positions, and minor parties including the Communists."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"","s":"Recall of a Saitama village mayor upheld on a tied recount; independent elected governor in an August prefectural election."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions Investigations","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"","s":"Nishio acquitted, Nakasone Army Uniform Case sentences, Kano case, Area Disposal and coal-mine bribery hearings, new charges against Miki and Kitamura."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"","s":"Ultranationalist groups dissolved in August; 377,788 screened and 201,333 purged by month end; indictments for purge violations."},{"t":"Economic Plans and Policies","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"","s":"Government pledge to the ESB ten-point policy, financial reorganization plan, wage dispute over the National Personnel Authority, payroll cuts, party anti-inflation and reconstruction programs."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[27,29],"pp":"45","s":"Hoarded goods (5.95 million yen recovered in July), black-market and bribery cases, June crime and arrest statistics, police actions, prison population, fires, and transfer of offshore policing to Maritime Safety Board."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,31],"pp":"51","s":"Supreme Court caseload, judicial conferences, domestic relations court, atrocity investigation table, 70 suspects apprehended, 713 tried to date; seven trials in the period, two death sentences; IMTFE in recess."},{"t":"Part III: Business","lv":1,"p":[32,130],"pp":"50","s":"Fourteen economic sections with extensive production, trade, price, finance and science statistics."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[33,36],"pp":"57","s":"Agricultural cooperative program and cabinet orders, Kanto Plain soil survey, mulberry and illegal tobacco growing, livestock, June marine catch fall to 150,018 tons, tuna export rejections, petroleum allocations and whaling."},{"t":"Agricultural cooperatives and administration","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"","s":"Dissolution of agricultural associations, posters, and three August cabinet orders on property transfers to cooperatives."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[34,36],"pp":"","s":"Smaller sardine and mackerel catches, spoilage from lack of ice, a new purse seiner, August fuel allocations and 2,210 tons of whale products."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[37,42],"pp":"","s":"Forestry charts, August coal output 3,542,000 tons (down about 300,000), deliveries and stockpiles, lignite, crude oil strike effects and drilling, and June mineral output with mercury up."},{"t":"Coal, oil and mining industry","lv":3,"p":[39,42],"pp":"","s":"Coal production by district, deliveries 2,669,000 tons in July, stockpiles, lignite, crude oil and mine production."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Raw Materials","lv":2,"p":[43,50],"pp":"77","s":"July coke 264,112 tons; pig iron and rolled steel at postwar peaks; light metals, copper, zinc, petroleum refining, cement down 12 percent, chemicals, salt and pulp and paper figures."},{"t":"Coke and metal industries","lv":3,"p":[43,47],"pp":"","s":"Coke production, deliveries and stocks; iron, steel, aluminum, copper, zinc, tin and ferroalloys; petroleum products."},{"t":"Cement, chemicals, salt, pulp and paper","lv":3,"p":[47,50],"pp":"","s":"Cement output down for coal and transport shortages; 21 of 28 chemicals up; caustic soda postwar peak; salt output up 50.6 percent; paper postwar peak."},{"t":"Section 4. Machinery, Equipment and Construction","lv":2,"p":[51,56],"pp":"93","s":"Machine tools, small tools, bearings, industrial and textile machinery, rolling stock and railway cars, electrical and communications equipment, truck output 1,388, coal miners' housing and building permits."},{"t":"Section 5. Manufactured Goods","lv":2,"p":[57,63],"pp":"105","s":"June food processing, brewing, cigarettes up 12.3 percent, glass, ceramics, rubber, leather, handicrafts, business machines, musical instruments, watches, sewing machines, cosmetics, plus ship completions and the Soviet tug contract."},{"t":"Section 6. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[64,79],"pp":"119","s":"Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damage to mills, raw cotton imports, cotton, raw silk at postwar peak of 11,521 bales, rayon, wool, bast fibers, knit goods and dyeing, with stock and machinery tables."},{"t":"Earthquake damage","lv":3,"p":[64,65],"pp":"","s":"Survey recommends 1.514 billion yen for repairs; 686 of 1,774 mills damaged; Reconstruction Finance Bank to lend 1.12 billion yen."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[65,67],"pp":"","s":"Imports rose to 43.9 million pounds in July; spinners' supplies, yarn and fabric production and machinery."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[67,70],"pp":"","s":"Raw silk production and stocks, testing, reeling mills, cocoon supplies, spun silk yarn and silk fabrics."},{"t":"Artificial fibers and wool","lv":3,"p":[71,73],"pp":"","s":"Rayon pulp imports, caustic soda bottleneck, rayon yarn and fabric; raw wool imports from Australia and South America."},{"t":"Hard and bast fibers, spun and reprocessed materials, knit goods, sundries, dyeing","lv":3,"p":[74,79],"pp":"","s":"Rope and cordage, jute and flax, thrown-spun and reprocessed yarns, knit goods, fish nets and fabrics processed."},{"t":"Section 7. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[80,86],"pp":"151","s":"Railway unrest in Shikoku, 519.9 million passengers, coal consumption, directive to Japan to assume port operations, coastwise shipping, July electric power 2.94 billion kWh and gas industry figures."},{"t":"Railways and water transportation","lv":3,"p":[80,83],"pp":"","s":"Communist-led absenteeism on Shikoku, 956 discharged; 17 August directive on port and terminal operations; coastal and foreign cargo."},{"t":"Electric power and gas","lv":3,"p":[84,86],"pp":"","s":"Generation, consumption, coal allocations, plant construction and public gas production and distribution."},{"t":"Section 8. Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,91],"pp":"165","s":"1,386,084 telephones after Telephone Bond Law, radio frequency changes, 6,858,814 licensed radio listeners, postal volume drop after the 11 July rate rise, postal insurance and personnel."},{"t":"Section 9. Labor","lv":2,"p":[92,97],"pp":"176","s":"Labor rally against the Ashida cabinet, union statistics, Hokkaido and Imperial Oil disputes, labor schools, wage increases for Occupation workers, labor standards inspections, women and minors."},{"t":"Labor relations and disputes","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"","s":"Hokkaido railway walkout, coal wage agreement, electric power dispute, Imperial Oil mediation, Toho studio eviction."},{"t":"Employment and unemployment","lv":3,"p":[97,98],"pp":"","s":"Employment Security Advisory Committee, coal mine employment recheck, vocational training and unemployment insurance."},{"t":"Section 10. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[98,104],"pp":"187","s":"Export controls eliminated, sterling-area trade plan for 242 million dollars, 10 million dollars of cotton contracts, Siam talks, June trade by country and commodity, first-half deficit of 271 million dollars."},{"t":"Section 11. Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[105,110],"pp":"201","s":"Record summer vegetable harvest, fish and sugar distribution, three more price stabilization groups, textile prices up about 90 percent, consumer price index 342.9, incentive goods and surplus Army cigarettes; economic enforcement text continues on page 111."},{"t":"Section 12. Finance","lv":2,"p":[111,117],"pp":"213","s":"Economic Investigation Board created 1 August; notes in circulation 264 billion yen; Reconstruction Finance Bank plan; securities sales, closing of 18 control associations, tax revenue, first prison terms for tax evasion, national debt."},{"t":"Section 13. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[118,122],"pp":"227","s":"Looted property status, Attorney-General to liquidate dissolved organizations, custody accounts, restricted company expenditures, holding company orders, Fair Trade Commission filings and machine tool shipments to claimants."},{"t":"Section 14. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[123,130],"pp":"","s":"New Industrial Technique Management Council, research funds, travel of Japanese physicists to Princeton, standards, FEC access to Japanese processes, patent statistics, and a long background survey of Japan's climate."},{"t":"Patents and standards","lv":3,"p":[124,127],"pp":"","s":"Forty Japanese Standards issued in July, FEC technical access order of 6 August, patent and utility-model statistics and prospective inventions."},{"t":"Climate of Japan","lv":3,"p":[127,130],"pp":"","s":"Storm tracks, typhoons, vegetation zones, rice and crop limits, frost, precipitation, ocean currents, temperature and hail."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[131,151],"pp":"283","s":"Public health and welfare; education, religion, cultural resources and media."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[132,144],"pp":"255","s":"Japanese B encephalitis and equine epidemics, training courses, model health centers, medical supplies, narcotics, public assistance and Fukui earthquake aid, social security, vital statistics, population density and repatriation of 44,625."},{"t":"Medical administration and supply","lv":3,"p":[135,138],"pp":"","s":"Veterinary and inspection data, nursing courses, production of penicillin, DDT, X-ray equipment and distribution of supplies."},{"t":"Narcotics, public assistance and social security","lv":3,"p":[138,140],"pp":"","s":"Handler registrations, welfare payments, new allowance rates, LARA shipments, 40 percent medical fee increase and insurance statistics."},{"t":"Vital statistics, population and repatriation","lv":3,"p":[141,144],"pp":"","s":"June birth rate lowest since 1929; population 80,170,815; census density maps; 6,024,896 repatriated by 2 September."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[145,151],"pp":"281","s":"Teachers' union cabinet ordinance, Board of Education Law and October elections, curriculum recommendations, university reorganization, textbooks, religion, press comment, Asahi Saitama case, radio, films and SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[145,148],"pp":"","s":"Redefined relations with the teachers' union, Board of Education Law, 219 university applications, textbooks and libraries."},{"t":"Religion, cultural resources and media of expression","lv":3,"p":[148,151],"pp":"","s":"Buddhist reform, Christian missions, press and radio themes, motion pictures and two new SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of the Third Occupation Year (1 September 1947 - 31 August 1948)","lv":1,"p":[152,173],"pp":"295","s":"Year-end review covering SCAP organization, economic recovery, living conditions and progress of democratization."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[153,153],"pp":"297","s":"Restitution Advisory Committee established 13 April; no other organizational change during the year; chart of GHQ organization follows."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Recovery","lv":2,"p":[154,161],"pp":"299","s":"Coal and power, industrial production index 43.1 to 59.9, textiles, trade and sterling arrangements, transport, communications, finance, property control and reparations over the year."},{"t":"Section 3. Living Conditions","lv":2,"p":[162,164],"pp":"315","s":"Nutrition, ration distribution, housing and rents, textile distribution, wage and price inflation, disaster relief, disease campaigns, health and child welfare laws; welfare and repatriation continue to page 165."},{"t":"Section 4. Progress of Democratization","lv":2,"p":[165,173],"pp":"321","s":"Diet sessions and reforms, police and civil service laws, Tribunal and war crimes totals, agrarian reform, antitrust, labor, education, religion, media and science."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-35-in-K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":35,"d":"1948-09","d2":"1948-10","t":"Republic of Korea Economic Summation, No. 35 (September-October 1948)","v":"Bound in K-33","host":"K-33","rg":[[486,591]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:35' on PDF p. 486; economic sections only","pg":106,"o":"Republic of Korea Economic Summation No. 35 covers September and October 1948, the first economic report after USAMGIK handed authority to the Republic of Korea, and continues the SKIG Activities series. It records transfer of American-run departments to Korean ministries and the end of the Office of Property Custody under US control on 12 October. Main topics are the grain collection programs, ration-roll inflation, falling electric power, coal imports, prices, trade, currency growth and finances. Part I covers refugees and vested property sales; Part II food, prices, industry, mining, allocations and trade; Part III transport, communications, utilities and public works; Part IV finance. Historians will find provincial quotas, ration estimates, trade statistics, power figures, and the barter suspension with North Korea, in statistical tables.","h":["Summer grain collection reached 107,766 metric tons, 100.4 percent of quota; rice goal set at 1,125,000 tons.","Ration rolls listed 9,987,587 persons; officials suspected a 'ghost population', about 500,000 in Seoul.","Office of Property Custody passed to Korean custodian 12 October; 1,279 cumulative sales worth about 2,611 million won.","Electricity distribution fell about 18 percent below August; Yongwol steam plant test reached 80,400 KW.","Currency in circulation rose 2,500 million won between 31 August and 31 October.","Bituminous coal imports from Japan reached 100,015 tons in September.","Minister Louise Yim suspended barter trade with North Korea pending Assembly approval.","Wholesale prices fell to 177 percent of 1947 average in October from 184 in August."],"k":["Republic of Korea","economic summation","transfer of authority","grain collection","rice quota","ration rolls","vested property","Office of Property Custody","electric power","coal imports","prices","foreign trade","barter trade North Korea","refugees","government finance","public works"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[486,493],"pp":"","s":"Cover and blank digitization pages of Republic of Korea Economic Summation No. 35 (September-October 1948), prepared by the Civil Affairs Section, USAFIK. The foreword calls it the first economic summation under the Republic, successor to SKIG Activities, confined to economics, with one more edition (No. 36) planned through December 1948. A table of contents follows."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[494,495],"pp":"1","s":"Reports the shift of agencies from American to Korean management. Summer grain collection reached 107,766 tons (100.4 percent of goal) and a 1,125,000-ton rice goal was set, amid suspected 'ghost' ration rolls (about 500,000 in Seoul). Electricity fell 18 percent, exports were 45 percent of imports, currency rose 2,500 million won, and 1,279 vested-property sales were made."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[496,503],"pp":"","s":"Population and vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[496,498],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee entries across the 38th parallel: 11,718 in September, 7,764 in October; 1948 total to date 106,463. Cumulative movement shows net inbound gain of 1,588,030 persons since October 1945. Population map."},{"t":"Refugee Migration","lv":3,"p":[496,497],"pp":"3","s":"Recorded refugee entries across the 38th parallel were 11,718 in September (up 57 percent on August) and 7,764 in October (down 33 percent), attributed to colder weather. Arrivals peak each April to June. January to October totals fell 16 percent in 1947 against 1946 and 34 percent in 1948 against 1947. Table 1 gives monthly counts, excluding uncounted crossings."},{"t":"Population Movement","lv":3,"p":[496,497],"pp":"3","s":"Table 2 covers movement in and out of South Korea from 1 October 1945 to 31 October 1948: 887,301 outbound (179,920 Japanese military, 705,056 Japanese civilians, plus Chinese, Formosans and Okinawans) and 2,475,331 inbound, including 1,117,819 Koreans from Japan and 646,034 from North Korea. Net immigration added 1,588,030 residents."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[499,503],"pp":"6","s":"Office of Property Custody transferred to Korean custodian on 12 October 1948; 1,279 cumulative sales worth about 2,611 million won; sales of immovables halted 18 September; KRMC low-cost housing plans; real estate returned by US forces."},{"t":"Administration Division","lv":3,"p":[499,499],"pp":"6","s":"Strength reports show the Office of Property Custody shrinking ahead of its 12 October transfer to Korean custodians. American staff fell from 96 on 1 July (75 civilians, 21 soldiers) to 58 (50 and 8). Korean employees fell from 2,948 to 2,635: national office 500 to 468, provincial offices 2,448 to 2,167."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[500,500],"pp":"7","s":"Sales rose steadily from 1 July to 12 October: 581 sales worth 1,358.4 million won, bringing the cumulative total to 1,279 sales. Sales of immovables and vested shares stopped 18 September under the US-Korean financial agreement, and provincial activity slowed before recovering in late October. OPC asked the US Army to ship 1,600 tons of war-booty steel billets to the United States."},{"t":"Korean Residence Management Corporation","lv":3,"p":[501,501],"pp":"8","s":"Operations planned spring construction of 500 low-cost KRMC houses, types B (100), C (100) and D (300), costing about 240 million won; larger A houses were dropped as unaffordable. Financing combines subsidy, bank loans, lot sales and 20 percent down payments, with 10-year, 20-year and rental monthly schedules. Police posted 149,056 pyung of KRMC lots against unauthorized building."},{"t":"Real Estate Transfers","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"The department accepted real property released by US forces, giving Korean government agencies priority under Procedural Memorandums 21 and 24; most Army construction was leased to the Korean Army and Navy. Releases comprised 280 dependent houses (205 Army-built) and 625 other properties. Rent claims received before 15 September were mostly settled, but the 30 September quarterly rent could not be paid without Korean authorization."},{"t":"Policy Division","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"The Claims Section finished hearings on disputed property claims by 18 September: of 1,394 cases transferred from the Property Claims Commission, 825 were allowed, 356 disallowed and 213 returned. Forms and procedures were distributed on 24 August under Ordinance No. 210 for Allied nationals and Korean Zaidans (largely missionary groups) seeking return of seized property. By 12 October the Custodian held shares in 38 corporations."},{"t":"Comptroller Division","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"To train Koreans to run the Office of Property Custody, the division prepared manuals on taxes, loans and auditing procedures and translated them into Korean, supplemented by periodic bulletins for field staff. Korean auditors, supervised by an American from the national office, made quarterly audits of all provincial offices, plus audits of custodian-controlled firms tied to loan and capital-expenditure requests."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[504,553],"pp":"","s":"Food, prices, industry, mining, allocations and foreign trade."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[504,512],"pp":"11","s":"Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry took over on 13 September; summer grain 107,766 tons (100.4 percent of quota); rice collection quota 1,125,000 tons with 18.04-liter personal-transport rider; ration rolls inflated; fertilizer imports, forestry and land payments."},{"t":"Administrative Changes and Rice Collection","lv":3,"p":[504,505],"pp":"11","s":"The Republic's Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry took over the Department of Agriculture, Controller of Commodities and National Food Administration on 13 September and the National Land Administration on 11 October. The Assembly's 30 September bill allowed 18.04 liters of rice per trip. A 1,125,000-ton quota was split by province, with fertilizer certificates worth 400 won per bag. Summer grain hit 100.4 percent, Cheju only 4 percent."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[506,509],"pp":"13","s":"Ration rolls kept rising, up nearly 1,000,000 in a year to 9,987,597 in October, and padding by provincial officials was suspected; the Food Bureau released grain on 9,285,000 recipients for October. Grain releases fell with summer harvests and vegetables. Stocks fell to 170,551 tons by October. Imports included 18,328 tons of milo maize and 325 tons of substandard Australian canned goods for the Korean National Army."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[510,510],"pp":"17","s":"Fertilizer imports fell in October 1948, and all September-October shipments came from the United States. Imports from October 1946 through October 1948 totaled 630,777 metric tons (340,858 ammonium nitrate, 244,645 superphosphate). Of 521,776 tons received by provinces since July 1947, 461,344 tons (88 percent) were sold to farmers, 52,554 were in distribution channels and 7,878 were apparent losses."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[511,511],"pp":"18","s":"Forestry output for September and October 1948 was: plywood 10,000 and 12,000 pieces (3 by 6 feet); logs 19,700 and 21,300 cubic meters; fuelwood 313,889 and 293,714 cubic meters; charcoal 3,660 and 3,960 metric tons. Logs, plywood and charcoal rose in October while fuelwood fell."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[512,512],"pp":"19","s":"Purchasers of land through the National Land Administration pay 20 percent of annual principal-crop output for 15 years. October installments came to 10,801 metric tons (70.5 percent of 15,315 due), strong in Seoul and Pusan but only 24.4 percent at Iri. Rents brought in 477 tons (58.8 percent of 815). Shortfalls were carried over to the rice purchase program."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[513,521],"pp":"20","s":"Wholesale index fell to 177 percent of 1947 average in October from 184 in August; rice and grains declined after floods eased; cotton cloth and firewood rose; retail followed wholesale; controlled price lists."},{"t":"Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[514,515],"pp":"21","s":"Wholesale indexes rose slightly in September, then fell in October to 177 percent of the 1947 average against 184 in August, on lower grains, protein foods and miscellaneous items. Rice, barley, wheat, millet and soybeans dropped sharply from July as transport recovered from floods and summer crops arrived. Cotton sheeting and sookso silk rose on power-cut output losses; firewood jumped on seasonal demand."},{"t":"Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[514,519],"pp":"21","s":"Seoul retail prices of 26 family commodities and the nine-city indexes followed wholesale prices. Grains fell by late October; cleaned rice went from 2,480 won per mal in September to 1,800. Firewood, Chinese cabbage and Korean turnips rose sharply for kimchi season. Table 6 put the national retail index at 175 in October, with fuel at 230."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[520,521],"pp":"27","s":"Controlled prices (table 7) were set by the former National Price Administration, now the Bureau of Prices in the Office of Planning of the Republic. No new prices were set and no commodities added after the Republic took over. The table lists food, textiles, footwear, fuel and miscellaneous items, such as cleaned rice at 300 won per large mal and nails at 5,200."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[522,529],"pp":"29","s":"Output improved slightly in September and October despite the power shortage, as industry adapted and added generators. Flour, noodles, coal briquettes (15,849 tons in October), electric insulators and oxygen set 1948 highs. Cement stopped in early November for lack of power; no carbide was made. Brick and rubber shoes rose, truck tires fell, paper was unchanged, and salt output stayed below its early-summer peak."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[530,536],"pp":"37","s":"Anthracite output fell for fifth month, 51 percent below April; lignite up to 5,496 tons; bituminous imports from Japan 100,015 tons in September; gold, silver, copper, tungsten and graphite recovered."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[530,532],"pp":"37","s":"South Korean anthracite is poor quality and mines never recovered from wartime deterioration. September output fell for the fifth straight month, 51 percent below April, because of power shortage. Lignite reached 5,496 tons, best since December 1947. The 70,500-ton quota proved unrealistic; Samchuk made 21,650 tons, Hwasun stayed weak after flooding, and Yongwol mine reached 19,636 short tons in October."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal and Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[533,533],"pp":"40","s":"Bituminous coal imports from Japan reached 100,015 metric tons in September, close to the post-liberation July peak and 73 percent above the 1947 monthly average; October imports (90,297 tons) fell 10 percent. Deliveries to consumers differ from production, import and allocation figures because of pilferage, evaporation, waste and transit losses. September supply was 18,474 tons of anthracite and 64,566 of bituminous."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[534,536],"pp":"41","s":"Production of minerals other than coal recovered in September from low summer levels. Gold rose to 15.9 kg and silver to 129.6 kg; copper concentrate reached 276 tons; tungsten ore 119.4 tons; zinc and lead resumed after flood stoppage in June to August (31.8 and 33.8 tons of ore). Tables 6 to 10 also cover crystalline graphite grades."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[537,546],"pp":"44","s":"Allocations Bureau of Office of Planning processed 1,211 allocations worth 2,551.8 million won; 37,181 tons of salt distributed; petroleum deliveries and prewar consumption; barter trade with North Korea suspended then resumed under restrictions."},{"t":"Allocation of Consumer Goods","lv":3,"p":[537,537],"pp":"44","s":"The Allocations Bureau of the new Office of Planning replaced the National Consumer Goods Office on 16 August. By end of October it had processed 1,211 allocations worth 2,551.8 million won, with farmers 37 percent. In September and October, 116 allocations worth 367.4 million won were made, over 80 percent incentive goods for farmers. October imports were 340,000 woolen drawers worth 139.4 million won."},{"t":"Distribution of Controlled Commodities","lv":3,"p":[538,538],"pp":"45","s":"Federation of Financial Associations purchases of controlled goods for government and vested-industry employees were tabulated. Purchases totaled 315.95 million won in September and 117.75 million in October, mainly cotton cloth and underwear, against 3,577.8 million since July 1946. Tables 6 and 7 show distribution from national to provincial FFA and onward to local associations, Table 6 including year-end bonus goods."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[538,538],"pp":"45","s":"The Monopoly Bureau reported 37,181 metric tons of salt distributed in September and October, mostly kimchi rations (28.3 million kg in October) for the winter. Stock on 31 October was 89,927 tons. Imports were 9,263 tons through Civilian Supply and 143 tons privately. Government salterns made 14,084 tons in the two months, 88,637 tons since April, or 66 percent of the F/Y 1948/49 estimate."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[538,545],"pp":"45","s":"The Petroleum Distribution Agency delivered less than allocated, for example 6.48 million liters of gasoline in October against 7.78 million allocated, owing to stock shortages and poor Pusan terminal facilities, though better than June and July. National agencies such as the Navy, Coast Guard and Marine Bureau drew separate quantities. Korean use fell from 409 million liters in 1937 to 48 million in 1944. Sales tables follow."},{"t":"Barter Trade with North Korea","lv":3,"p":[546,546],"pp":"53","s":"Minister of Commerce and Industry Louise Yim ordered the Bureau of Commerce to suspend trade with North Korea pending National Assembly approval, adopt a new import-export program, and bar exports of food, rubber, cotton cloth, ginned cotton and petroleum. By end of October the Assembly had approved and barter resumed under modified rules, mainly manufactured goods, to keep raw materials from the north."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[547,553],"pp":"54","s":"Private trade: imports up 22 percent and exports up 80 percent in September, exports 45 percent of imports; government shipments to US, Hong Kong and Japan; souvenir sales to Americans."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[547,550],"pp":"54","s":"August imports rose 118 percent over July while exports fell 18 percent; in September imports rose 22 percent and exports over 80 percent, giving exports 45 percent of imports. September imports were 1,155 million won, driven by newsprint, raw rubber, thread and plate glass; exports were 516 million, led by shellfish and agar. Hong Kong took over 93 percent of exports; Inchon handled about 96 percent of imports."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[551,552],"pp":"58","s":"No government shipments went to the United States from mid-August through September; over 3,600 tons followed in October (mostly 3,595.6 tons of scrap metal, plus concentrates and agar, worth $106,941). Hong Kong received agar agar and apples. Over 5,800 tons went to Japan from 15 August to 31 October, chiefly 4,108 tons of amorphous graphite. Pusan stocks fell to 1,422 tons."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[553,553],"pp":"60","s":"Sales of Korean-made souvenirs such as brassware, lacquer and postcards continued at the Army Exchange Service PX in Seoul through August, September and October, declining late in October as the PX returned to basic necessities. Deliveries were 10,414 pieces ($11,274) in August, 4,850 ($5,399) in September and 5,320 ($4,259) in October."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[554,572],"pp":"","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[554,560],"pp":"61","s":"Transportation department turned over 15 August; September expenditures rose 61 percent to postliberation high; railroad passengers up, tonnage slightly down, locomotives and employment declines, coal use up."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[554,559],"pp":"61","s":"The Department of Transportation passed to the Republic on 15 August; Min He-sik resigned 6 October and Ho Chung succeeded him. Ministry expenditures peaked in September (1,418.6 million won) on winter stockpiling and fell sharply in October. Railroad receipts rose in September, then dropped. Passenger and freight traffic rose, locomotives operable fell to 256, repairs increased, and employment declined while wages rose."},{"t":"Marine, Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[560,560],"pp":"67","s":"Railroad coal consumption rose to 45,919 metric tons in September and 49,923 in October, still well below 1947, with economy indexes worse because locomotive run fell. Marine income gained to 28.6 and 32.4 million won; September expenditures spiked to 206 million won on winter stockpiling, then fell to 28 million in October."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[561,566],"pp":"68","s":"The Department of Communications passed to the Republic of Korea on 13 September under Minister Yoon Suk-ku. September revenue neared double the year before on higher telephone rates; expenditure hit a postwar high of 201 million won, with 12,570 employees. Postal depositors rose 153,000; Seoul-Shanghai radio-telephone tests began and RCA training was arranged."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[567,569],"pp":"74","s":"Power deliveries were 45.6 million KWH in September and 46.1 million in October, about 18 percent below August, all from southern sources. Yongwol and Pusan plants were down for repairs in September; Yongwol's rehabilitated plant carried 80,400 KW in a 23 October test. A seven-man Korean Power Mission under Loyal R. Millburn arrived 19 October."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[570,572],"pp":"77","s":"Public Works became a bureau under Home Affairs on 13 September; the Fire Commissioners went to the National Police. Highway projects used 82,677 man-days and 41.3 million won; eight bridges advanced, Waegwon finished. Water plants in many cities were repaired, rates raised in four cities, and river works planned at 82.4 million won."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[573,585],"pp":"","s":"Government and non-government finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[573,578],"pp":"80","s":"Notes outstanding rose 2,500 million won from August to October, with over 1,500 million for Monopoly tobacco purchases. Spending exceeded revenue by 3,695 million won in September and 878 million in October, overdraft reaching 38,400 million. Appropriations totaled 43,749 million won by 12 November; Civilian Supply net proceeds were 40,971 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[579,585],"pp":"86","s":"Deposit balances rose since July to 52,328 million won at end of September: 38 percent government, 11 inter-bank, 51 public. Bank of Chosun held nearly 36 percent; Chobeung led public deposits. Cash balances reached 4,691.9 million. Loans outstanding fell 960 million won to 27,441 million; life and damage insurance continued to grow."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[586,591],"pp":"","s":"Back matter of the scanned volume: blank pages marked only 'Digitized by Google', a library due-date slip with a 2010 date, and a closing page with an English-information note. No report content or figures."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"}];
